/* The SBR mind map: SBR > the exam, the syllabus areas > chapters > sections > points. Built by ~/study/sbr/map/build.py from the
   Study Quest notes (my own wording) and the chapters shaped by hand; edit those, not this file. */
"use strict";
window.SbrMap = {"x":"root","t":"SBR","d":"Strategic Business Reporting (INT): the whole syllabus as one map.","k":[{"x":"group","t":"The exam","k":[{"x":"group","t":"3 hours 15 minutes: four compulsory questions; the pass mark is 50%","i":"57b97482a3"},{"x":"group","t":"Question 1 (30 marks): groups, every time","k":[{"x":"group","t":"A spreadsheet holds a draft consolidated statement to correct, worth 10–14 marks","i":"763f687fc8"},{"x":"group","t":"You also explain and discuss the numbers","i":"8b07179c5d"},{"x":"group","t":"Other topics can come into it too","i":"8077cfe960"}],"i":"73f70138a3"},{"x":"group","t":"Question 2 (20 marks): the reporting and the ethical implications of a scenario","k":[{"x":"group","t":"2 professional marks for applying the ethical principles to the facts","i":"04fd1073ad"}],"i":"38eb367d83"},{"x":"group","t":"Section B: two questions of 25 marks, on any part of the syllabus","k":[{"x":"group","t":"One always tests analysis, as a whole question or a part, with 2 professional marks","i":"df111faadf"}],"i":"9bf5c820d4"},{"x":"group","t":"About 1.95 minutes a mark (1.8 if you keep 15 minutes for reading)","i":"f9a42178f5"},{"x":"group","t":"Marks come mostly from applying the rules to the scenario, not from stating them","i":"53c35b3fff"}],"i":"0b7c5d04ad"},{"x":"area","a":"A","t":"A · Ethical and professional principles","k":[{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 2: The professional and ethical duty of the accountant","ch":2,"d":"Study Text pages 29–40.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"2.1 Ethics: the code and pressures","s":"sbr-02-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#0","x":"card","t":"Rules-based standards risk a 'rule-book mentality': following the letter, not the spirit","d":"Reference: Kaplan, approaches to standard setting. With detailed rules, people structure deals to just miss a rule, so the accounts no longer show the substance.","i":"180d933dc8"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#1","x":"card","t":"Principles-based IFRS creates ethical challenges because it relies on professional judgement","d":"Reference: Kaplan, approaches to standard setting; ACCA Code. Where judgement is needed, bias can creep in. That is why the ACCA Code matters so much in SBR.","i":"e4a0a91a9c"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#2","x":"card","t":"Integrity means being straightforward and honest in all relationships","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). E.g. telling the board you can implement new software when you have no experience of it breaches integrity.","i":"b89afe3397"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#3","x":"card","t":"Objectivity means not letting bias, conflicts of interest or undue influence override judgement","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). E.g. a finance director choosing a valuation because the board needs a stronger balance sheet for a loan.","i":"4aa4498802"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#4","x":"card","t":"Professional competence and due care means keeping knowledge and skills up to date and acting diligently","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Standards change constantly (e.g. IFRS 18 replacing IAS 1). Not knowing the rules is itself an ethical failure.","i":"2b00784525"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#5","x":"card","t":"Confidentiality means not disclosing information without authority, and not using it for personal advantage or for third parties","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Disclosure is allowed only with proper authority or a legal or professional right or duty to disclose.","i":"b1751c027e"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#6","x":"card","t":"Professional behaviour means complying with laws and avoiding anything that discredits the profession","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). E.g. ignoring new data privacy laws when introducing staff-monitoring software.","i":"a95fcc37b7"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#7","x":"card","t":"The three classic incentives to misstate are profit bonuses, getting finance, and achieving a listing","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Bonuses push profit up (or into next year once targets are hit); lenders and listings reward overstated assets and profits.","i":"43e8f4d823"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#8","x":"card","t":"Accountants stay competent through CPD, such as technical articles, seminars and training courses","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). CPD = continuing professional development. Without it, material errors creep into the statements.","i":"ce465acbe0"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#9","x":"card","t":"Insider trading is trading shares using confidential, non-public information","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). It breaches confidentiality (using information for personal gain) and integrity. Trading on public information is fine.","i":"50eda3bc4a"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#10","x":"card","t":"In the digital age, competence includes handling new software, AI and distributed ledgers","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Accountants must be honest about whether they can handle new technology and large volumes of sensitive data.","i":"03266d1791"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#11","x":"card","t":"The ethical risks of AI include system failure, job losses and bias","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). E.g. AI reviewing expense claims might unfairly target certain employees.","i":"6213c36360"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#12","x":"card","t":"Unethical reporting can lead to fines, loss of reputation, director bans, expulsion and prison","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Accountants are personally punished, not just their companies.","i":"8c73f5a045"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#13","x":"card","t":"Ethical behaviour can increase profit because it attracts green investors, ethical customers and good staff","d":"Reference: Kaplan, ethics and profit; ACCA Code. CSR costs money in the short term but can make the business more sustainable and more valuable.","i":"68b4989b30"},{"s":"sbr-02-1","c":"sbr-02-1#14","x":"card","t":"An ACCA student pressured to misstate should remind the boss of the principles, document it, then use the ACCA helpline or legal advice","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Temporary contracts create a self-interest threat, but students are still bound by the Code. Exam: Q2 always tests ethics (plus 2 professional marks).","i":"d89cf7e058"}],"i":"b488f82715"},{"x":"stage","t":"2.2 Ethics: more concepts","s":"sbr-02-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#0","x":"card","t":"Ethics matters in reporting because many user groups rely on the statements to make economic decisions, so they must not be misled","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Personal ethical beliefs can be too simple for complex real cases, which is why accountants study ethics.","i":"6dac3b680c"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#1","x":"card","t":"The ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct gives members a set of moral guidelines built on five fundamental principles","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Integrity, objectivity, professional competence and due care, confidentiality, professional behaviour.","i":"d5c72c371b"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#2","x":"card","t":"Following the Code makes it more likely the statements give a faithful representation, because the needs of users are put first","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework Chapter 2; ACCA Code. The Framework's faithful representation (complete, neutral, free from error) needs ethical preparers.","i":"48a1733bc6"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#3","x":"card","t":"Pressure from the board, a bonus or job insecurity creates a self-interest threat to objectivity and integrity","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). E.g. a finance director pushed to strengthen the balance sheet before raising finance.","i":"f0aac0b368"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#4","x":"card","t":"An employee on a temporary contract who fears losing the job faces a self-interest threat, but is still bound by the Code (students too)","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Being junior or a student is no excuse for going along with a misstatement.","i":"669c9c5971"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#5","x":"card","t":"The public trusts accountants because of their professional status, so breaking that trust harms the entity's reputation and the whole profession","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Stakeholders react more and more to a company's ethical stance.","i":"99d9572c9a"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#6","x":"card","t":"AI can extract data into reports, spot spending anomalies (fraud) and detect non-compliance, e.g. errors in expense claims, orders and invoices","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). It frees accountants for strategy and problem-solving.","i":"b8ed152e43"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#7","x":"card","t":"Distributed ledger technology means ledgers with no central store, recording transactions in many places at once","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Errors can have serious economic effects, so competence and honesty about one's abilities matter.","i":"305a019f8b"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#8","x":"card","t":"Data held by accountants (staff, customers, suppliers) must be protected because theft causes financial loss, reputational damage and legal consequences","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Accountants must know local and international data laws and design controls to comply.","i":"d565b19a0f"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#9","x":"card","t":"In SBR the ethics element appears in Question 2, usually 5–10 marks, plus 2 professional marks","d":"Reference: ACCA SBR exam format. Marks come from applying principles to the scenario and recommending how to resolve the dilemma.","i":"4dc33cff9c"},{"s":"sbr-02-2","c":"sbr-02-2#10","x":"card","t":"Ethics and the duty to maximise shareholder wealth can seem to conflict, but modern companies are seen as corporate citizens, and CSR can improve long-term results","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Ethical behaviour attracts green investors, ethical customers and staff; unethical behaviour invites boycotts.","i":"d703575146"}],"i":"caec9a268a"},{"x":"stage","t":"2.3 The five threats","s":"sbr-02-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-02-3","c":"sbr-02-3#0","x":"card","t":"A self-interest threat arises when the accountant or a close associate could benefit, financially or otherwise, from a relationship or transaction","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). It's an incentive to misstate. E.g. a controller who fears a reported covenant breach would close the company, and with it her job.","i":"8fe11263e8"},{"s":"sbr-02-3","c":"sbr-02-3#1","x":"card","t":"A self-review threat arises when an accountant has to evaluate their own earlier work or judgements","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). E.g. doing the goodwill impairment review on an acquisition you valued yourself: an impairment would expose your earlier work.","i":"c2e3055708"},{"s":"sbr-02-3","c":"sbr-02-3#2","x":"card","t":"An intimidation threat arises when objectivity is deterred by pressure, whether actual or only perceived","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). It stops independent judgement and scepticism. E.g. losing duties and a promised promotion after reporting a control weakness, so later issues go unreported.","i":"03f944a2a7"},{"s":"sbr-02-3","c":"sbr-02-3#3","x":"card","t":"A familiarity threat comes from a long or close relationship, leaving the accountant too accepting of the other person's work or too sympathetic to their interests","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). E.g. a superficial check of a close colleague's reconciliations after 15 years in the same department.","i":"ab13c98022"},{"s":"sbr-02-3","c":"sbr-02-3#4","x":"card","t":"An advocacy threat arises when an accountant promotes or defends a client's or employer's position so far that objectivity is compromised","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). E.g. an audit firm giving evidence in court for its client against the tax authorities: it may no longer be seen as independent.","i":"88f6bde4ae"}],"i":"34a71d9dc2"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"2.S Ethics in order","s":"sbr-02-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-02-structure-1","c":"sbr-02-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Answering an SBR ethics question","d":"Reference: ACCA SBR exam format. Marks go to applying the principles to the facts and to practical recommendations, not to listing the Code.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"State the correct accounting treatment","i":"4d7b592a1c"},{"x":"step","t":"Explain why the proposed treatment is wrong","i":"b23b1265b2"},{"x":"step","t":"Identify the principles at risk and the threat (e.g. self-interest)","i":"b5a4de1392"},{"x":"step","t":"Explain the consequences for users, the company and the profession","i":"fc4cdf3395"},{"x":"step","t":"Recommend what the accountant should do","i":"9e5547a3d8"}],"i":"b6990322f8"},{"s":"sbr-02-structure-1","c":"sbr-02-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"What a pressured accountant should do, step by step","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Escalate in proportion: persuade first, keep a record, then get outside help.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Remind the superior of the principles and try to persuade them","i":"07276a10f9"},{"x":"step","t":"Document the discussion","i":"e3e4fc0b5c"},{"x":"step","t":"If unresolved, contact the ACCA ethics helpline","i":"9e3d4edd1d"},{"x":"step","t":"Take legal advice before any further action","i":"645234d2ff"}],"i":"33bd7d4146"},{"s":"sbr-02-structure-1","c":"sbr-02-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Introducing new technology responsibly","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Rushing in without competence, legal checks or transparency breaches several principles at once.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Understand the technology and its risks","i":"1f8a453932"},{"x":"step","t":"Check it complies with the laws (e.g. data and privacy)","i":"ee636ad372"},{"x":"step","t":"Be honest about your experience and arrange training","i":"6f7bde6771"},{"x":"step","t":"Tell the people affected (e.g. staff being monitored)","i":"7c43f0ff2f"},{"x":"step","t":"Only then implement it","i":"ea749d2bbc"}],"i":"452ebff0fa"},{"s":"sbr-02-structure-1","c":"sbr-02-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"The incentive behind a misstatement: from pressure to effect","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Spotting the incentive explains why the accounting choice is suspicious.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"A goal or pressure (bonus, loan, listing)","i":"9ae584020d"},{"x":"step","t":"A biased choice of policy, estimate or valuation","i":"a8fb64f970"},{"x":"step","t":"Overstated assets or profits","i":"dbbdb0de3a"},{"x":"step","t":"Users misled in their decisions","i":"84555fd9ca"}],"i":"a7ad1763d8"}],"i":"a3b3842fc5"}],"i":"b92db5c164"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"2.C Which principle?","s":"sbr-02-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Telling the board new software will be easy to install, with no experience of it","a":"Integrity","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Integrity: not being straightforward and honest.","i":"8eea5f1130"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Choosing a valuation method because the board wants a stronger balance sheet","a":"Objectivity","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Objectivity: undue influence overriding judgement (integrity too).","i":"1117e57ef7"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Not knowing that recent data-privacy law changes apply to a new system","a":"Professional competence and due care","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Competence and due care: keeping knowledge up to date.","i":"dac93697f7"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Selling company shares before bad results are published","a":"Confidentiality","d":"Reference: ACCA Code (confidentiality). Confidentiality: using confidential information for personal advantage (integrity too).","i":"547a6f8c3e"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Introducing staff-monitoring software that breaks the law","a":"Professional behaviour","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Professional behaviour: complying with laws and regulations.","i":"6773b4edc1"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Not applying a new standard because nobody read the update","a":"Professional competence and due care","d":"Reference: ACCA Code (professional competence and due care). Competence: CPD (articles, seminars, courses) keeps knowledge current.","i":"41ed643a22"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Telling a friend a client is about to be taken over","a":"Confidentiality","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Confidentiality: disclosing without authority or a duty to do so.","i":"de6e1ce5cb"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Shifting profit into next year once this year's bonus target is reached","a":"Objectivity","d":"Reference: Kaplan, ethics and profit; ACCA Code. Objectivity and integrity: bias from the bonus.","i":"07d0719359"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Ignoring a quoted share price in favour of a higher self-made valuation","a":"Objectivity","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement; ACCA Code (objectivity). Objectivity (and competence if IFRS 13 isn't understood).","i":"b3b89969ff"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"A finance director's lifestyle bringing the firm into the news for the wrong reasons","a":"Professional behaviour","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Professional behaviour: avoiding anything that discredits the profession.","i":"8b064cec4c"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"The finance director wants to show a bank higher assets to secure a loan","a":"Financing incentive","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Lenders prefer valuable assets as security.","i":"bc0c620c35"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-1","c":"sbr-02-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"A company about to float wants higher profits and assets","a":"Listing incentive","d":"Reference: Kaplan, ethics and profit; ACCA Code. It wants to maximise what investors pay.","i":"424f042654"}],"i":"814029616e"},{"x":"stage","t":"2.C2 Which threat?","s":"sbr-02-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"A controller's bonus depends on the profit figure she is reporting","a":"Self-interest","d":"Reference: Kaplan, ethics and profit; ACCA Code. Self-interest: she benefits directly from the number. P/L category: Operating (revenue). Users: Both years are misstated, distorting growth and margin trends that investors use to forecast.","i":"0e85517c06"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"An accountant holds shares in the company whose results she prepares","a":"Self-interest","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Self-interest: a financial interest in the outcome.","i":"60ee3a783f"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"An accountant reviews the impairment of an asset she valued herself last year","a":"Self-review","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Self-review: admitting an impairment reflects on her own earlier work.","i":"d7aefc4e98"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"The designer of the inventory system is asked to test its controls","a":"Self-review","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Self-review: she's checking her own work.","i":"650c5e4418"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"A junior is told her probation will fail if she raises the cut-off error again","a":"Intimidation","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Intimidation: pressure to stay silent.","i":"ddeb9fefee"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"The chief executive hints that a contract won't be renewed unless a provision is dropped","a":"Intimidation","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Intimidation: the threat needn't be spelled out.","i":"58db4b13f2"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"A controller signs off payroll reconciliations unchecked after 12 years beside the payroll manager","a":"Familiarity","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Familiarity: too trusting after a long relationship.","i":"d786e2b635"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"An accountant waves through a supplier's invoices because its owner is an old friend","a":"Familiarity","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Familiarity: sympathy for the other person's interests.","i":"da3e75d8c1"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"An auditor speaks to investors in favour of a client's share issue","a":"Advocacy","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Advocacy: promoting the client's position.","i":"dc92d1586e"},{"s":"sbr-02-compare-2","c":"sbr-02-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"A finance director publicly defends the company's accounting against a regulator's challenge, whatever the facts","a":"Advocacy","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Advocacy: defending a position to the point where objectivity is lost.","i":"b0ad372fb3"}],"i":"a843fa7fa8"}],"i":"879af6db2e"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"2.W Ethics traps","s":"sbr-02-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"Rules-based standards always give the most faithful picture.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, approaches to standard setting. They can encourage following the letter not the spirit, so the substance is lost.","i":"0f8648c836"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Principles-based standards reduce the need for ethics.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, approaches to standard setting; ACCA Code. They need more judgement, which makes ethics more important.","i":"c53678aa38"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Confidential information may never be disclosed.","v":false,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). It can be disclosed with proper authority or a legal or professional right or duty.","i":"2059ba62f5"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Employees may trade in their company's shares using public information.","v":true,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). The ethical problem is trading on information that isn't public.","i":"b8d61cbe0e"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"ACCA students aren't bound by the Code until they qualify.","v":false,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Students are bound by it too.","i":"28ed6581e3"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"If the finance director orders it, a junior accountant is no longer responsible.","v":false,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). They should remind, persuade, document, and escalate (helpline, legal advice).","i":"3f1af09c40"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"A company's plan to hold shares long-term justifies valuing them above the quoted price.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement; ACCA Code (objectivity). Fair value is market-based; intention is irrelevant. Using a level 3 input to inflate value breaches IFRS 13 and integrity.","i":"0267eca3cf"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Not knowing the requirements of a standard can itself be an ethical failure.","v":true,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). It breaches professional competence and due care.","i":"9530f07350"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Ethical behaviour always lowers profit.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, ethics and profit; ACCA Code. It can raise costs, but it can attract investors, customers and staff and make the business more sustainable.","i":"65edee6610"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Accountants who deliberately misstate statements can be jailed.","v":true,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Also fined, expelled, banned as directors, and lose their reputation.","i":"9217d26e50"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"Using AI to monitor staff raises no ethical issues if it improves productivity.","v":false,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Legality, transparency with staff, fairness and possible bias all matter.","i":"ef4976b03c"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"The ethics marks in SBR reward listing the five principles.","v":false,"d":"Reference: ACCA SBR exam format. They reward applying them to the scenario and recommending actions.","i":"db53bc301e"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"An intimidation threat can exist even if nobody has actually made a threat.","v":true,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Pressure can be actual or only perceived; the fear of it can be enough.","i":"80c78b6db4"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#13","x":"trap","t":"A self-interest threat can come from what a close associate stands to gain, not just the accountant.","v":true,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). The benefit can go to the accountant or a close associate, and needn't be financial.","i":"d138f6e5ad"},{"s":"sbr-02-watch-1","c":"sbr-02-watch-1#14","x":"trap","t":"Reviewing your own earlier work is still a threat even if you are determined to be fair.","v":true,"d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). A threat is about the situation: you may be reluctant to expose weaknesses in your own work.","i":"9d5d1c911a"}],"i":"4f54c0fedd"}],"i":"dcccd13eb6"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"2.A Ethics scenarios","s":"sbr-02-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"A design firm's finance director, a qualified accountant, wants to install AI software that tracks staff emails and typing. She has never used it but tells the board it's easy. New privacy laws apply.","a":"Slow down: check the law, be open about her inexperience, train and tell staff","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Rule: an accountant must be honest, competent and law-abiding. Applied: (1) integrity: promising the board it's easy with no experience is not straightforward; (2) competence and due care: she must understand the new privacy laws before using staff data; (3) professional behaviour: breaking those laws would discredit the profession; (4) fairness: staff should be told they'll be monitored, or trust and reputation suffer. So: check the law, admit her inexperience, get training, consult staff, then decide. P/L category: Software and training costs go to Operating. Users: Legal and reputational risk could hit future cash flows and staff retention. Common mistake: judging only the productivity benefit.","i":"2fa6b1c0b2"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"A company holds listed shares at FVOCI. The finance director tells a temporary junior to value them by discounting expected dividends, which gives more than the quoted price, because a loan is being sought.","a":"Use the quoted price (level 1); the junior should escalate","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement; ACCA Code (objectivity, integrity). Accounting: IFRS 13 fair value is market-based, so the company's plan to hold doesn't matter; a quoted price in an active market (level 1) must be used, and a self-made dividend model (level 3) can't replace it. Ethics: the push for a loan creates a self-interest threat, and choosing a method because it gives a higher number breaches objectivity and integrity (or competence, if the director doesn't know the rule). The junior on a temporary contract feels pressure too, but students are bound by the Code: remind the director of the rules, record the discussion, then use the ACCA helpline and legal advice if needed. P/L category: None: gains on FVOCI equity investments go to OCI and are never recycled. Users: Overstated investments would mislead lenders about security and gearing. Common mistake: 'the director told me to'.","i":"79a3aef751"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A director plans to sell shares in his company next week, knowing results will be far below expectations.","a":"Don't trade: it's insider dealing","d":"Reference: ACCA Code (confidentiality, integrity). Rule: confidential information must not be used for personal advantage. Applied: the director knows results are worse than the market expects; selling before they're published lets him avoid a loss at other shareholders' expense. That's insider trading: illegal, a breach of confidentiality and a lack of integrity. Size doesn't matter. P/L category: None for the company. Users: Insider dealing damages market confidence and harms other shareholders. Common mistake: 'they're his own shares'.","i":"15d2dac63b"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"A finance director's bonus depends on this year's profit. The target has already been met, so she wants to push some revenue into next year.","a":"Refuse: it's biased and misstates both years","d":"Reference: Kaplan, ethics and profit; ACCA Code. Rule: income and expenses belong in the period they occur, and preparers must be unbiased. Applied: moving revenue to next year because this year's bonus target is already met understates this year and overstates next; the motive is personal (a self-interest threat), breaching objectivity and integrity. A note in the accounts doesn't fix a wrong figure. P/L category: Operating (revenue). Users: Both years are misstated, distorting growth and margin trends that investors use to forecast. Common mistake: 'it all evens out over two years'.","i":"777606a972"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A company wants to raise a loan and the board asks for higher asset values. The finance director has no basis to revalue.","a":"Refuse and explain the principles; record the discussion","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Rule: values must have a proper basis (e.g. IFRS 13 or IAS 16 rules), not be chosen to please a lender. Applied: raising asset values without evidence to win a loan is a misstatement; lenders would be misled. The finance director should refuse, explain the principles (objectivity, integrity), and record the discussion; if pressure continues, escalate. P/L category: A PPE revaluation would go to OCI, not P/L. Users: Lenders would misjudge security and gearing. Common mistake: thinking a small or 'unchecked' increase is acceptable.","i":"16d5a14a88"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"An accountant hasn't read about a new standard that changes revenue recognition this year.","a":"He's breaching competence and must update his knowledge","d":"Reference: ACCA Code (professional competence and due care); IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: accountants must keep their knowledge up to date (CPD: articles, seminars, courses). Applied: a new revenue standard applies this year; preparing statements without knowing it risks material errors that mislead users. He must learn it first. Consistency with last year isn't a reason to ignore a new requirement. P/L category: Operating (revenue). Users: Errors distort revenue, margins and comparability. Common mistake: waiting for the auditor to point it out.","i":"05f357cd38"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"A sustainability-focused company spends heavily on reducing emissions, lowering this year's profit. A shareholder says that breaks the duty to maximise wealth.","a":"Not necessarily: CSR can support long-term value","d":"Reference: Kaplan, ethics and profit. Rule of thumb from the chapter: maximising shareholder wealth and acting ethically aren't necessarily opposites. Applied: emissions spending lowers this year's profit, but it can attract 'green' investors, ethical customers and staff, and avoid boycotts and future risks, supporting long-term value. Hiding the cost would be dishonest. P/L category: Operating costs. Users: Lower short-term profit but potentially lower long-term risk; ESG investors may value it. Common mistake: judging ethics only by this year's profit.","i":"3a7746d8aa"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"An accountant is asked to deploy a ledger system he doesn't understand, holding sensitive customer data.","a":"Say he lacks the expertise; arrange training or experts","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Rule: be honest about your abilities (integrity, competence) and protect data (confidentiality). Applied: a distributed ledger holding sensitive customer data is complex; mistakes could cause financial loss and data breaches with legal and reputational damage. He should say he lacks the skills and get training or experts before taking it on. P/L category: Operating costs (training or experts). Users: A data breach could bring fines and future cash outflows. Common mistake: 'I'll learn on the job' with sensitive data.","i":"712e552a2a"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"A group accountant valued a subsidiary when it was bought last year. It is underperforming, and she is asked to do the goodwill impairment review.","a":"Flag the self-review threat and have someone else do or check it","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Rule: a self-review threat arises when you must evaluate your own earlier work. Why: an impairment would expose weaknesses in the original valuation, so she may be reluctant to report one. Applied: she should tell her superior and have a colleague do or independently review the test; goodwill must still be tested annually, so waiting isn't an option. Common mistake: treating her knowledge of the numbers as a reason to proceed.","i":"9dad326941"},{"s":"sbr-02-apply-1","c":"sbr-02-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"After reporting a control weakness to the audit committee, an accountant loses a key responsibility and a promised promotion. A second weakness has now appeared.","a":"Report it as well, document events and seek ACCA or legal advice","d":"Reference: ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct (fundamental principles). Rule: an intimidation threat arises when pressure, actual or perceived, deters objective action. Why: if it works, later problems go unreported and stakeholders lose a key safeguard. Applied: the principles of integrity and professional behaviour still apply, so he reports the issue, keeps a record of what happened to him and takes advice from the ACCA helpline or a lawyer. Common mistake: thinking the threat removes the duty.","i":"c316e1aa00"}],"i":"905c4b9a87"}],"i":"a0f8115d87"}],"i":"def5e68d5d"}],"i":"d09b5830e3"},{"x":"area","a":"B","t":"B · The financial reporting framework","k":[{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 1: Frameworks","ch":1,"d":"Study Text pages 1–28.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"1.1 Conceptual Framework: purpose and qualities","s":"sbr-01-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#0","x":"card","t":"The Conceptual Framework helps the Board write new standards on consistent concepts","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. It is the Board's 'constitution': every new IFRS should rest on the same ideas (assets, liabilities, relevance…), so standards don't contradict each other.","i":"30783d911c"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#1","x":"card","t":"When no standard covers a transaction, preparers use the Conceptual Framework to develop a policy","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements (selecting policies). E.g. cryptocurrency: no IFRS deals with it directly, so you test it against the CF definitions and qualities to choose a sensible treatment.","i":"dfb84f245a"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#2","x":"card","t":"If the CF and a standard disagree, the winner is the standard: the CF never overrides an IFRS","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. The CF is not a standard. Many older standards (e.g. IAS 37) still use older definitions, and those standards still apply.","i":"5848e927c0"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#3","x":"card","t":"The revised Conceptual Framework was issued in 2018","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. The earlier version was criticised for leaving out areas such as derecognition and measurement uncertainty, so the Board revised it.","i":"4302adbcca"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#4","x":"card","t":"The primary users of financial reports are existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 1 (objective). These are the people who provide money to the entity and cannot demand information directly, so the statements are written for them.","i":"b8f3205da8"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#5","x":"card","t":"Primary users need information to assess future cash flows and management's stewardship","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 1. They ask two questions: 'Will I get my money back with a return?' and 'Are the managers looking after the resources well?'","i":"94739f155c"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#6","x":"card","t":"The two fundamental qualitative characteristics are relevance and faithful representation","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2 (qualitative characteristics). Information is useful only if it can change a decision (relevance) AND shows what really happened (faithful representation). One without the other is useless.","i":"6e12b254f4"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#7","x":"card","t":"Information is material if omitting, misstating or obscuring it could influence users' economic decisions","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. Materiality is part of relevance. 'Obscuring' matters too: burying a key fact in clutter can be as misleading as leaving it out.","i":"9a7774e524"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#8","x":"card","t":"A faithful representation is complete, neutral and free from error","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2. Complete = nothing important missing; neutral = no bias to look better or worse; free from error = no mistakes in the process (not perfect accuracy). Substance over legal form.","i":"77bbcbfcac"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#9","x":"card","t":"Prudence means caution, but not deliberately understating assets or overstating liabilities","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. Prudence supports neutrality: don't overstate assets/income, but hiding profits in excessive provisions is just as biased.","i":"52638d3e12"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#10","x":"card","t":"The four enhancing characteristics are comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2. These make relevant and faithful information MORE useful. Memory hook: 'Can Very Tired Users understand?' (C-V-T-U).","i":"43e4d1e2ce"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#11","x":"card","t":"Reporting is limited by the cost constraint: the benefits of information must outweigh its costs","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2 (cost constraint). Example: IFRS 16 changed lessee accounting but left lessor accounting alone, because changing it would cost more than the benefit.","i":"f10c1eb835"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#12","x":"card","t":"Financial statements assume the entity is a going concern that will continue for the foreseeable future","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. If that assumption fails, assets can't be valued as if used for years; a different basis (e.g. break-up) is used and disclosed.","i":"d1b30e7090"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#13","x":"card","t":"Combined financial statements cover two or more entities that are not parent and subsidiary","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 3 (reporting entity). E.g. two companies owned by the same person. Hard question: where is the boundary of the reporting entity? The CF doesn't set rules for when to prepare them.","i":"1336bcbd9a"},{"s":"sbr-01-1","c":"sbr-01-1#14","x":"card","t":"Consolidated financial statements show the parent and its subsidiaries as a single economic entity","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 3. Parent investors' returns depend on the subsidiaries' profits, so they need the whole group's picture; the parent's own statements are not a substitute.","i":"9f36234469"}],"i":"0985ad94f8"},{"x":"stage","t":"1.2 Elements, recognition, measurement, presentation","s":"sbr-01-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#0","x":"card","t":"An asset is a present economic resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. Three tests: it exists now, the entity controls it (can use it and stop others), and it came from something that already happened.","i":"50712bef50"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#1","x":"card","t":"An economic resource is a right that has the potential to produce economic benefits","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. Only 'potential' is needed: the benefit doesn't have to be certain or probable for something to be an economic resource.","i":"7ef5ab1f08"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#2","x":"card","t":"A liability is a present obligation to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. You must have no practical ability to avoid it today, and it must come from something already done (e.g. goods already received).","i":"4358f07e1c"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#3","x":"card","t":"Equity is the residual interest in assets after deducting all liabilities","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 4. Equity is not measured on its own: it is whatever is left. That is why classing an instrument as debt or equity changes gearing.","i":"2b782ef2a2"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#4","x":"card","t":"Income is an increase in assets or a decrease in liabilities that increases equity, other than contributions from equity holders","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 4. A share issue raises equity but is not income: it's the owners putting money in, not the business earning it.","i":"4d11fc80ea"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#5","x":"card","t":"Expenses are decreases in assets or increases in liabilities that decrease equity, other than distributions to equity holders","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 4. Dividends reduce equity but are not expenses: they are payouts to owners, shown in the statement of changes in equity.","i":"42351a9f3c"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#6","x":"card","t":"An item is recognised only if it meets an element definition and recognition gives relevant information and a faithful representation","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5 (recognition). Meeting a definition is not enough. Example: an internally generated brand is an asset by definition but isn't recognised because it can't be measured reliably.","i":"7ab787d571"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#7","x":"card","t":"Recognition may not be relevant when existence is uncertain or the chance of an inflow/outflow is low","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5. That is why contingent liabilities and contingent assets are disclosed, not recognised.","i":"6b066b06e3"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#8","x":"card","t":"Recognition may not be faithful when measurement uncertainty is very high","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5. If any number would be a guess, putting it on the balance sheet misleads more than a disclosure would.","i":"e8262b7259"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#9","x":"card","t":"An asset is derecognised when the entity loses control of it (a liability: when the obligation ends)","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5 (derecognition). Derecognise what was transferred, recognise a gain or loss on it, and keep recognising anything retained.","i":"6cd9a95a62"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#10","x":"card","t":"Even after a legal sale, keep the asset if the seller keeps exposure to its changes in value (e.g. a cheap buy-back option)","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5 (derecognition). Example: sell a $7m building for $2m with the right to buy it back for $3m. You haven't lost control: keep the building and show the $2m as a loan.","i":"c000f89835"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#11","x":"card","t":"The two measurement bases are historical cost and current value (fair value, value in use, current cost)","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 6. Historical cost = what you paid (less use). Current value = today's value, from the market's view (fair value) or the entity's view (value in use).","i":"1606651d5e"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#12","x":"card","t":"When choosing a measurement basis, consider the item's characteristics and how it contributes to future cash flows","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 6. A machine earning cash with other assets → cost is usually most relevant. Property held for price rises → fair value tells users more.","i":"44cbf746c8"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#13","x":"card","t":"Offsetting assets and liabilities is generally not appropriate because it groups dissimilar items together","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7. Example: $3m cash earning 1% and a $1m overdraft costing 10% have different risks; showing net $2m hides that.","i":"918806d317"},{"s":"sbr-01-2","c":"sbr-01-2#14","x":"card","t":"Income or expense goes to OCI only when it comes from remeasuring to current value and makes P/L more relevant","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7. P/L is the main performance measure. Example: a $50m property revaluation goes to OCI because the building isn't held to trade. OCI is later recycled to P/L if that gives more relevant information.","i":"c915bd671b"}],"i":"7c734da2f9"},{"x":"stage","t":"1.3 IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement","s":"sbr-01-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#0","x":"card","t":"Fair value is the price received to sell an asset (or paid to transfer a liability) in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. It is an exit price from the market's point of view, today, in a normal (not forced) sale.","i":"ee86e06327"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#1","x":"card","t":"IFRS 13 does not apply to share-based payments (IFRS 2) and leases (IFRS 16)","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Those standards have their own measurement rules.","i":"c8a5a49636"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#2","x":"card","t":"Market participants pricing an asset consider its condition, location and restrictions on use","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Fair value reflects the asset as it is and where it is, as any buyer would see it.","i":"f4c17a5b83"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#3","x":"card","t":"The three valuation approaches are market, cost and income","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Market = recent prices of similar items; cost = what it would cost to replace; income = present value of future cash flows.","i":"429250530e"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#4","x":"card","t":"Fair value is market-based, so it ignores the entity's own intentions and views","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Example: directors saying 'we'll hold these shares long-term so they're worth more' is irrelevant; use the quoted price.","i":"159576c249"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#5","x":"card","t":"An active market is one where transactions happen often enough to give prices continually","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Active market prices are the best evidence of fair value.","i":"ed653a7c3e"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#6","x":"card","t":"Level 1 inputs are unadjusted quoted prices for identical assets in active markets","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. No judgement needed: the most reliable. Example: shares in a listed company.","i":"5b6fb09c21"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#7","x":"card","t":"Level 2 inputs are observable inputs other than level 1 (similar assets, less active markets, interest rates)","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Still based on market data, but adjusted. Example: valuing a building from recent sales of similar buildings nearby.","i":"99711b1780"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#8","x":"card","t":"Level 3 inputs are unobservable, e.g. the entity's own cash flow forecasts","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Most judgement and bias risk. A significant adjustment to a level 2 input makes it level 3. Extra disclosure is required.","i":"5c100b5611"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#9","x":"card","t":"The fair value hierarchy gives priority to level 1, with level 3 used only if nothing better exists","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Example: directors may not use their own discounted cash flows (level 3) if sales of similar properties (level 2) are available.","i":"ff708de648"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#10","x":"card","t":"The principal market is the market with the greatest volume and activity that the entity can access","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Fair value is measured there first, even if another market would give a better price.","i":"1cd114f3a5"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#11","x":"card","t":"If there is no principal market, use the most advantageous market (best net amount after transaction and transport costs)","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Example: Market 1 nets 21, Market 2 nets 22 → Market 2 is most advantageous.","i":"cd66b01ee6"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#12","x":"card","t":"Transaction costs choose the market but are not deducted from fair value (transport costs are)","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Transaction costs belong to the market, not the asset; transport is needed to get the asset there. In the example: FV = 25 − 2 transport = 23.","i":"99cb5bf8d9"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#13","x":"card","t":"A non-financial asset's fair value is based on its highest and best use by market participants","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Current use is presumed best unless evidence shows otherwise. Example: industrial land worth 5 but 6 − 0.3 as housing → fair value 5.7.","i":"d9b3c2704f"},{"s":"sbr-01-3","c":"sbr-01-3#14","x":"card","t":"A use counts for highest and best use if it is physically possible, legally permissible and financially feasible","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. 'Legally permissible' doesn't need permission granted yet, only a realistic chance (e.g. neighbours already got planning permission).","i":"044e3e7f94"}],"i":"a67a87e078"},{"x":"stage","t":"1.4 Materiality and criticisms of financial reporting","s":"sbr-01-4","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#0","x":"card","t":"Practice Statement 2 'Making Materiality Judgements' is non-mandatory guidance","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2 Making Materiality Judgements. It helps preparers apply materiality but is not a standard; you don't have to follow it to comply with IFRS.","i":"c802b475df"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#1","x":"card","t":"Materiality is judged using quantitative factors (revenue, profit, assets, cash flows) and qualitative factors","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2; Conceptual Framework. Small amounts can still be material because of their nature: related party deals, unusual items, geography, economic uncertainty.","i":"63c790d7f7"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#2","x":"card","t":"Materiality is judged for the needs of primary users as a group, not one individual investor","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2; Conceptual Framework. Example: one 2% investor wants details on spending in France; if it's immaterial to users as a group, it isn't disclosed.","i":"e388f4005c"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#3","x":"card","t":"Materiality judgements apply to recognition, measurement, presentation and disclosure","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2. Every decision in preparing the statements can be filtered by 'does it matter to users?'.","i":"5a415e61e8"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#4","x":"card","t":"An entity needn't apply a recognition or measurement rule if the effect is immaterial","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2. Example: expensing every fitting under $1,000 still complies with IAS 16 if the total effect is immaterial.","i":"7490fbf490"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#5","x":"card","t":"Immaterial disclosures can be left out, but extra disclosures are needed when users need them to understand the effect of transactions","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2. Disclosures are about usefulness, not ticking every requirement: cut clutter, add what's missing.","i":"a9445b0d3b"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#6","x":"card","t":"The four-step materiality process is identify, assess, organise, review","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2 Making Materiality Judgements. Identify possibly material information, assess whether it really is material, organise it in the draft statements, review the whole package.","i":"2d5a732856"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#7","x":"card","t":"Criticism 1 – historical information: statements show the past and are months out of date","d":"Reference: Kaplan's criticisms of financial reporting (no standard). Investors care about future profits; by publication the figures are old.","i":"ef8edaa4af"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#8","x":"card","t":"Criticism 2 – unrecognised assets: internally generated goodwill, reputation and staff skills are missing","d":"Reference: Kaplan's criticisms of financial reporting (no standard). Much of a company's value isn't on the balance sheet, so net assets can be far below market value.","i":"a30ed5d7b1"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#9","x":"card","t":"Criticism 3 – clutter: long, generic disclosures hide what matters","d":"Reference: Kaplan's criticisms of financial reporting (no standard). More pages don't mean more information; relevant points get lost.","i":"61ed061037"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#10","x":"card","t":"Criticism 4 – non-financial gaps: little about governance, risk management and sustainability","d":"Reference: Kaplan's criticisms of financial reporting (no standard). Long-term success depends on these, which is why integrated and sustainability reporting have grown.","i":"a04b2524ae"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#11","x":"card","t":"Criticism 5 – estimates: they are subjective, can be manipulated and reduce comparability","d":"Reference: Kaplan's criticisms of financial reporting (no standard). Example: changing useful lives to hit a profit target. The cash flow statement partly helps, but cash can be window-dressed too.","i":"91962d63fe"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#12","x":"card","t":"Criticism 6 – professional judgement: subjective choices (e.g. lease classification by lessors) add bias","d":"Reference: Kaplan's criticisms of financial reporting (no standard). Two honest accountants can reach different answers on the same facts.","i":"2cf4476a73"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#13","x":"card","t":"Criticism 7 – historical cost: in times of rising prices, profit is not sustainable","d":"Reference: Kaplan's criticisms of financial reporting (no standard). Depreciation on old costs is too low compared with replacing the asset today, so profit is overstated.","i":"d9286d4874"},{"s":"sbr-01-4","c":"sbr-01-4#14","x":"card","t":"Criticism 8 – policy choices: cost vs fair value models (IAS 16, IAS 40) hurt comparability","d":"Reference: Kaplan's criticisms of financial reporting (no standard). Two identical companies can report different assets and profits just by choosing different models.","i":"43080d1006"}],"i":"24a5845387"},{"x":"stage","t":"1.5 More Framework concepts","s":"sbr-01-5","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#0","x":"card","t":"Besides guiding the Board, the Framework helps preparers when no standard applies or a standard offers a choice of accounting policy, and helps everyone understand and interpret the standards","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. It isn't a standard itself, so it never overrides one.","i":"2d86a3267b"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#1","x":"card","t":"Financial statements are provided through a statement of financial position, statements of financial performance, and other statements and notes (e.g. cash flows)","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 3. They report the five elements: assets, liabilities, equity, income and expenses.","i":"23d83e846e"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#2","x":"card","t":"A parent's unconsolidated statements are useful for things like the level of distributable reserves, but don't replace consolidated statements","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 3. Investors in the parent need the group picture because their returns depend on the subsidiaries.","i":"8f7766a1c4"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#3","x":"card","t":"Besides income and expenses, other changes in resources and claims are contributions from and distributions to owners, and exchanges of assets and liabilities that don't change equity","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 4. E.g. buying inventory for cash: one asset for another, no income or expense.","i":"4db353c585"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#4","x":"card","t":"Effective presentation balances flexibility to report relevant information with requirements that allow comparison year to year and between entities","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7. The Board prefers entity-specific information to standard wording, and says duplication reduces understandability.","i":"fa180c2375"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#5","x":"card","t":"Splitting an item into components gives relevant information when the components have different characteristics","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7. E.g. $10m debt shown as $1m current and $9m non-current helps users judge cash flows and solvency.","i":"aecf1b18fc"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#6","x":"card","t":"Aggregation summarises items with shared characteristics, but too much of it obscures relevant information","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7. The statements may be highly aggregated while the notes break the figures down.","i":"be62254f77"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#7","x":"card","t":"IFRS 13 is a single source of guidance on how to measure fair value when another standard requires or allows it","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. IFRS 13 says how to measure fair value, not when to use it: that's in the other standards.","i":"3d556316c4"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#8","x":"card","t":"Non-financial assets include PPE and intangibles, while financial assets include receivables and investments in equity shares","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Highest and best use applies only to non-financial assets.","i":"ffce5cd41f"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#9","x":"card","t":"Disclosing fair values by level tells investors how much judgement, and risk of bias, lies behind each valuation","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Level 3 amounts get extra disclosure on methods and assumptions; heavy reliance on them may put some investors off.","i":"50b659e03f"},{"s":"sbr-01-5","c":"sbr-01-5#10","x":"card","t":"IFRS 13 improves usefulness: level 1 inputs are neutral, observable inputs are verifiable, and measuring from a market participant's view improves comparability between entities","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement and Conceptual Framework Chapter 2. Principal-market and highest-and-best-use rules also reduce management bias; level 3 disclosures aid understandability.","i":"7f7a61c163"}],"i":"579a5b2624"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"1.S Sequences and decision paths","s":"sbr-01-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-structure-1","c":"sbr-01-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Developing a policy when no standard applies","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. IAS 8's hierarchy; the Framework never overrides a standard that does apply.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Look for a standard dealing with similar and related issues","i":"82f2948379"},{"x":"step","t":"Then use the Conceptual Framework's definitions, recognition criteria and measurement concepts","i":"d1cf27fab3"},{"x":"step","t":"Choose the policy that gives relevant and reliable information","i":"8ddee8fefd"},{"x":"step","t":"Disclose the judgement made","i":"dcc2fbaa9a"}],"i":"46c2b25cf9"},{"s":"sbr-01-structure-1","c":"sbr-01-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Deciding whether to recognise an item","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5. Meeting a definition is only the first gate: uncertain existence, low probability or very high measurement uncertainty can stop recognition, with disclosure instead.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Does it meet the definition of an element?","i":"6a0e500f55"},{"x":"step","t":"Would recognition give relevant information?","i":"8911c3793c"},{"x":"step","t":"Would it give a faithful representation?","i":"9cc414af9b"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise it; if not, consider disclosure","i":"3bbde20d9f"}],"i":"1451a050e2"},{"s":"sbr-01-structure-1","c":"sbr-01-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Accounting for derecognition","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5 (derecognition). This shows the change in net assets faithfully. If the entity keeps exposure to the asset's value changes, it doesn't derecognise at all.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Remove the part transferred, expired or consumed","i":"1354b03769"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise a gain or loss on that part","i":"6eb9bdebba"},{"x":"step","t":"Keep recognising any part retained","i":"e5256094a8"}],"i":"8ff288e9fe"},{"s":"sbr-01-structure-1","c":"sbr-01-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"The fair value hierarchy, highest priority first","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Use the highest level available; level 3 only when nothing observable exists.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Level 1: quoted prices, identical assets, active markets","i":"5c115d4df0"},{"x":"step","t":"Level 2: other observable inputs","i":"03eaf58564"},{"x":"step","t":"Level 3: unobservable inputs","i":"d1c9926dfc"}],"i":"defefb829b"},{"s":"sbr-01-structure-1","c":"sbr-01-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"Choosing where to measure fair value","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Transaction costs help choose the most advantageous market but are not deducted from fair value.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Is there a principal market the entity can access? Use it","i":"6d7b21c30c"},{"x":"step","t":"If not, find the most advantageous market (best net amount)","i":"1bcdde3f5c"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the price there, deducting transport costs but not transaction costs","i":"f41574de46"}],"i":"c3f85e1038"},{"s":"sbr-01-structure-1","c":"sbr-01-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"The materiality process in Practice Statement 2","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2 Making Materiality Judgements. A systematic four-step process; guidance, not mandatory.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify information that could be material","i":"3a971f3bfe"},{"x":"step","t":"Assess whether it is actually material","i":"b170860c1a"},{"x":"step","t":"Organise it in the draft statements","i":"e2a4d361cf"},{"x":"step","t":"Review the draft as a whole","i":"b274b4fa5d"}],"i":"0a14925fc5"},{"s":"sbr-01-structure-1","c":"sbr-01-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"Fair value of land that could be used differently","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. E.g. industrial land worth 5 that could become housing worth 6 less 0.3 conversion → fair value 5.7.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Start from the current use (presumed highest and best)","i":"2663a9080e"},{"x":"step","t":"Look for evidence market participants would use it differently","i":"74717b233e"},{"x":"step","t":"Test the other use: physically possible, legally permissible, financially feasible","i":"16d704dfa0"},{"x":"step","t":"Measure at the higher value, after conversion costs","i":"d433f55fd3"}],"i":"7c3591ad68"}],"i":"93daadc550"}],"i":"4367b5ffba"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"1.C1 Qualities, bases, elements","s":"sbr-01-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Relevance","a":"Fundamental characteristic","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2. The fundamental ones are relevance and faithful representation.","i":"907469175d"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Comparability","a":"Enhancing characteristic","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2. Enhancing: comparability, verifiability, timeliness, understandability.","i":"bb2e7a6185"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Timeliness","a":"Enhancing characteristic","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2. It makes useful information more useful; it can't make irrelevant information useful.","i":"5fdb4d161f"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Faithful representation","a":"Fundamental characteristic","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2. It needs completeness, neutrality and freedom from error.","i":"f0ecbcab57"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Materiality is an aspect of …","a":"Relevance","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. Material = could influence decisions: an entity-specific aspect of relevance.","i":"202233ec56"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Neutrality and prudence support …","a":"Faithful representation","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. Prudence supports neutrality; neither allows deliberate over- or understatement.","i":"4bd04f165b"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Value in use is a form of …","a":"Current value","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 6. Current value: fair value, value in use (fulfilment value for liabilities), current cost.","i":"dd8263c695"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"A machine used with other assets to make products is usually best measured at …","a":"Historical cost","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 6. Its cash flows come indirectly, through use with other assets, so cost tends to be most relevant.","i":"fe6dafaeb8"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Statements for two companies owned by the same person, not parent and subsidiary","a":"Combined statements","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 3. Consolidated statements are for a parent and its subsidiaries as one economic entity.","i":"995da60655"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Equity rises because new shares were issued for cash. That rise is …","a":"A contribution from owners","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 4. Income excludes contributions from equity holders.","i":"a20fb47c9a"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Equity falls because a dividend was paid. That fall is …","a":"A distribution to owners","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 4. Expenses exclude distributions to equity holders.","i":"daa71e701b"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-1","c":"sbr-01-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"A revaluation gain on a head office building normally goes to …","a":"Other comprehensive income","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment; Conceptual Framework Chapter 7. The building is used over many periods, not traded; the gain in P/L would not faithfully show performance.","i":"a328b1812f"}],"i":"995ec3c4b1"},{"x":"stage","t":"1.C2 Fair value inputs, markets, materiality","s":"sbr-01-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"The quoted price of a listed company's shares in an active market","a":"Level 1","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Identical asset, active market, unadjusted.","i":"c284f0702e"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Price per square metre from recent sales of similar buildings nearby","a":"Level 2","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Observable, but for similar rather than identical assets.","i":"74329cb67c"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"An interest rate observable in the market","a":"Level 2","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Observable inputs that aren't prices are level 2.","i":"93fd3151e6"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"A cash flow forecast built from the entity's own data","a":"Level 3","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Unobservable inputs are level 3.","i":"dad6608b2d"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"A level 2 price that needed a significant adjustment","a":"Level 3","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. A significant adjustment makes it level 3.","i":"419c01145e"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"The market with the greatest volume and activity that the entity can access","a":"Principal market","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Used whenever it exists, even if another market would pay more.","i":"c53a8f51d1"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"The market giving the best net amount after transaction and transport costs","a":"Most advantageous market","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Used only when there is no principal market.","i":"a28774f64f"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Broker and legal fees for selling the asset","a":"Not deducted in fair value","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Transaction costs belong to the market, not the asset.","i":"bb1be03f84"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"The cost of carrying the asset to the market","a":"Deducted in fair value","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Location is a characteristic of the asset, so transport costs are deducted.","i":"003d85be23"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Revenue, profit, assets and cash flows as materiality factors","a":"Quantitative","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2. Measures of size.","i":"2ea7b3e42c"},{"s":"sbr-01-compare-2","c":"sbr-01-compare-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Related party and unusual transactions as materiality factors","a":"Qualitative","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2. Their nature can make small amounts material; geography and economic uncertainty are qualitative too.","i":"5ebbc265c5"}],"i":"c6c3cde16b"}],"i":"673ddf5b9d"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"1.W1 Traps: the Framework","s":"sbr-01-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"When the Conceptual Framework and a standard conflict, the standard wins.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. The Framework is not a standard and never overrides one.","i":"766d1a8e1e"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"IAS 16 recognises PPE using the 2018 Framework's recognition criteria.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment; Conceptual Framework. Many standards keep older criteria (probable benefits, reliable measurement); the standard's own criteria apply.","i":"90a128e82e"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Faithful representation means the numbers must be perfectly accurate.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2. Free from error means no errors in the description and process; estimates can still be faithful if clearly described.","i":"91c4e1f45f"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Prudence doesn't allow a cautious entity to understate assets on purpose.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. Deliberate understatement isn't neutral; prudence only stops overstatement of assets/income and understatement of liabilities/expenses.","i":"d952f96537"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"An item that meets the definition of an asset must always be recognised.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5. Recognition also needs relevant information and a faithful representation (e.g. internally generated brands stay off).","i":"dd6215c161"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"A low probability of an inflow can be a reason not to recognise an asset.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5. Low probability or uncertain existence can make recognition irrelevant; disclosure may be needed instead.","i":"74f80e79bc"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"An entity that legally sold an asset must always derecognise it.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5 (derecognition). If it keeps exposure to the asset's value changes (e.g. a cheap buy-back right), control hasn't passed.","i":"28bfbb4096"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Income and expenses from remeasuring items to current value go to OCI only if a standard says so.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7. Only when the Board decides in a standard that OCI makes P/L more relevant or more faithful.","i":"08e13fb48a"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Amounts in OCI must always be recycled to P/L later.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7. Recycling happens when it makes P/L more relevant; the Board may decide not to if there's no clear basis for amount or timing.","i":"ef9cd569aa"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Offsetting is generally inappropriate because it groups dissimilar items together.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7. E.g. cash earning 1% and an overdraft costing 10% have different risks.","i":"c5b0c7dfcd"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"The cost constraint is why IFRS 16 left lessor accounting largely unchanged.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 2 (cost constraint). Stakeholders didn't think lessor rules were broken; changing them would cost more than the benefit.","i":"788b68d395"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-1","c":"sbr-01-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"Consolidated statements treat the parent and subsidiaries as separate economic entities.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 3. They present them as a single economic entity.","i":"7d0de0455c"}],"i":"86b04e1408"},{"x":"stage","t":"1.W2 Traps: fair value and materiality","s":"sbr-01-watch-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#0","x":"trap","t":"Fair value reflects what the entity itself expects to get from the asset.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Fair value is market-based, not entity-specific; the owner's intentions don't matter.","i":"21534b45ce"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#1","x":"trap","t":"Fair value assumes a forced sale if the entity is short of cash.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. It assumes an orderly transaction; market participants are not forced.","i":"3267f04132"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#2","x":"trap","t":"Fair value is measured net of transaction costs.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Transaction costs are not deducted; transport costs are.","i":"c61da07ef3"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#3","x":"trap","t":"The current use of a non-financial asset is presumed to be its highest and best use.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Unless there's evidence market participants would use it differently.","i":"17342290a0"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#4","x":"trap","t":"A use is 'legally permissible' only once planning permission has actually been granted.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. It needs to be legally possible, not yet approved, e.g. similar plots have been given permission.","i":"aecb2a8ce9"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#5","x":"trap","t":"Directors may use their own discounted cash flows even when sales of similar properties are observable.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Level 3 is lowest priority: use the observable level 2 data and adjust for location and condition.","i":"baaaad95f3"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#6","x":"trap","t":"If a principal market exists, fair value is measured in the market that gives the highest net price.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. The principal market is used even if another market would give a better price.","i":"80d63b80a0"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#7","x":"trap","t":"IFRS Practice Statement 2 'Making Materiality Judgements' must be applied for financial statements to comply with IFRS.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2 Making Materiality Judgements. It's non-mandatory guidance from the IASB on how to judge materiality (identify, assess, organise, review). Not following it isn't a breach in itself; what matters is whether material information was properly presented and disclosed.","i":"357f0b855a"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#8","x":"trap","t":"Materiality is judged by the needs of primary users as a group.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2; Conceptual Framework. Not by one investor's special interest (e.g. one 2% holder's interest in French spending).","i":"75703770ad"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#9","x":"trap","t":"A disclosure required by a standard must be given even if immaterial.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2. Disclosure requirements only apply when the information is material.","i":"b8cd5cd77c"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#10","x":"trap","t":"An entity may expense small fittings below a threshold and still comply with IAS 16.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. As long as the total effect is immaterial.","i":"76f031c145"},{"s":"sbr-01-watch-2","c":"sbr-01-watch-2#11","x":"trap","t":"Sometimes extra disclosures beyond the standards are needed.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2. When necessary for users to understand the effect of transactions.","i":"14799a95ef"}],"i":"5f48e925f9"}],"i":"b6522830a3"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"1.A Scenarios","s":"sbr-01-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"A publisher's famous brand was built up internally and has never been bought. Should it go on the statement of financial position?","a":"No: the cost can't be separated and the value is too uncertain, so recognition wouldn't be faithful","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; Conceptual Framework Chapter 5. Rule: an item is recognised only if it meets an element's definition AND recognition gives relevant information and a faithful representation. Why: the brand is a resource the publisher controls that brings benefits, so it is an asset by definition; but its cost can't be separated from the cost of running the business, and brands are unique, so any value would be a guess. Applied: recognition would not be faithful, so it stays off, which is what IAS 38 requires for internally generated brands. P/L category: No P/L effect: nothing is recognised (building the brand was already expensed in Operating). Users: Net assets and return on assets understate brand-rich companies, so investors rely on disclosures and market value. Common mistake: 'it meets the definition, so it must be recognised'.","i":"7c0de67ae8"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Reasoning only from the Conceptual Framework, how might a trader measure a digital currency it holds to sell when its price rises?","a":"At fair value, with changes in P/L","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework. Rule: when no standard applies, build a policy from the Framework. Applied: (1) it's an asset: a controlled resource that can bring benefits; (2) recognise it: relevant to users predicting cash flows; (3) measure at current value: it is held to sell soon and its price swings, so cost would tell users little; (4) gains and losses in P/L: it's traded short-term and P/L is the main source of information about performance. Also: IFRS itself puts crypto under IAS 38 (cost, or revaluation through OCI), or under IAS 2 if it's held for sale in the ordinary course of business, where a broker-trader measures it at fair value less costs to sell, through P/L. P/L category: Investing (gains and losses on an asset held for returns); Operating if trading such assets is the main business. Users: Profit becomes volatile, showing investors the real risk of the holding. Common mistake: putting the swings in OCI, which suits long-term holdings, not trading.","i":"e995432df3"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A company sells a building worth $9m for $3m and can buy it back for $4m in five years. Property prices are rising.","a":"Keep the building and record the $3m received as a loan","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 5 (derecognition). Rule: derecognise only when control is lost; keeping exposure to the asset's value changes means control is kept. Applied: the company can buy back a $9m building for $4m, so it will clearly do so and it still gets the benefit of rising prices. Control hasn't passed, so the building stays on the balance sheet and the $3m received is a loan (a financial liability). P/L category: No gain on 'sale'; interest on the loan goes to Financing. Users: Gearing rises because a loan appears, instead of a misleading profit: lenders see the true debt. Common mistake: following the legal form (a sale) instead of the substance (borrowing secured on the building).","i":"00b1f4fe59"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"A company owes the bank $20m; $2m is due within 12 months. How should the loan be presented?","a":"$2m current and $18m non-current","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements (current/non-current). Rule: split an item into components when the parts have different characteristics. Applied: $2m must be paid within 12 months and $18m later; showing them apart tells users how much cash is needed soon (liquidity) and how much is long-term (solvency). P/L category: No P/L effect (classification only). Users: The current part feeds liquidity ratios and tells lenders how much cash is needed within a year. Common mistake: showing the whole loan as one line.","i":"63e208f133"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A company has $5m on deposit at one bank and a $2m overdraft at another. How should they be shown?","a":"Cash $5m and overdraft $2m separately","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework, Chapter 7; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements (offsetting). Rule: offsetting is generally not appropriate because it lumps dissimilar items together. Applied: the deposit earns interest and is an asset; the overdraft costs interest, is with another bank and is a liability; netting them to $3m would hide the borrowing and its cost. So show $5m cash and a $2m overdraft. P/L category: Interest earned goes to Investing; overdraft interest to Financing; shown separately. Users: The real borrowing and its cost stay visible, so liquidity and gearing aren't flattered. Common mistake: netting because 'overall the company has $3m'.","i":"b028bae1c1"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"An investment property's directors want to value it by discounting their own forecast rents. Many similar buildings nearby have recently sold.","a":"Use the recent sales (level 2), adjusted for location and condition","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Rule: IFRS 13 gives priority to observable inputs; level 3 (unobservable) inputs are used only if nothing better exists. Applied: recent sales of similar buildings are observable level 2 data, so they must be used, adjusted for this building's location and condition. The directors' own discounted rents are level 3. Also: fair value is a market view, so the directors' plans don't matter. P/L category: Fair value changes on investment property go to Investing. Users: Values based on observable sales are more reliable; a level 3 value would raise bias risk and need extra disclosure. Common mistake: 'our forecast reflects how we'll use the building'.","i":"c839395f7a"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"An asset sells for 40 in Market A (transaction costs 5, transport 3) and 38 in Market B (transaction costs 1, transport 3). Market A is the principal market. What is its fair value?","a":"37","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Step 1 (which market): Market A is the principal market, so IFRS 13 says use it, even though Market B would give more cash after costs. Step 2 (price): 40. Step 3 (costs): deduct transport (3), because fair value is the price for the asset where it is now and it must be moved to sell; don't deduct transaction costs (5), because broker and legal fees belong to the deal, not the asset. So 40 − 3 = 37. P/L category: No P/L effect by itself (measurement); if the asset is remeasured through P/L, e.g. investment property, the change goes to Investing. Users: Market-based values are comparable between entities. Common mistake: 35 is the answer when there's no principal market.","i":"d7c990e37d"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"An asset sells for 40 in Market A (transaction costs 5, transport 3) and 38 in Market B (transaction costs 1, transport 3). Neither is the principal market. What is its fair value?","a":"35","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Step 1 (which market): there's no principal market, so use the most advantageous one: the market that leaves the most cash after ALL costs. A: 40 − 5 − 3 = 32. B: 38 − 1 − 3 = 34. B wins. Step 2 (fair value): take B's price and deduct only transport, because transaction costs belong to the deal, not the asset: 38 − 3 = 35. P/L category: No P/L effect by itself (measurement); if the asset is remeasured through P/L, e.g. investment property, the change goes to Investing. Users: Market-based values are comparable between entities. Common mistakes: 34 (deducting transaction costs too) or 37 (Market A's price, though A isn't the best market here).","i":"aa86c9cdd9"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"Industrial land is worth 8 in its current use. Neighbouring plots got permission for flats; as a flat site it's worth 10 less 1 of clearing costs. What is its fair value?","a":"9","d":"Reference: IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement. Rule: a non-financial asset is valued at its highest and best use by market participants; the current use is presumed best unless evidence shows otherwise. Applied: neighbours got permission for flats (so it's legally permissible even without approval yet), it's physically possible and financially worth it. As a flat site it's worth 10, less 1 to clear it = 9, more than 8 in its current use. So fair value = 9. P/L category: No P/L effect by itself; in an acquisition it raises net assets and so lowers goodwill. Users: Net assets reflect what a buyer would actually pay for the land. Common mistakes: 8 (sticking with current use despite evidence) or 10 (forgetting the costs of conversion).","i":"e8d11c466a"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"One shareholder with 2% of the shares wants details of spending in one small country, which is immaterial to users as a group.","a":"No need to disclose it","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2 (primary users as a group). Rule: materiality is judged against the common information needs of primary users (investors, lenders, other creditors) as a group. Applied: one 2% shareholder's special interest doesn't make the information material for users as a group, so it needn't be disclosed. P/L category: No P/L effect (a disclosure decision). Users: Notes stay focused on what investors and lenders as a group need; clutter is avoided. Common mistake: treating any shareholder request as making something material.","i":"0ce11d0b39"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#10","x":"example","t":"A retailer buys furniture under $500 per item and expenses it all. The total is small.","a":"Acceptable if the total effect is immaterial","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 2; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Rule: IFRS requirements only need to be applied when the effect is material. Applied: expensing items under $500 is a practical policy; if the total effect on assets and profit is immaterial, the statements still comply with IAS 16. P/L category: Operating expense. Users: Immaterial, so ratios aren't affected in any way that matters. Common mistake: thinking every item of PPE must be capitalised whatever the amount.","i":"8fcdf40a61"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#11","x":"example","t":"A company buys an office block in a fast-rising area. It plans to rent it out for years and maybe sell later. IAS 40 lets it choose the cost or fair value model. What should guide the choice?","a":"Which gives more relevant information: the asset's characteristics and how it brings in cash (here, value changes matter a lot)","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property; Conceptual Framework Chapter 6. The Framework says to consider the item's characteristics and how it contributes to future cash flows. Prices sensitive to the market point to fair value even if there's no plan to sell, since rising values also support higher rents. P/L category: Fair value model: value changes in Investing. Cost model: depreciation in Operating. Users: Fair value makes profit volatile but shows current value; with the cost model, investors use the disclosed fair value to compare.","i":"a049feb7c4"},{"s":"sbr-01-apply-1","c":"sbr-01-apply-1#12","x":"example","t":"A land-holding entity revalues land and buildings from 120 to 150 under IAS 16. Where does the 30 go?","a":"OCI, into a revaluation surplus","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment; Conceptual Framework Chapter 7. Rule: under IAS 16, a revaluation gain goes to OCI and a revaluation surplus. Why (Framework): the property is used over many years, not traded, so putting the gain in P/L would mix it with trading results and not faithfully show performance. It is never recycled to P/L. P/L category: None: other comprehensive income, never recycled. Users: Equity and net assets rise, so gearing falls; profit and EPS are unaffected. Common mistake: putting it in P/L like an investment property at fair value.","i":"e09e9ca22d"}],"i":"48a4dbd9c6"}],"i":"8cc8b03296"}],"i":"10ec880159"}],"i":"f6de7fc675"},{"x":"area","a":"C","t":"C · Reporting financial performance","k":[{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 3: Performance reporting","ch":3,"d":"Study Text pages 41–82.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"3.1 IFRS 18: purpose and general features","s":"sbr-03-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-1","c":"sbr-03-1#0","x":"card","t":"IFRS 18 replaces IAS 1 for periods starting on or after 1 January 2027","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. SBR is examined on IFRS 18 already, so learn the new categories and subtotals.","i":"dd47fdfa9b"},{"s":"sbr-03-1","c":"sbr-03-1#1","x":"card","t":"IFRS 18 decides what goes in the main statements or the notes, what to include and how much detail to show","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It is about presentation and disclosure, not recognition or measurement.","i":"6f8cfdbb23"},{"s":"sbr-03-1","c":"sbr-03-1#2","x":"card","t":"A complete set of financial statements includes performance, position, changes in equity, cash flows and accounting policies and other notes","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Annual-report items like a financial review are outside the financial statements and outside IFRS 18.","i":"de5b979668"},{"s":"sbr-03-1","c":"sbr-03-1#3","x":"card","t":"The role of the primary statements is to give structured summaries for an overview, comparisons and pointers to the notes","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Too much detail on the face defeats the purpose; detail belongs in the notes.","i":"9c73436c08"},{"s":"sbr-03-1","c":"sbr-03-1#4","x":"card","t":"The role of the notes is to give material information needed to understand the statements and to assess future cash flows and management's stewardship","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Notes explain and add to the summaries.","i":"b45fe1b10e"},{"s":"sbr-03-1","c":"sbr-03-1#5","x":"card","t":"When judging materiality, users are assumed to have reasonable knowledge of business and accounting","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. So you don't need to explain basic concepts, but you must not hide material facts.","i":"80844e9266"},{"s":"sbr-03-1","c":"sbr-03-1#6","x":"card","t":"IFRS 18's five general features are consistency, comparatives, aggregation, labelling and offsetting","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Memory hook: 'CCALO'.","i":"5c5bafdb98"},{"s":"sbr-03-1","c":"sbr-03-1#7","x":"card","t":"Presentation may change from year to year only if it's clearly more appropriate or a standard requires it","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Consistency protects comparability over time.","i":"e34bb946b3"}],"i":"d2af3d48c1"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.2 IFRS 18: aggregation, labelling, offsetting","s":"sbr-03-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-2","c":"sbr-03-2#0","x":"card","t":"Items are aggregated when they share characteristics, and split when they don't","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. The level of aggregation must never hide material information. Splitting is required when the result is material.","i":"e177fdcd4c"},{"s":"sbr-03-2","c":"sbr-03-2#1","x":"card","t":"Shared characteristics include nature, function, size and whether the item is recurring or non-recurring","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. E.g. a one-off goodwill impairment may be shown separately from recurring expenses.","i":"0905d4051e"},{"s":"sbr-03-2","c":"sbr-03-2#2","x":"card","t":"Examples of items often shown separately in P/L are inventory write-downs, impairments, restructuring and gains or losses on disposals","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. They are unusual or volatile, so users want to see them.","i":"289abdb582"},{"s":"sbr-03-2","c":"sbr-03-2#3","x":"card","t":"Admin and marketing costs lumped into 'other operating expenses' are over-aggregated: different functions should be shown separately","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. GreenTech example: rental income (recurring) and dividends (one-off) also differ, so separate them if material.","i":"b6b21bd42a"},{"s":"sbr-03-2","c":"sbr-03-2#4","x":"card","t":"The label 'other' should be avoided, or made as precise as possible","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. E.g. call store closure costs 'store closure costs', not 'other expenses'.","i":"e1acf49a27"},{"s":"sbr-03-2","c":"sbr-03-2#5","x":"card","t":"Under IFRS 18, assets and liabilities, or income and expenses, are offset only when a standard requires or permits it","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Offsetting hides information users need to assess cash flows.","i":"7b7920a1f6"}],"i":"49c1af9d03"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.3 IFRS 18: the statement of profit or loss","s":"sbr-03-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#0","x":"card","t":"Every income and expense goes into one of five categories: operating, investing, financing, income taxes and discontinued operations","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. In SBR answers, always say which category an item belongs in.","i":"9806165778"},{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#1","x":"card","t":"The operating category holds the main business and anything that is not investing or financing (it's the default)","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Revenue, cost of sales, depreciation, PPE disposal gains, pension service cost.","i":"82c000563e"},{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#2","x":"card","t":"The investing category holds income from associates and JVs, cash and equivalents, and assets that earn returns independently, like financial assets and rented property","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. E.g. interest on bonds, dividends, rental income, fair value gains on FVPL shares.","i":"fcba39c6a6"},{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#3","x":"card","t":"The financing category holds income and expenses from liabilities that only raise finance, and the interest part of other liabilities (leases, pensions, provisions)","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. E.g. unwinding the discount on a provision is financing; the original provision expense is operating.","i":"f78789f5d4"},{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#4","x":"card","t":"Foreign exchange differences go in the same category as the item they relate to","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. E.g. FX on a trade receivable → operating; on a loan → financing.","i":"7e0ea1ef21"},{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#5","x":"card","t":"The three subtotals IFRS 18 requires are operating profit, profit before financing and income taxes, and profit for the year","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Other subtotals like 'profit before tax' are allowed but optional.","i":"5c11618fb7"},{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#6","x":"card","t":"Extra subtotals are allowed if they use IFRS amounts, are consistent and are not more prominent than the required ones","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. They must help give a useful structured summary.","i":"f96fbf047f"},{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#7","x":"card","t":"Interest income on bonds in a normal company belongs in investing, below operating profit","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Inuk example: putting it above operating profit was wrong.","i":"b527b28283"},{"s":"sbr-03-3","c":"sbr-03-3#8","x":"card","t":"Store closure costs are operating expenses, so they go above operating profit, with a clear label","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Being non-recurring doesn't move them below operating profit.","i":"068a97eab2"}],"i":"443d39d690"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.4 IFRS 18: expenses, OCI and special entities","s":"sbr-03-4","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#0","x":"card","t":"Operating expenses can be presented by function, by nature, or a mix of both","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Choose whatever gives the most useful structured summary.","i":"68d649f5cf"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#1","x":"card","t":"Presenting by function (COS, selling, admin) lets users calculate gross margin","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Function shows what the cost was for.","i":"3f99986a8d"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#2","x":"card","t":"Presenting by nature (materials, staff, depreciation) helps users forecast each kind of cost","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Nature shows what the cost is.","i":"9f92248745"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#3","x":"card","t":"To choose function or nature, consider the profit drivers, how the business is managed and whether allocating costs to functions would be arbitrary","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. E.g. impairments are hard to allocate to functions, so show them by nature.","i":"9c139dab00"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#4","x":"card","t":"OCI items that may be recycled to P/L include FX on foreign operations, cash flow hedges and FVOCI debt instruments","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. They return to P/L when the hedged item or investment affects P/L or is sold.","i":"faf2d3e487"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#5","x":"card","t":"OCI items never recycled include revaluation surplus, pension remeasurements and FVOCI equity investments","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. OCI must be shown in these two groups.","i":"c8bc7ec703"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#6","x":"card","t":"Tax on OCI is shown either net for each item or as one tax line for each group","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. IFRS 18 requires the tax on each component of OCI to be presented or disclosed.","i":"8262d95313"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#7","x":"card","t":"OCI is criticised because users often ignore it and recycling records gains in a different period from the change itself","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. That conflicts with the CF definitions of income and expenses.","i":"d9251809ac"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#8","x":"card","t":"For a bank, interest on customer deposits is operating (like cost of sales)","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. For entities whose main business is investing or financing, those results are operating.","i":"b5f7c82804"},{"s":"sbr-03-4","c":"sbr-03-4#9","x":"card","t":"For an insurer investing as its main business, investment income is operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Equity-accounted associates still go to investing.","i":"b254b1a978"}],"i":"4fbefd6104"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.5 IFRS 18: position, equity, notes, MPMs","s":"sbr-03-5","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-5","c":"sbr-03-5#0","x":"card","t":"An asset is current if it's realised in the operating cycle, held for trading, or realised within 12 months","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Mouse example: cheese maturing for 15 months is still a current asset because it's sold in the normal cycle.","i":"b1da03fc92"},{"s":"sbr-03-5","c":"sbr-03-5#1","x":"card","t":"A liability is current if it's settled in the cycle, held for trading, due within 12 months, or there's no right at the reporting date to defer settlement beyond 12 months","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Whether the entity intends to use its right to defer doesn't matter.","i":"602d332ce8"},{"s":"sbr-03-5","c":"sbr-03-5#2","x":"card","t":"Under IFRS 18, goodwill must be presented on a separate line, not combined with other intangibles","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. This changed from IAS 1.","i":"d1dd984389"},{"s":"sbr-03-5","c":"sbr-03-5#3","x":"card","t":"The statement of changes in equity shows owner transactions (share issues, dividends) separately from other changes","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. A comparative SOCE is also required.","i":"497b9acbb9"},{"s":"sbr-03-5","c":"sbr-03-5#4","x":"card","t":"Notes must be in a systematic order, e.g. most relevant first or the order of the statements","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. This makes them easier to navigate.","i":"211fc65ed2"},{"s":"sbr-03-5","c":"sbr-03-5#5","x":"card","t":"A management-defined performance measure (MPM) is a subtotal of income and expenses used publicly to show management's view","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. E.g. operating profit adjusted for one-off items, used in the annual report.","i":"35d621fbb6"},{"s":"sbr-03-5","c":"sbr-03-5#6","x":"card","t":"Not MPMs: ratios, liquidity measures, non-financial measures and subtotals IFRS requires or commonly used ones (gross profit, PBT)","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Oblong example: adjusted profit is an MPM; profit before financing and ROCE are not.","i":"76f7f0849a"},{"s":"sbr-03-5","c":"sbr-03-5#7","x":"card","t":"For each MPM, a single note must give what it shows and why it's useful, how it's calculated, and a reconciliation to the nearest IFRS subtotal with tax and NCI effects","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It must also say MPMs are management's view and may not be comparable to other companies.","i":"c11f28b4ee"}],"i":"eb0ca20f76"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.6 IFRS 5 discontinued operations","s":"sbr-03-6","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-6","c":"sbr-03-6#0","x":"card","t":"A discontinued operation is a component sold or held for sale that is a separate major line or area, part of a plan to sell one, or a subsidiary bought to resell","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Users need it shown separately to predict future profits.","i":"99e46eb59e"},{"s":"sbr-03-6","c":"sbr-03-6#1","x":"card","t":"Held for sale needs: available now, sale highly probable within a year, management committed, actively marketed, reasonable price and the plan unlikely to change","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. France example: no buyer because the price is too high, so it isn't held for sale.","i":"f88afc1e99"},{"s":"sbr-03-6","c":"sbr-03-6#2","x":"card","t":"A discontinued operation is shown in P/L as one post-tax amount, analysed on the face or in the notes","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. The single amount = its post-tax result + the post-tax gain or loss on remeasurement or disposal.","i":"d0423e8672"},{"s":"sbr-03-6","c":"sbr-03-6#3","x":"card","t":"When an operation becomes discontinued, the comparatives are re-presented as discontinued too","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. So users compare like with like.","i":"91a95384df"},{"s":"sbr-03-6","c":"sbr-03-6#4","x":"card","t":"If the decision to sell is made after the year end, the operation is not discontinued: disclose it as a non-adjusting event","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Croatia example: put up for sale in July, after a June year end.","i":"52157676c8"}],"i":"2dd2e386bd"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.7 IAS 8 basis of preparation","s":"sbr-03-7","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#0","x":"card","t":"IAS 8's new title is Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. It took over some IAS 1 content when IFRS 18 was issued.","i":"151e627127"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#1","x":"card","t":"IAS 8's four general matters are fair presentation, compliance with IFRS, going concern and the accruals basis","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Accruals: transactions are recognised when they occur, not when cash moves.","i":"4910216dd9"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#2","x":"card","t":"Compliance with IFRS must be stated explicitly and without reservation","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. You can't claim compliance 'except for' something.","i":"11434a8643"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#3","x":"card","t":"Going concern is assessed looking at least 12 months from the reporting date","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Material uncertainties must be disclosed; if not a going concern, say so, the basis used and why.","i":"25f856c4cb"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#4","x":"card","t":"Signs of going concern doubt include lack of cash, net liabilities, rising overdrafts, big debt repayments, and negative operating cash flows (and big legal claims or impairments)","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Also longer payables days and high gearing.","i":"bbcdd325fe"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#5","x":"card","t":"Where no standard applies, management develops a policy using standards on similar issues, then the Conceptual Framework","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. The policy must give relevant and reliable information.","i":"f53b134609"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#6","x":"card","t":"A policy can be changed only if a standard requires it or it gives more reliable and relevant information","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Changes are applied retrospectively unless the standard has transitional rules.","i":"6f0b093c59"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#7","x":"card","t":"A retrospective change of policy means restating opening equity and the comparatives","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. It keeps the trend comparable year to year.","i":"9c799ae6f2"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#8","x":"card","t":"Material accounting policies to disclose are those that changed, were chosen from options, involve significant judgement or are complex","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Distinction example: revenue policy disclosed because of judgement on allocation and timing.","i":"a500d10577"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#9","x":"card","t":"A change in accounting estimate is applied prospectively, in current and future periods","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Geranium example: switching to units-of-production depreciation is an estimate change, so no $0.5m catch-up charge.","i":"0660193c95"},{"s":"sbr-03-7","c":"sbr-03-7#10","x":"card","t":"Material prior period errors are corrected retrospectively, restating opening equity and comparatives","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Criticism: corrections never hit current P/L, which can be used to manage earnings.","i":"100cd74ed6"}],"i":"8ccccc5e2e"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.8 IAS 34 Interim reporting","s":"sbr-03-8","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-8","c":"sbr-03-8#0","x":"card","t":"IAS 34 itself does not require interims; they are required by law or listing rules","d":"Reference: IAS 34 Interim Financial Reporting. IAS 34 sets the principles and minimum content if interims are prepared.","i":"9f17aa937c"},{"s":"sbr-03-8","c":"sbr-03-8#1","x":"card","t":"An interim report includes condensed statements of performance, position, equity and cash flows, and selected notes (plus EPS for IAS 33 entities)","d":"Reference: IAS 34 Interim Financial Reporting. Condensed = main headings and subtotals from the last annual statements.","i":"d2c5456a31"},{"s":"sbr-03-8","c":"sbr-03-8#2","x":"card","t":"The interim SoFP is compared with the end of the previous full financial year","d":"Reference: IAS 34 Interim Financial Reporting. P/L is compared with the same interim period (and year to date) last year.","i":"48de5a8bfb"},{"s":"sbr-03-8","c":"sbr-03-8#3","x":"card","t":"Interims use the same policies as annual statements, measured on a year-to-date basis","d":"Reference: IAS 34 Interim Financial Reporting. They rely more on estimates than annual statements. Their value: timeliness.","i":"17c502e770"}],"i":"6436a2d370"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"3.S1 Statements and decisions in order","s":"sbr-03-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-structure-1","c":"sbr-03-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"The statement of profit or loss under IFRS 18","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Only three subtotals are compulsory: operating profit, profit before financing and income taxes, and profit.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Revenue less operating expenses","i":"e6e2bc207c"},{"x":"step","t":"Operating profit","i":"b076aa0319"},{"x":"step","t":"Investing income, such as associates and dividends","i":"0078f2afd4"},{"x":"step","t":"Profit before financing and income taxes","i":"396926ef15"},{"x":"step","t":"Financing expenses","i":"b7c9bf1b61"},{"x":"step","t":"Income tax","i":"ff720e93ef"},{"x":"step","t":"Discontinued operations","i":"cf51b52f44"},{"x":"step","t":"Profit for the year","i":"f4cfb95aba"}],"i":"284f05bc82"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-1","c":"sbr-03-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Presenting an unusual expense","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Non-recurring costs of the main business stay in operating. Not part of it: Move it below operating profit because it's non-recurring; Net it against related income to show its true effect.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Decide its category by what it arises from","i":"b3ba47130d"},{"x":"step","t":"Leave it there even if it's non-recurring","i":"ae1c1f7410"},{"x":"step","t":"Show it separately if that's material","i":"70d98dee60"},{"x":"step","t":"Label it clearly","i":"db010e91dc"},{"x":"step","t":"Avoid burying it in 'other'","i":"170a1c93f2"}],"any":true,"i":"5e040462d5"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-1","c":"sbr-03-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Deciding whether a measure is an MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Ratios and measures of liquidity fail at the first question.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Is it a subtotal of income and expenses?","i":"28d85ef080"},{"x":"step","t":"Is it used outside the statements, in public communications?","i":"5aa8886bc2"},{"x":"step","t":"Does it show management's view of performance?","i":"90a00c5b1c"},{"x":"step","t":"Is it not required by IFRS or commonly used?","i":"b103c9ec3b"},{"x":"step","t":"If all yes, it's an MPM and needs the note","i":"e03a73e862"}],"i":"3b46a89f34"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-1","c":"sbr-03-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"The note on an MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. All in a single note. Not part of it: An audit opinion on the measure; A forecast of the measure for next year.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"What it reflects and why it's useful","i":"e5ae5771f3"},{"x":"step","t":"How it's calculated","i":"c4b56dd31f"},{"x":"step","t":"Reconciliation to the closest IFRS subtotal","i":"a1caf1515b"},{"x":"step","t":"Tax and NCI effect of each reconciling item","i":"f71ae7ac71"},{"x":"step","t":"How the tax effect was determined","i":"4e5ca53c65"},{"x":"step","t":"Statement that it's management's view and may not be comparable","i":"1b954492b4"}],"any":true,"i":"033a0a9a99"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-1","c":"sbr-03-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"Testing for a discontinued operation at the reporting date","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. It must qualify by the reporting date.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Is it a component of the entity?","i":"83e6d1d466"},{"x":"step","t":"Is it a separate line of business or area, or bought only to resell?","i":"34ce304edc"},{"x":"step","t":"Has it been sold, or met the held-for-sale conditions?","i":"a0d0497a9e"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, show one post-tax amount and re-present comparatives","i":"a45735c278"},{"x":"step","t":"If the decision came after the year end, only disclose it","i":"a12e3b25b0"}],"i":"ed12aee83c"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-1","c":"sbr-03-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"Dealing with going concern","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Disclosure and basis are separate decisions.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Look at least 12 months from the reporting date","i":"59ef77b77d"},{"x":"step","t":"Weigh the indicators and the plans","i":"4551aa4aa6"},{"x":"step","t":"No material uncertainty: use the going concern basis","i":"7f36e9c8d4"},{"x":"step","t":"Material uncertainty: use it and disclose the uncertainty","i":"6b149b0f8e"},{"x":"step","t":"If it isn't appropriate, say so and use another basis, likely break-up","i":"66edaf18c1"}],"i":"c48aee1f0d"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-1","c":"sbr-03-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"Changing an accounting policy","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. This protects comparability.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Check a standard requires it, or it gives more reliable and relevant information","i":"a0b27c966d"},{"x":"step","t":"Follow any transitional rules","i":"59df495b5e"},{"x":"step","t":"Otherwise apply it retrospectively","i":"e5bc2e7614"},{"x":"step","t":"Adjust opening equity","i":"87a7418013"},{"x":"step","t":"Restate the comparatives","i":"f88d1f3402"}],"i":"91562e5fb5"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-1","c":"sbr-03-structure-1#7","x":"steps","t":"Correcting a material prior period error","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Otherwise it would distort this year's performance. Not part of it: Correct it through this year's P/L as an exceptional item; Change it prospectively, like an estimate.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify the period it arose in","i":"21ccd54db1"},{"x":"step","t":"Restate the comparatives for that period","i":"2dd7ca6a8d"},{"x":"step","t":"Adjust opening equity if it arose earlier","i":"6672cf49a3"},{"x":"step","t":"Don't take it through this year's P/L","i":"43964f188d"},{"x":"step","t":"Disclose the error and its effects","i":"135fc96a12"}],"any":true,"i":"d3e8394435"}],"i":"ab3621bfbc"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.S2 Which IFRS 18 category?","s":"sbr-03-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Revenue from customers","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Main business.","i":"42ea4e287e"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill impairment loss","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. IFRS 18's example shows it above operating profit.","i":"2561e52a08"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Gain on disposal of PPE","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. PPE is used with other assets in the business.","i":"6b76df2a98"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Current service cost of a pension scheme","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Only the interest element goes to financing.","i":"864d689aff"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Share of profit of an associate","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Investing.","i":"1a7c118add"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Dividends from an equity investment","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. A return on an independent asset.","i":"c42d9be934"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Interest income on a deposit account","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Cash and cash equivalents.","i":"9c1d12ea0e"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Rental income from investment property","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Generates income independently.","i":"2ea3b1a115"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Interest on a bank loan","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Raising finance only.","i":"26a0a3a934"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Interest on lease liabilities","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. The interest element only.","i":"996dbd3558"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Unwinding of the discount on a provision","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. The interest element.","i":"efdf4976c5"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#11","x":"sort","t":"Interest cost on pension liabilities","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. The interest element.","i":"c0a40487bc"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#12","x":"sort","t":"Income tax expense","a":"Income taxes","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Its own category.","i":"8da398a903"},{"s":"sbr-03-structure-2","c":"sbr-03-structure-2#13","x":"sort","t":"Post-tax result of a sold line of business","a":"Discontinued","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. One amount, with the analysis shown there or in a note.","i":"8eddb1849d"}],"i":"7173218f99"}],"i":"ed3e203763"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"3.C1 IFRS 18 side by side","s":"sbr-03-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Exchange loss on a trade receivable","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Follows the item it relates to.","i":"8f166a4980"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Exchange loss on a foreign bank loan","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Follows the item it relates to.","i":"2195124287"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Exchange gain on a foreign currency bond held as an investment","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Follows the item it relates to.","i":"1b85e4c58c"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Translating a foreign operation","a":"May be recycled","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. On disposal of the operation.","i":"bff2cdb386"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Cash flow hedge gains","a":"May be recycled","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. When the hedged flow affects profit.","i":"c35b0a47f6"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Fair value changes on FVOCI debt","a":"May be recycled","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. On disposal.","i":"cbc0de8e19"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Revaluation surplus on PPE","a":"Never recycled","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It can only move to retained earnings.","i":"eece5bb819"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Pension remeasurements","a":"Never recycled","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Never.","i":"95547c1ea3"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Fair value changes on FVOCI equity","a":"Never recycled","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Never.","i":"6be544937c"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Lets users work out gross margin","a":"By function","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Cost of sales is shown.","i":"92722ba94a"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Helps forecast staff and material costs","a":"By nature","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Shows each kind of cost.","i":"b1c18076c9"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Interest on customer deposits at a bank","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Like cost of sales for a lender.","i":"36b3114ba4"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Investment income of an insurer investing as its main business","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It's part of what the entity does.","i":"3f19d734b1"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Share of profit of an equity-accounted associate at that insurer","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Equity-accounted investments stay in investing.","i":"9eaa1d7260"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-1","c":"sbr-03-compare-1#14","x":"sort","t":"Interest on bonds a bank raised to buy its head office","a":"Policy: operating or financing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Not related to providing finance to customers.","i":"ab72f5f0d9"}],"i":"b846636cf9"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.C2 IAS 8, IFRS 5 and MPMs","s":"sbr-03-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Changing from FIFO to weighted average for inventory","a":"Policy","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. A change in how cost is determined.","i":"53123ebbc0"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Moving from cost to fair value for investment property","a":"Policy","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements; IAS 40 Investment Property. A choice between models.","i":"ec9bd020cd"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Revising the useful life of a machine","a":"Estimate","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. New information about the same asset.","i":"2b1ae8a8dc"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Revising the size of a warranty provision","a":"Estimate","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Judgement updated.","i":"76f3d882f4"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Finding last year's closing inventory was counted twice","a":"Error","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. A mistake in earlier statements.","i":"e62610b38b"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"A policy change with no transitional rules","a":"Retrospective","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. As if the new policy had always applied.","i":"a9e51bbccc"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"A material prior period error","a":"Retrospective","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Comparatives are restated.","i":"dd2500dee4"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"A change in accounting estimate","a":"Prospective","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Current and future periods only.","i":"fc7e5b0f2a"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"A major line of business sold during the year","a":"Discontinued at year end","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Sold.","i":"92b3475672"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"A major subsidiary in advanced talks, with the sale expected within months of the year end","a":"Discontinued at year end","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Meets the conditions.","i":"dc1b651d0c"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#10","x":"sort","t":"A major subsidiary whose price is deterring buyers","a":"Not discontinued","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Not at a reasonable price.","i":"882b9d211c"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#11","x":"sort","t":"A major subsidiary put up for sale just after the year end","a":"Not discontinued","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. A non-adjusting event.","i":"edde6e6383"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#12","x":"sort","t":"Adjusted revenue","a":"Not an MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Only income, not income and expenses.","i":"4b50ff5822"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#13","x":"sort","t":"Operating profit adjusted for restructuring costs, used in the annual report","a":"MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Management's view of performance.","i":"94888d37d2"},{"s":"sbr-03-compare-2","c":"sbr-03-compare-2#14","x":"sort","t":"Profit before tax","a":"Not an MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Commonly used.","i":"7a3efd67e9"}],"i":"dc96052e7d"}],"i":"2a85732c55"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"3.W IFRS 18, IAS 8 and IAS 34 traps","s":"sbr-03-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"Store closure costs can go below operating profit if they are non-recurring.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. They come from the main business, so they stay in operating, shown separately if material.","i":"3a6178bb48"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Interest income on bonds belongs above operating profit for a retailer.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It's investing.","i":"c418d933f6"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"The category of an exchange difference follows the related item.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. IFRS 18 doesn't say which line, only which category.","i":"d306e4daa9"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"An unwinding discount on a provision is a financing expense.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. The initial expense goes to operating.","i":"f7335e0d1f"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"Extra subtotals can be more prominent than the required ones if useful.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. They mustn't be more prominent.","i":"24313688cc"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"'Other expenses' is acceptable for material items of different kinds.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. They should be split, and 'other' avoided or made precise.","i":"6e6b0568b2"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"Goodwill must now be shown on its own line.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Under IAS 1 it could be combined with other intangibles.","i":"85cd6ecb68"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Cheese maturing for 15 months can still be a current asset.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It's realised in the normal trading cycle.","i":"54bd038242"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"A loan the entity has a right to roll over for two years is current if it plans to repay it soon.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Classification ignores how likely it is to use the right.","i":"99851e837e"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Offsetting income and expenses is allowed if it makes the statement clearer.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Only when a standard requires or permits it.","i":"e81786c0aa"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"Reclassifying from OCI can be criticised for recording gains in a later period than the change.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It contradicts the Framework's income and expense definitions.","i":"74fcd47007"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"Comparative figures are re-presented when an operation becomes discontinued.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. So users can see the continuing trend.","i":"e2143a3e50"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"A subsidiary bought only to resell can be a discontinued operation.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. One of the routes in the definition.","i":"3def2e4d78"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#13","x":"trap","t":"A material prior period error is corrected through this year's P/L.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. It's retrospective.","i":"893d940ee3"},{"s":"sbr-03-watch-1","c":"sbr-03-watch-1#14","x":"trap","t":"IAS 34 requires listed entities to publish interim reports.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 34 Interim Financial Reporting. That comes from law or listing rules.","i":"33d73903a7"}],"i":"66668c581e"}],"i":"c21ec1ee87"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"3.A1 Presenting performance","s":"sbr-03-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Alma's draft: gross profit 600; selling (100); admin (120); other operating (52, a 40 provision plus 12 unwinding); interest income on bonds 10; then store closure costs (30) below operating profit. What's correct operating profit?","a":"310","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: categories follow the source, not how unusual the item is. Applied: 600 − 100 − 120 − 40 − 30 = 310. The 10 moves to investing, the 12 to financing, and the closure costs up into operating. Users: operating profit becomes comparable. Common mistake: moving only some items.","i":"05a372ba02"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Lumo shows 'other operating expenses' of $6m made up of admin and marketing, and 'other income' with dividends and sublease rent. Which comment is best?","a":"They hide items that should be split","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: disaggregate items with different characteristics if material, and avoid 'other'. Applied: admin and marketing have different functions; dividends and rent are investing, not operating. Users: they can't assess costs or returns. Common mistake: thinking accuracy of totals is enough.","i":"595acd95cd"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A company's only foreign loan produces a $2m exchange loss, and its overseas receivables a $1m exchange gain. Where do they go?","a":"Financing and operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: exchange differences follow the related item. Common mistake: putting all exchange differences in one line.","i":"239e81f105"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"A bank pays interest on customers' deposits and also on a bond it issued to fund its new headquarters. How is this presented?","a":"Operating, and a policy choice for the bond","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: a lender's interest on funds for lending is operating; on other finance-only liabilities, there's a policy choice of operating or financing. Users: margins are comparable with those of other banks. Common mistake: using the ordinary company's categories.","i":"d692e567b8"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Moor's new wine needs another 18 months to mature before sale, and it normally takes that long in the business. The directors want it shown as non-current. Are they right?","a":"No: it's current","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: an asset realised in the normal trading cycle is current, even beyond 12 months. Common mistake: applying the 12-month test on its own.","i":"de51036562"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"A loan falls due in 9 months, but at the year end the entity has the right to extend it for two years. It intends to repay it early. How is the loan classified?","a":"Non-current","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: a liability is current if there's no right to defer at the reporting date; intention is ignored. Common mistake: following management's plans.","i":"56b5c3179c"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"An annual report features adjusted profit (operating profit plus one-off costs), profit before financing and income taxes, and ROCE. Which is an MPM?","a":"Adjusted profit only","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: an MPM is a subtotal of income and expenses showing management's view that isn't required or commonly used. Applied: the second is required by IFRS 18; ROCE is a ratio. Common mistake: treating any headline figure as an MPM.","i":"69449a0b45"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"Tilt highlights 'underlying operating profit' in its annual report. Its only explanation is a footnote saying it's 'before one-offs', with no reconciliation. What's wrong?","a":"The single MPM note is missing","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: for each MPM, a single note must explain it, show the calculation, and reconcile it, with the tax and NCI effects. Why: it brings these measures within the audited statements. Users: they can check what's excluded. Common mistake: banning it, rather than disclosing it.","i":"653112e5fe"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"A company adds a line for 'EBITDA before one-offs' in large type directly above operating profit. Is this allowed?","a":"Not with more prominence","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: extra subtotals must use IFRS amounts, be consistent and not be more prominent than the required ones. Common mistake: thinking usefulness outweighs prominence.","i":"3e102d8808"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-1","c":"sbr-03-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"Which of these goes in the OCI group that will not be recycled?","a":"A pension remeasurement gain","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: revaluation surplus, pension remeasurements and FVOCI equity are never recycled. Applied: the other three can be, on specific conditions. Common mistake: mixing up FVOCI debt and equity.","i":"c2f2d1d5ee"}],"i":"2572063bdb"},{"x":"stage","t":"3.A2 Discontinued operations, IAS 8 and IAS 34","s":"sbr-03-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"At a 30 June year end, Alba was sold in January; Bree is in advanced talks, with a sale due by October; Cove has an agent, but its asking price deters buyers; Dene was put up for sale in July. All are major lines. Which are discontinued?","a":"Alba and Bree","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Rule: sold, or held for sale at the reporting date. Applied: Bree meets the conditions; Cove isn't at a reasonable price; Dene's decision came later: a non-adjusting event. Common mistake: including anything management intends to sell.","i":"2ca3a4c6aa"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"A discontinued line lost $8m after tax in the year, and the loss on remeasuring it to fair value less costs to sell was $3m after tax. Last year it lost $1m. How is it shown?","a":"One amount of $11m, with $1m as comparative","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Rule: one post-tax amount, with analysis in the statement or notes; comparatives re-presented. Users: they can see continuing profit as a base for forecasts. Common mistake: leaving the comparatives alone.","i":"3ad57629fd"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"Hawk has net liabilities, rising overdrafts and a large loan due in 10 months, but the bank has signed a binding refinancing. Management sees no material uncertainty. How does it prepare its statements?","a":"Going concern basis","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: indicators prompt the assessment, and a material uncertainty requires disclosure. Applied: with refinancing secured, there isn't one here. If it weren't signed, the uncertainty would be disclosed. Common mistake: seeing indicators as automatically meaning break-up.","i":"4b8c24ffdb"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"Reo moves from FIFO to weighted average because it's more reliable. Opening inventory would have been $2m higher (ignore tax). What's recorded?","a":"$2m to opening retained earnings; comparatives restated","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: with no transitional rules, policy changes are retrospective. Users: the trend stays comparable. Common mistake: taking the effect to current-year profit.","i":"ba2c29357d"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"Reo decides that a machine will last 3 years rather than 5 from now on. What's the treatment?","a":"Prospective change in depreciation","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: estimates are changed prospectively. Common mistake: treating it as a policy change.","i":"88f30ae067"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#5","x":"example","t":"Soon after publishing, Reo finds that last year's closing inventory was overstated by $3m. What's the treatment this year?","a":"Restate comparatives, reducing last year's profit by $3m","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: material prior period errors are retrospective. Why: otherwise this year's performance is distorted. Applied: comparatives and opening retained earnings fall, with disclosure. Common mistake: charging it to this year's results.","i":"205f7e413a"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#6","x":"example","t":"A new kind of digital asset isn't covered by any standard. How does management pick a policy?","a":"Similar standards, then the Framework","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: where no standard applies, judge using standards on similar issues, then the Conceptual Framework, aiming for relevant and reliable information. Ethics: judgement must not be used to flatter results. Common mistake: treating it as a free choice.","i":"85d468a02b"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#7","x":"example","t":"A company's notes copy out every IFRS policy in full, including for items it doesn't have. What should it do?","a":"Disclose only material accounting policies","d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: disclose material policies: those that changed, were chosen from options, need judgement or are complex. Why: boilerplate obscures useful information. Common mistake: thinking more disclosure is always better.","i":"2a007e1dac"},{"s":"sbr-03-apply-2","c":"sbr-03-apply-2#8","x":"example","t":"A listed company prepares half-year statements to 30 June 20X5 under IAS 34. Which comparative goes with the statement of financial position?","a":"31 December 20X4","d":"Reference: IAS 34 Interim Financial Reporting. Rule: the interim position is compared with the previous year end; profit or loss is compared with the same interim period last year. Common mistake: using last year's interim date for both.","i":"0183c337a7"}],"i":"911ee0e3f2"}],"i":"ae9f97087c"}],"i":"99449706c2"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 4: Revenue","ch":4,"d":"Study Text pages 83–108.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"4.1 IFRS 15: steps 1–2","s":"sbr-04-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#0","x":"card","t":"Revenue excludes sales tax and proceeds from selling non-current assets","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Revenue is income from ordinary activities only. Investors rely on it for market share and margins.","i":"2c316edb64"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#1","x":"card","t":"IFRS 15 replaced IAS 18 because IAS 18 was hard to apply and revenue was often manipulated","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. IFRS 15 (2014) gives one robust model, improving comparability.","i":"fe95d9f0f2"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#2","x":"card","t":"The five steps are: identify the contract, identify obligations, set the price, allocate it, and recognise revenue when (or as) each obligation is satisfied","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Exam questions usually test one or two steps applied to a scenario, not all five recited.","i":"b3681faca5"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#3","x":"card","t":"A contract is an agreement creating rights and obligations; it doesn't have to be written","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. It can arise from customary business practice.","i":"f178491579"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#4","x":"card","t":"A contract can be accounted for only if it's approved with identifiable rights, has payment terms, has commercial substance and payment is probable","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Aluna example: the customer's economy collapsed, payment not probable → no revenue.","i":"6cfefd1ce7"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#5","x":"card","t":"A performance obligation is a promise to transfer a distinct good or service","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. E.g. a car plus one year's free servicing = two obligations.","i":"0c802c1d08"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#6","x":"card","t":"A good or service is distinct if the customer can benefit from it on its own and it is separately identifiable in the contract","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Starling example: site clearing, foundations and wiring for a school are integrated into one output → one obligation.","i":"5df0cb3acb"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#7","x":"card","t":"An entity is a principal if it controls the good or service before it reaches the customer","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. A principal recognises gross revenue.","i":"6d07d06de7"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#8","x":"card","t":"An agent recognises revenue equal to its fee or commission","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Deals4U example: supplier sets prices and ships → website is an agent.","i":"2e9041df80"},{"s":"sbr-04-1","c":"sbr-04-1#9","x":"card","t":"A warranty that just assures the product works is an IAS 37 provision, not separate revenue","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. If the customer can buy the warranty separately, it's a distinct obligation and gets part of the price.","i":"c4f461aac0"}],"i":"2ac02e92cd"},{"x":"stage","t":"4.2 IFRS 15: step 3 transaction price","s":"sbr-04-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-2","c":"sbr-04-2#0","x":"card","t":"The transaction price is the consideration the entity expects, excluding sales tax","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Four things adjust it: variable consideration, financing, non-cash consideration, payments to the customer.","i":"96e1b37cf7"},{"s":"sbr-04-2","c":"sbr-04-2#1","x":"card","t":"Variable consideration is included only if it's highly probable that there will be no significant reversal of revenue later","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Bristow example: experienced → include the $3m bonus; no experience → leave it out.","i":"938bccabd2"},{"s":"sbr-04-2","c":"sbr-04-2#2","x":"card","t":"Variable consideration is estimated using the expected value or the most likely amount (best when there are two outcomes)","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Expected value suits many similar contracts; most likely amount suits all-or-nothing bonuses.","i":"a28c1cdad3"},{"s":"sbr-04-2","c":"sbr-04-2#3","x":"card","t":"With a right of return, revenue is recognised only for goods not expected back, plus a refund liability and an asset for goods expected to be returned","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Nardone: 50 sold, 2 expected back → revenue 48,000, refund liability 2,000, asset 800 (at cost).","i":"7f13c6a657"},{"s":"sbr-04-2","c":"sbr-04-2#4","x":"card","t":"A significant financing component is suggested by a gap between promised and cash price, or a long time between transfer and payment","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rudd: $1m due in two years at 10% → revenue $826,446 now; the rest is interest.","i":"47d8502d7b"},{"s":"sbr-04-2","c":"sbr-04-2#5","x":"card","t":"The discount rate for a financing component is the rate the customer would borrow at","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Afterwards the receivable follows IFRS 9.","i":"6605ba41e7"},{"s":"sbr-04-2","c":"sbr-04-2#6","x":"card","t":"Non-cash consideration is measured at fair value at contract inception","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Dan & Stan: 1,000 shares at $4 → revenue $4,000. Later share price changes go to IFRS 9, not revenue.","i":"43d51b3c64"},{"s":"sbr-04-2","c":"sbr-04-2#7","x":"card","t":"A payment to a customer that isn't for a distinct good or service is a reduction of the transaction price","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Golden Gate: $1m on a $20m deal = 5% less on every sale → revenue 3.8m on 4m of goods.","i":"cba23441ab"}],"i":"a6a1fd5348"},{"x":"stage","t":"4.3 IFRS 15: steps 4–5","s":"sbr-04-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#0","x":"card","t":"The price is allocated to obligations in proportion to their stand-alone selling prices","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. The best evidence of a stand-alone price is the observable price when sold separately.","i":"142c36807c"},{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#1","x":"card","t":"If a stand-alone price isn't observable, it is estimated, using as many observable inputs as possible","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Methods: adjusted market price, expected cost plus margin, residual (only in limited cases).","i":"0b8991f991"},{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#2","x":"card","t":"A bundle discount is spread across all obligations in proportion to stand-alone prices","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Shred: machine 95 + support 30 = 125 for 100 → 76 and 24.","i":"e48e6fcf7b"},{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#3","x":"card","t":"Revenue is triggered by satisfying an obligation, not by receiving payment, even non-refundable payment","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. The over-time/point-in-time decision is made at contract inception.","i":"98ee62c99e"},{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#4","x":"card","t":"An obligation is satisfied over time if the customer consumes the benefit as it's performed, controls the asset as it's built, or the asset has no alternative use and there's an enforceable right to payment to date","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Evans payroll: customer consumes as performed → over time.","i":"1239b3f389"},{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#5","x":"card","t":"A specialised factory where only the deposit is enforceable is recognised at a point in time","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Crawford: no right to payment for work done to date → wait until control passes.","i":"c367172741"},{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#6","x":"card","t":"Progress over time is measured by output methods or input methods, e.g. costs incurred / total costs","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Baker: $1.0m of $1.5m costs → 2/3 of $2m revenue. The uncertain bonus is excluded.","i":"a16484cc34"},{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#7","x":"card","t":"If progress can't be measured reliably, revenue is limited to recoverable costs incurred","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. No profit is recognised until progress can be measured.","i":"e451849f09"},{"s":"sbr-04-3","c":"sbr-04-3#8","x":"card","t":"Indicators that control has passed: right to payment, legal title, physical possession, risks and rewards, and customer acceptance","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Clarence magazines: possession passed, but title, payment rights and return risk stayed → no revenue yet.","i":"9aa0134b1f"}],"i":"b2972bf8e6"},{"x":"stage","t":"4.4 IFRS 15: consignments and repurchases","s":"sbr-04-4","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-4","c":"sbr-04-4#0","x":"card","t":"A consignment is when goods go to a dealer but control doesn't pass, so no revenue until the dealer sells","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Gazebo can recall goods and isn't paid until the end sale → consignment.","i":"5bdc2cd29e"},{"s":"sbr-04-4","c":"sbr-04-4#1","x":"card","t":"Signs of consignment: the entity controls goods until an event, can demand them back, and the dealer has no unconditional obligation to pay","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Revenue waits until the dealer sells to the end customer.","i":"a521b3b651"},{"s":"sbr-04-4","c":"sbr-04-4#2","x":"card","t":"The three repurchase forms are a forward, a call option and a put option (the customer can force a buy-back)","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 16 Leases. Common with property and maturing goods like whisky or cheese.","i":"e8d6192ebd"},{"s":"sbr-04-4","c":"sbr-04-4#3","x":"card","t":"With a forward or call, if the repurchase price is at least the sale price, it's a financing arrangement: keep the asset, record a loan","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Control never passes because the seller can or must take it back.","i":"088be06ab5"},{"s":"sbr-04-4","c":"sbr-04-4#4","x":"card","t":"With a forward or call, if the repurchase price is below the sale price, it's a lease (lessor accounting)","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. The customer is paying to use the asset for a while.","i":"0cb902bcb5"},{"s":"sbr-04-4","c":"sbr-04-4#5","x":"card","t":"A put option is treated as financing or a lease when the customer has a significant incentive to exercise it","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Incentive = repurchase price above expected market value. Lentil: $5m vs $4.5m → loan of $4m.","i":"a6a1b81aa2"},{"s":"sbr-04-4","c":"sbr-04-4#6","x":"card","t":"A put option with no incentive to exercise is a sale with a right of return","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Treat as variable consideration.","i":"2b39b92b0d"}],"i":"d65f84ff48"},{"x":"stage","t":"4.5 IFRS 15: modifications, costs, presentation","s":"sbr-04-5","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-5","c":"sbr-04-5#0","x":"card","t":"A modification is a separate contract if it adds distinct goods and the price rises by their stand-alone prices","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Salty (a): 200 extra at $57 SSP → separate contract; the original 900 × $60 continues.","i":"61bd32f61f"},{"s":"sbr-04-5","c":"sbr-04-5#1","x":"card","t":"If remaining goods are distinct but it's not a separate contract, it's treated as ending the old contract and starting a new one","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Salty (b): (300 × 60 + 200 × 40) / 500 = $52 for each remaining product.","i":"2d6b8e065f"},{"s":"sbr-04-5","c":"sbr-04-5#2","x":"card","t":"If remaining goods aren't distinct, the modification is part of the original contract with a cumulative catch-up","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. E.g. changing a building's floor plan midway.","i":"a06c747a6f"},{"s":"sbr-04-5","c":"sbr-04-5#3","x":"card","t":"The costs of obtaining a contract are capitalised only if they are incremental (e.g. a sales commission for winning)","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Strangers: research fees and travel happen win or lose → expense.","i":"c91127a911"},{"s":"sbr-04-5","c":"sbr-04-5#4","x":"card","t":"The costs of fulfilling a contract are capitalised if not in another standard and expected to be recovered","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Capitalised contract costs are amortised as revenue is recognised. Wasted materials are expensed.","i":"8bee31cee6"},{"s":"sbr-04-5","c":"sbr-04-5#5","x":"card","t":"Revenue recognised before payment, where the right is conditional, is a contract asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. If only time must pass before payment, it's a receivable instead.","i":"762cff06ed"},{"s":"sbr-04-5","c":"sbr-04-5#6","x":"card","t":"Cash received before revenue is recognised is a contract liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. E.g. a full year's support fee received in advance.","i":"cf2854a287"},{"s":"sbr-04-5","c":"sbr-04-5#7","x":"card","t":"IFRS 15 requires disclosure of revenue, contract balances, cost assets and significant judgements and changes in them","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Judgement runs through every step, which is why ethics matters here.","i":"ccb4a2b2de"}],"i":"975da0baaa"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"4.S1 Working through revenue","s":"sbr-04-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-structure-1","c":"sbr-04-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"The IFRS 15 model","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Exam questions usually test one or two steps applied to a scenario.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify the contract","i":"95db2b70e3"},{"x":"step","t":"Identify the separate performance obligations","i":"f91ea6f72e"},{"x":"step","t":"Determine the transaction price","i":"9861c808f5"},{"x":"step","t":"Allocate the price to the obligations","i":"132579588b"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise revenue when or as each obligation is satisfied","i":"b4b63dc5c2"}],"i":"fc3489ff82"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-1","c":"sbr-04-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Variable consideration","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Experience of similar contracts supports including it.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify the uncertain amount","i":"ae36f6960c"},{"x":"step","t":"Estimate it: most likely amount for two outcomes, expected value for many","i":"b741ff516a"},{"x":"step","t":"Ask if a significant reversal is highly unlikely","i":"f46abc94b6"},{"x":"step","t":"Include it in the price only if so","i":"992abeaf9c"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise it as the obligation is satisfied","i":"a5b0d07fe4"}],"i":"43a4ce7690"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-1","c":"sbr-04-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"A sale with a right of return","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. The asset is measured at the cost of the items expected back.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Estimate how many items will come back","i":"bfad54b0f8"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise revenue for those expected to be kept","i":"c9c0db9d86"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise a refund liability for the rest","i":"f3b2ee2d1e"},{"x":"step","t":"Derecognise the inventory","i":"3a5beca3d5"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise an asset for the items expected back, crediting cost of sales","i":"c5b48b86e5"}],"i":"1f4090b8f4"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-1","c":"sbr-04-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Allocating a bundle discount","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. A residual figure is unreliable if it's far below what similar services earn.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"List the observable stand-alone prices","i":"f478b57dbc"},{"x":"step","t":"Estimate any others, e.g. expected cost plus margin","i":"c07b47f874"},{"x":"step","t":"Add them up","i":"fe04d9637e"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare with the bundle price to find the discount","i":"fef0bd6c91"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply the discount to every obligation in proportion","i":"69153e495b"}],"i":"11f15cff8e"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-1","c":"sbr-04-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"A significant financing component","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Only the present value is revenue; the rest is interest.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Notice the gap between transfer and payment","i":"e575e679fb"},{"x":"step","t":"Find the rate at which the customer would borrow","i":"e9c93ca574"},{"x":"step","t":"Discount the amount due","i":"4686d8c608"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise revenue and a receivable at the present value","i":"5adb08cfe0"},{"x":"step","t":"Unwind the discount under IFRS 9","i":"3f42b9ec10"}],"i":"e27f697b97"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-1","c":"sbr-04-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"Over time or at a point in time?","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Decide this at contract inception.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Does the customer receive and consume the benefits as work is done?","i":"95ad713ee6"},{"x":"step","t":"Does the work create or enhance an asset the customer controls?","i":"53566ce402"},{"x":"step","t":"Is there no alternative use and an enforceable right to payment to date?","i":"c64d4055c3"},{"x":"step","t":"If any answer is yes, recognise over time","i":"4d867499c5"},{"x":"step","t":"If not, find the point when control passes","i":"2639378d15"}],"i":"e6a10d9e3e"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-1","c":"sbr-04-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"A sale with a repurchase term","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. For a put, first ask whether the customer has an incentive to exercise.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify who holds the right: seller (forward or call) or customer (put)","i":"af305fd1cf"},{"x":"step","t":"Seller: control doesn't pass, so no sale","i":"bd69ded9e1"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare the repurchase price with the sale price","i":"80b5b5bd48"},{"x":"step","t":"At or above the sale price: a loan","i":"eb34cc6230"},{"x":"step","t":"Below the sale price: a lease","i":"ce868632d4"}],"i":"bd0213547e"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-1","c":"sbr-04-structure-1#7","x":"steps","t":"A contract modification","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Each route gives a different revenue figure.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Are distinct goods added at their stand-alone prices?","i":"ab96875f54"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, treat as a separate contract","i":"c27541dd53"},{"x":"step","t":"If not, are the remaining goods distinct from those already transferred?","i":"5291962ee7"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, end the old contract and start a new one","i":"babef17252"},{"x":"step","t":"If not, adjust the original contract with a catch-up","i":"f5a66e2431"}],"i":"1844639b58"}],"i":"9969f09f36"},{"x":"stage","t":"4.S2 Which step, which balance?","s":"sbr-04-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"The customer's finances collapse, so payment is no longer probable","a":"Step 1: the contract","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. No contract can be accounted for.","i":"1db10b799b"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Deciding if a two-year service plan is separate from the car sold","a":"Step 2: performance obligations","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Is it distinct?","i":"915241fe83"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Deciding if a building's wiring and foundations are separate from the build","a":"Step 2: performance obligations","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. They're integrated into one output.","i":"fa13a5a18d"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"A performance bonus that may or may not be earned","a":"Step 3: transaction price","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Variable consideration.","i":"4c35e3f16e"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Paying a customer to refit its stores","a":"Step 3: transaction price","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. A reduction of the price.","i":"156d9d40b0"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Spreading a bundle discount over a machine and support","a":"Step 4: allocation","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. In proportion to stand-alone prices.","i":"3d2b40d8de"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Deciding if a delivered item's control has passed","a":"Step 5: recognition","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Point-in-time indicators.","i":"cd11d2fd95"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Work is done and invoiced, and only time must pass before payment","a":"Receivable","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. An unconditional right.","i":"cae9891429"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Revenue is recognised, but payment depends on finishing the next stage","a":"Contract asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. A conditional right.","i":"5ea293d904"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"A full year's fee is received in advance","a":"Contract liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Revenue still to be earned.","i":"946cd08d35"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Commission paid to staff for winning a contract","a":"Capitalise","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Only incurred because the contract was won.","i":"6d1bac946e"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#11","x":"sort","t":"Set-up costs of a contract, not in another standard, expected to be recovered","a":"Capitalise","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Costs of fulfilling a contract.","i":"3180621fda"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#12","x":"sort","t":"Travel to present a bid","a":"Expense","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Incurred win or lose.","i":"399013464c"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#13","x":"sort","t":"Wasted labour on a project","a":"Expense","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. It's expensed as incurred.","i":"9deb70fc7e"},{"s":"sbr-04-structure-2","c":"sbr-04-structure-2#14","x":"sort","t":"General overheads","a":"Expense","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. It's expensed as incurred.","i":"5551d653c4"}],"i":"c6bbe8e516"}],"i":"6da0e0ea7c"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"4.C1 Alike but different","s":"sbr-04-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Controls the good before it reaches the customer","a":"Principal","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Recognises gross revenue.","i":"0e493b53b3"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Recognises only its commission","a":"Agent","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. It arranges the sale.","i":"e64246c40f"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Website where suppliers set prices and deliver goods","a":"Agent","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Control stays with the supplier.","i":"9df6e70aa4"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Retailer that buys stock and sets its own prices","a":"Principal","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. It controls the goods first.","i":"b42ff70540"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Warranty that the product works as intended","a":"IAS 37 provision","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. An assurance warranty.","i":"6a267ddd7f"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Warranty the customer could choose to buy separately","a":"Distinct performance obligation","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Part of the price is allocated to it.","i":"5f19dbb5e9"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Many similar contracts with a range of outcomes","a":"Expected value","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. A probability-weighted estimate.","i":"71345d6914"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"A bonus that is either earned in full or not at all","a":"Most likely amount","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Only two outcomes.","i":"9a1f01e441"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Output measure, such as a survey of work done or time elapsed","a":"Output method","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Measures what has been delivered.","i":"7265b114b8"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Costs incurred as a share of total expected costs","a":"Input method","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Measures effort put in.","i":"634aaa62fd"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Dealer can return goods and pays only when it sells them","a":"Consignment: no revenue yet","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Control hasn't passed.","i":"474ef8882a"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-1","c":"sbr-04-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Customer has title, must pay within 30 days and can't return goods","a":"Sale: revenue on delivery","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Control has passed.","i":"c4a649e280"}],"i":"8f320020df"},{"x":"stage","t":"4.C2 Repurchases and modifications","s":"sbr-04-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Seller has a call option at a price above the sale price","a":"Loan","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 16 Leases. Financing: the asset stays.","i":"5ae7af4fa5"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Seller must buy back at the same price","a":"Loan","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Equal to or above the sale price.","i":"ec9b422493"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Seller has a call option at a price below the sale price","a":"Lease","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 16 Leases. The customer is paying for use.","i":"90e377290d"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Customer's put, likely to be exercised, at above the sale price","a":"Loan","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. An incentive exists, and the price is at or above the sale price.","i":"c32a60ac94"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Customer's put, likely to be exercised, at below the sale price","a":"Lease","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. An incentive exists, and the price is below the sale price.","i":"1ce8d87a3c"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Customer's put at far below the expected market value","a":"Sale with right of return","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. No incentive, so no obligation.","i":"064b36c43d"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Extra distinct goods at their stand-alone price","a":"Separate contract","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Original contract is untouched.","i":"c01d94b9a5"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Extra distinct goods at a discount","a":"New contract replaces the old","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Remaining price is spread over remaining goods.","i":"dc7a8aa16a"},{"s":"sbr-04-compare-2","c":"sbr-04-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"A changed floor plan on a building still under construction","a":"Catch-up on the original","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Still one performance obligation.","i":"3a429630c6"}],"i":"ea28cfd548"}],"i":"de0ade18db"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"4.W Revenue traps","s":"sbr-04-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"A contract has to be in writing for IFRS 15 to apply.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. It can arise through customary business practice.","i":"b3c434d94f"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"A non-refundable deposit is revenue when it is received.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Revenue depends on satisfying the obligation, not on payment.","i":"6a9db872ac"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Past behaviour can create performance obligations not stated in the contract.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. It can set customer expectations.","i":"3a413fb91c"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Revenue is at present value when payment is long deferred.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. The difference is interest.","i":"74ab06694a"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A later rise in the value of shares received as consideration increases revenue.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Non-cash consideration is measured at contract inception; the change falls under IFRS 9.","i":"49afd25a1e"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"An uncertain bonus can be included if the entity has good experience of earning it.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. If a significant reversal is highly unlikely.","i":"cbc3d6f031"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"A payment to a customer is always an expense.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. If it isn't for a distinct good or service, it reduces the price.","i":"200ba7f115"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"A bundle discount goes only to the item with the highest margin.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Spread in proportion, unless there's evidence otherwise.","i":"b56de9494d"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Physical possession alone proves that control has passed.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. It's only one indicator; title, payment rights and risks matter too.","i":"12c6d8a8ac"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"An asset with no alternative use is recognised over time, whatever the payment terms.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. There must also be an enforceable right to payment to date.","i":"e2c5037317"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"If progress can't be measured reliably, revenue is limited to recoverable costs.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. No profit until progress can be measured.","i":"bbf7ffdb32"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"An entity with a call option on an asset it sold hasn't really sold it.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 16 Leases. The customer can't direct its use or obtain most of its benefits.","i":"35b3098d69"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"A customer's put option always makes the deal a financing arrangement.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Only with an incentive to exercise; otherwise it's a right of return.","i":"5b88389982"},{"s":"sbr-04-watch-1","c":"sbr-04-watch-1#13","x":"trap","t":"Judgement in revenue increases the risk of manipulation, so ethics matters.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Many scandals have involved revenue.","i":"c63c7ae6fe"}],"i":"acc1e18fe4"}],"i":"487fd6ed3b"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"4.A1 The contract, the price and allocation","s":"sbr-04-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Merlin starts a 12-month service on 1 October for $24m, with $6m more if error targets are met. It has done this often, and earning the bonus is highly probable. What is the revenue to 31 December?","a":"$7.5m","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: include variable consideration if a significant reversal is highly unlikely. Applied: price $30m; three of 12 months gives $7.5m. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: leaving out the bonus, which is right only if the entity lacks experience.","i":"158376593d"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Merlin takes on the same service with no experience of this work, and doesn't know if it will earn the $6m. What is the revenue to 31 December?","a":"$6.0m","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: without confidence that there will be no reversal, the bonus stays out. Applied: $24m × 3/12 = $6.0m. Users: revenue will jump later if the bonus is earned. Common mistake: including a best guess.","i":"e1b162a5ab"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Vale sells 100 units for $500 each, cost $200, with a 30-day full refund. It expects 5 back and can resell them at a profit. What revenue and refund liability are recorded?","a":"$47,500 and $2,500","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: a right of return makes consideration variable. Applied: 95 × $500 = $47,500 revenue; refund liability 5 × $500 = $2,500; asset 5 × $200 = $1,000, credited to cost of sales. Common mistake: recording the full $50,000, or measuring the liability at cost.","i":"74b8915879"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Rook sells equipment on 31 December with $2m due in two years. The customer can borrow at 8%. What revenue is recorded this year?","a":"$1,714,678","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: a significant financing component means discounting at the customer's rate. Applied: $2m / 1.08² = $1,714,678. The $285,322 is interest over the two years. Users: revenue isn't inflated by a financing deal. Common mistake: using the entity's rate.","i":"5ef6905e71"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Dale sells goods for 2,000 of the customer's shares, worth $6 each at the time. At the year end they are worth $8. What revenue is recorded?","a":"$12,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: non-cash consideration is measured at fair value at inception. Applied: 2,000 × $6. The later $4,000 rise follows IFRS 9, outside revenue. Common mistake: using the year-end value.","i":"485236e3c2"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"Galt commits to pay a retailer $0.5m towards store changes in return for its $10m purchase commitment. By the year end it has delivered goods with a sales value of $3m. What is revenue?","a":"$2.85m","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: a payment that's not for a distinct good or service reduces the price. Applied: 0.5 / 10 = 5%; $3m × 95% = $2.85m. Common mistake: expensing the payment, which overstates revenue and margins.","i":"07ee17a061"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"A machine and a year's support are sold for $180,000. The machine sells alone for $160,000. Support isn't sold alone, costs $30,000 to provide, and similar services earn a mark-up of one third. What is allocated to the machine?","a":"$144,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Step 1 (support price): $30,000 × 4/3 = $40,000. Step 2 (discount): 200,000 vs 180,000 is 10%. Step 3: machine $160,000 × 90% = $144,000; support $36,000. Common mistake: the residual method, giving support only $20,000, below its cost.","i":"e2dde5e147"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"A travel site sells flights for airlines, which set prices and fly the passengers. It keeps 10% of $2m of sales. What is its revenue?","a":"$200,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: an agent recognises only its fee. Why: the site doesn't control the service before it's transferred. Users: margins and market share would be distorted if gross sales were shown. Common mistake: taking the cash handled as revenue.","i":"284b22d804"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"A builder must clear the site, lay foundations, wire and decorate a school under one contract. Each service could be bought separately. How many performance obligations are there?","a":"One","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: to be distinct, a promise must also be separately identifiable. Applied: the builder integrates all the services into one output, the school. Common mistake: stopping at 'could be bought separately'.","i":"ee8372686a"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-1","c":"sbr-04-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"On 30 September Pell signs a contract to deliver an asset at the year end for $1m, due next June. By the year end the customer's position has collapsed, and payment isn't probable. What does Pell record?","a":"No revenue","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: a contract can be accounted for only if payment is probable. Applied: step 1 fails, so no revenue is recognised, despite delivery. Common mistake: recognising the revenue and impairing the receivable.","i":"563befe2da"}],"i":"3b920ef12d"},{"x":"stage","t":"4.A2 Recognition and special deals","s":"sbr-04-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"Hart builds a specialised block for $3m, with estimated costs of $2.4m and a possible $0.5m bonus it can't rely on. If terminated, it can claim costs to date plus a margin. Costs so far are $0.6m. What's revenue this year?","a":"$0.75m","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: no alternative use plus an enforceable right to payment means over time. Applied: 0.6 / 2.4 = 25%; $3m × 25% = $0.75m; the bonus is excluded. Gross profit $0.15m. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: including the bonus gives $0.875m.","i":"9d6b428c1e"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"Cray builds a specialised factory. If the customer defaults, Cray keeps only the deposit, and the balance is due on handover. When is revenue recognised?","a":"At a point in time","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: over-time recognition needs both no alternative use and an enforceable right to payment for work to date. Applied: only the deposit is enforceable, so the second test fails. Common mistake: looking only at the specialised asset.","i":"e4f0b25f18"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"Hale ships $4m of goods to dealers just before the year end. It keeps title, can recall them, and dealers pay only on selling. Its FD wants the revenue this year to meet a bonus target. What's right?","a":"No revenue until the dealers sell","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Rule: a consignment has no transfer of control. Applied: the goods stay in inventory. Ethics: the bonus is a self-interest threat, and recording this breaches integrity. Users: sales and receivables would be overstated. Common mistake: relying on physical delivery.","i":"3ce1542b19"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"A publisher delivers magazines to a supermarket on 31 December for sale from January. Title stays with the publisher, unsold copies come back, and it invoices in February for copies sold. What's revenue at 31 December?","a":"Nil","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: judge control using all the indicators. Applied: possession has passed, but not title or the right to payment, and the publisher bears obsolescence risk. Common mistake: treating it as a right of return when control hasn't passed.","i":"5c3e0576a6"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"Tay sells maturing whisky for $1m and can buy it back in two years for $1.2m. How is this accounted for?","a":"A loan of $1m, with interest","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: with a call option, control doesn't pass; at or above the sale price it's financing. Applied: the whisky stays, Dr Cash, Cr Liability, and the $0.2m is interest over two years. P/L category: Financing. Users: the debt would otherwise be hidden. Common mistake: following the legal sale.","i":"c5a72cd526"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#5","x":"example","t":"Ivor sells a machine for $1m with a call option to buy it back for $0.8m. How is this accounted for?","a":"As a lease","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: a repurchase below the sale price means the customer is paying for use. Applied: lessor accounting under IFRS 16. Common mistake: treating every repurchase as a loan.","i":"1f0c1691da"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#6","x":"example","t":"Lamb sells a block with a carrying amount of $5m for $8m. The buyer can require Lamb to buy it back for $9m in two years, when the market value is expected to be $8.5m. What does Lamb record?","a":"A financial liability of $8m","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: a put is an obligation if the customer has an incentive to exercise. Applied: $9m exceeds both the expected value and the sale price, so it's financing: no sale, the block stays, interest goes to financing. Common mistake: ignoring the put.","i":"6eb038397f"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#7","x":"example","t":"Lamb sells a block with a carrying amount of $5m for $8m. The buyer can require Lamb to buy it back for $9m in two years, when the market value is expected to be $9.5m. What's the treatment?","a":"A sale with a right of return","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: with no incentive to exercise, no obligation is created. Applied: the buyer would rather keep or sell at market. Treat it as variable consideration. Common mistake: judging the put by its price against the sale price alone.","i":"e73d721448"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#8","x":"example","t":"Sayle must supply 800 units at $50. After 500, it adds 100 more at $45, the stand-alone price at that date. By the year end 700 have been delivered. What is revenue?","a":"$35,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: distinct goods at their stand-alone price make a separate contract. Applied: the 700 delivered all come from the original contract, at $50. Common mistake: blending the prices.","i":"ed71f57e26"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#9","x":"example","t":"Sayle must supply 800 units at $50. After 500, it adds 100 more at $30 each, though their stand-alone price is $45. By the year end 700 have been delivered. What is revenue?","a":"$34,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Step 1 (not separate): the price isn't stand-alone. Step 2 (new contract): (300 × $50 + 100 × $30) / 400 = $45. Step 3: 500 × $50 + 200 × $45 = $34,000. Common mistake: keeping the old price for the remaining units.","i":"a2efae5ef8"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#10","x":"example","t":"Brock wins a contract after paying $40,000 for research on the customer, $25,000 for bid travel and $18,000 in commission for winning. What's capitalised?","a":"$18,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: only incremental costs of obtaining a contract are capitalised, if recoverable. Applied: research and travel were spent win or lose. P/L category: Operating, including the later amortisation. Common mistake: capitalising everything spent on the bid.","i":"88659718fe"},{"s":"sbr-04-apply-2","c":"sbr-04-apply-2#11","x":"example","t":"A customer pays $120,000 on 1 October for 12 months of support. What goes in the statements at 31 December?","a":"$30,000 revenue, $90,000 contract liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: cash received before revenue is earned is a contract liability. Applied: 3/12 is earned. Common mistake: confusing contract liabilities with assets.","i":"5895497b0f"}],"i":"9758ff0c15"}],"i":"9d82bdc737"}],"i":"dd49b9f77c"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 5: Non-current assets","ch":5,"d":"Study Text pages 109–154.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"5.1 IAS 16 Property, plant and equipment","s":"sbr-05-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#0","x":"card","t":"PPE is tangible items held for production, rental or admin and expected to be used for more than one period","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Tangible = has physical substance.","i":"16e5874809"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#1","x":"card","t":"Spare parts, stand-by and servicing equipment are PPE if they meet its definition (used over more than one period); otherwise they are inventory","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment; IAS 2 Inventories. They're classified by the PPE definition, not by being called spares.","i":"36dc841b74"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#2","x":"card","t":"PPE is recognised when future benefits are probable and its cost can be measured reliably","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. IAS 16 uses its own (older) criteria, not the 2018 CF ones; the standard wins.","i":"eda0dcc30a"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#3","x":"card","t":"The cost of PPE is the purchase price, directly attributable costs and the estimated cost of dismantling and restoring the site","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Cap example: legal fees capitalised; recoverable sales tax and admin costs are not.","i":"b1eb99709d"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#4","x":"card","t":"Never capitalise admin overheads, abnormal costs, start-up losses, launch and training costs, relocation costs, or costs after the asset is ready for use","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Unless later costs increase the benefits the asset brings.","i":"1cb5403fde"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#5","x":"card","t":"Proceeds from selling samples made while testing PPE go to P/L (operating) with their costs","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. They used to be deducted from the asset's cost; not any more.","i":"0584745332"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#6","x":"card","t":"Under the revaluation model, revaluations must be regular and cover the whole class of assets","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. No cherry-picking which buildings to revalue.","i":"5d3ecc37f3"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#7","x":"card","t":"A revaluation gain goes to OCI and the revaluation surplus (never recycled)","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. A later loss first uses that asset's surplus, then hits P/L (operating).","i":"0967c8301a"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#8","x":"card","t":"The depreciable amount is cost less residual value (today's net proceeds as if at the end of its life)","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Residual value uses today's prices, not inflated future ones.","i":"9114817ff5"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#9","x":"card","t":"Depreciation starts when the asset is available for use and continues even when it's idle, until derecognised","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Only stops on derecognition or held-for-sale classification.","i":"06e0ed7c16"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#10","x":"card","t":"Depreciation based on revenue is not allowed","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Revenue reflects prices, inflation and volumes, not how the asset is used up.","i":"dcd5ef54be"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#11","x":"card","t":"Method, residual value and useful life are reviewed every year; changes are prospective (estimates)","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Cap machine: life cut to 4 remaining years → spread the carrying amount over 4 years from then.","i":"179df764ad"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#12","x":"card","t":"Significant parts with different lives are depreciated separately; replacements capitalised, old parts derecognised","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Major inspections can also be capitalised as a component.","i":"6b5af16b14"},{"s":"sbr-05-1","c":"sbr-05-1#13","x":"card","t":"On disposal, a revalued asset's surplus may be transferred to retained earnings (not through P/L)","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Gain or loss on disposal = net proceeds − carrying amount.","i":"a703f39f95"}],"i":"2f984934c6"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.2 IAS 20 Government grants","s":"sbr-05-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-2","c":"sbr-05-2#0","x":"card","t":"A government grant is a transfer of resources in return for complying with conditions","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. It excludes assistance that can't be valued and normal trade with government.","i":"d22bd66aee"},{"s":"sbr-05-2","c":"sbr-05-2#1","x":"card","t":"Government assistance like advice or procurement policies is not recognised (it can't be valued)","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Only its existence may be disclosed.","i":"d6d0d1fa7b"},{"s":"sbr-05-2","c":"sbr-05-2#2","x":"card","t":"A grant is recognised when the conditions are met and there is reasonable assurance it will be received","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Clock example: grant receivable on paying for the building.","i":"1e6262d608"},{"s":"sbr-05-2","c":"sbr-05-2#3","x":"card","t":"Grants are recognised in P/L to match the costs they are meant to cover","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. A job-creation grant matches the costs of creating the jobs.","i":"a0cfb86d5b"},{"s":"sbr-05-2","c":"sbr-05-2#4","x":"card","t":"An income grant is shown as other income or deducted from the related expense","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Separate income helps comparison; netting suits costs incurred only because of the grant.","i":"52abe03816"},{"s":"sbr-05-2","c":"sbr-05-2#5","x":"card","t":"An asset grant is either deducted from the asset's cost or held as deferred income and released over the asset's life","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Netting shows fewer assets and a higher ROCE; deferred income shows more liabilities.","i":"4fe7cba125"},{"s":"sbr-05-2","c":"sbr-05-2#6","x":"card","t":"A grant that becomes repayable is treated as a change in estimate","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Deferred income is used first, then P/L; for netted grants, add back to cost and catch up the depreciation.","i":"e59b5b8391"},{"s":"sbr-05-2","c":"sbr-05-2#7","x":"card","t":"IAS 20 requires disclosure of the policy, the grants recognised and unfulfilled conditions","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Users see what might still have to be paid back.","i":"8befa79d87"}],"i":"5fe76715cb"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.3 IAS 23 Borrowing costs","s":"sbr-05-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-3","c":"sbr-05-3#0","x":"card","t":"Borrowing costs are interest and other costs of borrowing funds","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs.","i":"683d6e30db"},{"s":"sbr-05-3","c":"sbr-05-3#1","x":"card","t":"Borrowing costs must be capitalised for qualifying assets: those taking a substantial time to get ready","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs. Other borrowing costs are expensed (financing).","i":"f89435026e"},{"s":"sbr-05-3","c":"sbr-05-3#2","x":"card","t":"Capitalisation starts when expenditure, borrowing costs and the necessary activities are all in progress","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs. Hi-Rise: construction began in March but the loan in April → capitalise from April.","i":"3478216238"},{"s":"sbr-05-3","c":"sbr-05-3#3","x":"card","t":"Capitalisation is suspended during extended interruptions of active development","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs. Hi-Rise: no capitalisation for July's labour shortage.","i":"fc526dc988"},{"s":"sbr-05-3","c":"sbr-05-3#4","x":"card","t":"Capitalisation stops when substantially all the work is complete","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs. Even if final touches remain.","i":"301d91c000"},{"s":"sbr-05-3","c":"sbr-05-3#5","x":"card","t":"For specific borrowings, capitalise the interest less income from temporarily investing the funds","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs. Only the net cost relates to the asset.","i":"200a5d0d8d"},{"s":"sbr-05-3","c":"sbr-05-3#6","x":"card","t":"For general borrowings, apply the weighted average borrowing rate to spending on the asset","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs. Disclose the amount capitalised and the rate used.","i":"68fd0bf3bf"}],"i":"e616bbf10e"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.4 IAS 40 Investment property","s":"sbr-05-4","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#0","x":"card","t":"Investment property is land or buildings held to earn rentals, for capital appreciation, or both (owned or right-of-use)","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Includes land held for an undecided future use.","i":"d2b1fd7a83"},{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#1","x":"card","t":"Property rented to the company's own employees is owner-occupied (IAS 16), not investment property","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Lavender example: so it's revalued under IAS 16 with gains to OCI.","i":"84a243d812"},{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#2","x":"card","t":"Also not investment property: property for sale in the business (IAS 2), built for others (IFRS 15) or leased out under a finance lease (IFRS 16)","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Each goes under the standard that fits its use.","i":"6513a99a19"},{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#3","x":"card","t":"After recognition, choose the cost model or the fair value model for all investment property","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Under the cost model you must still disclose fair value.","i":"17e0432245"},{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#4","x":"card","t":"Under the fair value model, property is remeasured every year, isn't depreciated, and gains go to P/L (investing)","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Unlike IAS 16 revaluations, which go to OCI.","i":"baecbcc42f"},{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#5","x":"card","t":"Transfers into or out of investment property happen only when use changes","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Intention alone isn't enough.","i":"e3bb2f30c7"},{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#6","x":"card","t":"Owner-occupied property becoming investment property at fair value is first revalued under IAS 16, with the gain to OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. ABC example: 500 → 600 gain to OCI, then 600 → 625 to P/L.","i":"f9a7a8eae9"},{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#7","x":"card","t":"Investment property becoming owner-occupied or inventory uses fair value at the date of change as its deemed cost","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Then IAS 16 or IAS 2 applies.","i":"f5b62d9eec"},{"s":"sbr-05-4","c":"sbr-05-4#8","x":"card","t":"Inventory becoming investment property at fair value: the difference goes to P/L (operating)","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Choosing a model: think about how the property generates cash (CF factors).","i":"37eb62e87c"}],"i":"4025aa7989"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.5 IAS 38 Intangible assets","s":"sbr-05-5","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#0","x":"card","t":"An intangible asset is an identifiable, non-monetary asset without physical substance","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. E.g. software, patents, licences, customer lists, franchises.","i":"e562caf911"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#1","x":"card","t":"Identifiable means separable, or arising from contractual or legal rights","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Goodwill isn't identifiable, which is why it's dealt with in IFRS 3.","i":"ddda162c02"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#2","x":"card","t":"Intangibles are recognised if identifiable, controlled, bringing future benefits and measurable reliably","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Costs already expensed can never be capitalised later.","i":"c6c27822f9"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#3","x":"card","t":"An internally generated brand is not recognised because its costs can't be separated from running the business","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Innovate example: the 'Our Sports' brand stays off; the bought 'Pets & Me' rights go on at $1m.","i":"9810b31690"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#4","x":"card","t":"Intangibles can be revalued only if there is an active market (homogeneous, always buyers and sellers, public prices)","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Rare: milk quotas or exchange seats, never brands or patents.","i":"0ff585f59f"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#5","x":"card","t":"A finite-life intangible is amortised, usually straight-line with zero residual value, from when it's available for use","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. An indefinite life means no foreseeable limit to cash inflows.","i":"5b1bd914a8"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#6","x":"card","t":"An indefinite-life intangible is not amortised but tested for impairment every year","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. JonJon: saying 'we can't foresee its life' because of change doesn't make a customer list indefinite.","i":"4af76b7f5f"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#7","x":"card","t":"Research spending is always expensed","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Tangible assets used in research are still PPE.","i":"00bf7838a5"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#8","x":"card","t":"Development is capitalised once the entity shows technical feasibility, intention to complete, future benefits, resources and reliable measurement of the costs","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Scone: only development after 1 May (when profitability was shown) is capitalised; market research and training are expensed.","i":"d99db279e4"},{"s":"sbr-05-5","c":"sbr-05-5#9","x":"card","t":"IAS 38 requires disclosure of R&D expensed, amortisation methods, revaluations, a reconciliation and the reasons for any indefinite-life assessment","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Users can challenge indefinite lives.","i":"fc33e33c2a"}],"i":"ae3a4eb902"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.6 IAS 36 Impairment","s":"sbr-05-6","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#0","x":"card","t":"An asset is impaired when its carrying amount exceeds its recoverable amount","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Recoverable amount = what you could get from it, by selling or using it.","i":"8dd68a425b"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#1","x":"card","t":"An annual impairment test is required for goodwill and indefinite-life intangibles","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Other assets are tested only when there's an indicator.","i":"ccc7413611"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#2","x":"card","t":"External indicators include falling market values, adverse changes, rising interest rates and net assets above market capitalisation","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Higher rates raise the discount rate and cut value in use.","i":"d60f2556c3"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#3","x":"card","t":"Internal indicators include obsolescence or damage, reduced use and worse performance than expected","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. An idle machine is a classic exam trigger.","i":"2efd25962c"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#4","x":"card","t":"Recoverable amount is the higher of fair value less costs to sell and value in use","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. If FVLCS already exceeds the carrying amount, no need to work out value in use.","i":"9b2b68afee"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#5","x":"card","t":"Value in use cash flows use recent budgets, the asset's current condition (no upgrades), and beyond 5 years a steady or declining growth rate","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. JonJon: inflated forecasts without working capital or capex breach IAS 36.","i":"675796d860"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#6","x":"card","t":"The value in use discount rate reflects the time value of money and risks specific to the asset not already in the cash flows","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Pre-tax rate.","i":"bd8b73b340"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#7","x":"card","t":"An impairment loss is charged to P/L unless the asset has a revaluation surplus, which is used first (via OCI)","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Then depreciate the new carrying amount over the remaining life.","i":"b0d293329e"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#8","x":"card","t":"A cash-generating unit is the smallest group of assets with largely independent cash inflows","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Timber example: A sells timber externally → its own CGU; B and C together form another.","i":"20d0340ef7"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#9","x":"card","t":"Goodwill and corporate assets (like head office) are allocated to CGUs reasonably and consistently","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. A CGU holding goodwill is tested every year.","i":"29235b4754"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#10","x":"card","t":"A CGU loss is allocated first to goodwill, then to other assets in proportion to their carrying amounts","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. But no asset goes below the highest of its FVLCS, value in use and zero (e.g. property worth more, receivables collected in full).","i":"0787fe3d14"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#11","x":"card","t":"A reversal of impairment goes to P/L (or OCI if it hit a surplus), up to the carrying amount it would have had without the impairment","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Allow for the depreciation that would have been charged meanwhile.","i":"36c390b898"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#12","x":"card","t":"An impairment of goodwill can never be reversed","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Because any later recovery would be internally generated goodwill.","i":"75b8d591ba"},{"s":"sbr-05-6","c":"sbr-05-6#13","x":"card","t":"When testing a CGU for impairment, liabilities are generally excluded unless a buyer of the CGU would have to take them on, so the recoverable amount can't be found without them","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Then deduct the liability from both the CGU's carrying amount and its value in use. Fair value less costs to sell already allows for it, so that figure isn't adjusted.","i":"ccc6d02b9c"}],"i":"c1a7b9388d"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.7 IFRS 5 Held for sale","s":"sbr-05-7","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#0","x":"card","t":"An asset is held for sale when its carrying amount will be recovered mainly through a sale rather than continued use","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. A disposal group = assets (and liabilities) sold together in one transaction.","i":"0841cc0a81"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#1","x":"card","t":"Held for sale needs: available now, highly probable, committed, buyer search under way, reasonable price, sale within a year, and the plan unlikely to change","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Assets to be abandoned are never held for sale.","i":"b85064b7bd"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#2","x":"card","t":"A property needing major repairs before it can be sold is not held for sale (not available in its present condition)","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Hyssop (a): wait until repaired.","i":"463eab38e0"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#3","x":"card","t":"A subsidiary priced above its fair value is not held for sale (not at a reasonable price)","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Hyssop (b): the price rejects realistic buyers.","i":"0424d929a2"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#4","x":"card","t":"Held-for-sale items are measured at the lower of carrying amount and fair value less costs to sell","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. A write-down is an impairment loss; for a disposal group, allocate it in the IAS 36 order.","i":"6aa6a7d6fb"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#5","x":"card","t":"A revalued asset becoming held for sale is first revalued to fair value, then costs to sell are charged to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Nash example: revalue to 1.1m, then 50k costs to sell → P/L.","i":"b8620b82be"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#6","x":"card","t":"A later increase in fair value less costs to sell is recognised only up to the cumulative impairment already recognised","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. No gains above the original carrying amount.","i":"f2433bcc0e"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#7","x":"card","t":"Held-for-sale assets are not depreciated, even if still in use","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. They are shown separately in current assets (with the group's liabilities shown separately).","i":"8973e54939"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#8","x":"card","t":"A sale delayed beyond a year can stay held for sale if the delay is outside the entity's control and it's still committed","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Otherwise it must be reclassified.","i":"99e2d9095c"},{"s":"sbr-05-7","c":"sbr-05-7#9","x":"card","t":"When an asset stops being held for sale, it's measured at the lower of its carrying amount had it never been HFS and its recoverable amount","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. The adjustment goes to P/L (operating).","i":"bb68a99901"}],"i":"4d03509f92"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"5.S1 Workings in order","s":"sbr-05-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-structure-1","c":"sbr-05-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"An impairment review","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. If fair value less costs to sell already exceeds carrying amount, value in use isn't needed.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Look for indications (annually for goodwill and indefinite-life intangibles)","i":"fbdce68ed0"},{"x":"step","t":"Find fair value less costs to sell","i":"8217e0d1a8"},{"x":"step","t":"If that is above carrying amount, stop: no impairment","i":"36c5a3d82f"},{"x":"step","t":"Otherwise find value in use","i":"69afc0d073"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the higher as recoverable amount","i":"9894910459"},{"x":"step","t":"Write down to it: surplus first, then P/L","i":"10f8cedb01"},{"x":"step","t":"Depreciate the new amount over the remaining life","i":"2fb7ab6106"}],"i":"77f047953e"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-1","c":"sbr-05-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Allocating an impairment across a CGU","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Assets worth more than their carrying amount, like net monetary assets, take nothing.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Work out the total loss for the unit","i":"ca1afa3712"},{"x":"step","t":"Write off goodwill first","i":"3bf32573ac"},{"x":"step","t":"Allocate the rest to other assets in proportion to carrying amount","i":"64d8dc4941"},{"x":"step","t":"Stop at each asset's floor: the highest of fair value less costs to sell, value in use and nil","i":"fb74b41c5a"},{"x":"step","t":"Re-spread anything left over the other assets","i":"7da892e308"}],"i":"3ace18f13b"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-1","c":"sbr-05-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Testing goodwill shared by several units","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. E.g. unit F loses 60, then the business as a whole is 30 too high, so goodwill loses 30.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Test each unit on its own, without goodwill","i":"9f21d8d44b"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise any losses in those units","i":"4d4aa4b4d6"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare the whole business with goodwill to its recoverable amount","i":"c5b631cae8"},{"x":"step","t":"Any further loss goes to goodwill","i":"6840e54524"}],"i":"584c4cb0e4"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-1","c":"sbr-05-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Reversing an impairment","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Goodwill's impairment is never reversed.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Check that the reasons for the loss no longer apply","i":"d37c38fea5"},{"x":"step","t":"Find the new recoverable amount","i":"7cdd5cc1d9"},{"x":"step","t":"Find what carrying amount would be had no impairment happened","i":"3527491f28"},{"x":"step","t":"Reverse up to the lower of the two","i":"f297369944"},{"x":"step","t":"Credit P/L, or OCI if the loss hit a surplus","i":"7f1e69abf0"},{"x":"step","t":"Revise future depreciation","i":"2eb38b43af"}],"i":"d7ad3b2c06"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-1","c":"sbr-05-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"A building moving from owner use to investment property at fair value","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. The first step up goes through OCI; later changes through P/L.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Depreciate under IAS 16 to the date of change","i":"d4abaff35f"},{"x":"step","t":"Revalue to fair value at that date","i":"b78b8be68a"},{"x":"step","t":"Take a gain to OCI (a loss to P/L)","i":"ca2702cac5"},{"x":"step","t":"Stop depreciating","i":"a6c36d9bad"},{"x":"step","t":"Remeasure at each year end, through P/L","i":"061ce25fe6"}],"i":"0aebe66873"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-1","c":"sbr-05-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"Capitalising borrowing costs","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs. Disclose the amount capitalised and the rate.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Check the asset takes a substantial period to get ready","i":"ac419ffe56"},{"x":"step","t":"Start when spending, borrowing and activity are all under way","i":"74d7e2bb84"},{"x":"step","t":"Suspend during extended interruptions","i":"02b79c6bdd"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct income earned on temporarily invested funds","i":"17a84e885a"},{"x":"step","t":"Stop when substantially all the work is done","i":"4df36c3793"}],"i":"0b7e8fe89e"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-1","c":"sbr-05-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"Accounting for development costs","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Once expensed, costs can't be capitalised later.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Expense all research","i":"3851689df7"},{"x":"step","t":"Test the development criteria: feasible, intention, benefits, resources, reliable costs","i":"b558f02bd5"},{"x":"step","t":"Expense development before the date they are met","i":"543b73d038"},{"x":"step","t":"Capitalise from that date","i":"7379f96798"},{"x":"step","t":"Don't go back and capitalise earlier expenses","i":"8a65ccc541"},{"x":"step","t":"Amortise from when the asset is available for use","i":"15013f1221"}],"i":"cd5bdfa6c6"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-1","c":"sbr-05-structure-1#7","x":"steps","t":"An asset becoming held for sale","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. A later rise is limited to the cumulative impairment recognised.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Check the conditions are met","i":"b7fe5709f9"},{"x":"step","t":"If at a revalued amount, first revalue to fair value","i":"ffb9c7a404"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare carrying amount with fair value less costs to sell","i":"cdbded8668"},{"x":"step","t":"Take any write-down as an impairment","i":"8412fbd71f"},{"x":"step","t":"Stop depreciating","i":"7bf54d34f0"},{"x":"step","t":"Present it separately on the SFP","i":"05bde86cde"}],"i":"e0b3fce5b6"}],"i":"b61fe25316"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.S2 Which standard, which line?","s":"sbr-05-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"An office block used as the company's head office","a":"IAS 16","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Owner-occupied.","i":"8a341e08c9"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Land held for an undecided future use","a":"IAS 40","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Treated as held for capital appreciation.","i":"4a0c941fb9"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"A block let to tenants under operating leases","a":"IAS 40","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Held to earn rentals.","i":"1d77c8dcfd"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"A flat let to the company's own staff","a":"IAS 16","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Treated as owner-occupied.","i":"ca076f6801"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Houses built by a developer for sale in its business","a":"IAS 2","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property; IAS 2 Inventories. Held for sale in the ordinary course of business.","i":"828cde8d6d"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"A building being built under contract for a customer","a":"IFRS 15","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Revenue from a contract.","i":"aa5aa40fe6"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"A building leased out on a finance lease","a":"IFRS 16","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property; IFRS 16 Leases. Lessor accounting applies.","i":"d2005d7b16"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Fair value gain on investment property","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Typically investing.","i":"187f6b6aaa"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Depreciation of a factory","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Unless included in the cost of another asset.","i":"be907db2ce"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Revaluation gain on the head office","a":"OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. To the revaluation surplus; never recycled.","i":"7ac382a89e"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Impairment beyond the revaluation surplus on PPE","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. The surplus takes the first part through OCI.","i":"b7dc4ff628"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#11","x":"sort","t":"Impairment charged against an existing surplus on that asset","a":"OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Up to the surplus.","i":"891d8422b9"},{"s":"sbr-05-structure-2","c":"sbr-05-structure-2#12","x":"sort","t":"Proceeds of samples made while testing a new machine","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. With their costs, not deducted from the asset.","i":"02e9982975"}],"i":"72c8979eba"}],"i":"eb2c510967"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"5.C1 Costs and choices","s":"sbr-05-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Legal fees for buying a building","a":"Capitalise","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Directly attributable.","i":"dddccbfc87"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Estimated cost of dismantling an oil rig","a":"Capitalise","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Included in cost, with a provision.","i":"e7ce5094b0"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Recoverable sales tax","a":"Expense","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. It's not a cost at all: it is recovered.","i":"839cfee3a4"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Allocated general overheads","a":"Expense","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Not directly attributable.","i":"216a0ee39a"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Costs of opening a new store, including training","a":"Expense","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Specifically excluded.","i":"ed28a3f8f1"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Operating losses while a machine builds up to capacity","a":"Expense","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Incurred after it's ready for use.","i":"fc5838c857"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Regular major inspection of an aircraft","a":"Capitalise","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Any remaining amount for the last inspection is derecognised.","i":"1ed98c610d"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Revaluation model","a":"Allowed for any class of PPE","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Revalue the whole class regularly.","i":"ab3934a44f"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Milk quotas at a revalued amount","a":"Allowed for intangibles only with an active market","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. A rare example of an active market.","i":"2e54d4fde9"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Grant deducted from the asset","a":"Capitalised method","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Lower assets, so a higher ROCE.","i":"0fc309f23a"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Grant shown as a liability and released over the life","a":"Deferred income method","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Higher assets, but higher liabilities too.","i":"44b7723319"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Assets bought at different times are recorded uniformly, whatever the grant policy","a":"Deferred income method","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. An argument given for it.","i":"2cc35837be"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Market research on a new product's profitability","a":"Research or expense","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Not development of the product.","i":"867c1f8db5"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Designing a tested prototype after the criteria are met","a":"Development, once criteria are met","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Before commercial production.","i":"9ee8a87250"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-1","c":"sbr-05-compare-1#14","x":"sort","t":"Staff training for the new product","a":"Research or expense","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Never an intangible.","i":"501e256e69"}],"i":"1e249464ac"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.C2 Impairment and held for sale","s":"sbr-05-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Net assets exceed the entity's market capitalisation","a":"External indication","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Market information.","i":"abb742f700"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Interest rates rise and cut the recoverable amount","a":"External indication","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. A higher discount rate.","i":"7e50c321eb"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"A new law damages the market for the product","a":"External indication","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. The legal environment.","i":"7dc85252a1"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"A machine is often left idle","a":"Internal indication","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Reduced use.","i":"e9ca83b870"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"An asset performs worse than budgeted","a":"Internal indication","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Economic performance.","i":"f74b87337a"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Evidence of physical damage","a":"Internal indication","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Obsolescence or damage.","i":"30026bb1e9"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Costs that would be saved by a planned future restructuring","a":"Value in use: excluded","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Current condition only.","i":"43d09bf9b5"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Budget cash flows for up to five years","a":"Value in use: included","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Longer only if justified.","i":"9751fd179f"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Spending to enhance the asset","a":"Value in use: excluded","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Excluded.","i":"d0356891c5"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"A surplus building on offer at a reasonable price, with a sale expected within a year","a":"Held for sale","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. All the conditions are met.","i":"a6883bc876"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#10","x":"sort","t":"A building needing major repairs before it can be sold","a":"Not held for sale","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Not available in its present condition.","i":"dc1a6d98c4"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#11","x":"sort","t":"A subsidiary priced well above its fair value","a":"Not held for sale","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Not at a reasonable price.","i":"6775f21a65"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#12","x":"sort","t":"A factory to be gradually wound down","a":"Not held for sale","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Not recovered through a sale.","i":"0c0e0011d4"},{"s":"sbr-05-compare-2","c":"sbr-05-compare-2#13","x":"sort","t":"A sale delayed past a year by a regulator, with the entity still committed","a":"Held for sale","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Beyond the entity's control.","i":"d080ba39ca"}],"i":"78ad0477ae"}],"i":"64f58862db"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"5.W1 PPE, grants and investment property","s":"sbr-05-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"Proceeds from selling test samples reduce the cost of the machine.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. They go to P/L with their costs, after the amendment to IAS 16.","i":"16ab148e44"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Revaluing one building means revaluing the whole class.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. This prevents cherry-picking.","i":"cc22dc6449"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"An idle machine isn't depreciated.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Depreciation continues until derecognition.","i":"92fb9d0920"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Depreciation based on revenue is not appropriate.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Revenue reflects prices and volumes as well as use of the asset.","i":"6f65caab62"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A revaluation surplus is recycled to P/L on disposal.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. It may be transferred to retained earnings, or left in place.","i":"d19cc981b0"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"A change of useful life is applied prospectively.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. It's a change in estimate.","i":"b1ba1798b3"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"A grant is recognised when the cash arrives.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. When conditions are met and receipt is reasonably assured.","i":"d61c1a3982"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Grant presentation affects ratios but not total profit.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Both methods give the same profit overall.","i":"6ad5139f20"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"A repayable grant is treated as the correction of an error.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. It's a revision of an estimate.","i":"3461eaa83f"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Investment property under the fair value model isn't depreciated.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Fair value changes go to P/L instead.","i":"bd36bf4e20"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"An entity may use the fair value model for its best properties and cost for the others.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. The policy applies to all.","i":"66486d6f1b"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"Under the cost model, the fair value of investment property must be disclosed.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. So users can compare with entities using fair value.","i":"3928e09e6c"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"Moving from owner use to investment property always gives a P/L gain.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. The gain up to the date of change goes to OCI.","i":"060baac4fa"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-1","c":"sbr-05-watch-1#13","x":"trap","t":"Moving inventory to investment property at fair value gives a difference in P/L.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. In operating.","i":"1d2be30f9a"}],"i":"34a3090c19"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.W2 Intangibles, impairment and held for sale","s":"sbr-05-watch-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#0","x":"trap","t":"An internally generated brand can be recognised if its value is well supported.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Its costs can't be separated from those of running the business.","i":"2bd1a496f0"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#1","x":"trap","t":"A brand bought from another company can be recognised.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. It has a cost that can be measured reliably.","i":"558bd574c5"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#2","x":"trap","t":"Development costs already expensed can be capitalised once the criteria are met.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Once expensed, always expensed.","i":"927b8dcc2f"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#3","x":"trap","t":"Uncertainty about an asset's life makes it indefinite.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Indefinite means no foreseeable limit, not hard to estimate.","i":"4ad29be546"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#4","x":"trap","t":"Goodwill must be tested for impairment every year.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Even with no indication.","i":"c96676925d"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#5","x":"trap","t":"Value in use should take in the benefits of a planned upgrade.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. It reflects the asset's current condition.","i":"107945d454"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#6","x":"trap","t":"If fair value less costs to sell exceeds carrying amount, value in use isn't needed.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. The asset can't be impaired.","i":"5d23661b37"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#7","x":"trap","t":"Directors should raise forecasts after a poor year to support the carrying amount.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Forecasts need reasonable assumptions, and past accuracy must be checked.","i":"b4356ba850"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#8","x":"trap","t":"Liabilities in a CGU are always excluded from an impairment test.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Not if a buyer must take them on, as they affect recoverable amount.","i":"ca1d05951d"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#9","x":"trap","t":"A CGU's impairment can take an asset below its own fair value less costs to sell.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. That amount sets a floor.","i":"6def81688b"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#10","x":"trap","t":"An impairment of goodwill can never be reversed.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. A later rise would be internally generated goodwill.","i":"69352407a8"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#11","x":"trap","t":"An asset held for sale still in use continues to be depreciated.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Depreciation stops on classification.","i":"4b11ae0395"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#12","x":"trap","t":"A held-for-sale gain can exceed the losses already recognised.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. It's limited to the cumulative impairment.","i":"0b83f7d117"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#13","x":"trap","t":"An asset being abandoned can't be classified as held for sale.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. It won't be recovered through a sale.","i":"0c730ec23c"},{"s":"sbr-05-watch-2","c":"sbr-05-watch-2#14","x":"trap","t":"A plan to sell made after the reporting date is a non-adjusting event.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations; IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Details are disclosed in the notes.","i":"ae5316db8a"}],"i":"afc34dc314"}],"i":"79e016bc6d"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"5.A1 PPE, grants, property and intangibles","s":"sbr-05-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Bale buys a building for $5m, with legal fees of $0.2m, allocated admin costs of $0.3m and recoverable sales tax of $0.8m. What is its cost?","a":"$5.2m","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Rule: cost is the price plus directly attributable costs. Applied: 5 + 0.2. The admin costs are overheads, and the tax is recovered. Common mistake: including whatever was invoiced.","i":"a774d04a0e"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Bale's building cost $5.2m and has a 50-year life. After 4 years, with a carrying amount of $4.784m, it's revalued to $7m. What's the gain, and next year's depreciation?","a":"$2.216m in OCI; $152,174","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Rule: revaluation gains go to OCI, and depreciation is on the new value over the remaining life. Applied: 7 − 4.784 = 2.216; 7m / 46 = $152,174. Users: equity rises and ROCE falls. Common mistake: using the original 50-year life.","i":"76ed329e6f"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A $200,000 machine bought on 1 January 20X3 had a 10-year life. On 1 January 20X5 its remaining life is cut to 4 years. What's the 20X5 depreciation?","a":"$40,000","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Rule: a change in useful life is an estimate, applied prospectively. Applied: 200,000 × 8/10 = 160,000; 160,000 / 4 = 40,000. Common mistake: restating past depreciation.","i":"28b6dcfdb6"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"A company buys a $20m office with a $2m grant, conditional on paying for it. Its 50-year life starts on 1 October. What's the effect on profit to 31 December?","a":"$0.09m fall under either method","d":"Reference: IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Rule: grants are matched with the related cost, and the two methods give the same profit. Applied: 18m / 50 × 3/12 = $0.09m; or 0.10 depreciation less 0.01 released. Users: the methods differ in assets, liabilities and ROCE. Common mistake: delaying the grant until the cash arrives.","i":"203de3e244"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A $6m loan at 10% is taken out on 1 April for a building begun on 1 March. Work stops throughout August for a labour shortage. The building is unfinished at 31 December. How much interest is capitalised?","a":"$0.40m","d":"Reference: IAS 23 Borrowing Costs. Rule: capitalise from when spending, borrowing and activity all exist, and suspend during extended interruptions. Applied: nine months from April, less August: 6m × 10% × 8/12 = 0.40m; the other 0.05m is expensed. P/L category: Financing, for the amount expensed. Common mistake: capitalising through the stoppage.","i":"b605bc5bbb"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"A head office with a carrying amount of $800,000 is vacated when worth $1m and let to tenants. By the year end it's worth $1.1m. The entity uses the fair value model. What's recorded?","a":"$200,000 in OCI, $100,000 in P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Rule: revalue under IAS 16 at the date of change, then use IAS 40. Applied: 1,000,000 − 800,000 goes to OCI; 1,100,000 − 1,000,000 goes to P/L. P/L category: Investing. Common mistake: taking the whole gain to one place.","i":"1864953abb"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"Owen lets a block to its own employees at market rents and wants to use fair value through P/L. What's the treatment?","a":"Owner-occupied, under IAS 16","d":"Reference: IAS 40 Investment Property. Rule: property let to employees is owner-occupied. Applied: fair value is possible only through the IAS 16 revaluation model, with gains in OCI and continuing depreciation. Common mistake: thinking that earning rent is enough.","i":"c4e82cff90"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"Inez's game series is a hit. The directors value the brand they built at $20m. They also paid $1m for rights to another series. What goes on the balance sheet?","a":"$1m only","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Rule: internally generated brands aren't recognised; bought rights have a reliable cost. Applied: $1m, amortised over its life. Users: this is one reason net assets are below market value. Common mistake: treating a forecast-based value as cost.","i":"5fe9a5ec11"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"Scott spends $1m on research, $0.5m on market research, $0.3m on training and $1.2m on development, evenly over the year. The criteria are met from 1 May. What is capitalised?","a":"$0.8m","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Rule: capitalise development only from when the criteria are met. Applied: 1.2 × 8/12 = 0.8; the rest is expensed. Common mistake: capitalising all the project's costs. P/L category: Operating, for what's expensed.","i":"bfa63a54e1"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-1","c":"sbr-05-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"A finance director stops amortising a purchased customer list, calling its life 'indefinite' because technology and consumer habits make it impossible to predict. Is this right?","a":"No: it's finite and must be amortised","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Rule: indefinite means no foreseeable limit to the cash flows. Why: customer lists wear out, and uncertainty only makes the estimate harder. Users: profit is flattered. Common mistake: mixing up 'unknown' and 'indefinite'.","i":"6bd9a87e3b"}],"i":"5719039a9c"},{"x":"stage","t":"5.A2 Impairment and held for sale","s":"sbr-05-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"A machine's carrying amount is $600,000. Its fair value is $400,000 with disposal costs of $30,000. It would earn $250,000 at the end of each of two years, at 10%. What is the impairment?","a":"$166,116","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Step 1 (FVLCS): 370,000. Step 2 (VIU): 250,000/1.1 + 250,000/1.21 = 433,884. Step 3: the recoverable amount is the higher, 433,884. Step 4: 600,000 − 433,884 = 166,116. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: using fair value less costs alone.","i":"58269dbc40"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"A machine with a $20,000 revaluation surplus has an impairment loss of $166,116. How is the loss recorded?","a":"$20,000 in OCI, $146,116 in P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Rule: an impairment uses the asset's own surplus first. Applied: 166,116 − 20,000 = 146,116 goes to P/L. Common mistake: charging it all to P/L.","i":"58f3c4f4e1"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"A CGU with a loss of $50m has goodwill 10, property 30 (worth 40), machinery 60, vehicles 20, patents 20 and net monetary assets 10. What is machinery's carrying amount after allocation?","a":"$36m","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Step 1: goodwill takes 10. Step 2: property and net monetary assets take nothing: each is worth at least its carrying amount. Step 3: 40 across machinery, vehicles and patents (100): machinery takes 24, so 60 − 24 = 36. Common mistake: spreading it across every asset.","i":"5b75ea2b15"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"A $40,000 asset with a 10-year life is written down to $20,000 after 2 years. At the end of year 5 its recoverable amount is $50,000. What's the reversal?","a":"$7,500","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Step 1: 20,000 over 8 years is 2,500 a year, so 12,500 after 3 more years. Step 2: without the impairment, 40,000 × 5/10 = 20,000. Step 3: the cap is 20,000, so 20,000 − 12,500 = 7,500 goes to P/L. Common mistake: reversing up to recoverable amount, or reversing the whole loss.","i":"7790e90a9f"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"A CGU's assets are $10m, including $2m of warranty claims a buyer would have to take on. Value in use before those claims is $9.0m, and fair value less costs to sell, already net of them, is $7.5m. What's the impairment?","a":"$0.5m","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Rule: when a buyer must take on a liability, deduct it from both carrying amount and value in use. Applied: carrying amount 8.0; value in use 7.0; FVLCS 7.5 unchanged; recoverable 7.5; loss 0.5. Common mistake: ignoring the liability gives 1.0.","i":"f056ef0124"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#5","x":"example","t":"After cash flows far below forecast, the directors raise next year's forecast with little reason, and compute it as budgeted profit plus depreciation, with no capex or working capital. Is this acceptable under IAS 36?","a":"No: it isn't reasonable or complete","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Rule: value in use needs reasonable assumptions, and budget accuracy must be checked. Applied: past shortfalls were ignored; capex and working capital are real cash flows. Ethics: this could hide an impairment. Common mistake: thinking that a recent budget is enough.","i":"4ccb0ac293"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#6","x":"example","t":"A building bought for $400,000 with a 50-year life has a carrying amount of $368,000 when put up for sale. Fair value is $220,000, and costs to sell $20,000. What's the write-down?","a":"$168,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Rule: held-for-sale items are at the lower of carrying amount and fair value less costs to sell. Applied: 220,000 − 20,000 = 200,000; 368,000 − 200,000 = 168,000. Depreciation stops. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: leaving out the costs to sell.","i":"cbbc7197d0"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#7","x":"example","t":"A building with a carrying amount of $368,000 is put up for sale. Its fair value is $440,000, and costs to sell are $20,000. What happens?","a":"It stays at $368,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Rule: the lower of the two means no write-up. Applied: 420,000 is above 368,000. The gain comes only when it is sold. Common mistake: remeasuring to fair value.","i":"7caae742f6"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#8","x":"example","t":"A building at a revalued $1.95m with a surplus becomes held for sale. Fair value is $1.85m and costs to sell $0.1m. What's recorded?","a":"$0.1m in OCI, $0.1m in P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Rule: revalue to fair value first, as IAS 16 would, then charge the costs to sell to P/L. Applied: 1.95 → 1.85 against the surplus; then 0.1 in P/L, leaving 1.75. Common mistake: taking the whole drop to one place.","i":"039848c1c2"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#9","x":"example","t":"An asset carried as held for sale at $300,000 is no longer for sale. Without the classification it would be at $280,000, and its recoverable amount is $310,000. What now?","a":"Measure at $280,000; $20,000 loss in P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Rule: the lower of the amount had it never been held for sale and the recoverable amount. Applied: 280,000 is lower. P/L category: Operating, in continuing operations. Common mistake: using recoverable amount.","i":"98e10764ab"},{"s":"sbr-05-apply-2","c":"sbr-05-apply-2#10","x":"example","t":"An asset written down from $500,000 to $400,000 when held for sale later has fair value less costs to sell of $520,000. Where does it go?","a":"$500,000, with a $100,000 gain","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Rule: a later gain can't exceed the cumulative impairment already recognised. Applied: 100,000 is the limit. Common mistake: moving straight to the new value.","i":"798a659eb1"}],"i":"abe8d48bfb"}],"i":"dd3e827de8"}],"i":"e719043f79"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 6: Agriculture and inventories","ch":6,"d":"Study Text pages 155–164.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"6.1 IAS 41 Agriculture","s":"sbr-06-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#0","x":"card","t":"A biological asset is a living plant or animal","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. A calf growing into a cow is biological transformation.","i":"3206d58d6a"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#1","x":"card","t":"Agricultural produce is the harvested product of a biological asset","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Harvest = detaching produce, or ending the asset's life processes.","i":"b0be4a66e6"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#2","x":"card","t":"Biological assets are measured, initially and at every year end, at fair value less costs to sell","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Changes go to P/L (operating). Cows example: 120 three-year-olds × $90 less 2% levy.","i":"12a4e73b61"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#3","x":"card","t":"A day-one gain arises when a lamb is born; a day-one loss arises from deducting the costs to sell","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Fair value less selling costs is below the price paid, so a small loss appears immediately.","i":"ddbbac16ab"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#4","x":"card","t":"IAS 41 recommends (but doesn't require) separate disclosure of physical changes and price changes","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Users want to know whether value grew because the herd grew or because prices rose.","i":"a45f666977"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#5","x":"card","t":"If fair value can't be measured, a biological asset is carried at cost less depreciation and impairment, until it can be","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. IAS 41 presumes fair value can normally be measured.","i":"a1a25b6486"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#6","x":"card","t":"At harvest, produce is measured at fair value less costs to sell, which becomes its deemed cost under IAS 2","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. After harvest IAS 41 stops and IAS 2 applies.","i":"a808a2d1aa"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#7","x":"card","t":"Bearer plants (grape vines, tea bushes) are PPE under IAS 16","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. But the grapes growing on them are biological assets under IAS 41.","i":"95130d4ab6"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#8","x":"card","t":"Land under a forest is accounted for separately under IAS 16; production quotas go under IAS 38","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Only living things are biological assets.","i":"c209c4c7fb"},{"s":"sbr-06-1","c":"sbr-06-1#9","x":"card","t":"A grant for a biological asset at fair value less costs to sell is recognised in P/L when receivable (or when its conditions are met)","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. If the asset is at cost, IAS 20 applies instead.","i":"9786c95211"}],"i":"036323b513"},{"x":"stage","t":"6.2 IAS 2 Inventories","s":"sbr-06-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-06-2","c":"sbr-06-2#0","x":"card","t":"The cost of inventory is purchase cost, conversion cost and other costs to bring it to its present condition and location","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Purchase cost: price less discounts, plus import duties, non-recoverable taxes, transport and handling.","i":"189fac5f11"},{"s":"sbr-06-2","c":"sbr-06-2#1","x":"card","t":"Fixed production overheads are allocated based on the normal level of activity","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. So a quiet year doesn't load extra overhead into each unit.","i":"f1673263f0"},{"s":"sbr-06-2","c":"sbr-06-2#2","x":"card","t":"Excluded from inventory cost are abnormal waste, storage, admin and selling costs","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. They are expensed when incurred.","i":"939e872bf5"},{"s":"sbr-06-2","c":"sbr-06-2#3","x":"card","t":"Allowed cost formulas are specific identification, FIFO and weighted average (specific identification if items aren't interchangeable)","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. LIFO is not allowed.","i":"bec017b459"},{"s":"sbr-06-2","c":"sbr-06-2#4","x":"card","t":"Inventory is measured at the lower of cost and NRV, item by item (similar items may be grouped)","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. NRV = estimated selling price less costs to complete and sell. Sales after the year end are strong evidence.","i":"dcd933e639"},{"s":"sbr-06-2","c":"sbr-06-2#5","x":"card","t":"Raw materials are not written down below cost if the finished product will still be sold at a profit","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Illustration: materials fell from 12,000 to 10,000 but the order is profitable → keep at 12,000.","i":"f1f371cc6f"}],"i":"f9d139a4ea"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"6.S In order","s":"sbr-06-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-06-structure-1","c":"sbr-06-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"A farm's produce from growth to sale","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. IAS 41 stops at harvest.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"The biological asset is at fair value less costs to sell","i":"e2d22c09cf"},{"x":"step","t":"Changes in value go to P/L","i":"90375f588e"},{"x":"step","t":"Harvest: produce is recognised at fair value less costs to sell","i":"73d5ca1aa9"},{"x":"step","t":"This becomes the deemed cost under IAS 2","i":"eaac97c682"},{"x":"step","t":"Later, the lower of cost and NRV applies","i":"c4519bb843"}],"i":"0746609978"},{"s":"sbr-06-structure-1","c":"sbr-06-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Inventory at the year end","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Later sales are strong evidence of NRV.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find cost: purchase, conversion and other costs to bring it to its present state","i":"eebef7451e"},{"x":"step","t":"Leave out abnormal waste, storage, admin and selling costs","i":"a1ec8c5ace"},{"x":"step","t":"Find NRV: selling price less completion and selling costs","i":"c3f4c17902"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare line by line","i":"35c68c0cd4"},{"x":"step","t":"Carry the lower","i":"28e213396a"}],"i":"74e0c8d043"},{"s":"sbr-06-structure-1","c":"sbr-06-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Measuring biological assets when fair value isn't reliable","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. A rebuttable presumption.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"IAS 41 presumes fair value can be measured","i":"43c6bfbfc9"},{"x":"step","t":"If no market prices are available, use cost less depreciation and impairment","i":"6b864737e7"},{"x":"step","t":"When fair value becomes reliable, move to fair value less costs to sell","i":"d28b9118a6"}],"i":"6e9547dc01"}],"i":"de7fa3160b"}],"i":"35efebcc04"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"6.C Side by side","s":"sbr-06-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"A dairy cow","a":"IAS 41","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. A living animal.","i":"b1ffebcf12"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Grapes still growing on a vine","a":"IAS 41","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Unharvested produce is a biological asset.","i":"16dfd4051b"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Grape vines","a":"IAS 16","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. A bearer plant.","i":"cc34f3edb4"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Farm land","a":"IAS 16","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Not a biological asset.","i":"46bbb6d6c7"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Milk in a storage tank","a":"IAS 2","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. After harvest.","i":"1db5ad524c"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Import duties","a":"In inventory cost","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Purchase cost.","i":"5536c4d987"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Fixed overheads at normal activity","a":"In inventory cost","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Conversion cost.","i":"55ba680ef3"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Abnormal wastage","a":"Expensed","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. It's excluded.","i":"42c7060491"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Storage of finished goods","a":"Expensed","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. It's excluded.","i":"76d486a70f"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Selling costs","a":"Expensed","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. It's excluded.","i":"d85df6c822"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Items that aren't ordinarily interchangeable","a":"Actual unit cost","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. It's required.","i":"7e294790eb"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Large quantities of identical items","a":"FIFO or weighted average","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Either is allowed.","i":"6c7376e849"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Unconditional grant on a biological asset at fair value less costs to sell","a":"P/L when receivable","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. No conditions to wait for.","i":"40cc97373c"},{"s":"sbr-06-compare-1","c":"sbr-06-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Conditional grant on such an asset","a":"P/L when conditions are met","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 20 Accounting for Government Grants and Disclosure of Government Assistance. Conditions come first.","i":"8b2f6e054b"}],"i":"300b8b2f4a"}],"i":"0ae204484f"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"6.W Traps","s":"sbr-06-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-06-watch-1","c":"sbr-06-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"Buying a biological asset at market price can create an immediate loss.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Selling costs are deducted from fair value.","i":"705c4f84ee"},{"s":"sbr-06-watch-1","c":"sbr-06-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"A newly born calf can create a gain.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. On first recognition.","i":"53d871084b"},{"s":"sbr-06-watch-1","c":"sbr-06-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Separate disclosure of physical and price changes is mandatory.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. It's recommended.","i":"0a178cef14"},{"s":"sbr-06-watch-1","c":"sbr-06-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Biological asset gains go to OCI.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. To P/L, in operating.","i":"4178bb6c00"},{"s":"sbr-06-watch-1","c":"sbr-06-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"Bearer plants are measured at fair value under IAS 41.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. They're under IAS 16; the produce on them is under IAS 41.","i":"f6332dbfb1"},{"s":"sbr-06-watch-1","c":"sbr-06-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"A fall in raw material prices always means a write-down.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Not if the finished goods will be sold at a profit.","i":"22d5305989"},{"s":"sbr-06-watch-1","c":"sbr-06-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"Inventories may be written down in similar groups sold in the same market.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Otherwise line by line.","i":"09dfd75f5e"},{"s":"sbr-06-watch-1","c":"sbr-06-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"IAS 41 applies to production quotas.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 38 Intangible Assets. They're intangibles under IAS 38.","i":"28d96508be"}],"i":"b83e09a447"}],"i":"90990951ff"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"6.A Scenarios","s":"sbr-06-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-06-apply-1","c":"sbr-06-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"A farmer has 50 cows at fair value less costs to sell of $6,000. Ten more are bought for $100 each. At the year end, cows of that type sell for $110, less a 2% levy. What are the herd's value and the P/L effect?","a":"$6,468; $532 loss","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Step 1: 60 × $110 × 98% = 6,468. Step 2: 6,468 − 6,000 − 1,000 = (532). P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: leaving out the levy, or calling it a gain because the herd's value rose.","i":"012d0287a4"},{"s":"sbr-06-apply-1","c":"sbr-06-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Grapes on vines are worth $500,000 at the start of the year. $100,000 of grapes are harvested, and those left are worth $520,000. What's the gain?","a":"$120,000","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture. Rule: unharvested produce is a biological asset at fair value less costs to sell. Applied: 100,000 + 520,000 − 500,000. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: ignoring the harvested part. The harvested grapes become inventory at their fair value.","i":"9297daea53"},{"s":"sbr-06-apply-1","c":"sbr-06-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A vineyard's vines are carried at $600,000 with 20 years left, and its land, under the revaluation model, rises from $2.0m to $2.1m. What's recorded?","a":"$30,000 depreciation; $0.1m in OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 41 Agriculture; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Rule: bearer plants and land are under IAS 16. Applied: 600,000 / 20; the surplus on land goes to OCI. Common mistake: applying IAS 41 to all of a farm's assets.","i":"73e975a416"},{"s":"sbr-06-apply-1","c":"sbr-06-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Raw materials cost $12,000 for a profitable order, and their price has since fallen to $10,000. At what amount are they carried?","a":"$12,000","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Rule: raw materials aren't written down if the finished goods sell at a profit. Common mistake: using replacement cost automatically.","i":"6fd0ccfdc3"},{"s":"sbr-06-apply-1","c":"sbr-06-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A machine built for a customer for $20,000 cost $19,000. Rule changes need $4,000 more, half paid by the customer. What's it carried at?","a":"$18,000","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Rule: the lower of cost and NRV. Applied: NRV 20,000 − 2,000 = 18,000. Common mistake: deducting the full 4,000.","i":"05fbfff40d"},{"s":"sbr-06-apply-1","c":"sbr-06-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"A winery's fixed overheads are $100,000 at normal activity of 10,000 bottles, and only 8,000 are made. What overhead goes into each bottle?","a":"$10","d":"Reference: IAS 2 Inventories. Rule: fixed overheads are allocated on the normal level of activity. Applied: 100,000 / 10,000; the rest is expensed. Common mistake: dividing by actual output, which inflates inventory.","i":"4d6864a689"}],"i":"5bb70f72fa"}],"i":"adb3e1983a"}],"i":"9e75165f94"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 7: Foreign currency in individual financial statements","ch":7,"d":"Study Text pages 165–180.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"7.1 Functional currency and translation","s":"sbr-07-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#0","x":"card","t":"Functional currency is the currency of the primary economic environment where the entity operates","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Presentation currency is simply the currency the statements are presented in, and can differ.","i":"873bab20f0"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#1","x":"card","t":"The primary factors are the currency driving sales prices, the country whose competition and rules set prices, and the currency driving labour and material costs","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Chive: 70% sales in $ but most costs in £ → judgement needed.","i":"d881cfff0f"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#2","x":"card","t":"If the primary factors are unclear, also look at the currency of financing and of retained receipts","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. These are secondary factors.","i":"20e68b0c72"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#3","x":"card","t":"A foreign subsidiary uses its parent's currency if it is an extension of the parent rather than autonomous","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Also consider transactions with the parent, cash sent up, and need for parent funding.","i":"a41f9f0663"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#4","x":"card","t":"A foreign currency transaction is first recorded at the spot rate on the date (or an average rate if stable)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. E.g. €48,000 at 3.2 = $15,000.","i":"4819d4bebe"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#5","x":"card","t":"On settlement, the difference from the original rate goes to P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Classified like the related item under IFRS 18 (receivable → operating).","i":"6b4877174e"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#6","x":"card","t":"At the year end, monetary items are retranslated at the closing rate, with differences to P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Monetary = cash, receivables, payables, loans.","i":"59e7eb3aef"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#7","x":"card","t":"Non-monetary items at cost are not retranslated (they stay at the historical rate)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Olympic land: 1.2m dinars / 4.0 = $300,000, unchanged.","i":"46e85b1f4c"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#8","x":"card","t":"Non-monetary items at fair value use the rate when fair value was measured","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Pallot: 1.5m / 3.0 = $500,000; the $200,000 gain goes to OCI as an IAS 16 revaluation.","i":"efd01267be"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#9","x":"card","t":"IAS 21 is criticised for a weak theoretical basis because monetary FX goes to P/L but translating foreign operations goes to OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Some argue FX on long-term items should go to OCI as it often reverses.","i":"8f075495d8"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#10","x":"card","t":"Another criticism: IAS 21 doesn't say how to calculate the average rate, allowing manipulation","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It also hurts comparability between entities.","i":"22c6759883"},{"s":"sbr-07-1","c":"sbr-07-1#11","x":"card","t":"Other criticisms: the monetary/non-monetary line is blurry and 'foreign operation' is based on legal form rather than substance","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Long-term items are retranslated at today's rate though repaid later.","i":"b0f4149625"}],"i":"ab6bb81a65"},{"x":"stage","t":"7.2 More on foreign currency","s":"sbr-07-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#0","x":"card","t":"An entity keeps its day-to-day records in its functional currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Any transaction in another currency is translated into the functional currency before it is recorded.","i":"2f51b4b652"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#1","x":"card","t":"Translating means converting a foreign-currency amount into the functional currency, using the rate for that kind of item","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Transactions use the date's spot rate; monetary balances at year end use the closing rate; non-monetary items keep their historical rate (or the rate when fair value was measured).","i":"e1d3b27e78"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#2","x":"card","t":"Monetary items are rights or obligations to receive or pay a fixed or determinable number of currency units","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Cash, receivables, payables and loans are monetary. PPE, inventory, intangibles and prepayments for goods are not.","i":"f0aba0f784"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#3","x":"card","t":"Exchange differences on settling or retranslating monetary items go to profit or loss, in the same IFRS 18 category as the item","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Trade receivable or payable → Operating; a borrowing → Financing; an investment in bonds → Investing.","i":"d83823eed2"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#4","x":"card","t":"When a non-monetary item is remeasured to fair value, its exchange difference follows the fair value gain: OCI if the gain goes to OCI, P/L if it goes to P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Land revalued under IAS 16 → OCI; investment property at fair value under IAS 40 → P/L (Investing).","i":"ae820dadb3"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#5","x":"card","t":"An average rate may be used instead of daily spot rates when rates haven't fluctuated significantly over the period","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. If rates swing, use the actual date's rate. IAS 21 doesn't say how to compute the average, which is a criticism.","i":"b1a62691a1"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#6","x":"card","t":"The functional currency is decided by the entity's economic environment, not by management's preference or where it is registered","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It changes only if the underlying environment changes; then the change is applied from that date.","i":"6a7510a52d"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#7","x":"card","t":"A presentation currency different from the functional currency means the whole statements are translated for presentation; the books stay in the functional currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Covered with foreign operations in Chapter 20.","i":"2a5fe6747a"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#8","x":"card","t":"A foreign subsidiary that acts with autonomy, trades with outsiders and raises its own finance has its own functional currency, not automatically the parent's","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. If it were just an extension of the parent (sells its goods, sends cash back, relies on it for funds), the parent's currency would be its functional currency.","i":"7509e2fcc0"},{"s":"sbr-07-2","c":"sbr-07-2#9","x":"card","t":"To find the exchange difference on a monetary balance, compare the closing balance at the closing rate with the balance built from historical rates","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Opening or initial amount at its rate, less payments at their rates, versus the closing amount at the closing rate: the difference is the gain or loss.","i":"5d181e136f"}],"i":"3ef74dc1da"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"7.S Foreign currency in order","s":"sbr-07-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-07-structure-1","c":"sbr-07-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"A foreign-currency purchase on credit, from start to year end","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Monetary items move with the rate until settled; non-monetary items are frozen at the transaction rate unless remeasured to fair value.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Record the purchase and the payable at the spot rate on the transaction date","i":"8421c24ed5"},{"x":"step","t":"On payment, record the cash at the spot rate on the payment date","i":"952beeeee3"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the difference on settlement to P/L","i":"60a8640940"},{"x":"step","t":"At year end, retranslate any unpaid balance at the closing rate","i":"c6f73fcb69"},{"x":"step","t":"Leave the non-monetary asset bought (e.g. inventory, PPE) at its historical rate","i":"36eaf4a7a8"}],"i":"bd28c853af"},{"s":"sbr-07-structure-1","c":"sbr-07-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Deciding an entity's functional currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Primary factors first; secondary ones settle unclear cases.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Look at the primary factors: currency of sales prices, of the competitive market and regulation, of costs","i":"82320f7e55"},{"x":"step","t":"If unclear, look at the secondary factors: currency of financing, currency receipts are kept in","i":"0c4bc0a9f6"},{"x":"step","t":"For a foreign operation, look at its link with the parent (autonomy, transactions, cash sent up, funding)","i":"e3cf2c1159"},{"x":"step","t":"Conclude on the currency of the primary economic environment","i":"c98f45f60a"}],"i":"4b785d8454"},{"s":"sbr-07-structure-1","c":"sbr-07-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Working out the exchange difference on a foreign loan","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. A simple 'currency, rate, dollars' table makes the balancing figure easy.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Start with the amount borrowed at the rate on the day","i":"c929828fa1"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct repayments at the rates on their dates","i":"62aa6d8045"},{"x":"step","t":"Translate the closing foreign balance at the closing rate","i":"e62df43308"},{"x":"step","t":"The balancing figure is the exchange gain or loss","i":"d0ad8e6239"},{"x":"step","t":"Classify it with the loan: Financing","i":"ec72c9f004"}],"i":"6d17b376f5"},{"s":"sbr-07-structure-1","c":"sbr-07-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"A non-monetary asset held at fair value, in foreign currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. The exchange element is part of the fair value change and follows it.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Record its cost at the rate on the purchase date","i":"c83f5ffee6"},{"x":"step","t":"Measure fair value in the foreign currency at the reporting date","i":"d5a9e76686"},{"x":"step","t":"Translate that fair value at the rate on the date it was measured","i":"c1eabf3097"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the change to OCI (IAS 16 revaluation) or P/L (IAS 40 fair value)","i":"3240852512"}],"i":"d342a92c24"}],"i":"fe98526db1"}],"i":"79306dfaa1"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"7.C1 Monetary or non-monetary?","s":"sbr-07-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-07-compare-1","c":"sbr-07-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"A trade receivable in euros","a":"Monetary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. A right to a fixed number of currency units: retranslate at the closing rate.","i":"691ae87971"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-1","c":"sbr-07-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"A bank loan in a foreign currency","a":"Monetary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. An obligation to pay currency: retranslate at the closing rate; difference to Financing.","i":"1560d1f773"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-1","c":"sbr-07-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Foreign cash held in a bank account","a":"Monetary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Cash is the clearest monetary item.","i":"189e101bd9"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-1","c":"sbr-07-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Machinery bought from an overseas supplier","a":"Non-monetary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. PPE keeps its historical rate (unless revalued).","i":"435fa294ce"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-1","c":"sbr-07-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Inventory bought in a foreign currency","a":"Non-monetary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Recorded at the transaction rate and not retranslated.","i":"66ee30ce27"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-1","c":"sbr-07-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"A prepayment for goods to be delivered next year","a":"Non-monetary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It will be settled with goods, not currency.","i":"27aa1b14e0"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-1","c":"sbr-07-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Land held at cost overseas","a":"Non-monetary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Stays at the purchase-date rate.","i":"2fd573cd6f"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-1","c":"sbr-07-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Interest payable on a foreign loan","a":"Monetary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. An amount of currency to pay.","i":"74581c49a4"}],"i":"c104813c41"},{"x":"stage","t":"7.C2 Factors, rates and where differences go","s":"sbr-07-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"The currency that mainly drives sales prices","a":"Primary factor","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Primary: sales prices, the competitive market and regulation, and costs.","i":"fdb01627a3"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"The currency in which loans are raised","a":"Secondary factor","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Secondary factors are used when the primary ones are unclear.","i":"ceda6eb5c6"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"The currency in which receipts are kept","a":"Secondary factor","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Secondary.","i":"f451f192f0"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"The currency that mainly drives labour and material costs","a":"Primary factor","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Primary.","i":"3b6f68e9b2"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"The rate used to record a sale invoiced in euros","a":"Spot rate on the transaction date","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Or an average rate if rates were stable.","i":"24a46b8f71"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"The rate used for an unpaid foreign receivable at year end","a":"Closing rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Monetary items are retranslated.","i":"9495809eef"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"The rate used for foreign PPE held at cost","a":"Historical rate kept for ever","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Non-monetary items at cost aren't retranslated.","i":"6ce1f07394"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"The exchange loss on retranslating a foreign bank loan","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. IFRS 18: same category as the item, and borrowings are financing.","i":"8b61728c86"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"The exchange gain on a trade receivable","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Trade items are operating.","i":"7ccf8a961d"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"The currency part of a fair value gain on investment property measured at fair value","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 40 Investment Property. IAS 40 fair value changes go to P/L in the investing category.","i":"c391a6aaf7"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#10","x":"sort","t":"The currency part of a revaluation of land under IAS 16","a":"OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. It follows the revaluation gain into OCI.","i":"1d9e811948"},{"s":"sbr-07-compare-2","c":"sbr-07-compare-2#11","x":"sort","t":"The currency the entity keeps its records in","a":"Functional currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Presentation currency is the one the statements are shown in.","i":"9751f248b3"}],"i":"cf56228b19"}],"i":"86e2c59768"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"7.W Foreign currency traps","s":"sbr-07-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"A foreign subsidiary always uses its parent's currency as its functional currency.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Only if it is in substance an extension of the parent; an autonomous subsidiary has its own.","i":"3ed4ea30dc"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Inventory bought in euros is retranslated at the closing rate at year end.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Inventory is non-monetary; it stays at the transaction rate (NRV tests aside).","i":"54792bce72"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"An exchange loss on a foreign bank loan is an operating expense.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Under IFRS 18 it goes with the loan, in the financing category.","i":"428d1a75a6"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"An exchange difference on settling a trade payable goes to OCI.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment; IAS 40 Investment Property. Exchange differences on monetary items go to P/L (here, operating).","i":"cc25d30565"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"An average rate for the year can always replace actual rates.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Only as an approximation when rates haven't moved significantly.","i":"b679ec3a9f"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"A prepayment for goods is retranslated because it is in a foreign currency.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It is non-monetary: it will be settled by goods, not cash.","i":"293ee25920"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"If fair value is measured in a foreign currency, it is translated at the rate when fair value was measured.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Not the purchase-date rate.","i":"f750a7b395"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Presenting the statements in another currency changes the functional currency.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Functional currency comes from the economic environment; presentation currency is a choice.","i":"91182ebe02"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Retranslating a long-term loan at the closing rate is criticised because the rate today may not be the rate on repayment.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Gains and losses may reverse before repayment, making profit volatile.","i":"7457177a02"},{"s":"sbr-07-watch-1","c":"sbr-07-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"IAS 21 is criticised for treating monetary items through P/L but foreign operations through OCI without a clear reason.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment; IAS 40 Investment Property. The lack of a theoretical basis is a known criticism, alongside the unspecified average rate and the blurry monetary/non-monetary line.","i":"d709566148"}],"i":"b0c17e9f5c"}],"i":"2186b95f7f"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"7.A Foreign currency scenarios","s":"sbr-07-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-07-apply-1","c":"sbr-07-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"A company with the dollar as functional currency buys goods for €60,000 on 10 Nov (€1.20 = $1) and pays on 5 Dec (€1.25 = $1). What happens on payment?","a":"A $2,000 exchange gain in P/L (operating)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: record at the spot rate on the transaction date; settle at the spot rate on the payment date; the difference goes to P/L. Applied: recorded at 60,000 / 1.20 = $50,000; paid 60,000 / 1.25 = $48,000; the company paid $2,000 less than it recorded, a gain. P/L category: Operating (exchange gain on a trade payable). Users: Settlement differences show how currency movements hit margins; users can't forecast them easily. Common mistake: dividing the wrong way (€ per $ versus $ per €) and getting a loss.","i":"bb43ecae48"},{"s":"sbr-07-apply-1","c":"sbr-07-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"A company borrows K200,000 on 1 Jan (K1 = $1.50), repays K50,000 on 1 Apr (K1 = $1.60). At 31 Dec K1 = $1.80. What is the exchange difference for the year?","a":"$50,000 loss in Financing","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: a loan is monetary, so it is retranslated at the closing rate and the difference goes to P/L. Applied: borrowed $300,000 (200,000 × 1.50); repaid $80,000 (50,000 × 1.60), leaving $220,000 on the books; closing balance K150,000 × 1.80 = $270,000; loss = 270,000 − 220,000 = $50,000. P/L category: Financing. Users: The loan's dollar burden rose, raising gearing; lenders and investors see currency risk on debt. Common mistake: $30,000 = K150,000 × (1.80 − 1.60), measuring the remaining loan only from the repayment date instead of from the original rate of 1.50.","i":"bf83e3feef"},{"s":"sbr-07-apply-1","c":"sbr-07-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A company buys overseas land for R90,000 (R9 = $1). At year end R12 = $1 and the land's fair value is R132,000. What is the land's carrying amount under the cost model?","a":"$10,000","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: non-monetary items at cost keep the historical rate. Applied: 90,000 / 9 = $10,000, and it isn't retranslated. P/L category: No P/L effect (cost model). Users: Assets abroad held at old rates may be far from today's value; users compare with disclosed fair values. Common mistakes: $7,500 (90,000 / 12, retranslating cost at the closing rate) or $11,000 (the fair value, which the cost model ignores).","i":"9288c2f051"},{"s":"sbr-07-apply-1","c":"sbr-07-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"A company bought overseas land for R90,000 when R9 = $1, a cost of $10,000. It's investment property at fair value under IAS 40, and at the year end its fair value is R132,000, when R12 = $1. Where does the change go?","a":"$1,000 gain in P/L (Investing)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 40 Investment Property. Rule: fair value measured in a foreign currency is translated at the rate when it was measured, and the change follows the fair value model. Applied: 132,000 / 12 = $11,000 versus $10,000 = $1,000 gain; IAS 40 fair value changes go to P/L, in Investing. Under an IAS 16 revaluation it would go to OCI instead. P/L category: No P/L effect (cost model). Users: Assets abroad held at old rates may be far from today's value; users compare with disclosed fair values. Common mistake: $4,667 = 132,000 / 9 − 10,000, using the old rate.","i":"5c047e9fb9"},{"s":"sbr-07-apply-1","c":"sbr-07-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Sales invoices: Dn300,000 when Dn6.0 = $1 and Dn420,000 when Dn7.0 = $1, both unpaid at year end, when Dn7.5 = $1. What is the year-end receivable and the difference?","a":"$96,000 receivable; $14,000 loss (operating)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: revenue and receivables at the spot rate on each invoice; unpaid receivables retranslated at the closing rate. Applied: revenue 50,000 + 60,000 = $110,000; closing receivable 720,000 / 7.5 = $96,000; loss $14,000 to operating (trade item). Revenue is not changed. P/L category: Revenue in Operating; the exchange loss also in Operating. Users: Receivables are worth less in dollars: liquidity and cash collection fall short of revenue. Common mistake: restating revenue at the closing rate.","i":"0ec98de073"},{"s":"sbr-07-apply-1","c":"sbr-07-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"Plant bought on credit for Dn500,000 (Dn10 = $1); Dn200,000 paid when Dn8 = $1; at year end Dn9 = $1. What is the year-end payable?","a":"$33,333, with a loss of $8,333","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: the payable is monetary (retranslate); the plant is non-monetary (keep at $50,000 less depreciation). Applied: recorded $50,000; paid 200,000 / 8 = $25,000, leaving $25,000; closing 300,000 / 9 = $33,333; loss $8,333 in P/L. P/L category: Operating (loss on the payable); depreciation in Operating on $50,000. Users: Currency moves increase the cash needed to pay suppliers. Common mistake: retranslating the plant too.","i":"83488eddf5"},{"s":"sbr-07-apply-1","c":"sbr-07-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"A subsidiary in Country L sets its prices in L-currency, competes in L's market, but buys most materials in euros; it borrows in L-currency, keeps receipts in L, and runs itself with little contact with its euro-based parent. Functional currency?","a":"L-currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: primary factors first (sales prices, competitive market and regulation, costs), then secondary (financing, receipts), and for a foreign operation, its link with the parent. Applied: prices and market point to L; costs point to the euro, so the primary factors are mixed; financing and receipts in L settle it; the subsidiary is autonomous. P/L category: No P/L effect (a judgement that decides how everything is measured). Users: The choice changes where exchange differences appear and so the profit investors see. Common mistake: following the parent's currency.","i":"80f14643b8"},{"s":"sbr-07-apply-1","c":"sbr-07-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"A foreign sales outlet sells only its parent's goods, sends all cash straight back to the parent and is funded by it. Functional currency?","a":"The parent's currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: a foreign operation that is an extension of the parent uses the parent's functional currency. Applied: no autonomy, heavy transactions with the parent, cash remitted, parent funding: it is the parent's business operating abroad. P/L category: No P/L effect (judgement). Users: Treating the outlet as part of the parent avoids exchange differences that don't reflect its economics. Common mistake: looking only at the invoice currency.","i":"231e821723"}],"i":"fccd0fbe86"}],"i":"69d7f924f2"}],"i":"4c94783216"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 8: Leases","ch":8,"d":"Study Text pages 181–210.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"8.1 IFRS 16: identifying a lease","s":"sbr-08-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-1","c":"sbr-08-1#0","x":"card","t":"A lease is a contract conveying the right to use an asset for a period in exchange for consideration","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Lessor gives the right of use; lessee gets it and pays.","i":"a7644f014f"},{"s":"sbr-08-1","c":"sbr-08-1#1","x":"card","t":"A contract contains a lease if it conveys the right to control the use of an identified asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Getting this right matters because lessees then put an asset and liability on the SoFP.","i":"4734c07d4c"},{"s":"sbr-08-1","c":"sbr-08-1#2","x":"card","t":"The customer controls the use if it gets substantially all the benefits and directs how and for what the asset is used","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. AFG ship: AFG decides cargo, timing and ports → a lease, even though Splash crews it.","i":"4246fb348a"},{"s":"sbr-08-1","c":"sbr-08-1#3","x":"card","t":"Contract limits like a mileage cap or 'no pirate waters' define the scope of use, they don't remove control","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Protective restrictions don't stop the customer directing use within them.","i":"80b6396471"},{"s":"sbr-08-1","c":"sbr-08-1#4","x":"card","t":"There's no identified asset if the supplier can practically swap it and would benefit from doing so","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Coffee Bean: airport can relocate the space cheaply → no lease, just a service.","i":"d1a80fa167"}],"i":"2d9aa54114"},{"x":"stage","t":"8.2 IFRS 16: lessee accounting","s":"sbr-08-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#0","x":"card","t":"At commencement, the lessee recognises a right-of-use asset and a lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Both meet the CF definitions: controlled resource and present obligation.","i":"e3ca3be2c4"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#1","x":"card","t":"The lease liability is initially the present value of lease payments not yet paid","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Discounted at the rate implicit in the lease, or else the lessee's incremental borrowing rate.","i":"9453854b85"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#2","x":"card","t":"Lease payments include fixed payments, index-linked payments at today's index, residual value guarantees, likely purchase options and expected termination penalties","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Incentives receivable reduce the fixed payments.","i":"da470a3136"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#3","x":"card","t":"The right-of-use asset's cost is the liability plus payments made at or before the start, initial direct costs and estimated dismantling costs","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Less incentives received. Dynamic: direct costs 3,000 − 1,000 reimbursed = 2,000 added.","i":"2e551e0ec0"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#4","x":"card","t":"The lease term includes extension options reasonably certain to be used (and periods after termination options reasonably certain not to be used)","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Dynamic: 2 years + a certain 1-year option = 3-year term.","i":"088db2e9e3"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#5","x":"card","t":"Afterwards, the liability grows by interest charged to financing P/L, and falls by payments","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Swish crane: 130,715 + 6,536 interest − 48,000 = 89,251.","i":"3f95377317"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#6","x":"card","t":"The right-of-use asset is depreciated over the shorter of lease term and useful life (useful life if ownership transfers)","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Depreciation is operating; interest is financing.","i":"6ef006eb48"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#7","x":"card","t":"A contract with lease and service parts is split by stand-alone prices (or by election per class, treated all as a lease)","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Swish: 160/200 of each $60,000 = $48,000 lease, $12,000 maintenance expense.","i":"e0ab789794"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#8","x":"card","t":"When index-linked payments change (e.g. CPI), the liability is remeasured, with the same change to the ROU asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Kingfisher: new payment = 1m × 140/125. Discount rate unchanged.","i":"198b4abc9d"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#9","x":"card","t":"A revised discount rate is used when the lease term or the purchase option assessment changes","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Use the implicit rate for the remaining term, else the current incremental rate.","i":"4a49f6e8e2"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#10","x":"card","t":"Short-term leases (12 months or less) and low-value assets may be expensed straight-line instead","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Low value is judged when new: an old car is never low value.","i":"4fd6dba5b5"},{"s":"sbr-08-2","c":"sbr-08-2#11","x":"card","t":"Lessees disclose ROU depreciation, lease interest, short-term/low-value expense, cash outflows, additions, ROU carrying amount and a maturity analysis of lease liabilities","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. ROU assets are shown separately or within the line they'd be in if owned.","i":"4000fdb7dc"}],"i":"d3bff2961d"},{"x":"stage","t":"8.3 IFRS 16: lessors, subleases, sale and leaseback","s":"sbr-08-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#0","x":"card","t":"A lessor classifies a lease as finance if it transfers substantially all the risks and rewards of the asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Otherwise it's an operating lease. Lessor rules were barely changed from IAS 17 (cost constraint).","i":"beeac7568b"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#1","x":"card","t":"Finance lease indicators include ownership transfer, a bargain purchase option, most of the life, PV of payments ≈ fair value, and a specialised asset only the lessee can use","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Also: lessee covers cancellation losses, gets residual gains/losses, or can extend at very low rent. DanBob → finance lease.","i":"13d3b71c6a"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#2","x":"card","t":"For a finance lease, the lessor derecognises the asset and recognises a receivable equal to the net investment in the lease","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Net investment = PV of lease payments + unguaranteed residual value. Any gain or loss on disposal is recognised.","i":"ced49b2792"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#3","x":"card","t":"The finance lease receivable grows by finance income and falls by cash received","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Income is operating or investing depending on the business.","i":"19fccc2db5"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#4","x":"card","t":"Under an operating lease, the lessor keeps and depreciates the asset and recognises rent straight-line over the lease term","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Oroc: 175,000 + 6 × 70,000 spread evenly over 7 years. Direct costs are added to the asset.","i":"890c70735e"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#5","x":"card","t":"An intermediate lessor classifies a sublease by reference to the right-of-use asset, not the underlying asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. If the head lease was short-term/low-value, the sublease is always operating.","i":"5f0ecb2dc8"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#6","x":"card","t":"When a sublease is a finance lease, the intermediate lessor derecognises the ROU asset, recognises the net investment and keeps the head lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Poppy: Dr receivable 1m, Cr ROU 0.9m, Cr gain 0.1m.","i":"41a9afb793"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#7","x":"card","t":"When a sublease is an operating lease, the intermediate lessor keeps the ROU asset and liability, and recognises sublease income","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. TS Ltd: 2-year sublease of a 5-year head lease → operating.","i":"6fad956165"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#8","x":"card","t":"In a sale and leaseback, first ask whether the transfer is a sale under IFRS 15","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. If not a sale: seller keeps the asset and records a financial liability; buyer records a financial asset.","i":"cf92715600"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#9","x":"card","t":"If it is a sale, the seller's ROU asset is the share of the old carrying amount that relates to the rights kept","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Gain is only recognised on the rights transferred. Mosaic: ROU 1.7/2.8 × 1.2m = 0.73m.","i":"389d21b4b8"},{"s":"sbr-08-3","c":"sbr-08-3#10","x":"card","t":"Sale proceeds above fair value are treated as extra financing (below fair value: a prepayment of rent)","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Mosaic: 3m price vs 2.8m fair value → 0.2m financial liability.","i":"9aa7b3b0ec"}],"i":"3efbc59ed0"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"8.S1 Lease accounting in order","s":"sbr-08-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-structure-1","c":"sbr-08-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Does a contract contain a lease?","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Contract restrictions on mileage or location only define the scope of use.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Is there an identified asset?","i":"628e03b0ea"},{"x":"step","t":"Can the supplier practically and profitably substitute it? If so, no lease","i":"908a118fe8"},{"x":"step","t":"Does the customer get substantially all its benefits?","i":"c73fd658a8"},{"x":"step","t":"Does the customer direct its use?","i":"546446639f"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, it's a lease","i":"4966ce6f4a"}],"i":"a9da143615"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-1","c":"sbr-08-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Measuring the lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Include guarantees, index-linked payments at today's index, and purchase options reasonably certain to be exercised.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Decide the lease term, including options reasonably certain to be used","i":"312ef4e992"},{"x":"step","t":"List the payments not yet made","i":"35313ee08f"},{"x":"step","t":"Use the rate implicit in the lease, or else the incremental borrowing rate","i":"85fe3cdfe2"},{"x":"step","t":"Discount the payments","i":"5fa0fc239b"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise the present value","i":"14ba5c20e6"}],"i":"be3f933a8f"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-1","c":"sbr-08-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Building the right-of-use asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Depreciate over the shorter of term and useful life, unless ownership passes.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Start with the lease liability","i":"c3e547c2d6"},{"x":"step","t":"Add payments made at or before commencement","i":"88d0581e2b"},{"x":"step","t":"Add initial direct costs, less anything reimbursed","i":"c0015efc62"},{"x":"step","t":"Add estimated dismantling costs","i":"03600a1680"},{"x":"step","t":"This is the asset's cost","i":"bb87850459"}],"i":"c265af6379"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-1","c":"sbr-08-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Each year as a lessee","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Payments in advance reduce the liability first, before interest.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Opening liability","i":"c317f79a2e"},{"x":"step","t":"Add interest (financing)","i":"8f9503949f"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct the payment","i":"b1fa538cca"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing liability","i":"5a35e6aeb8"},{"x":"step","t":"Depreciate the asset (operating)","i":"b07647a7c2"}],"i":"296e8a126e"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-1","c":"sbr-08-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"Changes in lease payments","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. No P/L entry at the time of the change.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify the change","i":"d4c5bcd453"},{"x":"step","t":"If the term or a purchase option changed, use a revised rate","i":"483c3a412c"},{"x":"step","t":"If only an index changed, keep the original rate","i":"5df4ceddcf"},{"x":"step","t":"Recalculate the liability","i":"8ea8fbd211"},{"x":"step","t":"Adjust the right-of-use asset by the same amount","i":"db3b91177b"}],"i":"c68dc3ffa1"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-1","c":"sbr-08-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"Accounting for a finance lease as a lessor","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Finance income goes to operating or investing, by the nature of the business.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Derecognise the leased asset","i":"4669213c3b"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise a receivable at the net investment","i":"61ce4fb639"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise any gain or loss","i":"c23baeacc7"},{"x":"step","t":"Add finance income each year","i":"4c80460380"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct the cash received","i":"b91170f3c8"}],"i":"5b7d4a6890"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-1","c":"sbr-08-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"A sublease as an intermediate lessor","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The liability to the head lessor stays whatever happens.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Classify it by reference to the right-of-use asset","i":"043f276bb5"},{"x":"step","t":"For a finance lease, derecognise the right-of-use asset","i":"b896255bf9"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise the net investment in the sublease","i":"827652161c"},{"x":"step","t":"Take any difference to P/L","i":"57308139ea"},{"x":"step","t":"Keep the head lease liability","i":"3c1ef8c219"}],"i":"db7003890e"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-1","c":"sbr-08-structure-1#7","x":"steps","t":"A sale and leaseback that is a sale","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. If the sale isn't at fair value, adjust for financing or a prepayment first.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Use IFRS 15 to check that control has passed","i":"10fda65007"},{"x":"step","t":"Derecognise the asset","i":"adc9c5d2f0"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise the lease liability","i":"b77626ed57"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise the right-of-use asset as the share of the old carrying amount retained","i":"cc34ffbd99"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise only the gain on the rights transferred","i":"d07e098bff"}],"i":"f8e3c38e47"}],"i":"59b6f25df7"},{"x":"stage","t":"8.S2 What goes in?","s":"sbr-08-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Fixed lease payments not yet made","a":"In the lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The core of the liability.","i":"c1298ce1a0"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Payments linked to an index, at today's index","a":"In the lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Later index changes lead to a remeasurement.","i":"5847547fb3"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Amount expected under a residual value guarantee","a":"In the lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Part of what's expected to be paid.","i":"64362da3a9"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Option to buy reasonably certain to be used","a":"In the lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. It's included.","i":"893030b640"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"A purchase option the entity doesn't expect to use","a":"Not in the lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Left out.","i":"227a2e930d"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Rent for an extension period not reasonably certain","a":"Not in the lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. It's outside the lease term.","i":"0d52bfbb91"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Payments for a separately priced maintenance service","a":"Not in the lease liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. A non-lease component, unless the entity elects to combine them.","i":"54a0f949bf"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Payment on the first day of the lease","a":"In the right-of-use asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Already paid, so not in the liability.","i":"5c7cb259ae"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Initial direct costs, less amounts reimbursed","a":"In the right-of-use asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Added to cost.","i":"30561cd9c7"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Estimated cost of removing the asset at the end","a":"In the right-of-use asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Added to cost, with a provision.","i":"74912f9acb"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Lease payments for a tablet, under the low-value exemption","a":"Not in the right-of-use asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Expensed straight-line.","i":"9e04396238"},{"s":"sbr-08-structure-2","c":"sbr-08-structure-2#11","x":"sort","t":"A 10-month rental, under the short-term exemption","a":"Not in the right-of-use asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Expensed straight-line.","i":"2b4a688759"}],"i":"d9c78b7406"}],"i":"642d3e58de"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"8.C1 Lease or not, finance or operating","s":"sbr-08-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"A specified ship; the customer decides cargo and route; the owner crews it","a":"Lease","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The customer directs its use. The crew doesn't change that.","i":"9b94f72843"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Floor space that the airport can move at any time at little cost","a":"Service","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Substantive substitution right.","i":"d75f565b2a"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"A car with a mileage cap","a":"Lease","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. A cap only sets the scope of the right.","i":"8947c4cb87"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Delivery capacity on any of a supplier's vans","a":"Service","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. No identified asset.","i":"6b79674bf9"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Ownership passes to the lessee at the end","a":"Probably finance","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Risks and rewards have transferred.","i":"19092b053d"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Lease term is the major part of the asset's life","a":"Probably finance","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. An indicator.","i":"a3c0c8970c"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"PV of payments is substantially all of fair value","a":"Probably finance","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. An indicator.","i":"f42b8427a3"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Specialised asset that only this lessee could use","a":"Probably finance","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. An indicator.","i":"2799e6495c"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"A two-year rental of a machine with a 20-year life","a":"Probably operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Risks and rewards stay with the lessor.","i":"e99dc85117"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Lessee can continue at far below market rent","a":"Probably finance","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. An indicator.","i":"1cab02d921"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Tablets for staff","a":"Low value","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. An example in IFRS 16.","i":"a64e7c8040"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"An old car","a":"Not low value","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Judged on value when new.","i":"ddd0a72918"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-1","c":"sbr-08-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"A small telephone system of many phones","a":"Low value","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. An example is telephones.","i":"38814ecfb0"}],"i":"0c9c60c990"},{"x":"stage","t":"8.C2 Lessors, subleases and leasebacks","s":"sbr-08-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Derecognises the asset and shows a receivable","a":"Finance lease (lessor)","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The asset has effectively been sold.","i":"6e231784b8"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Keeps and depreciates the asset","a":"Operating lease (lessor)","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rental income straight-line.","i":"2e115c97e2"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Direct costs are added to the underlying asset","a":"Operating lease (lessor)","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Expensed over the lease term.","i":"a598a294a1"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Sublease for the rest of the head lease's term","a":"Finance sublease","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. All the risks and rewards of the right-of-use asset pass.","i":"5e81c1ad24"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Two-year sublease of a five-year head lease","a":"Operating sublease","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Not the major part of the right-of-use asset's life.","i":"74d34da365"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Sublease where the head lease used the short-term exemption","a":"Operating sublease","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Always operating.","i":"38423940b8"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Control passes to the buyer","a":"Sale","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. IFRS 15 decides.","i":"3c07257493"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"The seller has the right to buy the asset back","a":"Not a sale: financing","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Control hasn't passed.","i":"8ae75ce01f"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Proceeds above fair value","a":"Additional financing","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The excess is a loan.","i":"155098e333"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Proceeds below fair value","a":"Prepaid lease payments","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The shortfall is rent paid in advance.","i":"e2ea311f79"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#10","x":"sort","t":"A variable-rate payment index changes","a":"Keep the original rate","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Only the payments change.","i":"014a5d4916"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#11","x":"sort","t":"The lease term changes after an option is reassessed","a":"Use a revised rate","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The rate at reassessment.","i":"413614c685"},{"s":"sbr-08-compare-2","c":"sbr-08-compare-2#12","x":"sort","t":"The assessment of a purchase option changes","a":"Use a revised rate","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The rate at reassessment.","i":"da6b68e799"}],"i":"c6f8131c75"}],"i":"5462503562"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"8.W Lease traps","s":"sbr-08-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"All leases go on a lessee's balance sheet except short-term and low-value ones.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. IFRS 16 removed the old operating lease treatment for lessees.","i":"54ff12ecfd"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"A restriction on where a vehicle can go means the customer doesn't direct its use.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. It only defines the scope of the right of use.","i":"6a0732ec90"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"A supplier's right to swap the asset always prevents a lease.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. It must be practical and economically beneficial for the supplier.","i":"6d4336673d"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Lease payments linked to an index are measured at the expected future index.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Use the index at commencement, then remeasure.","i":"bec58d970c"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A right-of-use asset is depreciated over the asset's useful life if ownership will pass.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Otherwise the shorter of term and life.","i":"7c1b4cbf01"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"An extension option is in the lease term only if reasonably certain to be exercised.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The term also includes periods after a termination option if reasonably certain not to be used.","i":"be9cc96919"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"A remeasured liability creates a gain or loss at that date.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The change goes against the right-of-use asset.","i":"3fed472b96"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Combining lease and service elements lowers the liability.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. It raises the liability, which may make the entity look worse.","i":"04f969a28b"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"A right-of-use asset can be at fair value if the entity uses fair value for investment property.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. If it meets the definition, it must be.","i":"1c94580788"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"IFRS 16 significantly changed lessor accounting.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. It changed lessees; lessor rules carried on, because the benefits wouldn't outweigh the costs.","i":"4ad23d3914"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"An intermediate lessor classifies a sublease by the underlying asset.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. By the right-of-use asset.","i":"570b9b2b66"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"A finance sublease removes the head lease liability.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The payments are still owed to the head lessor.","i":"c008e3718a"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"In a leaseback that qualifies as a sale, only part of the gain is recognised.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Only the part on rights transferred.","i":"bbc1f9a5d6"},{"s":"sbr-08-watch-1","c":"sbr-08-watch-1#13","x":"trap","t":"A leaseback that doesn't qualify as a sale is treated as a loan.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. The asset stays, and the proceeds are a financial liability.","i":"35cc4c9e1f"}],"i":"fa0c3333b1"}],"i":"77ad018097"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"8.A1 Lessee accounting","s":"sbr-08-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Pop rents floor space at a mall to run its own portable stall. The mall can move the stall's location at any time, with little cost and plenty of suitable space. Is there a lease?","a":"No: the mall has a real right to substitute","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: no identified asset exists if the supplier can practically and profitably substitute. Applied: rental is expensed as a service. Users: no asset or liability, so gearing isn't affected. Common mistake: focusing on the amount of space.","i":"e185b729f2"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Dart contracts to use a named ship for five years, deciding cargo, timing and ports. The owner crews it and bans hazardous cargo. Is there a lease?","a":"Yes: Dart directs its use","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: control means substantially all benefits plus the right to direct use. Applied: a named ship, no substitution right, and Dart decides how it is used; restrictions only protect the owner. Users: the liability is on the balance sheet. Common mistake: looking at who operates the asset.","i":"888b6c13d2"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Fenn leases a truck for two years at $20,000 a year in arrears, with a one-year extension at $30,000 it's reasonably certain to use. Its direct costs are $6,000, of which $2,000 is reimbursed. At 5%, what's the right-of-use asset?","a":"$67,103","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Step 1 (liability): 20,000/1.05 + 20,000/1.1025 + 30,000/1.1576 = 63,103. Step 2 (asset): 63,103 + 6,000 − 2,000 = 67,103. Common mistake: leaving out the extension gives 37,188 + 4,000 = 41,188.","i":"f966eb37fc"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Fenn's truck lease has a right-of-use asset of $67,103 and a liability of $63,103, over a 3-year term at 5%, with $20,000 paid at the end of year 1. The truck has a 10-year life and ownership doesn't pass. What are the first year's depreciation and closing liability?","a":"$22,368 and $46,258","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Step 1 (depreciation): shorter of 3-year term and 10-year life: 67,103 / 3 = 22,368, in operating. Step 2 (liability): 63,103 + 5% interest of 3,155 − 20,000 = 46,258; interest in financing. Common mistake: depreciating over the useful life when ownership doesn't pass.","i":"987f6cbb02"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A crane contract costs $90,000 a year in arrears for three years, with maintenance included. Stand-alone prices are $120,000 for the lease and $30,000 for maintenance. The rate is 5%. What is first recognised as the liability?","a":"$196,074","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Step 1 (split): 120/150 × 90,000 = 72,000 lease; 18,000 maintenance, expensed in operating. Step 2: 72,000 × (1/1.05 + 1/1.1025 + 1/1.1576) = 196,074. Users: combining them, if chosen, would raise the liability. Common mistake: discounting the full payment.","i":"e271233605"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"Rent of $2m a year, paid in advance, is index-linked. At the start of year 2 the index has risen 10%, with two payments left. The liability, including year 1 interest, is $3.905m. What's the entry at the original 5% rate?","a":"Liability and asset up by about $0.39m","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: a change in an index means recalculating the liability, against the right-of-use asset, at the original rate. Applied: 2.2 + 2.2/1.05 = 4.295; 4.295 − 3.905 = 0.39. Common mistake: taking it to P/L, or revising the rate.","i":"40fe79998e"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"Which of these can use the low-value exemption?","a":"A batch of tablets","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: low value is judged when new; IFRS 16's examples include tablets, small computers, telephones and small furniture. Common mistake: looking at current value, so that an old vehicle looks cheap.","i":"dd573b26a3"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"Cleo hires a machine under a 10-month agreement and wants no asset or liability. What's the treatment?","a":"Expense it straight-line: short-term","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: for 12 months or less at inception, payments can be recognised straight-line in operating. Users: expense is disclosed, so they can see it. Common mistake: expensing it as cash is paid.","i":"a64e47ae2c"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-1","c":"sbr-08-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"Mara presents a three-year vehicle contract as a 'service' to keep gearing down before a loan. It has named vehicles and directs their use. What's right?","a":"It's a lease, and calling it a service is misleading","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Rule: substance decides: right to control an identified asset. Ethics: the motive creates a self-interest threat, and integrity is at risk. Users: lenders would underestimate debt. Common mistake: treating maintenance as making it a service.","i":"e56bc2dfe7"}],"i":"31fb1d88e0"},{"x":"stage","t":"8.A2 Lessors, subleases and sale and leaseback","s":"sbr-08-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"A building with a 50-year life is let for 30 years. The tenant pays 40% of its value upfront, and 6% a year for 30 years. It can continue indefinitely at far below market rent, and must compensate the lessor if it cancels. What's the classification?","a":"Finance lease","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: a finance lease transfers substantially all risks and rewards. Applied: payments of 40% plus 6% × 30 well exceed fair value in present-value terms; there is also below-market continuation and cancellation compensation. Common mistake: looking only at whether title passes.","i":"48682ced04"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"Veer leases out machinery under a finance lease at $3,000 a year in arrears for four years. PV is $8,565 at 15%. What's the first year's finance income and closing receivable?","a":"$1,285 and $6,850","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: the receivable grows by finance income and falls with cash. Applied: 8,565 × 15% = 1,285; 8,565 + 1,285 − 3,000 = 6,850. P/L category: Operating or investing, by the nature of the business. Common mistake: treating the rental as income.","i":"7bce11f0c3"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"Oak lets a crane on a seven-year operating lease: $140,000 on day one, then six annual payments of $70,000. What is rental income each year?","a":"$80,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: operating lease income is straight-line. Applied: 140,000 + 6 × 70,000 = 560,000; / 7 = 80,000. The extra $60,000 in year 1 is deferred. The crane stays and is depreciated. Common mistake: taking cash as income.","i":"1693180293"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"Poole has a five-year head lease, first recognised at $2m. After two years it sublets for the remaining three, with a PV of $1.4m. What's recognised?","a":"$0.2m gain; head liability stays","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: a sublease covering the rest of the term is a finance lease, by reference to the right-of-use asset. Applied: asset 2 × 3/5 = 1.2; net investment 1.4; gain 0.2, in operating. The liability stays because payments are still owed. Common mistake: removing the liability.","i":"8f3322947f"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"Tess has a five-year head lease and sublets for two years. It uses historical cost whenever possible. How does it treat this?","a":"Operating sublease: keeps asset and liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: two of five years isn't the major part of the right-of-use asset's life. Applied: depreciation over five years, interest on the liability, and sublease income systematically over two. Common mistake: looking at the underlying asset rather than the right-of-use asset.","i":"b5c3aff861"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#5","x":"example","t":"Saul sells a machine with a carrying amount of $2m for its $5m fair value and leases it back. The PV of the lease payments is $2m. Control passes. What are the right-of-use asset and gain?","a":"ROU $0.8m; gain $1.8m","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Step 1 (share kept): 2/5 = 40%. Step 2 (asset): 40% × 2 = 0.8. Step 3 (gain): total 3.0 × 60% transferred = 1.8. Entry: Dr Cash 5, Dr ROU 0.8, Cr Machine 2, Cr Lease liability 2, Cr Gain 1.8. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: taking the whole gain.","i":"950b3296c4"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#6","x":"example","t":"Saul sells a machine with a carrying amount of $2m for $5.3m, against a fair value of $5m, and leases it back. The PV of the lease payments is $2.3m, and control passes. What's the extra element?","a":"$0.3m financial liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: proceeds above fair value are additional financing, and are accounted for under IFRS 9. Applied: lease part 2.0; asset and gain as before. Payments are split in proportion to present values. Common mistake: taking the excess to profit.","i":"82f03285df"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#7","x":"example","t":"Una sells a building for $4m, below its $4.5m fair value, and leases it back. What's the $0.5m?","a":"Prepaid lease payments","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: below-market terms are treated as a prepayment of lease payments. Common mistake: mixing up the two directions.","i":"93fc2cf1a1"},{"s":"sbr-08-apply-2","c":"sbr-08-apply-2#8","x":"example","t":"Lark 'sells' a factory, but can buy it back at any time. What's the accounting?","a":"Keep the factory; show a financial liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 16 Leases; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: if the transfer isn't a sale under IFRS 15, the seller keeps the asset and records the proceeds as a financial liability. Users: gearing rises, as it should. Common mistake: following the legal form.","i":"9754027559"}],"i":"8c50cce9f2"}],"i":"c311e53037"}],"i":"028c9eeb14"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 9: Employee benefits","ch":9,"d":"Study Text pages 211–240.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"9.1 IAS 19: types and pension plans","s":"sbr-09-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-09-1","c":"sbr-09-1#0","x":"card","t":"IAS 19 covers post-employment, short-term, termination and other long-term benefits","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Each type has its own rules.","i":"7dfc30bff8"},{"s":"sbr-09-1","c":"sbr-09-1#1","x":"card","t":"A defined contribution plan is one where the entity pays fixed contributions and has no obligation to pay more if the fund falls short","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. The employee bears the investment risk.","i":"80729f22fb"},{"s":"sbr-09-1","c":"sbr-09-1#2","x":"card","t":"A defined benefit plan is any post-employment plan that isn't defined contribution","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. The entity bears the risk of paying the promised pension.","i":"20c64f0a64"},{"s":"sbr-09-1","c":"sbr-09-1#3","x":"card","t":"A plan the company could cancel can still be defined benefit if its past practice creates a constructive obligation","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Deller: generous history, public praise and trusted leaders → constructive DB plan.","i":"678807d923"},{"s":"sbr-09-1","c":"sbr-09-1#4","x":"card","t":"A DC plan is accounted for by expensing the contributions due for the period (accrue or prepay any difference)","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Example: 5% × $2.7m = $135,000 expense; paid 120,000 → accrual 15,000.","i":"06c1239e52"}],"i":"229723852b"},{"x":"stage","t":"9.2 IAS 19: defined benefit accounting","s":"sbr-09-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#0","x":"card","t":"On the SoFP, a DB plan is shown as PV of the obligation less FV of plan assets","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Usually a deficit (liability). An actuary is recommended for the measurements.","i":"48d1962f16"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#1","x":"card","t":"The movement in the net deficit is: opening + net interest + service cost − contributions ± remeasurement = closing","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Benefits paid reduce both obligation and assets, so they don't change the net figure.","i":"2114de6a08"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#2","x":"card","t":"Net interest = opening discount rate × opening net deficit (or surplus), in financing","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Celine: 4% × 60 = 2.4.","i":"ee79ac920e"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#3","x":"card","t":"The service cost includes current service cost, past service cost and gains or losses on settlement (all operating)","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Current service cost = the increase in the obligation from this year's work.","i":"52c8d8eb68"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#4","x":"card","t":"Past service cost (from improving or curtailing the plan) is recognised immediately, whether the benefits have vested or not","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. At the earlier of the amendment/curtailment and any related restructuring or termination costs. Example: 270,000 at once.","i":"800f161df2"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#5","x":"card","t":"A curtailment is a significant cut in the number of employees covered","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. AB: obligation down 10% × 600,000 = 60,000 gain in service cost.","i":"6c4e73fd9b"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#6","x":"card","t":"A settlement eliminates all or part of the obligation; the gain or loss is assets paid out vs obligation removed","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Mickleover: paid 0.3m to settle 0.2m → 0.1m loss.","i":"f5190ea963"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#7","x":"card","t":"Employer contributions into the plan don't affect P/L or OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. They raise plan assets and cut the net deficit.","i":"2e8d513f07"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#8","x":"card","t":"The remeasurement component is the balancing figure, taken to OCI and never recycled","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. It holds actuarial gains/losses and the return on assets above interest. McVeigh: releasing it over working lives or recycling is wrong.","i":"c0e168e9eb"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#9","x":"card","t":"After a plan amendment, curtailment or settlement, the rest of the year uses updated assumptions for service cost and net interest","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. McVeigh: after 1 March, net interest should use 3%, not 4%.","i":"8c255b88a4"},{"s":"sbr-09-2","c":"sbr-09-2#10","x":"card","t":"A surplus is capped by the asset ceiling: the PV of refunds or reductions in future contributions","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Example: surplus 150, ceiling 70 → recognise 70; the reduction goes to OCI.","i":"b9d29e5ebd"}],"i":"84bf914b14"},{"x":"stage","t":"9.3 IAS 19: other benefits and criticisms","s":"sbr-09-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#0","x":"card","t":"Short-term benefits like wages and bonuses are expensed on the accruals basis as the work is done","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. A bonus is recognised when there's an obligation that can be measured.","i":"11424f1f23"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#1","x":"card","t":"Accumulating absences like carried-forward holiday are accrued as the employee earns them","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Non-accumulating absences (sick pay) are expensed only when they happen.","i":"031a0d1d94"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#2","x":"card","t":"Termination benefits are recognised at the earlier of when the offer can't be withdrawn and when IAS 37 restructuring costs are recognised","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Birch-Hazel: $8,000 per employee is termination; the extra $16,000 for staying is earned over the 6 months.","i":"e6b44afaec"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#3","x":"card","t":"Other long-term benefits (long-service leave) are measured like DB plans but remeasurements go to P/L, not OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. There's no OCI cushion for these.","i":"9e5f116276"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#4","x":"card","t":"DB disclosures include assumptions, a reconciliation, the components of the charge, and a sensitivity analysis","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. It shows how much the deficit moves if, say, life expectancy rises a year.","i":"04714c8384"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#5","x":"card","t":"IAS 19 is criticised for hard classification, volatile assets, complexity and including unvested benefits and future salaries in the obligation","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Some argue these aren't present obligations under the CF definition.","i":"6091cb4d5c"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#6","x":"card","t":"Termination benefits are payable because employment ends, by the employer's decision or an employee accepting voluntary redundancy","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. They're normally a lump sum.","i":"a2cddabfc8"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#7","x":"card","t":"A termination offer can no longer be withdrawn once a detailed plan has been communicated to the employees affected","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. From then the liability and expense are recognised, unless IAS 37 restructuring costs came first.","i":"873f43a483"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#8","x":"card","t":"Termination benefits wholly settled within 12 months are short-term benefits; otherwise they're treated as other long-term benefits (an enhanced pension follows the pension rules)","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. They're measured when first recognised; later changes follow the kind of benefit.","i":"af006a2f49"},{"s":"sbr-09-3","c":"sbr-09-3#9","x":"card","t":"When staff get more for staying until a closure, the amount paid regardless is a termination benefit, and the extra is pay for their service, recognised over the period they stay","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. E.g. $8,000 each payable anyway is termination; the extra $16,000 for staying the 6 months is earned over them.","i":"68ccc4b734"}],"i":"dc5122acd2"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"9.S1 Pension workings in order","s":"sbr-09-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-09-structure-1","c":"sbr-09-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"The net defined benefit movement","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. The remeasurement goes to OCI and is never recycled.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Opening net deficit (obligation less assets)","i":"cc18bc59e2"},{"x":"step","t":"Add net interest at the opening discount rate","i":"413dad8c79"},{"x":"step","t":"Add the service cost","i":"1f66a1dd0e"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct contributions paid in","i":"c806e13204"},{"x":"step","t":"Benefits paid: no effect on the net figure","i":"022c8fa8c9"},{"x":"step","t":"Remeasurement is the balancing figure","i":"cf9ee493e4"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing net deficit from the actuary","i":"37febd5f20"}],"i":"32bd24e6a8"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-1","c":"sbr-09-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Is it a defined benefit plan?","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. A right to cancel doesn't help if cancelling isn't realistic.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Look at what the entity has promised","i":"72b1db77cd"},{"x":"step","t":"Is it only to pay fixed contributions?","i":"a249e62fd0"},{"x":"step","t":"Would it have to pay more if the fund fell short?","i":"dbc3e6a012"},{"x":"step","t":"Consider constructive obligations from past practice","i":"dbcb12d5b8"},{"x":"step","t":"If more could be due, it's defined benefit","i":"0d814f9cb9"}],"i":"f9e7bd7063"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-1","c":"sbr-09-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Applying the asset ceiling","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. An asset is only recognised if it can bring economic benefits.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find the surplus: assets less obligation","i":"8b5a95d3dc"},{"x":"step","t":"Find the PV of future refunds and reduced contributions","i":"1396d29d73"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise the lower","i":"57837490ed"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the reduction to the remeasurement","i":"632ac9e0b4"},{"x":"step","t":"Show it in OCI","i":"6cd60aa017"}],"i":"f40bf94984"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-1","c":"sbr-09-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"After a plan amendment, curtailment or settlement","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. The updated assumptions give the most faithful view.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Measure the deficit before the event on updated assumptions","i":"8718bf1aa5"},{"x":"step","t":"Measure it after the event","i":"9e226016c0"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the difference to service cost","i":"ee5a7f2f83"},{"x":"step","t":"Use the updated assumptions for service cost for the rest of the year","i":"c683c0686a"},{"x":"step","t":"Use the remeasured deficit and new rate for interest for the rest of the year","i":"d5f1e3246d"}],"i":"684096f509"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-1","c":"sbr-09-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"Termination benefits","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. An offer can't be withdrawn once a detailed plan has been communicated. Not part of it: Recognise it only when the employees actually leave; Spread it all over the remaining service of every employee.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify what's payable because employment ends","i":"c22fd242fb"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise it at the earlier of when the offer can't be withdrawn and when restructuring costs are recognised","i":"50531215fe"},{"x":"step","t":"Treat amounts earned by staying on as pay for that service","i":"0f143dd2b9"},{"x":"step","t":"If settled within 12 months: short-term benefit","i":"db67126493"},{"x":"step","t":"If later: other long-term benefit","i":"48b19922a1"}],"any":true,"i":"d82ceb7666"}],"i":"ce35dfa415"},{"x":"stage","t":"9.S2 Where does it go?","s":"sbr-09-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Current service cost","a":"P/L: operating","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. This year's work.","i":"0081cef851"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Past service cost from improving a plan","a":"P/L: operating","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Immediately, whether or not vested.","i":"2fe9e95ebd"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Gain on a curtailment","a":"P/L: operating","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Part of the service cost.","i":"d208d594b3"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Loss on a settlement","a":"P/L: operating","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Part of the service cost.","i":"34220d62ba"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Net interest on a pension deficit","a":"P/L: financing","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. The passage of time.","i":"6481811f7b"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Actuarial gain from new mortality assumptions","a":"OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Remeasurement.","i":"579bc17106"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Return on plan assets above the interest element","a":"OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Remeasurement.","i":"e3e9a633e0"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Reduction in a surplus from the asset ceiling","a":"OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Remeasurement.","i":"0d9b967353"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Contributions paid into the plan","a":"No effect on P/L or OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. It's cash into the plan, moving the net figure.","i":"dc417ed2d7"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Pensions paid to retired staff from the plan","a":"No effect on P/L or OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Both assets and obligation fall.","i":"43b6b9bd87"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Remeasurement on a long-service award","a":"P/L: operating","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Other long-term benefits have no OCI.","i":"89102b908f"},{"s":"sbr-09-structure-2","c":"sbr-09-structure-2#11","x":"sort","t":"Contributions due to a defined contribution plan","a":"P/L: operating","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. An expense in the period.","i":"b297d0a89f"}],"i":"d24cb738dd"}],"i":"3bd2c6aad7"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"9.C Side by side","s":"sbr-09-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Fixed contributions with no further obligation","a":"Defined contribution","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. The risk stays with employees.","i":"734a4f0828"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Pension of 1/60 of final salary for each year worked","a":"Defined benefit","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. The entity bears investment and longevity risk.","i":"4116ce7c8e"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"A company makes up any shortfall in the fund","a":"Defined benefit","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. A further obligation.","i":"7f48344f88"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"A voluntary fund paying by final salary, at a company known for generous benefits","a":"Defined benefit","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. A constructive obligation.","i":"e37cce3f32"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Net figure measured with assets at fair value and obligation at PV","a":"Defined benefit","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Usually a deficit.","i":"addbed6109"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Holiday that can be carried forward","a":"Accumulating: accrue","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Earned over time.","i":"a13a4c9d55"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Sick pay that lapses if unused","a":"Non-accumulating: when taken","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. No liability until the absence.","i":"f297ef0252"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Wages and an annual bonus earned this year","a":"Short-term","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. On an accruals basis.","i":"638fb992b1"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Long-service award after 20 years","a":"Other long-term","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Similar to a pension, but with no OCI.","i":"51ea153a77"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Long-term disability benefit","a":"Other long-term","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Payable beyond 12 months.","i":"0dc07d3c40"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Voluntary redundancy lump sum","a":"Termination","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Because employment ends.","i":"cb5e375916"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Significantly fewer employees covered, after a closure","a":"Curtailment","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. A reduction in those covered.","i":"61fd4f67f5"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Transferring a leaver's fund to the new employer's plan","a":"Settlement","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Eliminates the obligation for those benefits.","i":"7f5080688d"},{"s":"sbr-09-compare-1","c":"sbr-09-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Improving the benefit from 2% to 2.5% a year","a":"Plan amendment","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. A change in what's promised.","i":"67381801f4"}],"i":"194c5d25c6"}],"i":"3aa82385b3"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"9.W Pension traps","s":"sbr-09-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"A defined benefit obligation is measured at present value.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Plan assets are at fair value.","i":"11694828e2"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Benefits paid to pensioners change the net deficit.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Assets and obligation fall by the same amount.","i":"dd830f6cc2"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Contributions reduce the expense in profit or loss.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. They only move the net balance.","i":"0f2b04d00b"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Net interest uses the discount rate at the start of the year.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Applied to the opening net figure.","i":"ebbc6e42fd"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"Past service costs wait until the benefits vest.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Vesting isn't relevant: recognise them at the earlier of the event and any related restructuring or termination costs.","i":"1763893ef9"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"A remeasurement loss can be spread over employees' remaining working lives.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. It goes to OCI in full, immediately.","i":"2976a4cb9a"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"The remeasurement component can be recycled to P/L later.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. There's no clear basis for the amount or timing.","i":"80292640b2"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"A surplus is limited to refunds and reductions in future contributions.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. The asset ceiling.","i":"9c1ff46836"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"A plan the company could legally cancel can still be defined benefit.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. If past practice creates a valid expectation.","i":"f7f90a3907"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Using an actuary removes all uncertainty in pension figures.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. It reduces bias, but the assumptions can still change a lot.","i":"e26d468ddf"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"Sensitivity analysis on pension assumptions must be disclosed.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Investors can see the exposure.","i":"a5c7593852"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"An unused non-accumulating absence creates a liability.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. It's only recognised when the absence occurs.","i":"cb2ed8907d"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"Reflecting future salary rises seems to conflict with the Framework's liability definition.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits; Conceptual Framework. A stated criticism: there's no current obligation to pay them.","i":"42d7878392"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#13","x":"trap","t":"Long-service award remeasurements go to OCI.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. They go to P/L.","i":"245f3638a7"},{"s":"sbr-09-watch-1","c":"sbr-09-watch-1#14","x":"trap","t":"Termination benefits are recognised when the employees actually leave.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. At the earlier of when the offer can't be withdrawn and when IAS 37 restructuring costs are recognised.","i":"7235aad76e"}],"i":"279f2f9822"}],"i":"848af740c4"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"9.A Employee benefit scenarios","s":"sbr-09-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Deno has a defined contribution scheme and adds a voluntary fund paying pensions by service and final salary. It can legally cancel the fund, but it's known for generous benefits and has been praised for this. What is the new fund?","a":"Defined benefit","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: a plan that isn't only fixed contributions is defined benefit, and constructive obligations count. Applied: payments depend on final salary, and cancelling is unrealistic. Users: a liability appears. Common mistake: relying on the legal right to cancel.","i":"8cbb555fc3"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"A company contributes 6% of gross salaries of $3m to a defined contribution plan, paying $12,000 a month with the rest due next month. What's the expense and the balance?","a":"$180,000; $36,000 accrual","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: the expense is the contribution due. Applied: 6% × 3m = 180,000; paid 144,000; 36,000 owed. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: expensing the cash paid.","i":"694a0dd86d"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Opening obligation 500, assets 400, discount rate 5%. Service cost 60, contributions 40, benefits paid 30. Closing obligation 620, assets 470. What's in operating, financing and OCI?","a":"60, 5 and 25","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Step 1: net opening 100. Step 2: interest 100 × 5% = 5, in financing. Step 3: service 60, in operating. Step 4: 100 + 5 + 60 − 40 = 125; closing net 150; remeasurement loss 25, in OCI. Users: the profit line isn't hit by short-term swings. Common mistake: letting benefits paid affect the movement.","i":"43c54836cb"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"On 1 January a plan improves benefits for all past service, adding $400,000 to the obligation. $150,000 relates to staff who haven't yet met the five-year service condition. When is it recognised?","a":"All $400,000 now","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: past service cost is recognised at the earlier of the amendment and related restructuring costs; vesting doesn't matter. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: spreading it.","i":"9e3eddb18c"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A closure cuts the pension obligation of $1,000,000 by 15%, with $800,000 of assets remaining in the plan. What's the gain, and the net position?","a":"$150,000 gain; $50,000 deficit","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: a curtailment reduces the obligation, with the gain in service cost. Applied: 1,000,000 × 15% = 150,000; 850,000 − 800,000 = 50,000. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: taking it to OCI.","i":"ec00367da8"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"A plan pays $0.5m to settle an obligation with a present value of $0.4m. What's the treatment?","a":"$0.1m loss in service cost","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: a settlement's gain or loss is the difference between assets paid and obligation removed. Common mistake: treating it as a remeasurement.","i":"7967df368d"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"A May year end: net deficit 200 at 1 June, with a 5% rate. After a settlement on 1 March, the deficit is 220 and the rate is 4%. What's the year's net interest?","a":"9.7","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Step 1 (9 months): 200 × 5% × 9/12 = 7.5. Step 2 (3 months, updated): 220 × 4% × 3/12 = 2.2. Step 3: 9.7. Rule: after an amendment, curtailment or settlement, use the remeasured deficit and updated rate. Common mistake: using opening figures all year, which gives 10.0.","i":"cdf034b1fe"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"Directors release a pension remeasurement loss to OCI over employees' remaining working lives, and show it as an item that may be recycled. Is this right?","a":"No: it's all in OCI now, and it's never recycled","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: the remeasurement goes to OCI when it arises, and isn't reclassified. Ethics: smoothing can hide the deficit's real movement. Common mistake: following the old corridor-style deferral.","i":"45d7cfbd64"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"Plan assets are 1,200, the obligation is 1,000, and the PV of refunds and reduced contributions is 150. What's recognised?","a":"Asset of 150, with 50 to OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: the surplus is limited by the asset ceiling, and the reduction is part of the remeasurement. Applied: 200 restricted to 150. Common mistake: taking the reduction to profit.","i":"d933ef0aeb"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"A factory closes in 6 months. There are 200 staff. Those who stay to the end get $30,000; those who leave earlier get $10,000. Forty are expected to leave early. What's recognised at once as a termination benefit?","a":"$2.0m","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: the part payable whatever the employee does is the termination benefit; the extra for staying is pay for future service. Applied: 200 × $10,000 = $2.0m now; 160 × $20,000 = $3.2m over the 6 months. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: recognising everything immediately.","i":"b8b4b0cfc5"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#10","x":"example","t":"Staff can carry forward unused holiday. At the year end, unused days would cost $70,000 and are expected to be taken. Sick pay is unused too, and lapses. What's accrued?","a":"$70,000 for holiday only","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: accumulating absences are accrued as earned; non-accumulating ones are recognised when they occur. Common mistake: treating all absences the same way.","i":"0dc9238da7"},{"s":"sbr-09-apply-1","c":"sbr-09-apply-1#11","x":"example","t":"A pension note shows that a one-year increase in life expectancy would add 20% to a deficit. What does this tell investors?","a":"The deficit is exposed to assumptions","d":"Reference: IAS 19 Employee Benefits. Rule: IAS 19 requires sensitivity analysis. Why: it shows the risk in the figures. Users: risk-averse investors may look elsewhere. Common mistake: seeing the disclosure as an error.","i":"3597573fe8"}],"i":"66bf4d4870"}],"i":"af3ac2a95b"}],"i":"4a074886dd"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 10: Share-based payment","ch":10,"d":"Study Text pages 241–262.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"10.1 IFRS 2: equity-settled schemes","s":"sbr-10-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#0","x":"card","t":"A share-based payment is buying goods or services by paying in shares, options or cash based on the share price","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. IFRS 2 makes the expense visible even though no cash leaves.","i":"efbee05848"},{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#1","x":"card","t":"For an equity-settled scheme the entry is Dr expense (or asset), Cr equity","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The credit goes to other components of equity until vesting.","i":"2a4a80d7bb"},{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#2","x":"card","t":"Equity-settled awards to employees are measured at the fair value of the equity instruments at grant date","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Never remeasured afterwards.","i":"e32480028b"},{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#3","x":"card","t":"Equity-settled purchases from non-employees are measured at the fair value of the goods or services received","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Liminal: patent worth $3m recorded at $3m, not 1m shares × $3.50.","i":"38ec840b1f"},{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#4","x":"card","t":"The expense is spread over the vesting period, as services are received","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Grant date = when the parties agree; vesting date = when the holder becomes entitled.","i":"e377ce03b9"},{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#5","x":"card","t":"Each year the cumulative expense is expected number vesting × grant-date FV × time passed, less expense to date","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Estimates of leavers change the number; the fair value stays fixed.","i":"80ee9e5119"},{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#6","x":"card","t":"A market condition (share-price target) is already in the grant-date fair value, so the expense is recognised even if it's not met","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The service condition still matters: leavers reduce the expense.","i":"6e3a3b692b"},{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#7","x":"card","t":"Non-market conditions (sales or profit targets) affect the number of instruments expected to vest","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Blueberry: expected sales growth decides 100, 200 or 300 options each.","i":"1e38f62e8b"},{"s":"sbr-10-1","c":"sbr-10-1#8","x":"card","t":"After vesting, total equity is not adjusted, even if options lapse","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. A transfer within equity to retained earnings is allowed.","i":"9ef6156a8f"}],"i":"49f051a80c"},{"x":"stage","t":"10.2 IFRS 2: cash-settled and other issues","s":"sbr-10-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-10-2","c":"sbr-10-2#0","x":"card","t":"A cash-settled scheme (e.g. share appreciation rights) is recorded as Dr expense, Cr liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. SARs pay cash equal to the rise in share price.","i":"ae47bbdb3b"},{"s":"sbr-10-2","c":"sbr-10-2#1","x":"card","t":"The cash-settled liability is remeasured to fair value at every reporting date until settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Unlike equity-settled, where grant-date value is fixed. Expense is still spread over vesting.","i":"fedc5878fc"},{"s":"sbr-10-2","c":"sbr-10-2#2","x":"card","t":"A SAR's fair value is its intrinsic value plus time value; on exercise it's settled at intrinsic value","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. At expiry intrinsic value equals fair value.","i":"3620d1f61b"},{"s":"sbr-10-2","c":"sbr-10-2#3","x":"card","t":"If the entity chooses how to settle, it's cash-settled only if it has an obligation to settle in cash","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Otherwise treat as equity-settled.","i":"ccf4d6faeb"},{"s":"sbr-10-2","c":"sbr-10-2#4","x":"card","t":"If the employee chooses, the award is a compound instrument: equity part = FV of the share alternative − FV of the cash alternative at grant","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The liability part is remeasured like a cash-settled scheme.","i":"6c3e6b3ec9"},{"s":"sbr-10-2","c":"sbr-10-2#5","x":"card","t":"In a group, the entity receiving the services recognises the expense whichever group entity settles it","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The subsidiary still books the cost.","i":"94809cc09c"},{"s":"sbr-10-2","c":"sbr-10-2#6","x":"card","t":"The Board says equity-settled awards are expenses because the employee service is an asset received and immediately consumed","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Critics say no asset fell and no liability rose, so it's not an expense under the CF; investors support the expense.","i":"b51516310d"}],"i":"3ff2664682"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"10.S Workings in order","s":"sbr-10-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-10-structure-1","c":"sbr-10-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Equity-settled options for employees, each year","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The fair value is fixed at grant date, but the numbers are updated.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Estimate the staff expected to stay until vesting","i":"cf804e4185"},{"x":"step","t":"Multiply by options each and the fair value at grant date","i":"e28d865fdf"},{"x":"step","t":"Multiply by the fraction of the vesting period elapsed","i":"f4e9134445"},{"x":"step","t":"This is the cumulative equity balance","i":"c275b2c40c"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct last year's balance to get the expense","i":"bb3c97db54"}],"i":"50bca73ae1"},{"s":"sbr-10-structure-1","c":"sbr-10-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Cash-settled rights, each year","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The liability is remeasured at every reporting date.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Estimate the staff expected to stay","i":"ff63d56ee9"},{"x":"step","t":"Multiply by rights each and today's fair value","i":"e873de0a28"},{"x":"step","t":"Multiply by the fraction of the vesting period elapsed","i":"c8ad8bb829"},{"x":"step","t":"This is the liability","i":"37c7d0be97"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct last year's liability, adding back any cash paid, to get the expense","i":"a892e85795"}],"i":"2285cbdcf4"},{"s":"sbr-10-structure-1","c":"sbr-10-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"When options are exercised","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. No gain or loss, as it's a transaction with owners.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Receive the exercise price in cash","i":"d9c9a3e519"},{"x":"step","t":"Transfer the balance in other components of equity","i":"42de800731"},{"x":"step","t":"Credit share capital with the nominal value","i":"1143ca4a9d"},{"x":"step","t":"Credit share premium with the rest","i":"22e1feec82"}],"i":"2e28309fdd"},{"s":"sbr-10-structure-1","c":"sbr-10-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Rights after vesting","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Fair value can exceed intrinsic value while time remains.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Pay exercised rights at intrinsic value","i":"9e1ae68fb7"},{"x":"step","t":"Measure remaining rights at fair value","i":"dc62ef1b0b"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare with the opening liability","i":"9a06cfdaee"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the balance to P/L (operating)","i":"793339f11c"},{"x":"step","t":"At the end of the period, clear any balance","i":"482a28c492"}],"i":"c8395d52b5"},{"s":"sbr-10-structure-1","c":"sbr-10-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"A scheme where the employee chooses shares or cash","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. For non-employees, use the fair value of the goods or services received, less the cash alternative.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Treat it as a compound instrument","i":"c0028a1c73"},{"x":"step","t":"Fair value of the equity alternative at grant","i":"2426482989"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct the fair value of the cash alternative at grant","i":"145e20eb05"},{"x":"step","t":"The difference is the equity element, spread over vesting","i":"700a4d4ce2"},{"x":"step","t":"Measure the liability element as cash-settled at each year end","i":"c0fb09676d"}],"i":"dfa500a0fa"}],"i":"5372affb70"}],"i":"7379e09369"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"10.C Side by side","s":"sbr-10-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Fair value fixed at grant date","a":"Equity-settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Not remeasured.","i":"7e23291184"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Remeasured at each reporting date","a":"Cash-settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. A liability at fair value.","i":"3750d7b310"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Credit goes to other components of equity","a":"Equity-settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. No cash outflow.","i":"070c5ad6fe"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Credit goes to liabilities","a":"Cash-settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Future cash.","i":"6680b769be"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Share appreciation rights","a":"Cash-settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Paid in cash, based on the share price.","i":"c8cff0d50e"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Entity may choose, and has a practice of paying cash","a":"Cash-settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. An obligation to settle in cash.","i":"fc22560a48"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Entity may choose, and has no obligation to pay cash","a":"Equity-settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Treated as equity.","i":"1560a94a20"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Share price must reach $5 by vesting","a":"Market condition","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Already in the grant-date fair value.","i":"c57ddcd8b4"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"EPS must grow by 5% a year","a":"Non-market condition","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Affects the number expected to vest.","i":"cdc3f516dc"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Sales volume must rise by 10% a year","a":"Non-market condition","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Not related to the share price.","i":"72335efbb5"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Employee must stay for three years","a":"Service condition","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Affects the number expected to vest.","i":"a740c175f6"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Target missed, but expense is still recognised","a":"Market condition","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Already factored into fair value.","i":"8ddc06a85e"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Target missed, so the expense reverses","a":"Non-market condition","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Nothing is expected to vest.","i":"1b5da12d7e"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Goods bought from a supplier for shares","a":"Fair value of goods received","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Unless it can't be estimated reliably.","i":"23b4faee24"},{"s":"sbr-10-compare-1","c":"sbr-10-compare-1#14","x":"sort","t":"Employees' services","a":"Fair value of shares at grant","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Services are hard to value directly.","i":"b84118b68e"}],"i":"db12c1cf0e"}],"i":"c5a5ad25cf"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"10.W Share-based payment traps","s":"sbr-10-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"No cash leaves, so an equity-settled scheme has no expense.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Services are consumed, and investors support recognising the expense.","i":"3c67e9977e"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Options that vest immediately are expensed in full on grant.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. No further service is required.","i":"ff22bad2f2"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"The fair value of equity-settled options is updated each year.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. It's fixed at grant date.","i":"1d0a0ff62b"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"The number of options expected to vest is updated each year.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. With a catch-up in that year's expense.","i":"a76aac83d6"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"An expense stays recognised if a market condition isn't met.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. As long as service conditions are met.","i":"93fe3fc5c3"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"An expense stays recognised if a profit target isn't met.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. A non-market condition reverses it.","i":"6a53de1f94"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"Equity is adjusted if vested options lapse.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Total equity isn't adjusted after vesting, though a transfer within equity is allowed.","i":"23e7d12e3c"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"A liability for rights after vesting is at fair value.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Until they are exercised.","i":"9232cb7459"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Rights are paid out at their fair value.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. At intrinsic value on exercise.","i":"6a0b31dcbf"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"A subsidiary recognises an expense even if its parent grants the options.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The entity receiving the services recognises it.","i":"288f9f4a5c"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"IFRS 2's expense clearly fits the Framework's definition of an expense for equity schemes.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment; Conceptual Framework. It can be argued it doesn't; the Board says services are an asset received and immediately consumed.","i":"316536b5bc"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"Omitting the expense would raise concerns about stewardship.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Especially for schemes with directors.","i":"369471e283"},{"s":"sbr-10-watch-1","c":"sbr-10-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"Transactions with non-employees are always valued from the shares.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. They use the fair value of goods or services received if reliable.","i":"da6f380181"}],"i":"6e517a79f1"}],"i":"4950f4711c"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"10.A Share-based payment scenarios","s":"sbr-10-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Lane buys a patent worth $2m by issuing 500,000 shares worth $5 each. The year ends six months later, and the patent has a 4-year life. What are its cost and the amortisation?","a":"$2.0m and $0.25m","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: for goods from non-employees, use the fair value of the goods if it's reliable. Applied: 2m; 2m / 4 × 6/12 = 0.25m. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: using the share value.","i":"acad822d46"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"A company grants 50 options each to 200 staff, with fair value $12 and a three-year service condition. At the end of year 1, 20% are expected to leave overall; at the end of year 2, 15%. What's the expense for year 2?","a":"$36,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Step 1 (year 1): 160 × 50 × 12 × 1/3 = 32,000. Step 2 (year 2): 170 × 50 × 12 × 2/3 = 68,000. Step 3: 68,000 − 32,000 = 36,000, in operating. Common mistake: stopping at the cumulative balance.","i":"fc644a7e42"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A company granted 50 options each to 200 staff, fair value $12, with a three-year service condition; the cumulative expense at the end of year 2 was $68,000. By the end of year 3, 25 staff have left in total. What's the expense for year 3?","a":"$37,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: at vesting, use the number that actually vest. Applied: 175 × 50 × 12 = 105,000; 105,000 − 68,000 = 37,000. Common mistake: keeping the estimate.","i":"8bb5abb1ee"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"100 staff get 40 options each, worth $2, vesting in two years if still employed and the share price passes $5. It now looks unlikely. 20 staff in total are expected to leave. What is the year 1 expense?","a":"$3,200","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: market conditions are in the grant-date fair value, so they don't stop the expense. Applied: 80 × 40 × 2 × 1/2. Users: profit reflects services received, whatever the share price. Common mistake: reversing because the target looks unlikely.","i":"c61dae68d3"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Options vest only if EPS grows 5% a year. Halfway through, the growth is clearly out of reach. What happens to the expense recognised so far?","a":"It's reversed","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: non-market conditions affect the number expected to vest. Applied: none are expected now, so the cumulative figure is nil. Common mistake: treating it as a market condition.","i":"15f88e1d47"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"A scheme's options were recognised at $0.3m in equity. All 1m options are exercised at $2, with a $1 nominal share price. What goes to share premium?","a":"$1.3m","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: on exercise, the cash and the equity balance move to capital. Applied: Dr Cash 2.0, Dr Other components of equity 0.3; Cr Share capital 1.0; Cr Share premium 1.3. Common mistake: taking a gain to P/L.","i":"625d042db8"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"Vested options recognised at $0.3m in equity lapse unexercised, because the share price stayed below the $2 exercise price. What happens to the $0.3m?","a":"It stays in equity; it may move to retained earnings","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: no adjustment to total equity after vesting. Why: services were received regardless. Common mistake: reversing the expense.","i":"b5641d35b4"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"100 rights each go to 200 staff, vesting over three years. At the end of year 2, 170 staff are expected to vest, with a fair value of $12; year 1's liability was $56,667. What's the year 2 expense?","a":"$79,333","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Step 1 (liability): 170 × 100 × 12 × 2/3 = 136,000. Step 2: 136,000 − 56,667 = 79,333. P/L category: Operating. Users: the liability moves with the share price, adding volatility. Common mistake: using the grant-date value.","i":"0117ec2b85"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"After vesting, a liability of $70,000 covers 10,000 rights. 6,000 are exercised at an intrinsic value of $7; the remaining 4,000 have a fair value of $8 at the year end. What's the expense?","a":"$4,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Step 1 (cash paid): 6,000 × 7 = 42,000. Step 2 (closing): 4,000 × 8 = 32,000. Step 3: 32,000 + 42,000 − 70,000 = 4,000. Common mistake: ignoring the time value left in the remaining rights.","i":"374ce4d87e"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"A company can settle awards in cash or shares and has always paid cash. How's the scheme treated?","a":"Cash-settled","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: with an obligation to pay cash, it's cash-settled. Applied: a practice creates that obligation. Common mistake: looking only at the legal choice.","i":"7c46aa64dd"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#10","x":"example","t":"Employees may take 1,000 options worth $10 each or cash equal to 900 shares, worth $10 each at grant. What's the equity element per employee at grant?","a":"$1,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: with employees, equity is the fair value of the equity alternative less the fair value of the cash alternative. Applied: 10,000 − 9,000. The liability element is remeasured as cash-settled. Common mistake: treating the whole as a liability.","i":"c8ee977ab5"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#11","x":"example","t":"A parent grants its shares to staff of its subsidiary. Who recognises the expense in its own statements?","a":"The subsidiary","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: the entity receiving the services recognises the expense, whoever settles it. Users: the subsidiary's profit reflects its true costs. Common mistake: following who issues the shares.","i":"dfd0c6944b"},{"s":"sbr-10-apply-1","c":"sbr-10-apply-1#12","x":"example","t":"A finance director leaves out a large directors' option scheme because 'no cash has moved'. How should this be seen?","a":"Non-compliant, with an ethical issue","d":"Reference: IFRS 2 Share-based Payment; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Rule: IFRS 2 requires the expense. Ethics: directors benefit from a higher profit, which is a self-interest threat. Users: without it, comparability and stewardship suffer. Common mistake: accepting the 'no cash' argument.","i":"eefefec556"}],"i":"3d39e049cf"}],"i":"318d4b73f8"}],"i":"b203562184"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 11: Events after the reporting period, provisions and contingencies","ch":11,"d":"Study Text pages 263–282.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"11.1 IAS 10 Events after the reporting period","s":"sbr-11-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-11-1","c":"sbr-11-1#0","x":"card","t":"Events after the reporting period happen between the reporting date and the date the statements are authorised for issue","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Statements reflect conditions at the reporting date.","i":"b38bac338b"},{"s":"sbr-11-1","c":"sbr-11-1#1","x":"card","t":"An adjusting event gives evidence of conditions that existed at the reporting date","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. E.g. inventory sold below cost, customer insolvency, fraud found, court case settled.","i":"be828f41eb"},{"s":"sbr-11-1","c":"sbr-11-1#2","x":"card","t":"A non-adjusting event reflects conditions that arose after the reporting date","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. E.g. a fire, a big acquisition, a restructuring announced, FX moves, dividends declared.","i":"a37e027340"},{"s":"sbr-11-1","c":"sbr-11-1#3","x":"card","t":"Material non-adjusting events require disclosure of their nature and estimated financial effect","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. The numbers in the statements aren't changed.","i":"4ec35c9946"},{"s":"sbr-11-1","c":"sbr-11-1#4","x":"card","t":"If after the year end management decides to liquidate, the statements are not prepared on a going concern basis","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. The one exception to 'conditions at the reporting date': use the break-up basis and disclose it.","i":"acae8f551f"}],"i":"16f6e4ca53"},{"x":"stage","t":"11.2 IAS 37 Provisions and contingencies","s":"sbr-11-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-11-2","c":"sbr-11-2#0","x":"card","t":"A provision is a liability of uncertain timing or amount","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. IAS 37 still uses the older liability definition.","i":"4defbf102a"},{"s":"sbr-11-2","c":"sbr-11-2#1","x":"card","t":"A provision is recognised when there's a present obligation from a past event, a probable outflow and a reliable estimate","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Without all three, at most disclose.","i":"7cee866a9d"},{"s":"sbr-11-2","c":"sbr-11-2#2","x":"card","t":"A constructive obligation comes from past practice or published policies creating a valid expectation","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. A legal obligation comes from a contract or law.","i":"b6f8ad5a24"},{"s":"sbr-11-2","c":"sbr-11-2#3","x":"card","t":"A single obligation is measured at the most likely amount; a large population at the expected value","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Use judgement, experience and experts. Discount if material at a pre-tax, risk-specific rate.","i":"beecb6f071"},{"s":"sbr-11-2","c":"sbr-11-2#4","x":"card","t":"The unwinding of the discount on a provision is an interest expense in financing","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Provisions are remeasured to the best estimate at each reporting date.","i":"fe3b6b4970"},{"s":"sbr-11-2","c":"sbr-11-2#5","x":"card","t":"A provision may be used only for the purpose it was made for, and is reversed if no longer probable","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. No cookie-jar provisions for smoothing profit.","i":"d16f9b643c"},{"s":"sbr-11-2","c":"sbr-11-2#6","x":"card","t":"A contingent liability is a possible obligation, or a present one that isn't probable or measurable; it is disclosed unless remote","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. If an outflow becomes probable, it becomes a provision.","i":"5b37bb66f1"},{"s":"sbr-11-2","c":"sbr-11-2#7","x":"card","t":"A contingent asset is disclosed if an inflow is probable; it's recognised only when virtually certain (then it's no longer contingent)","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Insurance claims after a disaster are a typical case.","i":"e8ed656605"}],"i":"62a8c12b9c"},{"x":"stage","t":"11.3 IAS 37 in specific situations","s":"sbr-11-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#0","x":"card","t":"No provision is made for future operating losses because they relate to future events and can be avoided","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Expected losses are an impairment indicator instead (IAS 36).","i":"e951a4408b"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#1","x":"card","t":"An onerous contract is one where unavoidable costs exceed the benefits expected from it","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Provide for the lower of the cost of fulfilling and the cost of exiting (penalties).","i":"929bf4ac0e"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#2","x":"card","t":"The cost of fulfilling an onerous contract includes incremental costs and an allocation of other directly related costs (e.g. depreciation)","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Impair any assets dedicated to the contract first.","i":"6dc362fc89"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#3","x":"card","t":"No provision is made for future repairs or overhauls, even if legally required, because they can be avoided by selling the asset","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Danboy: repairs needed at year end are expensed when done.","i":"4786f34bff"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#4","x":"card","t":"At the reporting date, a new law requiring smoke filters by later in the year gives no provision for fitting costs (no past obligating event yet)","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. The obligation arises only if the deadline passes without compliance (e.g. fines).","i":"0820fe2373"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#5","x":"card","t":"An environmental provision is recognised only for damage already caused and a legal or constructive obligation to fix it","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Oil rig: retrospective law virtually certain to be enacted → provide now.","i":"c9d5c545a8"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#6","x":"card","t":"A decommissioning provision is recognised in full when the obligation arises, discounted, with the same amount added to the asset's cost","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Scrubber: PV of $3m in 3 years at 10% added to PPE and depreciated; interest unwinds yearly.","i":"993be84543"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#7","x":"card","t":"A restructuring provision needs a detailed formal plan and a valid expectation (announced or started) at the reporting date","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. A board decision alone is not enough. Shane scheme 1 (announced in June) → no provision.","i":"63d168353a"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#8","x":"card","t":"A restructuring provision includes only direct costs that are necessary and not related to ongoing activities (e.g. redundancies)","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Excluded: retraining, relocation, marketing, new systems, future losses and asset sale profits.","i":"988888bc5b"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#9","x":"card","t":"If the actual restructuring cost is known before approval, use it, because it's an adjusting event","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Delta: provide the actual $20m redundancy cost.","i":"969f7719b5"},{"s":"sbr-11-3","c":"sbr-11-3#10","x":"card","t":"IAS 37 is criticised for judgement, inconsistency with IFRS 9 on 'probable', unclear best estimates and risk adjustments, and the undefined term 'virtually certain'","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Also: mismatched timing between the two parties, and contradictory guidance on obligating events.","i":"51fb3cc153"}],"i":"674e00e376"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"11.S Decisions in order","s":"sbr-11-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-11-structure-1","c":"sbr-11-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Should a provision be recognised?","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. An obligation is something the entity can't avoid.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Is there a present obligation, legal or constructive?","i":"27667b2987"},{"x":"step","t":"Did it arise from a past event?","i":"8aa25c5ed4"},{"x":"step","t":"Is an outflow probable?","i":"06fcbb3195"},{"x":"step","t":"Can it be estimated reliably?","i":"e21439fd82"},{"x":"step","t":"If all yes, recognise a provision","i":"5c266784a5"},{"x":"step","t":"If not, consider a contingent liability","i":"f44bbabec1"}],"i":"c96cf46ebf"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-1","c":"sbr-11-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Measuring a provision","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Judgement is supported by experience and experts. Not part of it: Always use the highest possible outflow, to be prudent; Discount at the entity's post-tax borrowing rate.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Single obligation: most likely amount","i":"4467f67139"},{"x":"step","t":"Large population: expected value","i":"2c7edcb47b"},{"x":"step","t":"Discount if the time value is material","i":"de3d59c932"},{"x":"step","t":"Use a pre-tax, risk-specific rate","i":"38488ea476"},{"x":"step","t":"Unwind the discount through financing","i":"6b57aaf468"},{"x":"step","t":"Remeasure at each reporting date","i":"49a868f143"}],"any":true,"i":"8f2cbb790e"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-1","c":"sbr-11-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"A restructuring provision","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. A board decision alone isn't enough.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Is there a detailed formal plan?","i":"2619819fc6"},{"x":"step","t":"Has it been announced, or has it started, by the reporting date?","i":"e1365ea1ca"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, a constructive obligation exists","i":"ea2378826f"},{"x":"step","t":"Include only direct costs not tied to ongoing activities","i":"5b4014f0a8"},{"x":"step","t":"Leave out retraining, marketing, new systems and future losses","i":"9b29d830fe"}],"i":"7585564de1"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-1","c":"sbr-11-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Clean-up costs when damage comes with building the site","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Only damage already done creates an obligation.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify the obligation, legal or constructive","i":"f1af656f4d"},{"x":"step","t":"Discount the full cost to present value","i":"f3bf5f4d38"},{"x":"step","t":"Credit the provision","i":"48c3742cb5"},{"x":"step","t":"Debit the asset, if it brings future benefits","i":"ead5a231ea"},{"x":"step","t":"Depreciate the asset and unwind the discount","i":"415cdd71d9"}],"i":"b31b41d20d"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-1","c":"sbr-11-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"An onerous contract","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Future operating losses in general are never provided for. Not part of it: Provide for the higher of the two costs; Provide for the expected future operating losses of the business.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Check for impairment of assets bought for the contract","i":"ec6902a02d"},{"x":"step","t":"Find the unavoidable cost of fulfilling it, net of benefits","i":"219a049944"},{"x":"step","t":"Find the cost of terminating it","i":"32862b8986"},{"x":"step","t":"Provide for the lower","i":"364d94e5b1"},{"x":"step","t":"Fulfilment costs include a share of other necessary costs, like depreciation","i":"3690a4ea6c"}],"any":true,"i":"1c864f6242"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-1","c":"sbr-11-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"An event after the reporting date","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Going concern is the exception to the 'conditions at the reporting date' rule.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Did it happen before the statements were authorised?","i":"b3d9c8757e"},{"x":"step","t":"Does it give evidence of conditions at the reporting date?","i":"1e31a3adb8"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, adjust","i":"3e1f51a469"},{"x":"step","t":"If not, disclose it if material","i":"c54e18023c"},{"x":"step","t":"If liquidation is now expected, use the break-up basis","i":"bbafc5301a"}],"i":"cc989e2832"}],"i":"bda69d058e"},{"x":"stage","t":"11.S2 What is recognised?","s":"sbr-11-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"A court case the entity will probably lose, with a good estimate","a":"Provision","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. All three tests are met.","i":"a15b55a5ab"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"A published promise to clean up all its sites, with damage done","a":"Provision","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. A constructive obligation.","i":"514bd4a9bd"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"A retrospective clean-up law, virtually certain to pass","a":"Provision","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Treated as an obligation.","i":"1864ffa28a"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"A court case the entity will probably win","a":"Contingent liability: disclose","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. A possible obligation, disclosed unless remote.","i":"6e89a00ae8"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"A present obligation that can't be estimated reliably","a":"Contingent liability: disclose","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Very rare.","i":"ca064d584c"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"A claim whose chance of succeeding is remote","a":"Nothing","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. No disclosure either.","i":"9046a2f1a4"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Next year's expected losses at a shop","a":"Nothing","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Not a past event, and they can be avoided.","i":"2611e5bf92"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Next year's legally required aircraft overhaul","a":"Nothing","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. It could be avoided by selling the aircraft.","i":"d5ddeca322"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"A law requiring filters by December, with none fitted at June","a":"Nothing","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. There's no obligation at the reporting date.","i":"5dcfbc89df"},{"s":"sbr-11-structure-2","c":"sbr-11-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"A board decision to close a factory, not yet announced","a":"Nothing","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. There's no constructive obligation.","i":"44f97da50a"}],"i":"62a0e51593"}],"i":"872fac6486"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"11.C Side by side","s":"sbr-11-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"A major customer goes bankrupt after the year end","a":"Adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Confirms the receivable was impaired.","i":"de416459d8"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Inventory is sold below cost after the year end","a":"Adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Evidence of net realisable value.","i":"9edde1f418"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"A court case is settled after the year end","a":"Adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Confirms the obligation at the reporting date.","i":"b835e79dff"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"A fraud from before the year end is discovered","a":"Adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. The statements were wrong.","i":"75c6d20a95"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"A factory burns down after the year end","a":"Non-adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. It happened afterwards.","i":"feba2487b5"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Equity dividends are proposed after the year end","a":"Non-adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. No obligation at the reporting date.","i":"d37aea25a7"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Exchange rates move sharply after the year end","a":"Non-adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. A later condition.","i":"ec4738a0fb"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"A major acquisition is made after the year end","a":"Non-adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Disclosed.","i":"e4233c6653"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Announcing a restructuring after the year end","a":"Non-adjusting","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Disclosed.","i":"455e627f3d"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Asset whose inflow is virtually certain","a":"Recognise","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. No longer contingent.","i":"d2f017c161"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Asset whose inflow is probable","a":"Disclose","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Contingent asset.","i":"ba55b79108"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Asset whose inflow is only possible","a":"Ignore","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Not disclosed.","i":"9be7d85141"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Redundancy payments in a closure","a":"In a restructuring provision","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Direct and not tied to ongoing activities.","i":"cf5ec7cd00"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Retraining staff who remain","a":"Not in a restructuring provision","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Tied to ongoing activities.","i":"fc3286adf6"},{"s":"sbr-11-compare-1","c":"sbr-11-compare-1#14","x":"sort","t":"Profit on selling a closed factory's machines","a":"Not in a restructuring provision","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Not netted off.","i":"b1d9ee184c"}],"i":"2c3aaee191"}],"i":"d261f50ffa"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"11.W Provision traps","s":"sbr-11-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"A constructive obligation can arise from published policies.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. If it creates a valid expectation.","i":"5d52fbf125"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"A board minute to restructure creates a constructive obligation.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Needs a detailed plan and a valid expectation among those affected.","i":"b381434576"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"A provision may be used for any cost that comes along.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Only for what it was originally recognised for.","i":"464e8f160b"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"A provision is reversed if the outflow is no longer probable.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. It's reassessed at each reporting date.","i":"6aaa996317"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"Causing environmental damage creates an obligation by itself.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. It takes a law or a constructive obligation.","i":"fb06ab871a"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"Future clean-up of damage not yet done is provided for now.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. The entity could stop operating.","i":"6944a5ddf8"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"A decommissioning provision can create an asset as well.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. If it brings future economic benefits.","i":"ea748ab47e"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Repairs required by law are always provided for.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. The entity could sell the asset and avoid them.","i":"321116e6b7"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"An expectation of future losses is a sign of possible impairment.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Do an IAS 36 review.","i":"8d15395015"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Actual restructuring costs known after the year end can be used.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. They're an adjusting event for measurement.","i":"a1b33fc43b"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"Proposed dividends are recorded as a liability at the year end.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. They're non-adjusting.","i":"00c0a708bb"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"Going concern doubts arising after the year end can change the basis of preparation.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. An exception to the normal rule.","i":"88044f5779"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"Contingent liabilities are always disclosed.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Not if an outflow is remote.","i":"8f74fe93eb"},{"s":"sbr-11-watch-1","c":"sbr-11-watch-1#13","x":"trap","t":"IAS 37 explains clearly how to apply risk adjustments.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. A stated criticism: it doesn't explain when or how.","i":"9652536fff"}],"i":"aa9a1baead"}],"i":"e254d45e77"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"11.A Provision scenarios","s":"sbr-11-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Ferro sells 400 machines with a warranty on the last day of the year. Past experience suggests 15% will need repairs averaging $60. What's the provision?","a":"$3,600","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: for a large population, use an expected value. Applied: 400 × 15% × $60. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: using a most likely amount, which would be nil.","i":"9965de548a"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Ness has spent $8m on a site and is known for restoring land. It expects $4m of clean-up in 4 years. The rate is 10%. What's the provision on day one, and the year 1 unwind?","a":"$2.73m and $0.27m","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: full cost at present value, as soon as the obligation arises. Applied: 4 / 1.1⁴ = 2.73; the asset is 10.73, depreciated over 4 years; unwinding 10% × 2.73 = 0.27. P/L category: Financing, for the unwinding. Common mistake: building the provision up each year.","i":"1ff93cd411"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A shop will lose $50,000 next year. Another needs $10,000 of repairs, which are done after the year end for $12,000. What's provided?","a":"Nothing","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Rule: no provision for future losses or for repairs that could be avoided. Applied: consider impairment of the shop instead. Common mistake: treating the repair after the year end as adjusting.","i":"a02cc68f70"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"By law, an entity must fit filters by 31 December. At its 30 June year end, it hasn't done so. Is a provision needed?","a":"No: no obligating event yet","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: at the reporting date there's no obligation, as the entity could still avoid the cost by changing how it operates. Common mistake: taking the law as the obligating event.","i":"9a5861b4fa"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A contract's fulfilment would cost $500,000 and bring in $300,000. Cancelling means a $150,000 penalty. What's the provision?","a":"$150,000","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: an onerous contract is provided at the lower of the net cost of fulfilling it and the cost of terminating it. Applied: 200,000 against 150,000. First, test any dedicated assets for impairment. Common mistake: providing for the whole cost.","i":"f632372ac8"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"In January, a board approves two closures for a March year end. Plant A will be announced in June. For Plant B, staff talks began in March; costs are $60,000 after $140,000 of profit on machines. What is provided?","a":"Nil for A; $200,000 for B","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: a constructive obligation needs a detailed plan and a valid expectation by the reporting date. Applied: A has only a board decision. B's staff talks create the expectation; provide gross, as profit on disposal is left out. Common mistake: netting the profit.","i":"3a672cc392"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"A division's closure is decided on 30 June and announced on 15 July, with a 31 July year end. It closes on 31 August, before approval. Redundancy was estimated at $30m and turned out at $28m; retraining is $12m; August's operating loss is $15m; property profit is $5m. What's the provision?","a":"$28m","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: only direct costs not tied to ongoing activities; actual amounts known before approval are used. Applied: redundancy only, at $28m. Common mistake: adding retraining and operating losses, or netting the profit.","i":"12fac544a1"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"An oil rig in a country with no clean-up law has been causing damage for years. The entity cleans up only when forced. A retrospective law is virtually certain. What's recognised?","a":"A provision, for damage already done","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: a law virtually certain to be passed, with retrospective effect, creates an obligation. Applied: only for past damage. Common mistake: waiting for enactment, or including future damage.","i":"10a281516b"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"Two weeks after the year end, a key customer who owed $1m at the year end goes into liquidation. What's the treatment?","a":"Adjust the receivable","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Rule: an event giving evidence of conditions at the reporting date adjusts. Applied: the bankruptcy confirms that the balance was impaired. Common mistake: looking only at when the event happened.","i":"698cbd21f2"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"A warehouse holding uninsured inventory burns down after the year end. Inventory lost is material. What's the treatment?","a":"Disclose the event and its effect","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period. Rule: non-adjusting events are disclosed if material. Users: they need to know about future effects. Common mistake: adjusting because the loss is big.","i":"d172b27501"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#10","x":"example","t":"After the year end, management decides to liquidate the company. On what basis are the statements prepared?","a":"A break-up basis, disclosed","d":"Reference: IAS 10 Events after the Reporting Period; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: going concern is the exception: if it's no longer appropriate, the basis changes. Common mistake: following the 'conditions at the reporting date' rule here.","i":"8cb8a0d1b5"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#11","x":"example","t":"A company expects to recover $2m from a lawsuit; lawyers call it probable. How's this treated?","a":"Disclose a contingent asset","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: contingent assets aren't recognised unless virtually certain, and are disclosed if probable. Common mistake: treating gains and losses alike. Users: the prudence is deliberate, but timing can differ between the two parties.","i":"d5f3028ce9"},{"s":"sbr-11-apply-1","c":"sbr-11-apply-1#12","x":"example","t":"A finance director increases a warranty provision by far more than experience suggests in a record year, saying 'we may need it in a bad year'. What's the issue?","a":"Bias: this smooths profit","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Rule: provisions are a best estimate, and can't be used for other purposes. Ethics: objectivity and integrity are at risk. Users: performance would be misstated in both years. Common mistake: calling it prudence.","i":"943e074ba4"}],"i":"7cf55c3f9b"}],"i":"7067c21a51"}],"i":"4578dd8d3b"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 12: Financial instruments","ch":12,"d":"Study Text pages 283–346.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"12.1 Definitions and debt vs equity","s":"sbr-12-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#0","x":"card","t":"A financial instrument is a contract that gives one entity a financial asset and another a financial liability or equity instrument","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation.","i":"0cc7215a0a"},{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#1","x":"card","t":"A financial asset is cash, another entity's equity, a right to receive cash or a financial asset, a favourable exchange right, or a contract to receive a variable number of own shares","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. E.g. receivables, shares held, loans made.","i":"a1c237d798"},{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#2","x":"card","t":"A financial liability is an obligation to deliver cash or a financial asset, an unfavourable exchange, or a contract to deliver a variable number of own shares","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Settling with 'shares worth $3m' is like paying $3m: it's a liability.","i":"78fca0786b"},{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#3","x":"card","t":"An equity instrument is a contract evidencing a residual interest in assets after all liabilities","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. No obligation to deliver cash.","i":"68ecaf4093"},{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#4","x":"card","t":"IAS 32 covers presentation, IFRS 7 covers disclosures, and IFRS 9 covers recognition, measurement and hedge accounting","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 7 Financial Instruments: Disclosures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Know which standard answers which question.","i":"aab213088f"},{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#5","x":"card","t":"Instruments are classified as liability or equity by their substance, at initial recognition","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Coasters: redeem for a fixed 3m shares → equity; non-mandatory with discretionary dividends → equity.","i":"a519c2dd75"},{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#6","x":"card","t":"Dividends on preference shares classed as liabilities are interest expense in P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Equity dividends go through the SOCE.","i":"44dabb6a34"},{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#7","x":"card","t":"A financial asset and liability are offset only with a legally enforceable right to set off and the intention to settle net or simultaneously","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation.","i":"bca71dcc9c"},{"s":"sbr-12-1","c":"sbr-12-1#8","x":"card","t":"Classifying an instrument as a liability rather than equity raises gearing and puts its cost in P/L, cutting EPS","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. That's why directors are tempted to call debt 'equity'.","i":"b44dc96ae7"}],"i":"72cd9b5515"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.2 Financial liabilities and compound instruments","s":"sbr-12-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#0","x":"card","t":"A financial liability is initially recognised at fair value, with transaction costs deducted (or expensed if at FVPL)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. E.g. 50,000 − 16% discount − 2,000 costs = 40,000.","i":"bc230883b9"},{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#1","x":"card","t":"Most financial liabilities are later measured at amortised cost using the effective interest rate","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Opening + interest at EIR − cash paid = closing. Interest is financing.","i":"c6aec8bacf"},{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#2","x":"card","t":"The effective interest rate spreads all costs (coupons, discount, premium, fees) over the life","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. It's the IRR. James: total cost 27,111 spread at 12%.","i":"84df7bdc4e"},{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#3","x":"card","t":"Liabilities measured at FVPL are those held for trading, derivatives with negative value, and those designated to remove an accounting mismatch","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. McGrath: rates rise to 10% → fair value falls → gain in P/L.","i":"07245e851e"},{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#4","x":"card","t":"For a liability designated at FVPL, the change from own credit risk goes to OCI; the rest to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Bean: 30m fall, 10m from own credit → 10m OCI, 20m P/L.","i":"d07dc013a1"},{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#5","x":"card","t":"A compound instrument (e.g. a convertible bond) is split into a liability and an equity component","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Split at issue only; never revisited.","i":"803a0d3a50"},{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#6","x":"card","t":"The liability component of a convertible is the PV of payments at the rate for similar debt without conversion","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Daniels: 44,291.9 liability; equity = 50,000 − 44,291.9 = 5,708.1.","i":"f7f7c5f728"},{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#7","x":"card","t":"Interest on the liability component is charged at the market rate for non-convertible debt, not the coupon","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. The liability grows back to par by redemption.","i":"00ddf3d9ba"},{"s":"sbr-12-2","c":"sbr-12-2#8","x":"card","t":"On conversion, the liability and equity component are transferred to share capital and share premium (no gain or loss)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. If not converted, the liability is repaid and the equity part stays in equity.","i":"25810e0695"}],"i":"cfb3535f77"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.3 Financial assets: equity investments","s":"sbr-12-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-3","c":"sbr-12-3#0","x":"card","t":"A financial asset is recognised when the entity becomes party to the contract's provisions","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments.","i":"f811f6799c"},{"s":"sbr-12-3","c":"sbr-12-3#1","x":"card","t":"Forwards and options are recognised on the commitment date, not on delivery","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. A plain purchase order isn't recognised until one party performs.","i":"2973aad3f6"},{"s":"sbr-12-3","c":"sbr-12-3#2","x":"card","t":"Equity investments are normally measured at FVPL, with transaction costs expensed","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Gains and losses go to P/L every year.","i":"4774fbcaef"},{"s":"sbr-12-3","c":"sbr-12-3#3","x":"card","t":"An equity investment may be designated FVOCI if it's not held for trading and the choice is made irrevocably at initial recognition","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Measured at fair value plus transaction costs.","i":"6eee2af03d"},{"s":"sbr-12-3","c":"sbr-12-3#4","x":"card","t":"FVOCI equity gains and losses stay in OCI and are never recycled, even on disposal","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Jones: sold for 70 → revalue to 70 first, gains stay in equity.","i":"4e12f30afc"},{"s":"sbr-12-3","c":"sbr-12-3#5","x":"card","t":"Designating short-term trading shares as FVOCI to hide volatility is not allowed, and an ethical breach","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Americano: breaches objectivity and integrity.","i":"d8023f320a"}],"i":"ab4b136fc5"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.4 Financial assets: debt investments","s":"sbr-12-4","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#0","x":"card","t":"A debt asset is at amortised cost if the business model is to collect cash flows and the cash flows are solely principal and interest (SPPI)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Both tests must be met.","i":"8234fb5651"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#1","x":"card","t":"Occasional sales when cash runs short don't break a 'hold to collect' model","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Paloma: bond at amortised cost.","i":"f530a37353"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#2","x":"card","t":"A convertible bond held as an asset fails SPPI because its return includes the conversion option","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. So it's measured at FVPL.","i":"ad25c8a333"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#3","x":"card","t":"A debt asset is at FVOCI if the business model is both collecting and selling","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Interest at EIR in P/L; fair value changes in OCI, recycled on disposal.","i":"0e81e11ea4"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#4","x":"card","t":"Debt assets that are neither amortised cost nor FVOCI are at FVPL, with costs expensed","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. E.g. bonds traded in the short term.","i":"1870732b69"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#5","x":"card","t":"Debt assets are reclassified only when the business model for managing them changes","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rare in practice.","i":"485e81685a"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#6","x":"card","t":"At amortised cost or FVOCI, debt assets start at fair value plus transaction costs","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Tokyo: 95,000 + 2,000 = 97,000, interest at 8%.","i":"d54537f47f"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#7","x":"card","t":"An interest-free loan to a supplier is first recognised at the PV of its repayment at the market rate","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The difference is expensed (not a loan) and then unwinds as interest income.","i":"0a95086ff6"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#8","x":"card","t":"The business model drives measurement because fair value predicts proceeds from selling, amortised cost the contractual flows","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; Conceptual Framework. Relevance under the CF.","i":"686fd4035f"},{"s":"sbr-12-4","c":"sbr-12-4#9","x":"card","t":"Income from financial assets usually goes in investing; costs of finance-only liabilities go in financing (operating for banks and investment businesses)","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. IFRS 18 categories apply.","i":"f5e561dabb"}],"i":"05d69ecd8f"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.5 Impairment: expected credit losses","s":"sbr-12-5","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#0","x":"card","t":"Loss allowances are needed for debt assets at amortised cost and FVOCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Not for FVPL assets or equity investments.","i":"9629c91f5b"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#1","x":"card","t":"If credit risk hasn't increased significantly, the allowance is 12-month expected credit losses","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Stage 1. Interest on the gross carrying amount.","i":"6c34d99a40"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#2","x":"card","t":"If credit risk has increased significantly, the allowance is lifetime expected credit losses","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Stage 2. Still interest on the gross amount.","i":"43f698412b"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#3","x":"card","t":"A credit loss is the PV of the difference between contractual cash flows and expected cash flows","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Expected credit losses = probability-weighted credit losses.","i":"cbb47844af"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#4","x":"card","t":"12-month ECL are the lifetime losses from defaults possible within 12 months after the reporting date","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Napa: 0.5% × PV of loss if default.","i":"1a6fe07943"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#5","x":"card","t":"A significant increase in credit risk compares the risk of default now with the risk at initial recognition, using forward-looking information","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Tahoe: falling sales and a bond-price slump → lifetime ECL.","i":"e19c06a315"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#6","x":"card","t":"Credit risk is presumed to have risen significantly if payments are more than 30 days overdue","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. And presumed not to have risen if credit risk is low at the reporting date.","i":"a6a6cde959"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#7","x":"card","t":"ECL must be unbiased, probability-weighted, reflect time value and use past, current and forecast information","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. A provision matrix by days overdue is allowed (Janne).","i":"5de6d337be"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#8","x":"card","t":"Credit-impaired signs: significant financial difficulty, breach or default, concessions, and probable bankruptcy","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Eve: Fern in difficulty → credit-impaired.","i":"84f8a324d0"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#9","x":"card","t":"For a credit-impaired asset, the allowance is the gross amount less the PV of expected cash flows at the original effective rate","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Interest is then on the net amount (stage 3). Use the original rate, not today's market rate.","i":"0c23178258"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#10","x":"card","t":"Purchased or originated credit-impaired assets use a credit-adjusted effective rate; allowance only for later changes","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Losses expected at purchase are already in the rate.","i":"4dbb56929f"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#11","x":"card","t":"For FVOCI debt, the loss allowance is charged to P/L but held in OCI, not deducted from the asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The asset stays at fair value.","i":"9c6a16eebc"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#12","x":"card","t":"For trade receivables without a financing component, the allowance is always lifetime ECL (simplified approach)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. With financing, and for lease receivables, it's a policy choice.","i":"bb66727b9d"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#13","x":"card","t":"Changes in the loss allowance go to P/L (operating for trade receivables, investing for investments)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Reductions are gains when risk falls.","i":"8af73ba49a"},{"s":"sbr-12-5","c":"sbr-12-5#14","x":"card","t":"A loss allowance on a debt asset is first set up at initial recognition, at 12-month ECL, and then reassessed at each reporting date","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Napa: an $800 allowance from day one rises to $2,934, so the P/L charge is only the increase of $2,134 (investing category for an investment).","i":"64d9b4972f"}],"i":"6ae12f3f82"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.6 Derecognition and modification","s":"sbr-12-6","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-6","c":"sbr-12-6#0","x":"card","t":"A financial asset is derecognised when the rights expire or it's transferred with substantially all risks and rewards","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Ming receivable 1: no recourse → derecognise, 20,000 loss.","i":"48da15279f"},{"s":"sbr-12-6","c":"sbr-12-6#1","x":"card","t":"If the seller keeps substantially all risks and rewards, it keeps the asset and records a loan","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Ming receivable 2: with recourse → keep 100,000 and a 70,000 loan.","i":"7794508307"},{"s":"sbr-12-6","c":"sbr-12-6#2","x":"card","t":"Shares sold with a right to buy back at fair value are derecognised (risks and rewards passed)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Case: the directors' plan to keep them on the SoFP is wrong.","i":"14ed81cc7b"},{"s":"sbr-12-6","c":"sbr-12-6#3","x":"card","t":"A financial liability is derecognised when the obligation is discharged, cancelled or expires","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The gain or loss goes to P/L (financing).","i":"3dc41ed2f7"},{"s":"sbr-12-6","c":"sbr-12-6#4","x":"card","t":"On disposal, FVOCI equity gains stay in equity, while FVOCI debt gains are recycled to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Revalue to fair value just before disposal.","i":"851a2eaff6"},{"s":"sbr-12-6","c":"sbr-12-6#5","x":"card","t":"A renegotiated loan is 'substantially different' if the PV of new cash flows (with fees) at the original EIR differs by 10% or more from the PV of the old","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Ariel: 11.1% → extinguish; 9.1% → modify.","i":"63c611b118"},{"s":"sbr-12-6","c":"sbr-12-6#6","x":"card","t":"If substantially different, the old loan is derecognised, the new one recognised at fair value, and fees and the difference go to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Fees are expensed (financing).","i":"f5bcad8a06"},{"s":"sbr-12-6","c":"sbr-12-6#7","x":"card","t":"If not substantially different, the loan is restated to the PV of new flows at the original EIR, fees deducted","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Difference goes to P/L; the original EIR continues.","i":"d46599dc74"}],"i":"32bfef6287"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.7 Derivatives","s":"sbr-12-7","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-7","c":"sbr-12-7#0","x":"card","t":"A derivative changes value with an underlying, needs little or no initial investment, and is settled at a future date","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Underlyings: interest rates, prices, FX rates, indices, credit ratings.","i":"3490e5c2dd"},{"s":"sbr-12-7","c":"sbr-12-7#1","x":"card","t":"A contract to buy a non-financial item is a derivative only if it can be settled net and isn't for the entity's own use","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Own-use contracts are executory contracts.","i":"171519979b"},{"s":"sbr-12-7","c":"sbr-12-7#2","x":"card","t":"Net settlement is indicated by contract terms, a practice of net settling, buying and quickly reselling, or the item being readily convertible to cash","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Any one of these can make it a derivative.","i":"8cf633eeaf"},{"s":"sbr-12-7","c":"sbr-12-7#3","x":"card","t":"An executory contract (equally unperformed) is not recognised unless onerous","d":"Reference: Conceptual Framework; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. E.g. a contract to pay staff for hours not yet worked.","i":"a8f80bc912"},{"s":"sbr-12-7","c":"sbr-12-7#4","x":"card","t":"A forward and a future both oblige a later purchase or sale at a fixed price; a future is standardised and exchange-traded","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Forwards are tailor-made, not traded.","i":"2bdcec8908"},{"s":"sbr-12-7","c":"sbr-12-7#5","x":"card","t":"A swap exchanges payments over time; an option gives the right, but not the obligation, to buy or sell","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. E.g. a fixed-for-floating interest rate swap.","i":"0a744adb0e"},{"s":"sbr-12-7","c":"sbr-12-7#6","x":"card","t":"Derivatives are measured at FVPL, with transaction costs expensed","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Hoggard: options bought for $5 are remeasured; lapsed options are written off.","i":"fbf2ea12b6"},{"s":"sbr-12-7","c":"sbr-12-7#7","x":"card","t":"Gains on a speculative derivative linked to raising finance go to financing; others go to operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Entity D: option tied to a share issue → financing; Entity A: option to buy shares → operating.","i":"f977e41e07"}],"i":"2c60632773"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.8 Hedge accounting","s":"sbr-12-8","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#0","x":"card","t":"Hedge accounting matches the gain or loss on a hedging instrument with the opposite change on the hedged item","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Without it, the derivative alone hits P/L and creates volatility.","i":"2e067a0151"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#1","x":"card","t":"A hedged item can be a recognised asset or liability, a firm commitment or a highly probable forecast transaction","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. A firm commitment is binding: quantity, price and date fixed.","i":"ae71137488"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#2","x":"card","t":"A hedging instrument is a designated derivative (or non-derivative financial item)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Its value must be expected to offset the item's changes.","i":"e2ede43392"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#3","x":"card","t":"Hedge accounting needs eligible items, formal documentation at inception and all effectiveness requirements met","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. You can't designate a hedge after the event.","i":"9744fb5ca8"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#4","x":"card","t":"In a fair value hedge, the instrument is remeasured and the hedged item is adjusted for its fair value change since inception, both to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Gold example: inventory ends at $7m, neither cost nor NRV. For FVOCI equity, both go to OCI.","i":"7eea782c79"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#5","x":"card","t":"A firm commitment hedged as a fair value hedge leads to the commitment's FV change being recognised as an asset or liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Chive: without hedge accounting, only the derivative is recognised.","i":"c2aefa43f3"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#6","x":"card","t":"In a cash flow hedge, the effective gain or loss goes to OCI, and any excess over the hedged item's change goes to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Example: instrument 10,000, item 9,100 → 9,100 OCI, 900 P/L.","i":"d00491e6e8"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#7","x":"card","t":"When the hedged item is a non-financial asset, the OCI amount is added to or deducted from that asset's cost (not via OCI)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Grayton: $10,000 loss adjusts the plant's cost. For financial items it is recycled when the hedged flows hit P/L.","i":"2af5b2ad4f"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#8","x":"card","t":"Effectiveness needs an economic relationship and credit risk not dominating, and a hedge ratio matching the quantities actually used","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Oil: hedge 40,000 of 120,000 gallons → designate only 40,000.","i":"bbc2510ee0"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#9","x":"card","t":"If the hedge ratio drifts but the objective is unchanged, the entity must rebalance the quantities and continue","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. No accounting entry at the rebalance, just updated documents.","i":"2417d278cd"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#10","x":"card","t":"Hedge accounting stops if the instrument ends, the criteria fail, or the forecast transaction is no longer highly probable","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Prospectively: entries so far aren't reversed.","i":"a62a8f8115"},{"s":"sbr-12-8","c":"sbr-12-8#11","x":"card","t":"When a cash flow hedge stops, the OCI reserve is recycled to P/L if the transaction won't happen; otherwise kept until it does","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Depends on why the hedge stopped.","i":"a0940d4ead"}],"i":"346881ccc7"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.9 IFRS 7 disclosures","s":"sbr-12-9","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-9","c":"sbr-12-9#0","x":"card","t":"IFRS 7 requires information on the significance of instruments for position and performance and the nature and extent of risks from them","d":"Reference: IFRS 7 Financial Instruments: Disclosures. Disclosed by class of instrument.","i":"60c8556525"},{"s":"sbr-12-9","c":"sbr-12-9#1","x":"card","t":"Qualitative risk disclosures cover exposures, management's objectives, policies and processes, and changes from the prior period","d":"Reference: IFRS 7 Financial Instruments: Disclosures. How risk is managed, in words.","i":"9b733fbd2d"},{"s":"sbr-12-9","c":"sbr-12-9#2","x":"card","t":"Quantitative risk disclosures are based on what's reported to key management and cover credit, liquidity and market risk, and concentrations","d":"Reference: IFRS 7 Financial Instruments: Disclosures. How much risk, in numbers.","i":"d90f18d2df"},{"s":"sbr-12-9","c":"sbr-12-9#3","x":"card","t":"Refusing to disclose debt-holders' risk exposure is wrong because lenders are primary users of the statements","d":"Reference: IFRS 7 Financial Instruments: Disclosures. Lizzer: 'excessive' is no excuse for omitting material information.","i":"dd6aa943d0"}],"i":"51678e5063"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"12.S1 Workings in order","s":"sbr-12-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Amortised cost of a financial liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Interest goes to financing, and the coupon to interest paid in the cash flow statement.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Net proceeds: fair value less issue costs","i":"7d53f7013f"},{"x":"step","t":"Add interest at the effective rate","i":"8c997a40a5"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct the cash coupon paid","i":"3b8553bfb4"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing liability on the SFP","i":"e997eeeef9"},{"x":"step","t":"Carry it forward as next year's opening balance","i":"2a9b07040e"}],"i":"7cfc1492b4"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Splitting a convertible bond at issue","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The split is made once, at issue, and never revisited.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find the rate for similar debt with no conversion rights","i":"76b77220d9"},{"x":"step","t":"Discount the coupons and the repayment at that rate","i":"cb0ee1b69a"},{"x":"step","t":"This present value is the liability component","i":"77768271d3"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct it from the proceeds","i":"81afc41009"},{"x":"step","t":"The remainder is the equity component","i":"e8ea3ad74d"}],"i":"87bc9cee05"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"A convertible bond at the end of its life, if converted","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. No gain or loss arises. If it's repaid in cash instead, the equity component stays in equity.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Bring the liability to its final carrying amount at the market rate","i":"40d33bbbda"},{"x":"step","t":"Add the equity component","i":"14ce6c630f"},{"x":"step","t":"Credit share capital with the nominal value of new shares","i":"6b6fb7cf7b"},{"x":"step","t":"Credit share premium with the rest","i":"e63479e0e1"}],"i":"d56d2970c0"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Classifying a debt investment","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Two tests: the contractual cash flows and the business model.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Are the cash flows solely principal and interest?","i":"f9608488e6"},{"x":"step","t":"If not, measure at FVPL","i":"c494f1f30b"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, is the model to hold and collect? Amortised cost","i":"8a7b063820"},{"x":"step","t":"Is the model to collect and sell? FVOCI","i":"5bfe37e812"},{"x":"step","t":"Any other model: FVPL","i":"8c7c6d7854"}],"i":"b737ed25d0"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"Measuring a loss allowance","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Trade receivables with no financing component skip the stages: always lifetime. Not part of it: Wait for a default before recognising any allowance; Use past default rates only, ignoring forecasts.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Recognise 12-month ECL at initial recognition","i":"b44af338b1"},{"x":"step","t":"At the reporting date, compare the risk of default now with the risk at initial recognition","i":"862f25362e"},{"x":"step","t":"No significant increase: 12-month ECL","i":"29c71cb3a9"},{"x":"step","t":"Significant increase: lifetime ECL","i":"82633d17eb"},{"x":"step","t":"Credit-impaired: gross amount less PV at the original effective rate","i":"5c1ecbffa4"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the change to P/L","i":"182caee010"}],"any":true,"i":"2e133d67cd"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"A renegotiated loan","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Ariel-type question: 11.1% is extinguished, 9.1% is modified.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"PV of the new cash flows and fees, at the original effective rate","i":"8a13154a9a"},{"x":"step","t":"PV of the remaining old cash flows","i":"ced72bc60e"},{"x":"step","t":"Express the difference as a percentage of the old PV","i":"c00815eef3"},{"x":"step","t":"10% or more: derecognise, recognise the new loan at fair value, expense fees","i":"7450a8fe7f"},{"x":"step","t":"Under 10%: restate to the new PV, deduct fees from the liability, keep the original rate","i":"ef4c821b59"}],"i":"c3720414b2"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"A fair value hedge at the reporting date","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The two changes offset, which removes the volatility.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Check documentation and effectiveness","i":"e1bc68bf23"},{"x":"step","t":"Remeasure the instrument to fair value","i":"39c34efa22"},{"x":"step","t":"Adjust the hedged item for its fair value change since inception","i":"446f1fdf1c"},{"x":"step","t":"Take both to P/L (OCI if the item is FVOCI equity)","i":"1baa7b541c"}],"i":"d77e22ff2b"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#7","x":"steps","t":"A cash flow hedge at the reporting date","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. If the instrument moved less than the item, it all stays in OCI.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Remeasure the instrument to fair value","i":"f6e5059619"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare its cumulative change with the hedged item's since inception","i":"81bb478e4b"},{"x":"step","t":"Put the lower amount in OCI","i":"4453eb8f7b"},{"x":"step","t":"Put any excess on the instrument in P/L","i":"52145ad33f"},{"x":"step","t":"Later recycle to P/L, or adjust the cost of a non-financial asset","i":"35f14878ac"}],"i":"c7f92f2263"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-1","c":"sbr-12-structure-1#8","x":"steps","t":"Rebalancing a hedge","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. E.g. 1:1 moves to 1:1.15: 11,500 tonnes hedged instead of 10,000.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"The hedge ratio no longer reflects the actual relationship","i":"cd499460e6"},{"x":"step","t":"The risk management objective remains the same","i":"e38cef109d"},{"x":"step","t":"Change the quantity of the instrument or the item","i":"08d34af91a"},{"x":"step","t":"Update the documentation","i":"ea7c9868c7"},{"x":"step","t":"Continue hedge accounting, with no entry for the rebalance","i":"3af53be3bb"}],"i":"c3bddadd76"}],"i":"12ee21fec0"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.S2 Which double entry?","s":"sbr-12-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Interest expense on a bond at the effective rate","a":"Dr P/L (financing), Cr liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It increases the liability.","i":"6cbe8bbf6f"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Cash coupon paid on a bond","a":"Dr liability, Cr cash","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It reduces the liability.","i":"6451ee7ef9"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"A liability held for trading falls in fair value","a":"Dr liability, Cr P/L (financing)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. A gain in P/L, typically financing.","i":"9d8618ea49"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Derivative gain in a fair value hedge of inventory","a":"Dr derivative, Cr P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 2 Inventories. Fair value hedge: both sides in P/L.","i":"8669849cdb"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Fall in value of the inventory in that hedge","a":"Dr P/L, Cr inventory","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 2 Inventories. The inventory leaves the normal IAS 2 rule.","i":"043b8040b0"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Derivative gain in a cash flow hedge, within the hedged item's loss","a":"Dr derivative, Cr OCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The effective part waits in OCI.","i":"da45db4b78"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Derivative loss in a cash flow hedge, within the hedged item's gain","a":"Dr OCI, Cr derivative","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Same rule in reverse.","i":"850807b072"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"A rise in value of a FVOCI bond","a":"Dr asset, Cr OCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Gains go to OCI, recycled on disposal.","i":"1c61bfb2ac"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"A fall in value of a FVOCI equity investment","a":"Dr OCI, Cr asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Never recycled.","i":"3170042b9a"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"A 12-month ECL on a FVOCI bond","a":"Dr P/L, Cr OCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The allowance is charged to P/L and held in OCI, so the bond stays at fair value.","i":"ebde71c170"},{"s":"sbr-12-structure-2","c":"sbr-12-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"A rise in value of shares at FVPL","a":"Dr asset, Cr P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Everything goes to P/L.","i":"3e353f7d65"}],"i":"56b4eed7c7"}],"i":"8001ba433a"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"12.C1 Liability or equity, and how measured","s":"sbr-12-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Preference shares redeemable in cash in three years","a":"Financial liability","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. A contractual obligation to deliver cash.","i":"cffe56808a"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Preference shares redeemable by issuing shares worth exactly $3m","a":"Financial liability","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. A variable number of shares works like cash: a liability.","i":"907f8fb0ad"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Preference shares redeemable by issuing exactly 3m shares","a":"Equity","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. A fixed number of own shares: equity.","i":"cf46b8b53c"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Non-redeemable shares paying a dividend only if ordinary dividends are paid","a":"Equity","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. No obligation to pay.","i":"8b901b2bbe"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"A bond held to collect cash, with solely principal and interest cash flows","a":"Amortised cost","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Both tests met.","i":"76a8d36803"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"A bond that may be sold if better returns appear, with those same cash flows","a":"FVOCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Collect and sell.","i":"7248488121"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"A bond traded in the short term","a":"FVPL","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Neither of the other models.","i":"c9b75b295b"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"A convertible bond held by the investor","a":"FVPL","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Fails the cash flow test because of the conversion option.","i":"cd7d299b9f"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Shares not held for trading, irrevocably designated on purchase","a":"FVOCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The only route to FVOCI for equity.","i":"7747a0d420"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Shares with no designation","a":"FVPL","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The default for equity.","i":"bbbe5e8878"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Costs of buying a bond at amortised cost","a":"Added to the asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Included in the initial carrying amount.","i":"a7f70e07b7"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Costs of buying a FVOCI investment","a":"Added to the asset","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Fair value plus transaction costs.","i":"b0949b2863"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Costs of issuing a bond at amortised cost","a":"Deducted from the liability","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Net proceeds are the starting point.","i":"17888f0c3e"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Costs of buying shares at FVPL","a":"Expensed","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Straight to P/L.","i":"9975201089"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-1","c":"sbr-12-compare-1#14","x":"sort","t":"Costs on a derivative","a":"Expensed","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Derivatives are at FVPL.","i":"e1780252f2"}],"i":"a3442d049f"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.C2 Impairment and derecognition","s":"sbr-12-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"A bond bought today","a":"12-month ECL","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Starts at 12-month ECL.","i":"0b58460079"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Credit risk is low at the reporting date","a":"12-month ECL","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. No significant increase may be assumed.","i":"b5c5e13d8d"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Contractual payments are 45 days overdue","a":"Lifetime ECL","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Over 30 days suggests a significant increase.","i":"4f00a37b11"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Trade receivables with no significant financing component","a":"Lifetime ECL","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The simplified approach.","i":"5f5c777cac"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Ratings under review, falling sales and a bond price slump","a":"Lifetime ECL","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Forward-looking signs of a significant increase.","i":"67e1d49b2f"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Interest income after a significant increase, but not credit-impaired","a":"On the gross amount","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The allowance is ignored.","i":"782b260747"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Interest income on a credit-impaired asset","a":"On the net amount","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Gross less allowance.","i":"f4d832a46c"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Receivable factored without recourse","a":"Derecognise","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Risks and rewards have passed.","i":"5a054ad99d"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Receivable factored with recourse if the customer doesn't pay","a":"Keep, and show a loan","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The seller still carries the credit risk.","i":"4ad4c38a56"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Shares sold with an option to buy back at fair value on that date","a":"Derecognise","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The seller no longer has the price risk.","i":"4d450d3f30"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Gain on disposal of FVOCI debt","a":"Recycled to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Debt is recycled.","i":"823611141c"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-2","c":"sbr-12-compare-2#11","x":"sort","t":"Gain on disposal of FVOCI equity","a":"Stays in equity","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Equity never is.","i":"c35df8790d"}],"i":"e61a0a0fc3"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.C3 Derivatives and hedges","s":"sbr-12-compare-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#0","x":"sort","t":"Tailor-made and not traded on an exchange","a":"Forward","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Agreed between two parties.","i":"235956235b"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#1","x":"sort","t":"Standard terms, traded on an exchange","a":"Future","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Otherwise very like a forward.","i":"623c07b087"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#2","x":"sort","t":"Exchange of periodic payments over time","a":"Swap","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. E.g. fixed for floating.","i":"4fc8f70601"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#3","x":"sort","t":"A right but not an obligation","a":"Option","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Can be left to lapse.","i":"f59ef1c397"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#4","x":"sort","t":"Hedge of the value of gold inventory","a":"Fair value hedge","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 2 Inventories. Exposure to fair value.","i":"25d71ed97a"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#5","x":"sort","t":"Hedge of a highly probable forecast purchase","a":"Cash flow hedge","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Variability in future cash flows.","i":"c746c9568b"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#6","x":"sort","t":"Hedge of a firm commitment to buy machinery, in the text's example","a":"Fair value hedge","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. The change in the commitment's value is recognised.","i":"bd5918be82"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#7","x":"sort","t":"The hedged item's carrying amount is adjusted","a":"Fair value hedge","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Inventory can end up at neither cost nor NRV.","i":"410b32a162"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#8","x":"sort","t":"The effective gain or loss waits in OCI","a":"Cash flow hedge","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Until the hedged cash flow happens.","i":"0301bad7ff"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#9","x":"sort","t":"Commodity contract used for the entity's own operating needs","a":"Executory contract","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Own-use contracts aren't derivatives.","i":"432f6d7450"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#10","x":"sort","t":"Commodity contract where the entity habitually takes delivery and quickly resells","a":"Derivative","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. That practice counts as net settlement.","i":"ff441b5ebd"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#11","x":"sort","t":"A forecast sale is no longer expected: OCI balance","a":"Taken to P/L at once","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. There will be nothing to match it against.","i":"d696b8c968"},{"s":"sbr-12-compare-3","c":"sbr-12-compare-3#12","x":"sort","t":"The instrument was sold but the forecast sale is still expected: OCI balance","a":"Kept until the transaction","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It still has something to match.","i":"dfe35fbfd7"}],"i":"a73b48e5ed"}],"i":"cfbcd67822"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"12.W1 Liabilities, equity and investments","s":"sbr-12-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"A contract settled by delivering a variable number of own shares is a liability.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Shares worth a fixed amount work just like cash.","i":"d273d2ae36"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Preference shares are always equity, because they're called shares.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Substance decides, not the name.","i":"292057c6a6"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Dividends on preference shares classed as liabilities are shown as interest in P/L.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. The treatment follows the instrument.","i":"a5aef9283a"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"The split of a convertible bond is reviewed at each reporting date.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It's made once, at issue.","i":"a4d88fdbab"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"No gain or loss arises when a convertible bond is converted into shares.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Carrying amounts move into share capital and premium.","i":"4fabcb2692"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"Interest on a convertible bond's liability is charged at the coupon rate.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It's charged at the market rate for similar non-convertible debt.","i":"50a27a4b02"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"An own credit gain on a liability designated at FVPL goes to P/L.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. That part goes to OCI, or an entity would profit from its own worsening credit.","i":"bfa5a091e9"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"An interest-free loan made starts below the amount of cash lent.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It's recognised at the present value of the repayment.","i":"7f1697c66a"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Selling a few bonds when cash runs short breaks a hold-to-collect model.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Occasional sales are consistent with it.","i":"5ba01c495b"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"A convertible bond held as an investment fails the solely-principal-and-interest test.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Its return includes the conversion option.","i":"e3a4cf7297"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"A FVOCI designation for equity can be changed if the directors change their minds.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It's irrevocable.","i":"9ead60b812"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"Reducing P/L volatility is a valid reason to put shares traded short-term in FVOCI.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Shares held for trading can't be designated, and doing so breaches objectivity.","i":"be9ff8437a"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-1","c":"sbr-12-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"Loss allowance movements on trade receivables are shown in the operating category.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. They arise from the main business.","i":"fad1ed53e2"}],"i":"caa85a8be9"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.W2 Impairment, derecognition and hedges","s":"sbr-12-watch-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#0","x":"trap","t":"A loss allowance is needed from the day a bond at amortised cost is bought.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. 12-month ECL from initial recognition.","i":"0ca335948c"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#1","x":"trap","t":"Equity investments at FVOCI need a loss allowance.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Only debt assets at amortised cost or FVOCI.","i":"5085120879"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#2","x":"trap","t":"ECL estimates can rely on past default rates alone.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. They must also reflect current conditions and forecasts.","i":"4299739aea"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#3","x":"trap","t":"A FVOCI bond's loss allowance doesn't reduce its carrying amount.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It stays at fair value; the allowance sits in OCI.","i":"ac4e40acdd"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#4","x":"trap","t":"A credit-impaired loan is discounted at today's market rate.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It's discounted at the original effective rate.","i":"35c3feac9a"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#5","x":"trap","t":"An asset that has been legally sold always leaves the statement of financial position.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. If the seller keeps the risks and rewards, it stays.","i":"d64b49c4e1"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#6","x":"trap","t":"A renegotiation with a 9% change in present value isn't an extinguishment.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Below 10%, the loan is modified.","i":"7fe575b572"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#7","x":"trap","t":"Fees on a modified loan are expensed.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. They're deducted from the liability; they are expensed on extinguishment.","i":"19efef7183"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#8","x":"trap","t":"A contract to buy goods for own use isn't normally a derivative.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; Conceptual Framework. It's an executory contract unless onerous.","i":"8dfdd76b0b"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#9","x":"trap","t":"A hedge can be designated once the derivative shows a gain.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Formal documentation is needed at inception.","i":"c31bd54dbc"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#10","x":"trap","t":"Hedge accounting can leave inventory at neither cost nor NRV.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 2 Inventories. A fair value hedge adjusts its carrying amount.","i":"9319587856"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#11","x":"trap","t":"Any difference between instrument and item in a cash flow hedge goes to P/L.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Only when the instrument moved more; otherwise it all stays in OCI.","i":"b19f9a6504"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#12","x":"trap","t":"Rebalancing a hedge needs no accounting entry.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Only the documentation changes.","i":"8dfdba139f"},{"s":"sbr-12-watch-2","c":"sbr-12-watch-2#13","x":"trap","t":"Discontinuing hedge accounting reverses the entries made so far.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It's prospective.","i":"f8cb1fd494"}],"i":"3fa59f465f"}],"i":"831eafe208"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"12.A1 Liabilities, equity and investments","s":"sbr-12-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Reed issues a $100,000 bond at a 5% discount with issue costs of $1,000. The coupon is 3% and the effective rate 8%. What is the liability after one year?","a":"$98,520","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Step 1 (start): 95,000 − 1,000 = 94,000. Step 2 (interest): 94,000 × 8% = 7,520. Step 3 (coupon): 100,000 × 3% = 3,000. Step 4: 94,000 + 7,520 − 3,000 = 98,520. P/L category: Financing, 7,520. Common mistake: charging interest on the nominal value, or leaving out the issue costs.","i":"a548adf45e"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Arlo issues a $10m three-year convertible at par with a 4% annual coupon. Similar debt without conversion rights costs 8% (discount factors 0.926, 0.857, 0.794). How are the proceeds split?","a":"Liability $8.97m, equity $1.03m","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: the liability is the PV of the payments at the rate for debt without conversion rights; equity is the remainder. Applied: $0.4m × (0.926 + 0.857 + 0.794) = $1.03m, plus $10m × 0.794 = $7.94m, gives $8.97m; equity $1.03m. Users: gearing is lower than if all were debt, and interest is charged at 8%, not 4%. Common mistake: discounting the repayment only.","i":"ac3de9e754"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Bell designated its bonds at FVPL to remove an accounting mismatch. Their fair value fell $40m, of which $15m relates to Bell's own credit risk. What is recorded?","a":"$25m gain in P/L, $15m gain in OCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: for a liability designated at FVPL, the own credit part goes to OCI and the rest to P/L. Why: otherwise profit would rise because the entity's credit worsened. P/L category: Financing. Common mistake: treating all of it as profit.","i":"abd80c7c26"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Dunn issues preference shares, to be redeemed by issuing as many ordinary shares as are worth $5m at that time. How are they classified?","a":"Financial liability","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Rule: a contract settled in a variable number of own shares is a liability. Why: the holder gets a fixed value, as with cash. Users: gearing rises and any return goes through P/L. Common mistake: looking only at what is delivered, not at who bears the share price risk.","i":"b5cea00417"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Reva buys shares to trade them in the short term. Its directors designate them at FVOCI because steadier profit would help the share price and their bonuses. Is that acceptable?","a":"No: they stay at FVPL, and this breaches objectivity","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Rule: FVOCI can't be used for equity held for trading. Applied: these must be at FVPL. Ethics: the motive is a self-interest threat, and the directors' duty to shareholders doesn't justify a misstatement. Users: profit volatility is hidden. Common mistake: thinking designation is a free choice.","i":"53c18631f5"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"Kite buys shares for $20m plus $0.5m costs, designated at FVOCI. At the year end they are worth $30m. Soon after, they are sold for $35m. What is the total result?","a":"$14.5m in OCI, none in P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: FVOCI equity starts at fair value plus costs, and gains stay in equity even on disposal. Applied: start 20.5; gain to 30 is 9.5; gain to 35 is 5; total 14.5 in OCI. Common mistake: recycling on disposal, which only applies to debt.","i":"f31a30d1eb"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"Moss lends a supplier $1m interest-free for two years when market rates are 10%. How is the loan first recorded?","a":"$826,446, with $173,554 expensed","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: financial assets start at fair value, here the PV of the repayment. Applied: $1m / 1.1² = $826,446; the difference is a loss. Interest income of $82,645 is recognised in year 1. P/L category: Investing. Users: an interest-free loan really does cost something. Common mistake: recording the cash lent.","i":"8e8fb93fe8"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"Pike buys a bond for $48,000 plus $1,000 costs. Coupon received is $2,000, effective rate 7%, and fair value at the year end $52,000. Its model is to hold but also to sell for better returns. What goes to P/L and OCI?","a":"$3,430 in P/L, $1,570 in OCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Step 1 (model): collect and sell means FVOCI. Step 2 (interest): 49,000 × 7% = 3,430 to P/L. Step 3 (amortised cost): 49,000 + 3,430 − 2,000 = 50,430. Step 4 (fair value): 52,000 − 50,430 = 1,570 to OCI. P/L category: Investing. Common mistake: taking the coupon as income.","i":"116821dccb"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-1","c":"sbr-12-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"The business model for a portfolio of bonds changes from selling them to holding them to collect the cash. What happens?","a":"All are reclassified to amortised cost","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: debt assets are classified on initial recognition, but a change of business model requires reclassification of all those affected. Why: measurement follows how the asset generates cash. Common mistake: thinking a single sale or a change of mind is enough; it must be a change of model.","i":"87a93e00e6"}],"i":"9988913046"},{"x":"stage","t":"12.A2 Impairment, derecognition and hedges","s":"sbr-12-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"Receivables with no financing component: $20m not overdue (0.5%), $5m 1–30 days overdue (2%) and $1m over 30 days (10%). The opening allowance is $0.1m. What are the allowance and the charge?","a":"$0.3m and $0.2m","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: a provision matrix is allowed, with lifetime ECL always. Applied: 0.1 + 0.1 + 0.1 = 0.3; less the opening 0.1 leaves 0.2. P/L category: Operating (trade receivables). Common mistake: charging the whole closing allowance.","i":"a4fb55e2c4"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"A $2m bond has a low credit risk. The initial allowance was $1,000. There is a 1% chance of default in the next 12 months, with a PV of credit loss of $500,000 if so. What is the charge?","a":"$4,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: low credit risk means 12-month ECL. Applied: $500,000 × 1% = $5,000 is the allowance; less the $1,000 already there, the charge is $4,000. P/L category: Investing. Common mistake: charging the whole allowance, or the whole loss.","i":"02c5490179"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"Holm is owed $20,000 at a 5% effective rate. The borrower is in financial difficulty. Holm now expects only $12,000 in two years and no interest. Market rates are 8%. The allowance is $2,000. What is the charge?","a":"$7,116","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Step 1 (PV at the original rate): 12,000 / 1.05² = 10,884. Step 2 (allowance): 20,000 − 10,884 = 9,116. Step 3 (charge): 9,116 − 2,000 = 7,116. Next year's interest is on the net amount: 10,884 × 5%. Common mistake: using the 8% market rate gives 7,712; ignoring discounting gives 6,000.","i":"2683f8e171"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"A FVOCI bond cost $5,000 and is now worth $4,700. Credit risk is unchanged and 12-month ECL is $80. What are the entries, and the bond's carrying amount?","a":"$300 Dr OCI; $80 Dr P/L, Cr OCI; $4,700","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: the bond stays at fair value, and the allowance is charged to P/L and credited to OCI, not to the asset. Applied: Dr OCI 300, Cr bond 300; Dr P/L 80, Cr OCI 80. Common mistake: deducting the allowance from the bond.","i":"f2e4e0103f"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"Gant factors a $300,000 receivable for $260,000. The factor can't return it if the customer fails to pay. What does Gant do?","a":"Derecognise it and recognise a $40,000 loss","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: derecognise when substantially all risks and rewards pass. Applied: without recourse, credit risk has passed. Dr Cash 260,000, Dr P/L 40,000, Cr Receivables 300,000. Common mistake: looking at the legal form only.","i":"bfc8c2181a"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#5","x":"example","t":"Gant factors a $100,000 receivable for $75,000. If the customer pays late, Gant must refund the factor, and it gets a further $10,000 if the customer pays on time. What does it do?","a":"Keep it and show a $75,000 loan","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: if substantially all risks and rewards are kept, there's no derecognition. Applied: Gant still bears the credit and late-payment risk, so the cash is a loan. Users: hiding this would understate gearing and overstate cash from operations. Common mistake: treating a financing deal as a sale.","i":"0d2b763724"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#6","x":"example","t":"A loan with a carrying amount of $10m (equal to the PV of the old terms) is renegotiated. The PV of new cash flows, including $0.4m fees, at the original rate is $10.8m. What is the result?","a":"Modified: liability $10.4m","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Step 1 (test): 0.8 / 10 = 8%, below 10%, so it's modified. Step 2: Dr P/L 0.8, Cr liability 0.8. Step 3 (fees): Dr liability 0.4, Cr cash 0.4, leaving 10.4, at the original rate. P/L category: Financing. Common mistake: expensing the fees, which is the treatment for an extinguishment.","i":"1ea8bf1f38"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#7","x":"example","t":"Howe buys 200 options at $4 each for the right to buy shares at $10. It later exercises them when the share price is $18 and holds the shares for trading. What is the gain on the options, ignoring earlier remeasurement?","a":"$800","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: options are at FVPL and shares start at fair value. Applied: shares 200 × 18 = 3,600; cash paid 2,000; option carried at 800; Dr Investment 3,600, Cr Cash 2,000, Cr Option 800, Cr P/L 800. P/L category: Operating, as it doesn't involve raising finance. Common mistake: forgetting the $800 cost of the options.","i":"10cc14093f"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#8","x":"example","t":"Copper inventory cost $5m and is worth $7m. A futures contract to sell it at $7m is designated as a fair value hedge. Its value rises $0.8m as the inventory falls to $6.2m. Where does the inventory end up, and what's the P/L effect?","a":"$4.2m, with no net effect","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 2 Inventories. Rule: a fair value hedge adjusts the hedged item for its change since inception. Applied: 5.0 − 0.8 = 4.2; derivative gain and inventory loss offset in operating. Users: less volatile EPS, as without hedging only the $0.8m gain would hit P/L. Common mistake: keeping the inventory at cost as IAS 2 normally requires.","i":"ca84755ee7"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#9","x":"example","t":"In a cash flow hedge, the hedged item's cumulative loss is $9,000. The instrument's cumulative gain is $10,000. Where do they go?","a":"$9,000 in OCI, $1,000 in P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: the gain in OCI is limited to the item's change; any excess goes to P/L. Common mistake: applying this the other way round: an instrument gain of $8,500 would all go to OCI, with nothing in P/L.","i":"b516fd9a75"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#10","x":"example","t":"Lund hedges a highly probable $200,000 machine purchase with a forward. By delivery, the machine costs $180,000 and the forward has lost $20,000, held in OCI. What is the machine's cost?","a":"$200,000","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: for a non-financial item, the OCI amount adjusts the asset's cost. Why: this gives the price that was locked in. Applied: 180,000 + 20,000 = 200,000. It isn't a reclassification, so it doesn't pass through OCI again. Common mistake: recycling it to P/L, which is for financial items.","i":"ef2eb98e41"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#11","x":"example","t":"Rhea Co owns 90,000 barrels of oil and buys one futures contract covering 30,000 barrels. It wants a fair value hedge of all its oil. What should be done?","a":"Designate only 30,000 barrels","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: the hedge ratio must match the quantities actually used. Why: a 3:1 ratio wouldn't offset, creating volatility. Applied: designate 30,000; the other 60,000 stay under IAS 2. Common mistake: thinking the hedge fails altogether.","i":"1df01e9514"},{"s":"sbr-12-apply-2","c":"sbr-12-apply-2#12","x":"example","t":"A securitisation company's debt-holders bear the risk of its investments. The directors say disclosing those risks in the notes would be excessive. Are they right?","a":"No: lenders are primary users, and IFRS 7 applies","d":"Reference: IFRS 7 Financial Instruments: Disclosures. Rule: IFRS 7 requires information on the nature and extent of risks, and lenders are among the primary users. Why: material information can't be omitted because it is long. Common mistake: thinking of shareholders alone.","i":"e8e913b6a0"}],"i":"66318ad95a"}],"i":"18eb362344"}],"i":"48dcb3e5b9"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 13: Tax","ch":13,"d":"Study Text pages 347–372.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"13.1 IAS 12: current tax and temporary differences","s":"sbr-13-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#0","x":"card","t":"The tax expense equals current tax plus or minus the movement in deferred tax","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Deferred tax isn't a tax the government charges; it's the accruals concept applied to tax.","i":"ab61c8563b"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#1","x":"card","t":"Current tax is measured using the tax laws and rates in force at the reporting date","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Over- or under-estimates are corrected in the next period's tax expense.","i":"28d5087376"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#2","x":"card","t":"Permanent differences, like fines and client entertaining, never reverse, so no deferred tax","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Only temporary differences create deferred tax.","i":"91190fdd27"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#3","x":"card","t":"Deferred tax smooths the after-tax profit because the tax effect is reported in the same period as the transaction","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Prudent example: without it, profit after tax falls from 1,760 to 1,280 just from timing; with it, 1,400 every year.","i":"3b881e6521"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#4","x":"card","t":"A temporary difference is the difference between an item's carrying amount and its tax base","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Tax base = the amount attributed to it for tax.","i":"82624ac2d5"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#5","x":"card","t":"An asset's tax base is the amount deductible against the taxable benefits it brings; if the benefits aren't taxable, it equals the carrying amount","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Machine: cost 100, tax allowances 30 → tax base 70.","i":"5a33f28155"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#6","x":"card","t":"A liability's tax base is its carrying amount less amounts deductible for tax in future","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Accrual deductible when paid → tax base nil.","i":"c78a47c342"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#7","x":"card","t":"When an asset's carrying amount exceeds its tax base, the difference is taxable and gives a deferred tax liability","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Accelerated tax allowances are the classic case.","i":"88254a1b7e"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#8","x":"card","t":"When an asset's tax base exceeds its carrying amount, the difference is deductible and gives a deferred tax asset","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. E.g. inventory written down but not yet deductible.","i":"1d7b6de5c4"},{"s":"sbr-13-1","c":"sbr-13-1#9","x":"card","t":"Common temporary differences: tax allowances, unrealised group profit, tax losses, revaluations, development costs and share options","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Each is timing, not a permanent difference.","i":"9ef6490ac2"}],"i":"8fbb731fbc"},{"x":"stage","t":"13.2 IAS 12: recognition and measurement","s":"sbr-13-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-13-2","c":"sbr-13-2#0","x":"card","t":"No deferred tax liability is recognised on initial recognition of goodwill or an asset affecting neither profit, outside a business combination, with no equal offsetting differences","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Enchilada: non-deductible factory → no DTL. There'd be nowhere sensible to post the debit.","i":"103ba6c7c3"},{"s":"sbr-13-2","c":"sbr-13-2#1","x":"card","t":"A deferred tax asset is recognised only if it's probable there will be taxable profits to use it","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Same exceptions as for DTLs apply.","i":"e58139ac75"},{"s":"sbr-13-2","c":"sbr-13-2#2","x":"card","t":"Deferred tax uses the rate expected on reversal that is enacted or substantively enacted by the reporting date","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Brick: a rate change passed after the year end is not used.","i":"09faee7efc"},{"s":"sbr-13-2","c":"sbr-13-2#3","x":"card","t":"Deferred tax is never discounted","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Even if reversal is decades away.","i":"b1117e5111"},{"s":"sbr-13-2","c":"sbr-13-2#4","x":"card","t":"Deferred tax is charged to P/L, OCI or equity, following the item it relates to","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Revaluation → deferred tax in OCI.","i":"bc2f92b70e"},{"s":"sbr-13-2","c":"sbr-13-2#5","x":"card","t":"Deferred tax balances are presented as non-current and offset only if there's a legal right to set off and the same tax authority","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Usually one net figure per tax authority.","i":"838e72b058"}],"i":"3a841dd9fd"},{"x":"stage","t":"13.3 IAS 12: specific situations","s":"sbr-13-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#0","x":"card","t":"Revaluing PPE creates deferred tax even if there's no plan to sell or rollover relief is available","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Dodge: carrying 90 vs tax base 50 → DTL on 40; the part from revaluation goes to OCI.","i":"e894f238da"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#1","x":"card","t":"Share options create a deferred tax asset because the tax deduction comes only on exercise, based on intrinsic value","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Splash: tax base = intrinsic value earned to date; DTA = × 30%.","i":"abe0d8c62f"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#2","x":"card","t":"If the expected tax deduction exceeds the cumulative share-based expense, the excess deferred tax goes to equity","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Only the part matching the expense goes to P/L.","i":"3c8c1cc762"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#3","x":"card","t":"If tax relief is given on the leased asset, at the start there's no temporary difference","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 16 Leases. ROU and liability both have tax bases equal to their carrying amounts.","i":"423c99d956"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#4","x":"card","t":"If tax relief is given on lease payments, at the start there are equal taxable and deductible differences, so both a DTL and a DTA","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 16 Leases. Bittern: 10m each → DTL 3m and DTA 3m (offset if allowed).","i":"2ebe3c9d7a"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#5","x":"card","t":"Unused tax losses give a DTA only to the extent of probable future taxable profits","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Red: DTA on 2.5m at 28% (the enacted future rate). Recurring losses are strong evidence against.","i":"b0f8ac291c"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#6","x":"card","t":"Fair value adjustments on acquiring a subsidiary create deferred tax that is part of net assets and so changes goodwill","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Tom: inventory up 5,000 → DTL 1,500, goodwill up 1,500. No deferred tax on goodwill itself.","i":"d76735a0b3"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#7","x":"card","t":"Unrealised profit in group inventory creates a deferred tax asset in the consolidated statements","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Mug: PURP 25,000 × 20% = DTA 5,000.","i":"d0d54211d9"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#8","x":"card","t":"Deferred tax on a subsidiary's undistributed profits isn't recognised if the parent controls the timing and no distribution is probable in the foreseeable future","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. That's why it can arise for associates but rarely for subsidiaries.","i":"9d2b8e79c3"},{"s":"sbr-13-3","c":"sbr-13-3#9","x":"card","t":"The tax reconciliation disclosure explains why the effective tax rate differs from the statutory rate","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. HCRM: prior-year adjustments and disallowables show whether the tax charge is sustainable.","i":"24271bfad4"}],"i":"a148df9548"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"13.S1 Deferred tax in order","s":"sbr-13-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-13-structure-1","c":"sbr-13-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Calculating deferred tax","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Carrying amount above tax base on an asset is taxable, giving a liability.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find the carrying amount","i":"b50a28485a"},{"x":"step","t":"Find the tax base","i":"99c737af5a"},{"x":"step","t":"The difference is the temporary difference","i":"688762396a"},{"x":"step","t":"Decide if it's taxable or deductible","i":"79b3beb178"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply the enacted or substantively enacted rate for when it reverses","i":"bcf34149f0"},{"x":"step","t":"Post the movement in the balance","i":"7f262bd1f2"}],"i":"ea3899dc42"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-1","c":"sbr-13-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Recognising a deferred tax asset on tax losses","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. A record of losses is strong evidence against recognition. Not part of it: Recognise all the losses if the directors are optimistic; Discount the asset to its present value.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Look at taxable temporary differences that can absorb them","i":"b24b720120"},{"x":"step","t":"Forecast taxable profits before the losses expire","i":"4abecb11a4"},{"x":"step","t":"Ask what caused the losses and if it may recur","i":"8b6077b425"},{"x":"step","t":"Consider tax planning opportunities","i":"b5978bea8f"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise only what is probable","i":"6429fdee74"}],"any":true,"i":"0a671e6db1"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-1","c":"sbr-13-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Deferred tax on a revaluation","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Required even if there's no plan to sell.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Revalue the asset","i":"cd55889653"},{"x":"step","t":"Tax base doesn't change","i":"7428023ea7"},{"x":"step","t":"Find the new temporary difference","i":"ef6bad4306"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply the rate","i":"d53951ec0d"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the increase to OCI","i":"ab923f737d"}],"i":"608cc82cf4"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-1","c":"sbr-13-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Deferred tax on share options","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The deduction comes only at exercise.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find the intrinsic value at the reporting date","i":"34abdfdbe3"},{"x":"step","t":"Multiply by the options expected to vest and the time elapsed","i":"7522e352f9"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply the tax rate: this is the asset","i":"7064dca214"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare with the cumulative expense × the tax rate","i":"7e374497d8"},{"x":"step","t":"Up to that amount to P/L; any excess to equity","i":"69093a037a"}],"i":"29e946bffd"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-1","c":"sbr-13-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"Deferred tax on a fair value adjustment on acquisition","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Goodwill itself attracts no deferred tax.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find the fair value uplift","i":"df71db849b"},{"x":"step","t":"Tax base stays at the subsidiary's old figure","i":"3b7612e486"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply the rate to the uplift","i":"d507dc59cb"},{"x":"step","t":"Include the liability in net assets acquired","i":"3d0e4f0dea"},{"x":"step","t":"Goodwill rises by the same amount","i":"dc849adc3b"}],"i":"a232f153f7"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-1","c":"sbr-13-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"Deferred tax on unrealised intra-group profit","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. The tax paid by the seller is matched to the later sale.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find the profit in inventory still held","i":"c0b4116bd8"},{"x":"step","t":"Remove it from the group's inventory","i":"332da69a1e"},{"x":"step","t":"The tax base stays at the buyer's cost","i":"918192fddd"},{"x":"step","t":"This is a deductible difference","i":"2eec851055"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise an asset at the rate","i":"a24349c747"}],"i":"e17d3d7ea3"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-1","c":"sbr-13-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"A lease where tax relief follows the payments","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 16 Leases. Payments in advance and direct costs are assessed separately.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Right-of-use asset has a tax base of nil","i":"30f53e07a2"},{"x":"step","t":"Lease liability has a tax base of nil","i":"b66d9d6000"},{"x":"step","t":"Equal taxable and deductible differences arise","i":"3acee8aaf3"},{"x":"step","t":"The exemption doesn't apply","i":"b6fd28c8c3"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise both a deferred tax liability and asset","i":"5e9aac85c9"}],"i":"e425907c45"}],"i":"99473d6f8c"},{"x":"stage","t":"13.S2 Which difference?","s":"sbr-13-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"A machine with a carrying amount above its tax written-down value","a":"Taxable: deferred tax liability","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. More tax to pay later.","i":"d7bb0c19fa"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Interest receivable that will be taxed when received","a":"Taxable: deferred tax liability","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Tax base is nil.","i":"10e914e60c"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Development costs capitalised but deducted for tax as incurred","a":"Taxable: deferred tax liability","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Tax relief came first.","i":"7d83d9d1a8"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"An upward revaluation not reflected for tax","a":"Taxable: deferred tax liability","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Carrying amount above tax base.","i":"582305222a"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Inventory written down for accounts, ignored for tax until sold","a":"Deductible: deferred tax asset","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. The deduction is still to come.","i":"6a0c0fb818"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"An accrual deductible only when paid","a":"Deductible: deferred tax asset","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Tax base of the liability is nil.","i":"36d7f2872d"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Share options expensed, with the deduction at exercise","a":"Deductible: deferred tax asset","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. The relief is delayed.","i":"8a22315fc2"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Unrealised profit in inventory on consolidation","a":"Deductible: deferred tax asset","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Group's carrying amount falls below the tax base.","i":"bc0ed292ed"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Tax losses that can be carried forward","a":"Deductible: deferred tax asset","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Subject to probable future profits.","i":"3a678d2e20"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"A trade receivable already included in taxable profit","a":"No temporary difference","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Tax base equals carrying amount.","i":"2c9f35113b"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"An accrued expense already deducted for tax","a":"No temporary difference","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Tax base equals carrying amount.","i":"910352856e"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#11","x":"sort","t":"Deferred tax on a revaluation of PPE","a":"OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Follows the gain.","i":"1d76d23105"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#12","x":"sort","t":"Deferred tax on accelerated tax allowances","a":"Profit or loss","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Follows depreciation.","i":"8ea3f963b0"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#13","x":"sort","t":"Deferred tax on a fair value uplift at acquisition","a":"Goodwill","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Part of the net assets acquired.","i":"17c3ceb0d4"},{"s":"sbr-13-structure-2","c":"sbr-13-structure-2#14","x":"sort","t":"Deferred tax on a pension remeasurement","a":"OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Follows the remeasurement.","i":"4423507517"}],"i":"114a0127de"}],"i":"0a5ecadf2c"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"13.C Alike but different","s":"sbr-13-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"A fine that the tax authority won't allow","a":"Permanent difference","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It never reverses, so no deferred tax.","i":"feb1ed0d81"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Client entertaining that's disallowed","a":"Permanent difference","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It never reverses.","i":"c681bb544a"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"A provision deductible only when paid","a":"Temporary difference","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It reverses on payment.","i":"b78de098ea"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Faster tax allowances than depreciation","a":"Temporary difference","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It reverses over the asset's life.","i":"6b2ebbe27d"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"A building with no tax deductions, bought outside a business combination","a":"Exempt","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Initial recognition exemption.","i":"b6dc5d598d"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill in a business combination","a":"Exempt","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Specifically excluded.","i":"5a0f6dcf73"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"A fair value uplift in a business combination","a":"Recognised","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Business combinations aren't exempt.","i":"8d8980658d"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"A lease creating equal taxable and deductible differences","a":"Recognised","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 16 Leases. Equal differences mean the exemption doesn't apply.","i":"81b0894d2b"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"A lease where relief follows the asset","a":"No difference at the start","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 16 Leases. Tax bases equal the carrying amounts.","i":"52efd41e8a"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"A lease where relief follows the payments","a":"Equal differences at the start","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 16 Leases. Tax bases are nil.","i":"8bdc621d2f"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Tax rate enacted before the reporting date, effective next year","a":"Use this rate","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Enacted, for when it reverses.","i":"949ff4f980"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Tax rate passed after the reporting date","a":"Don't use this rate","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Disclose its effect instead.","i":"9d0035c383"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Parent's investment in a subsidiary with undistributed profits it won't distribute","a":"Usually no deferred tax","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. The parent controls the timing.","i":"aaa840a063"},{"s":"sbr-13-compare-1","c":"sbr-13-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Investor's share of an associate's undistributed profits","a":"Deferred tax likely","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. It can't control the dividend policy.","i":"275ad41e3e"}],"i":"369adf9672"}],"i":"c87fbec528"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"13.W Tax traps","s":"sbr-13-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"Deferred tax is a tax that must be paid to the government.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It's an application of accruals; it isn't a levy.","i":"b69d41a7c9"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"A prior-year tax underestimate increases the current year's tax expense.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It isn't normally a prior period error.","i":"4a8a342642"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Deferred tax makes post-tax profit a better guide to performance.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It matches tax with the profit it relates to.","i":"7903a9853e"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Deferred tax balances are discounted.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. IAS 12 prohibits it.","i":"fc7e0b76df"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"Deferred tax balances are presented as non-current.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Always.","i":"912f70321e"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"Deferred tax on a revaluation can be ignored if the asset won't be sold.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. It's required, even with no intention to sell.","i":"1b9a277c37"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"Deferred tax is recognised on goodwill arising on an acquisition.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It's specifically excluded.","i":"bf5344da95"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"A deferred tax liability arising on an acquisition increases goodwill.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It lowers the net assets acquired.","i":"5594f953b2"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Losses caused by a one-off event are better evidence for a tax asset than recurring losses.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. If the cause won't recur, future profits are more credible.","i":"ef17e433cf"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"A deferred tax asset on losses is recognised in full if the directors are optimistic.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Only to the extent future profits are probable.","i":"15da121a08"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"Deferred tax assets and liabilities can be offset whenever they exist in the same group.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It needs a legal right of set-off and the same tax authority.","i":"6da11d2d37"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"Tax relief on share options often relates partly to equity.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. When the expected deduction exceeds the cumulative expense.","i":"bce2227198"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#12","x":"trap","t":"A rate passed after the year end is used if the statements haven't been issued.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Only enacted or substantively enacted by the reporting date.","i":"d3ef034825"},{"s":"sbr-13-watch-1","c":"sbr-13-watch-1#13","x":"trap","t":"A reconciliation of tax expense to accounting profit must be disclosed.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. It explains the relationship between them.","i":"dd40846f9a"}],"i":"6426ad2f56"}],"i":"91719e6b63"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"13.A Deferred tax scenarios","s":"sbr-13-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"A machine cost $200,000. Depreciation to date is $40,000, and tax allowances claimed $60,000. The rate is 30%. What's the deferred tax?","a":"$6,000 liability","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Step 1: carrying amount 160,000. Step 2: tax base 140,000. Step 3: taxable difference 20,000. Step 4: × 30% = 6,000 liability. P/L category: Income taxes. Common mistake: taking the allowances × rate.","i":"f98a069683"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Taxable differences total $22,000 and deductible differences $3,000. All can be offset, and the rate is 30%. What's the balance?","a":"$5,700 liability","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Rule: the balance is the net difference × the rate. Applied: 19,000 × 30%. Users: a future tax outflow. Common mistake: ignoring the deductible part.","i":"335bcb7dec"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"At a 30 April year end, the tax rate is 22%. On 6 June a law cuts it to 20% from next January. Which rate is used for deferred tax?","a":"22%","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Rule: rates must be enacted or substantively enacted by the reporting date. Applied: the new law came after it; disclose the effect. Common mistake: using the rate at which the difference will actually reverse, whatever the date.","i":"ce2ad44373"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"An asset cost $200,000. Depreciation is $80,000, tax allowances $100,000. It's revalued to $180,000. The rate is 30%. What's recognised in OCI?","a":"$18,000 charge","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Step 1: before, 120,000 − 100,000 = 20,000, so 6,000 already held. Step 2: after, 180,000 − 100,000 = 80,000, so 24,000. Step 3: the increase of 18,000 follows the gain into OCI. The other 6,000 went through P/L. Common mistake: taking the total to OCI.","i":"e9ee7945bc"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Options over 80,000 shares are granted to directors (fair value $8, exercise price $4, four-year vesting). After year 1 the share price is $14. The rate is 30%. How is the deferred tax split?","a":"$48,000 in P/L, $12,000 in equity","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Step 1 (expense): 80,000 × $8 × 1/4 = 160,000. Step 2 (tax base): 80,000 × ($14 − $4) × 1/4 = 200,000. Step 3 (asset): × 30% = 60,000. Step 4: up to 160,000 × 30% = 48,000 goes to P/L; the excess 12,000 goes to equity. Common mistake: using the fair value at grant, not intrinsic value.","i":"4997de65a8"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"A lease has a PV of $5m. Tax relief is given on the lease liability as payments are made. The rate is 30%. What deferred tax arises at the start?","a":"$1.5m liability and $1.5m asset","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: the exemption doesn't apply when equal taxable and deductible differences arise. Applied: both tax bases are nil, so both differences are $5m. Common mistake: applying the exemption because profit is unaffected.","i":"de9f0a4274"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"A lease has a PV of $5m, and in this jurisdiction tax relief is given on the leased asset. What deferred tax arises at the start?","a":"None","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: compare carrying amount and tax base. Applied: the asset's tax base is $5m, and the liability's is $5m less nil. No differences at the start; they may arise later. Common mistake: assuming the answer is the same in every jurisdiction.","i":"88e4e65e41"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"Reed has tax losses of $6m from a one-off restructuring. Forecasts show taxable profit of $4m over three years, with nothing reliable after that. A 25% rate has been enacted for the future. What's the asset?","a":"$1.0m","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Rule: recognise only to the extent future taxable profits are probable. Applied: 4m × 25%. Disclose the unrecognised amount. Users: the asset is a real future saving. Common mistake: recognising all the losses, or using the old rate.","i":"c7f106e76f"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"Finn buys 100% of a company for $500,000. Its net assets are $400,000, including inventory at $80,000, worth $90,000. The rate is 30%. What is goodwill?","a":"$93,000","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Step 1: uplift 10,000. Step 2: deferred tax 3,000. Step 3: net assets 400,000 + 10,000 − 3,000 = 407,000. Step 4: 500,000 − 407,000 = 93,000. Common mistake: ignoring the deferred tax gives 90,000.","i":"2048010dfc"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"A parent sells goods to its 80% subsidiary for $500,000 at a 30% margin. One fifth remains unsold. The rate is 20%. What's the deferred tax?","a":"$6,000 asset","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Step 1: unrealised profit 500,000 × 30% × 1/5 = 30,000. Step 2: the group's inventory falls, but the tax base doesn't: a deductible difference. Step 3: × 20% = 6,000. P/L category: Income taxes. Common mistake: applying the NCI percentage when the parent is the seller.","i":"cc9b35e509"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#10","x":"example","t":"A company buys a building for $1m, recognised as PPE, with no tax deductions available. The rate is 20%. What's the deferred tax?","a":"None: initial recognition exemption","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Rule: no deferred tax on initial recognition of an asset outside a business combination that doesn't affect profits or create equal differences. Why: the debit would have nowhere useful to go. Common mistake: posting it to PPE.","i":"997ce180a6"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#11","x":"example","t":"A group holds 30% of an associate with undistributed profits, and it doesn't control dividends. Should deferred tax be recognised on those profits?","a":"Yes","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Rule: no deferred tax applies only if the investor controls the timing of reversal and distribution is improbable. Applied: an investor doesn't control an associate's dividends. Common mistake: treating associates like subsidiaries.","i":"7d4119eb3a"},{"s":"sbr-13-apply-1","c":"sbr-13-apply-1#12","x":"example","t":"Last year's current tax estimate was $100,000, and the bill was $110,000. How's the $10,000 treated?","a":"Added to this year's tax expense","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Rule: tax estimates are settled through the next period's expense. Common mistake: treating it as a prior period error.","i":"572e724103"}],"i":"d1249ac4f4"}],"i":"6ad46a8fe9"}],"i":"1282fa8774"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 15: Related parties","ch":15,"d":"Study Text pages 383–396.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"15.1 IAS 24 Related parties","s":"sbr-15-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#0","x":"card","t":"A related party transaction is a transfer of resources, services or obligations between related parties, whether or not a price is charged","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. It can distort performance, e.g. a parent selling to its subsidiary above market.","i":"4c5be9630e"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#1","x":"card","t":"A person is related if they (or close family) have control, joint control or significant influence over the entity, or are key management of it or its parent","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures.","i":"f220feaf9a"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#2","x":"card","t":"Close family includes children, spouse or partner, the partner's children, and dependants","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Mrs T's influence over B makes A (Mr T's company) and B related.","i":"a90149742d"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#3","x":"card","t":"Entities are related if they're in the same group, one is an associate or JV of the other (or of its group), or both are JVs of, or a JV and associate of, the same third party","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Associates include their subsidiaries.","i":"3ecaceab46"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#4","x":"card","t":"Also related: post-employment plans for staff, entities controlled by a related person, and entities that provide key management services","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Mr P controls A and is KMP of B → A, B and C (B's sub) are related.","i":"eaf72137b8"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#5","x":"card","t":"A person who controls A and has significant influence over B makes A and B related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Control on one side is needed.","i":"d7a5725909"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#6","x":"card","t":"A person with only significant influence over both A and B does not make A and B related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Influence alone on both sides isn't enough.","i":"ae7e318478"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#7","x":"card","t":"Not related just because of a shared director, joint control of the same JV, or being a major customer or supplier","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Substance over form still applies.","i":"a4d786004f"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#8","x":"card","t":"Key management personnel are those responsible for planning, directing and controlling, including all directors, executive or not","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures.","i":"707472c9ef"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#9","x":"card","t":"Parent–subsidiary relationships must always be disclosed, with the parent's name and the ultimate controlling party","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Even if there were no transactions.","i":"03e655e7be"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#10","x":"card","t":"Key management compensation is disclosed in total and split into short-term, post-employment, termination and share-based payments","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rich: a 50% pay rise raises stewardship questions.","i":"92f817e5d7"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#11","x":"card","t":"For related party transactions, disclose the relationship, the transactions and amounts, balances and terms, and allowances and bad debts on those balances","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Patrol: removing JV and associate sales cut growth from 11% to 2.3%.","i":"d82c730f0e"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#12","x":"card","t":"A related party deal may be described as 'arm's length' only if that can be substantiated","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Picture: rent of 10,000 vs a market 100,000 between companies controlled by the same person.","i":"3d96085d9f"},{"s":"sbr-15-1","c":"sbr-15-1#13","x":"card","t":"Entities controlled or influenced by a government may give reduced disclosure of transactions with that government (name, relationship, significant deals)","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Other significant transactions are indicated in aggregate.","i":"afa8b96873"}],"i":"fc34a9e58d"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"15.S Working through IAS 24","s":"sbr-15-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-15-structure-1","c":"sbr-15-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Deciding if two parties are related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. The exclusions are common directors alone, two joint venturers alone, and major customers or suppliers. Not part of it: Treat a major supplier as related because the entity depends on it; Treat two entities as related just because they share a director.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Look at substance, not just legal form","i":"c22ab44a24"},{"x":"step","t":"Is a person or close family member in control, joint control, significant influence or key management?","i":"7c9a5ac979"},{"x":"step","t":"Are the entities in the same group, or an associate or joint venture?","i":"6d180f679e"},{"x":"step","t":"Does a controlling person also influence or manage the other entity?","i":"e4578347f0"},{"x":"step","t":"Check the listed exclusions","i":"5a0d3f5b19"}],"any":true,"i":"e2bae3f4ea"},{"s":"sbr-15-structure-1","c":"sbr-15-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Dealing with a related party transaction","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. A price needn't be charged for it to count. Not part of it: Leave it out if no price was charged; Describe the terms as arm's length as standard wording.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify the relationship","i":"77367c61ad"},{"x":"step","t":"Identify the transaction and balance","i":"075c0d5308"},{"x":"step","t":"Disclose the nature of the relationship","i":"497eaa4144"},{"x":"step","t":"Disclose amounts, balances and any allowance","i":"774a5a52d6"},{"x":"step","t":"Claim arm's length terms only if they can be substantiated","i":"4c25cabd7a"}],"any":true,"i":"c570a7f5a5"},{"s":"sbr-15-structure-1","c":"sbr-15-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Reading a related party note","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Related party sales can be used to prop up revenue.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find related party sales this year and last","i":"d42f3ada07"},{"x":"step","t":"Strip them from revenue","i":"4e2bd1ff87"},{"x":"step","t":"Recalculate growth","i":"41b6ffb7e4"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare with the market","i":"2b8cfff1d7"},{"x":"step","t":"Judge whether underlying performance is as good as it looks","i":"81334fa62e"}],"i":"6df1061120"}],"i":"6162756b76"}],"i":"376550f8be"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"15.C Related or not?","s":"sbr-15-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"A parent and its subsidiary","a":"Related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Same group.","i":"a45c7589a6"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Two fellow subsidiaries","a":"Related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Same group.","i":"9ee3bc2211"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"An entity and its associate","a":"Related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Significant influence.","i":"36f1baeca6"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Two joint ventures of the same third party","a":"Related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Listed in IAS 24.","i":"d225df4e27"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"A joint venture and an associate of the same third party","a":"Related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Listed in IAS 24.","i":"3b7d81f4b5"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"An entity and its employees' pension plan","a":"Related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Listed in IAS 24.","i":"05cfcf03e9"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"An entity and its director's spouse","a":"Related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Close family of key management.","i":"a0f333f4da"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Two entities: one controlled by a person, one significantly influenced by that person","a":"Related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. The second limb of the rule.","i":"3e884518cb"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Two entities significantly influenced by the same person","a":"Not related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Influence over both isn't enough.","i":"a410a84c93"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Two entities with one director in common","a":"Not related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Not on that alone.","i":"9b5d34ef88"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Two joint venturers in one joint venture","a":"Not related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Not on that alone.","i":"8806b71acc"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"An entity and a supplier giving it most of its materials","a":"Not related","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Economic dependence isn't enough.","i":"0f19e82111"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Salaries and bonuses of directors","a":"Short-term benefits","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. One disclosure category.","i":"55f92c4553"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Pension contributions for directors","a":"Post-employment benefits","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Another.","i":"52c78ce84d"},{"s":"sbr-15-compare-1","c":"sbr-15-compare-1#14","x":"sort","t":"Options granted to directors","a":"Share-based payment","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Another.","i":"c0350a9d02"}],"i":"4bfc097baf"}],"i":"bc960056b2"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"15.W Related party traps","s":"sbr-15-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"A related party transaction needs a price to be charged.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. A transfer counts, regardless of price.","i":"8df2ac368c"},{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Related party transactions are a normal part of business.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Users still need to know, as they can distort results.","i":"4e6c199810"},{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"A parent must be named, even if there were no transactions.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Parent and subsidiary relationships are always disclosed.","i":"036e2f59b0"},{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Disclosure can say 'at arm's length' as standard wording.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Only if it can be substantiated.","i":"4cde436561"},{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A non-executive director counts as key management.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Any director, executive or otherwise.","i":"2cd9bfddd2"},{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"A director's child is a close family member.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. So are a spouse or domestic partner, their children, and dependants.","i":"a1d00a09c7"},{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"Companies controlled by one person may keep their deals out of the notes if they aren't a group.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. They're related parties.","i":"9f523ec7a0"},{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Government-related entities must give all the usual disclosures.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. They may use an exemption, with alternative disclosures.","i":"f9afada271"},{"s":"sbr-15-watch-1","c":"sbr-15-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"A big rise in directors' pay is relevant to stewardship.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Users ask if performance justifies it.","i":"af73ba2bf5"}],"i":"7759d42c96"}],"i":"b61b78d065"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"15.A Related party scenarios","s":"sbr-15-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Group: A owns 60% of B and 70% of C, and C owns 35% of D. Who is related to D?","a":"A, B and C","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: an entity is related to an associate of any member of its group. Applied: D is C's associate, and A, B and C are in one group. Common mistake: looking only at the direct holder.","i":"65713d1069"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Dana controls Ash and has significant influence over Birch. Are Ash and Birch related?","a":"Yes","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: a person who controls one entity and significantly influences another makes them related. Common mistake: thinking both must be controlled.","i":"3e1ef0739d"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Dana has significant influence over both Ash and Birch, and controls neither. Are they related?","a":"No","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: significant influence over both isn't enough. Common mistake: treating all shared links the same.","i":"da4627e56c"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Eli owns all of Fern, and is key management of Gale, which owns all of Holly. How is Fern related?","a":"To both Gale and Holly","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: control of one entity plus key management of another makes them related, and this extends to the subsidiary. Common mistake: stopping at Gale.","i":"9796e649d2"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Mr Tate controls Ivy. His spouse has significant influence over Jade. Are Ivy and Jade related?","a":"Yes","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: a close family member is treated with the person. Applied: control plus family significant influence. Common mistake: looking only at one person.","i":"6ce0c4ec51"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"Kay owns 60% of Ash and 70% of Elm. Ash lets a warehouse to Elm at $10,000 a year; the market rent is $100,000. How's this handled in Ash's statements?","a":"Disclose it as a related party transaction","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: the two are related through common control. Applied: disclose the relationship, the rent and the balances; income is at what's contractually due. Users: Ash's profit is lower than it would be at arm's length. Common mistake: assuming no group means no disclosure.","i":"bd6f29f287"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"Revenue rose from $700m to $800m (14.3%), when the market grew 8%. Sales to joint ventures and associates rose from $10m to $60m. What's the underlying growth?","a":"About 7.2%","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: strip out related party sales to see trading with outsiders. Applied: 740 / 690 − 1 = 7.2%, below the market. Users: the headline growth may have been pushed through. Common mistake: taking the headline at face value.","i":"958fef0060"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"Key management pay rose 50% in a year of flat results, mostly from share-based payments. What should a user think?","a":"It may suggest weak stewardship","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: IAS 24 requires key management pay by category. Users: more pay can mean less cash for dividends, and raises governance questions. Common mistake: going beyond the evidence.","i":"3422a93f11"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"A shareholder controls Lark and Moss, which aren't in one group. Lark sells to Moss above normal prices just before the year end, and the finance director doesn't disclose it. What's the issue?","a":"Non-compliance and an ethical failure","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Rule: common control makes them related, so IAS 24 applies. Ethics: non-disclosure hides inflated profit, a breach of integrity and objectivity. Users: Lark's revenue and profit are distorted. Common mistake: thinking only groups are covered.","i":"fb86f61e11"},{"s":"sbr-15-apply-1","c":"sbr-15-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"A notes draft says all related party deals are 'on normal commercial terms', though no evidence has been gathered. What's the problem?","a":"It can't be claimed unless it can be substantiated","d":"Reference: IAS 24 Related Party Disclosures. Rule: arm's length terms can only be stated if substantiated. Common mistake: using it as a boilerplate.","i":"8f411ca4f1"}],"i":"3ebe333565"}],"i":"cecf8d9bf8"}],"i":"200b007980"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 17: Small and medium entities","ch":17,"d":"Study Text pages 405–410.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"17.1 IFRS for SMEs","s":"sbr-17-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-17-1","c":"sbr-17-1#0","x":"card","t":"The SMEs Standard is for entities with no public accountability that publish general purpose statements","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Typically owner-managed, smaller and simpler.","i":"91369b21eb"},{"s":"sbr-17-1","c":"sbr-17-1#1","x":"card","t":"The SMEs Standard has about 300 disclosure points instead of about 3,000","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. One document, updated roughly every three years.","i":"a76d209ff4"},{"s":"sbr-17-1","c":"sbr-17-1#2","x":"card","t":"Topics left out of the SMEs Standard are EPS, interim reporting, segments and assets held for sale","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Their cost outweighs the benefit for SMEs' users.","i":"c066c59606"},{"s":"sbr-17-1","c":"sbr-17-1#3","x":"card","t":"Under the SMEs Standard, goodwill is measured only with the proportionate NCI method and amortised (up to 10 years if its life is unclear)","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. No full-goodwill option and no impairment-only approach.","i":"3f86daa8da"},{"s":"sbr-17-1","c":"sbr-17-1#4","x":"card","t":"Under the SMEs Standard, intangibles use the cost model only, and borrowing costs and R&D are always expensed","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Unclear intangible life = 10 years.","i":"eee534050e"},{"s":"sbr-17-1","c":"sbr-17-1#5","x":"card","t":"Under the SMEs Standard, investment property is at fair value through P/L (cost only if FV involves undue cost or effort)","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Associates can be at cost, fair value or equity method.","i":"2eee406da1"},{"s":"sbr-17-1","c":"sbr-17-1#6","x":"card","t":"Other SME simplifications: depreciation estimates reviewed only on indication, FX not recycled on disposal, and simpler financial instruments (amortised cost for debt, FVPL for shares)","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Shares at cost less impairment if fair value isn't reliable.","i":"53638e1bb7"},{"s":"sbr-17-1","c":"sbr-17-1#7","x":"card","t":"The SMEs Standard saves time and money, but it hurts comparability and is still too complex for many small companies (leases, deferred tax)","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Other drawbacks: hard to define an SME; costly to switch if you outgrow it.","i":"bf4b34c9bd"}],"i":"ad810e255f"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"17.S In order","s":"sbr-17-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-17-structure-1","c":"sbr-17-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Can the SMEs Standard be used?","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. It's about public accountability, not size alone.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Are the entity's debt or equity instruments publicly traded?","i":"14b3cf30d9"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, it can't","i":"212f334d00"},{"x":"step","t":"Does it publish general purpose statements for outside users?","i":"715fac80a9"},{"x":"step","t":"If so, and it has no public accountability, it can","i":"802ffcd5d2"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply the single, simplified standard","i":"57e80d6425"}],"i":"1a73ed81aa"},{"s":"sbr-17-structure-1","c":"sbr-17-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Goodwill under the SMEs Standard","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Full IFRS doesn't amortise goodwill.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Cost of the business combination","i":"d1f07869fa"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct the fair value of net assets acquired","i":"264d4bdbdf"},{"x":"step","t":"No fair value option for the NCI","i":"e5b979d9f9"},{"x":"step","t":"Amortise over its useful life","i":"6373ac39f1"},{"x":"step","t":"If that can't be estimated reliably, use a best estimate of no more than ten years","i":"ac38a057dc"}],"i":"86cad0469a"}],"i":"13d3d22969"}],"i":"6ca5415350"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"17.C Full IFRS or SMEs Standard?","s":"sbr-17-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Borrowing costs on qualifying assets are capitalised","a":"Full IFRS","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Always expensed under the SMEs Standard.","i":"f5d8e6dafd"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Borrowing costs are always expensed","a":"SMEs Standard","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Financing category.","i":"3cafcc9aba"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Development costs are capitalised if the criteria are met","a":"Full IFRS","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. IAS 38.","i":"67ffebcec1"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"All research and development is expensed","a":"SMEs Standard","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Operating category.","i":"4a0c734103"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill is amortised","a":"SMEs Standard","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. No more than ten years if the life is unreliable.","i":"69952da3bf"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill is tested annually, not amortised","a":"Full IFRS","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. IFRS 3 and IAS 36.","i":"e896c8f282"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"The NCI can be measured at fair value","a":"Full IFRS","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Not available in the SMEs Standard.","i":"72e2b384dd"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Intangibles can be revalued where there's an active market","a":"Full IFRS","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Cost model only in the SMEs Standard.","i":"a4f7e7c4d4"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Exchange differences are recycled on disposal of an overseas subsidiary","a":"Full IFRS","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Not recycled under the SMEs Standard.","i":"e083bad237"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Depreciation estimates reviewed only if there's a sign of change","a":"SMEs Standard","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Otherwise reviewed annually under IAS 16.","i":"84482ecffe"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"An EPS figure is covered","a":"Full IFRS","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Omitted from the SMEs Standard.","i":"5daf2c2f98"},{"s":"sbr-17-compare-1","c":"sbr-17-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Associates may be held at cost or fair value in group statements","a":"SMEs Standard","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Simpler alternatives to equity accounting.","i":"6f51034dd9"}],"i":"009aac6804"}],"i":"38279f648a"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"17.W Traps","s":"sbr-17-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-17-watch-1","c":"sbr-17-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"A listed company can use the SMEs Standard if it's small.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. It has public accountability.","i":"984088298c"},{"s":"sbr-17-watch-1","c":"sbr-17-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"The SMEs Standard is updated about every three years.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Less often than full IFRS changes.","i":"773364c4c9"},{"s":"sbr-17-watch-1","c":"sbr-17-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"The SMEs Standard has about 300 disclosure points, against about 3,000.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. A large saving in time and cost.","i":"7a4bead6a8"},{"s":"sbr-17-watch-1","c":"sbr-17-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Segment reporting is required by the SMEs Standard.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. It's omitted, along with EPS, interims and held for sale.","i":"a338646b7d"},{"s":"sbr-17-watch-1","c":"sbr-17-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"Investment property under the SMEs Standard is generally at fair value through P/L.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Cost only if fair value isn't reliable or involves undue cost or effort.","i":"ef9e171abc"},{"s":"sbr-17-watch-1","c":"sbr-17-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"An intangible with an unreliable life is amortised over 10 years.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. The SMEs Standard assumes this.","i":"31b0eca58e"},{"s":"sbr-17-watch-1","c":"sbr-17-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"Comparing an SME with a company using full IFRS is straightforward.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. A stated disadvantage.","i":"83481c21a4"},{"s":"sbr-17-watch-1","c":"sbr-17-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Many say the SMEs Standard is still too complex for small companies.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Especially on leases and deferred tax.","i":"434999cf3a"}],"i":"293c368895"}],"i":"9b24b4fac0"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"17.A Scenarios","s":"sbr-17-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-17-apply-1","c":"sbr-17-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"A family company with no publicly traded shares or debt publishes statements for its bank. A large listed retailer has low profits. Which may use the SMEs Standard?","a":"Only the family company","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Rule: no public accountability, and general purpose statements for outsiders. Common mistake: judging by profit or size.","i":"fe4aeac701"},{"s":"sbr-17-apply-1","c":"sbr-17-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"An SME spends $2m on development meeting all the IAS 38 criteria. What does it do?","a":"Expense $2m","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Rule: the SMEs Standard always expenses research and development. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: applying IAS 38.","i":"da4c16c7d7"},{"s":"sbr-17-apply-1","c":"sbr-17-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"An SME has goodwill of $500,000, and can't reliably estimate its useful life. What's the most it can use as a life?","a":"Ten years","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Rule: goodwill is amortised, with a best estimate of no more than ten years. Applied: at least $50,000 a year. Common mistake: following the full IFRS impairment-only model.","i":"1307a2ff75"},{"s":"sbr-17-apply-1","c":"sbr-17-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"An SME pays $1m of interest on a loan to build its own factory over two years. What does it do with it?","a":"Expense it","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Rule: borrowing costs are always expensed. P/L category: Financing. Common mistake: applying IAS 23.","i":"351b30a256"},{"s":"sbr-17-apply-1","c":"sbr-17-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"An SME buys 80% of a company. Its directors want the NCI at fair value to show full goodwill. Is that allowed?","a":"No: only the proportionate method","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Rule: goodwill is cost less fair value of net assets; the fair value method for the NCI isn't available. Common mistake: assuming IFRS 3's choice carries over.","i":"838736fdd3"},{"s":"sbr-17-apply-1","c":"sbr-17-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"A director asks whether switching to the SMEs Standard could hide poor results. What's the honest view?","a":"It saves cost, but comparability suffers","d":"Reference: IFRS for SMEs Accounting Standard. Rule: the benefits are time and cost savings; the drawbacks include comparability. Ethics: reporting choices shouldn't aim to mislead. Common mistake: treating simplification as concealment.","i":"ff87b1878b"}],"i":"5b62391ed4"}],"i":"def971f3fe"}],"i":"b31346e7da"}],"i":"6f8e6f259b"},{"x":"area","a":"D","t":"D · Financial statements of groups","k":[{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 18: Group accounting – basic groups","ch":18,"d":"Study Text pages 411–472.","k":[{"x":"group","t":"Interests in other entities: which standard","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-1","c":"sbr-18-1#0","x":"card","t":"A parent is an entity that controls another entity (its subsidiary)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. A parent must prepare consolidated statements unless exempt.","i":"c202388680"},{"x":"group","t":"Subsidiary (control): consolidate it, IFRS 10 and IFRS 3","i":"ead2bec445"},{"x":"group","t":"Associate (significant influence): the equity method, IAS 28","i":"c1c3218303"},{"x":"group","t":"Joint arrangement (joint control): IFRS 11, a joint operation or a joint venture","i":"fd5c54211b"},{"x":"group","t":"No control, joint control or influence: an investment under IFRS 9","i":"3e780c5660"},{"x":"group","t":"What kind of investment?","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-compare-1","c":"sbr-18-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"60% of the votes, no other arrangements","a":"Subsidiary","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Control is normally presumed above 50%.","i":"aa5759d5a5"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-1","c":"sbr-18-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"16% of the shares, two of six board seats and key supplier links","a":"Associate","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Significant influence can exist below 20% when there's board representation, material transactions or shared managers.","i":"80c7732eca"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-1","c":"sbr-18-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"50% of a separate company; key decisions need both owners' consent; rights to its net assets","a":"Joint venture","d":"Reference: IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements. Joint control over a separate vehicle with rights to net assets is a joint venture.","i":"2bfd41803e"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-1","c":"sbr-18-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"8% of a listed company with no influence","a":"Investment (IFRS 9)","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Below significant influence: a financial asset.","i":"0b79e4e734"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-1","c":"sbr-18-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"40% of the votes, the rest held by thousands of tiny shareholders, and currently exercisable options taking it to 60%","a":"Subsidiary","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Dispersed holders and substantive potential voting rights can give control below 50%.","i":"2434cfbcfb"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-1","c":"sbr-18-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Two companies share a machine, each owning half of it and owing half of its loan, no separate entity","a":"Joint operation","d":"Reference: IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements. Rights to assets and obligations for liabilities: each books its share.","i":"095c443677"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-1","c":"sbr-18-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Two companies own a separate company and share its profits","a":"Joint venture","d":"Reference: IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements. Rights to the net assets of a separate vehicle: equity method.","i":"c460448093"}],"i":"99130a8ed7"}],"i":"4ea3c3d25b"},{"x":"group","t":"Control (IFRS 10)","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-1","c":"sbr-18-1#1","x":"card","t":"An investor controls an investee if it has power, exposure to variable returns, and the ability to use its power to affect those returns","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. All three are needed.","i":"99213443f2"},{"x":"group","t":"Power without a majority of votes","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-1","c":"sbr-18-1#3","x":"card","t":"Power can exist with under 50% of votes through contracts, potential voting rights, relationships, or a large holding among many small, dispersed shareholders","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Parsley: 40% + currently exercisable convertibles + synergies → control.","i":"143a42a7a9"},{"s":"sbr-18-1","c":"sbr-18-1#4","x":"card","t":"Potential voting rights count towards control if they are currently exercisable and substantive","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Even if slightly out of the money, if the holder would benefit (synergies).","i":"fd239fe76f"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"Owning under 50% of the votes can never give control.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Contracts, dispersed other holders, substantive potential voting rights or other relationships can give control.","i":"ee6a040650"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Potential voting rights that are slightly out of the money can still be substantive.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. If they are currently exercisable and the holder would benefit (e.g. synergies), they count.","i":"8a9d796b85"}],"i":"fead325050"},{"s":"sbr-18-1","c":"sbr-18-1#2","x":"card","t":"Consolidated statements present the parent and subsidiaries as a single economic entity","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. The parent's own statements show distributable reserves but are no substitute.","i":"c0424b6a67"},{"x":"group","t":"Groups in a chain","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"If P controls Q and Q controls R, R is a subsidiary of P even if P's effective interest is below 50%.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Control passes down the chain; the effective interest only decides the NCI share.","i":"b6fdd79dab"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-1","c":"sbr-18-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"P owns 70% of Q; Q owns 60% of R. How is R treated in P's group statements?","a":"Consolidated as a subsidiary (P controls it through Q), with NCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: control flows down the chain. Applied: P controls Q, which controls R, so R is in P's group. The effective 42% (70% × 60%) only sets the NCI share in R's post-acquisition profits. P/L category: Consolidated line by line; NCI shares R's profit. Users: Group figures include all of R; the large NCI (58%) tells investors how much belongs to others. Common mistake: using the effective interest to decide control.","i":"9837789409"}],"i":"f2b5f5f8df"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-apply-1","c":"sbr-18-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Wren Ltd owns 15% of Finch. It appoints one of four directors and supplies the technology Finch depends on; the other shares are widely held. How is Finch accounted for in Wren's group statements?","a":"As an associate, using the equity method","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Rule: significant influence (power to participate in policy decisions) is presumed at 20–50% but can exist below. Applied: a board seat and essential technology are classic indicators, so Finch is an associate. P/L category: Share of the associate's profit goes to Investing. Users: Investors see Finch's results as one line, not line by line; its debts stay off the group balance sheet. Common mistake: stopping at the 20% presumption.","i":"4f84a30e7f"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-1","c":"sbr-18-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Heron holds 45% of Lark's votes; the rest is spread over thousands of small holders. Heron also holds options, exercisable now, that would take it to 58%; they're slightly out of the money but would bring big synergies.","a":"Lark is a subsidiary: consolidate it","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: control needs power, exposure to variable returns and the link between them; substantive, currently exercisable potential voting rights count. Applied: a large holding among dispersed owners plus options Heron would benefit from exercising give power. P/L category: Consolidated line by line; NCI share of profit shown separately. Users: Gearing, revenue and margins now include Lark's figures in full. Common mistake: ignoring options because they're not in the money.","i":"d4c238b2f7"}],"i":"788f9f3b8d"}],"i":"4024c78560"},{"x":"group","t":"The workings (from FR)","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-structure-3","c":"sbr-18-structure-3#0","x":"steps","t":"The five workings for a group statement of financial position","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Every correction lands in one of these; knowing the map tells you which figures move.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"W1 Group structure: who is a subsidiary, associate, joint venture","i":"10bf169939"},{"x":"step","t":"W2 Subsidiary's net assets at acquisition and at the reporting date","i":"f0b659d004"},{"x":"step","t":"W3 Goodwill","i":"0c0a5931fb"},{"x":"step","t":"W4 Non-controlling interest","i":"8bc03c4c19"},{"x":"step","t":"W5 Group reserves","i":"12e1cd900c"}],"i":"762f3e9dad"},{"x":"group","t":"Consolidating the statement of financial position","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#0","x":"card","t":"To consolidate the SoFP, add parent and subsidiary assets and liabilities in full, replace the investment with goodwill, and show only the parent's share capital and premium","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. NCI and group reserves come from the workings.","i":"9294331cf9"},{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#1","x":"card","t":"W1, the group structure, decides each investment's status: over 50% usually a subsidiary; 20–50% an associate","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Borough: 45/75 = 60% subsidiary; 10.5/35 = 30% associate.","i":"5b44bdfe52"},{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#8","x":"card","t":"Intra-group receivables and payables are cancelled against each other","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). Cash in transit is added to group cash first if needed.","i":"8eb8280546"}],"i":"b3422afc5f"},{"x":"group","t":"W2 the subsidiary's net assets","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#2","x":"card","t":"W2 shows the subsidiary's net assets at acquisition and at the reporting date, including fair value adjustments and their extra depreciation","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Also the sub's own PURP if it's the seller; exclude any goodwill in the sub's books.","i":"8d3fc7fd8c"},{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#9","x":"card","t":"Extra depreciation on a fair value uplift reduces the subsidiary's post-acquisition reserves (and NCI)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Borough: 15,000 over 5 years = 3,000 a year.","i":"884a9a41e9"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-3","c":"sbr-18-structure-3#1","x":"steps","t":"Building W2 for a subsidiary","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. The movement between the two columns is the post-acquisition profit shared with the NCI.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Share capital and reserves (at acquisition and at the reporting date)","i":"f64de6e944"},{"x":"step","t":"Remove any goodwill in the subsidiary's own books","i":"ddb820b6d7"},{"x":"step","t":"Add fair value adjustments","i":"92da76c521"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct extra depreciation on them since acquisition","i":"6c4dc995d6"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct PURP where the subsidiary is the seller","i":"aacdc07642"}],"i":"d828ac1767"}],"i":"439d7d53bf"},{"x":"group","t":"W3 goodwill","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#3","x":"card","t":"W3 goodwill = consideration + NCI at acquisition − fair value of identifiable net assets at acquisition","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Then subtract impairment to date.","i":"071cc127df"},{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#4","x":"card","t":"Under the full goodwill method, NCI at acquisition is measured at its fair value","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Under the proportionate method: NCI % × fair value of net assets.","i":"415034713e"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-3","c":"sbr-18-structure-3#2","x":"steps","t":"Goodwill (W3)","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. A negative figure is a bargain purchase: re-check the numbers, then a gain in P/L.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Fair value of consideration","i":"129037e114"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI at acquisition (fair value or share of net assets)","i":"ab5ce22952"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct fair value of identifiable net assets at acquisition (W2)","i":"eab0314951"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct impairment to date","i":"61bc60531a"}],"i":"c3565905d3"}],"i":"22a97ccd63"},{"x":"group","t":"W4 non-controlling interest","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#5","x":"card","t":"W4 NCI = NCI at acquisition + NCI % of post-acquisition net asset changes − NCI share of goodwill impairment (fair value method only)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Borough: 55,000 + 8,800 − 2,800 = 61,000.","i":"309a8a29b9"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-3","c":"sbr-18-structure-3#4","x":"steps","t":"Non-controlling interest (W4)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. So any adjustment inside W2 changes the NCI.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"NCI at acquisition (as in W3)","i":"4615f1cf5d"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI share of post-acquisition change in net assets (W2)","i":"1703d9ccba"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct NCI share of goodwill impairment (fair value method only)","i":"ac7af42ecc"}],"i":"2fddc1bd79"}],"i":"cef855e574"},{"x":"group","t":"W5 group retained earnings","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#6","x":"card","t":"W5 retained earnings = parent's + group share of post-acquisition profits − group impairment − PURP where the parent is the seller","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Plus any bargain purchase gain; parent-level adjustments (like a missed provision) also go here.","i":"253d464a86"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-3","c":"sbr-18-structure-3#3","x":"steps","t":"Group retained earnings (W5)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Parent-level corrections (fees, unwinding, parent's PURP) hit here only.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Parent's retained earnings","i":"1cfb89d166"},{"x":"step","t":"Add group share of the subsidiary's post-acquisition reserves (W2)","i":"5e00dbc899"},{"x":"step","t":"Add group share of associates' post-acquisition reserves","i":"e154620730"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct group share of goodwill impairment","i":"cc8fb2c8da"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct PURP where the parent is the seller and other parent-level adjustments","i":"c231427c3b"}],"i":"6b0c58627a"}],"i":"19e2ea8a18"},{"x":"group","t":"Unrealised profit (PURP)","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-2","c":"sbr-18-2#7","x":"card","t":"Unrealised profit (PURP) is the profit on intra-group sales still in group inventory at the year end","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). Borough: 50% margin on 10,000, 20% unsold → 1,000.","i":"d9841a1d08"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#6","x":"trap","t":"PURP on goods sold by the parent to the subsidiary doesn't reduce the NCI.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). The parent made the profit, so only group reserves are reduced.","i":"27c23c4e45"}],"i":"1c8d7f446a"},{"x":"group","t":"Where each adjustment lands","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Uplifting the subsidiary's land to fair value at acquisition","a":"Goodwill (W3) only","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. It raises net assets at acquisition, lowering goodwill; land isn't depreciated, so no later effect.","i":"637b2ca97c"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Extra depreciation on a fair value uplift since acquisition","a":"Sub's net assets (W2): shared by group and NCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. It reduces the subsidiary's post-acquisition profit, shared with the NCI.","i":"005abcbfa3"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Unrealised profit on goods sold by the subsidiary to the parent","a":"Sub's net assets (W2): shared by group and NCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). The subsidiary made the profit, so NCI shares the adjustment.","i":"8a92b959e7"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Unrealised profit on goods sold by the parent to the subsidiary","a":"Group reserves (W5) only","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). The parent made the profit, so only group reserves are reduced.","i":"08dfdace3f"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"The P/L charge when acquisition legal fees are moved out of goodwill","a":"Group reserves (W5) only","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. The parent incurred the cost, so the expense reduces group reserves only (goodwill falls separately).","i":"d8b02c4005"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Recognising contingent consideration at fair value at acquisition","a":"Goodwill (W3) only","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. It raises consideration and so goodwill; the unwinding later is a parent finance cost.","i":"d9e0fb2b7b"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Unwinding the discount on deferred consideration","a":"Group reserves (W5) only","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. The parent owes the consideration, so the finance cost reduces group reserves only.","i":"164592a3b2"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"A provision the subsidiary should have made after acquisition","a":"Sub's net assets (W2): shared by group and NCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. It reduces the subsidiary's post-acquisition profit, shared with the NCI.","i":"62d7555c57"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Cancelling intra-group receivables and payables","a":"No effect on the workings","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). Both sides cancel; no profit effect.","i":"cd183fa3b1"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill impairment when NCI is measured at fair value","a":"Shared between group reserves and NCI","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Full goodwill includes the NCI's goodwill, so the loss is shared.","i":"97c809c5bb"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-2","c":"sbr-18-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill impairment when NCI is measured at its share of net assets","a":"Group reserves only","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Only the parent's goodwill is recognised, so only the parent bears the loss.","i":"354f7be252"}],"i":"a4f3b3c10f"},{"x":"group","t":"The consolidated P/L","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-3","c":"sbr-18-3#0","x":"card","t":"Consolidated P/L adds parent and subsidiary items line by line, using only post-acquisition results","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Mid-year acquisitions are pro-rated.","i":"3521dba804"},{"s":"sbr-18-3","c":"sbr-18-3#1","x":"card","t":"Intra-group sales are removed by Dr revenue, Cr cost of sales","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). Then add the PURP to cost of sales.","i":"e03506eb31"},{"s":"sbr-18-3","c":"sbr-18-3#2","x":"card","t":"Dividends received from a subsidiary are removed from consolidated P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. The group can't earn income from itself.","i":"d999850515"},{"s":"sbr-18-3","c":"sbr-18-3#3","x":"card","t":"Extra depreciation on fair value uplifts is charged to cost of sales or wherever depreciation is shown","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Illustration 2: 10,000 / 10 years = 1,000.","i":"a0b91c7ffd"},{"s":"sbr-18-3","c":"sbr-18-3#4","x":"card","t":"The NCI's share of profit = subsidiary profit − sub-seller PURP − extra depreciation − impairment (FV method only), times the NCI %","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Parent-seller PURP doesn't affect the NCI.","i":"a6d8e6b398"},{"s":"sbr-18-3","c":"sbr-18-3#5","x":"card","t":"Under the proportionate method, a goodwill impairment is borne entirely by the parent","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Under the fair value method it's split by ownership.","i":"321f509686"},{"s":"sbr-18-3","c":"sbr-18-3#6","x":"card","t":"Profit and total comprehensive income are each split between owners of the parent and the NCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. The parent's share is the balancing figure.","i":"cb3847ba5f"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Intra-group sales and balances are eliminated","a":"Subsidiary (consolidation)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). The associate is outside the group: only the group's share of unrealised profit is removed.","i":"15356c8f88"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#7","x":"trap","t":"Dividends received from a subsidiary are eliminated from consolidated P/L.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. They are intra-group and are eliminated.","i":"ecc1930e28"}],"i":"016a46ed9d"},{"s":"sbr-18-1","c":"sbr-18-1#6","x":"card","t":"A subsidiary bought mid-year is consolidated from the date control is gained","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Its results are time-apportioned (covered further in chapter 19).","i":"9bf762880d"}],"i":"b5fc6b97c0"},{"x":"group","t":"Associates (IAS 28)","k":[{"x":"group","t":"Significant influence","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#0","x":"card","t":"An associate is an entity over which the investor has significant influence (not control or joint control)","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures.","i":"43c970ccbb"},{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#1","x":"card","t":"Significant influence is the power to participate in policy decisions; it's presumed at 20–50% of the votes","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. It can exist below 20%.","i":"938e93885e"},{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#2","x":"card","t":"Evidence of significant influence includes a board seat, policy input, material transactions, shared managers and essential technical information","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Sparrow: influence exists, so equity accounting, not a financial asset.","i":"c5e4c04920"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Owning under 20% means an investment can't be an associate.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. 20% is only a presumption; board seats, key transactions or shared managers can give significant influence.","i":"d6e2a67397"}],"i":"e7a4efef86"},{"x":"group","t":"The equity method","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#3","x":"card","t":"Associates are accounted for using the equity method, not consolidation","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. No line-by-line adding.","i":"494bba951f"},{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#4","x":"card","t":"The investment in an associate = cost + share of post-acquisition reserves − impairment − share of PURP (parent seller) and of extra depreciation","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Paint: 2m + 120k − 12k − 50k = 2,058k.","i":"e9835b6901"},{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#5","x":"card","t":"Share of the associate's profit (investing) = share of profit after tax − impairment − share of PURP (associate seller) and extra depreciation","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Paint: 120k − 50k − 12k − 30k = 28k.","i":"575b7e44d0"},{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#8","x":"card","t":"Dividends received from an associate are removed from P/L and reduce the investment","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Profit is already counted through the share of profit.","i":"159a7e1575"},{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#9","x":"card","t":"The associate's statements must be within three months of the investor's date and use the same accounting policies","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. The investor also discloses its share of the associate's contingencies.","i":"d7255a2a7f"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Shown as one line in the investing category of P/L","a":"Associate (equity method)","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Share of profit after tax, less impairment and adjustments.","i":"f71503d8e0"}],"i":"06e7ac5077"},{"x":"group","t":"Transactions with the associate","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#6","x":"card","t":"Transactions and balances with an associate are not eliminated, but the group share of unrealised profit is","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. The associate isn't part of the group.","i":"a8c65bcdd0"},{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#7","x":"card","t":"When the associate sells to the group, the PURP adjustment is Dr share of associate's profit, Cr inventory","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. When the group sells to the associate: Dr cost of sales, Cr investment.","i":"596d3c9daa"},{"s":"sbr-18-4","c":"sbr-18-4#10","x":"card","t":"When an investor contributes an asset to an associate, it recognises only the part of the gain attributable to the other investors","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. The rest is unrealised.","i":"08403d4546"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#8","x":"trap","t":"Transactions with an associate are fully cancelled on consolidation.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Only the group's share of unrealised profit is removed; balances stay.","i":"85bcb33bb4"}],"i":"b26c6bcda5"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked example","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-apply-3","c":"sbr-18-apply-3#2","x":"example","t":"A company buys 25% of an associate for 300. In the year after, the associate earns 120 and pays a dividend from which the investor receives 8. What is the investment at year end?","a":"322","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Rule: equity method: cost + share of post-acquisition profit − dividends received. Applied: 300 + 30 (25% × 120) − 8 = 322; the 30 is shown as share of profit (investing) and the dividend isn't income in the group P/L. P/L category: Share of profit (30) in Investing; the dividend isn't group income. Users: Investors see associates' results as one line, so margins aren't affected. Common mistake: 330 (keeping the dividend as well).","i":"5db73fee9e"}],"i":"c0437406fb"}],"i":"36c87a10af"},{"x":"group","t":"Is it a business? (IFRS 3)","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-6","c":"sbr-18-6#0","x":"card","t":"The acquisition method applies only when the entity acquired is a business; otherwise it's an asset purchase","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Asset purchases give no goodwill.","i":"e6fdbaf9b1"},{"s":"sbr-18-6","c":"sbr-18-6#1","x":"card","t":"A business has inputs and processes that together can contribute to creating outputs","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Processes can be documented systems or the know-how of a skilled workforce.","i":"56c7b4c1a7"},{"x":"group","t":"The concentration test","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-6","c":"sbr-18-6#2","x":"card","t":"The optional concentration test says it's not a business if substantially all the fair value is in one asset or group of similar assets","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Taco/Fajita: the 20 houses → not a business.","i":"6f383b58cd"},{"s":"sbr-18-6","c":"sbr-18-6#3","x":"card","t":"Houses and office blocks of similar value fail the concentration test because they are not similar assets","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Shepherd/Pie: the test fails, but with no workforce or substantive process it's still not a business.","i":"662a8b97f5"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Buying a company that owns only identical let warehouses is always a business combination.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. If the concentration test is met (substantially all fair value in similar assets), it's an asset acquisition.","i":"6798ec1020"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"A set of similar assets bought with no workforce or processes","a":"Asset acquisition","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. No goodwill; cost is allocated to the assets.","i":"d4da3968cf"}],"i":"cf3cd528a1"},{"x":"group","t":"A substantive process","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-6","c":"sbr-18-6#4","x":"card","t":"Without outputs, an acquired process is substantive only if it's critical and there's an organised, skilled workforce to perform it","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Tahini/Kofta: skilled scientists + R&D → a business.","i":"bd26f0d3a7"},{"s":"sbr-18-6","c":"sbr-18-6#5","x":"card","t":"With outputs, a process is substantive if it's critical with a skilled workforce, or it significantly contributes and is rare or hard to replace","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Outputs aren't required for a business to exist.","i":"a205ce3df6"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"A company with no revenue yet can still be a business.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. If it has inputs and a substantive process, e.g. an organised skilled workforce.","i":"8138850a51"}],"i":"5dbc435d7d"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-apply-1","c":"sbr-18-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"A company buys all the shares of a business owning 12 almost identical warehouses let to tenants. It has no staff. How is it accounted for?","a":"As an asset acquisition: no goodwill","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: the optional concentration test treats a purchase as not a business when substantially all the fair value is in one asset or a group of similar assets. Applied: similar warehouses, no workforce or processes. P/L category: No goodwill; later rent income and any fair value changes follow IAS 40 (Investing if investment property). Users: No goodwill and no deferred tax on initial recognition, so net assets and future impairment risk differ from a business combination. Common mistake: assuming buying shares always means a business combination.","i":"1c7246b95f"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-1","c":"sbr-18-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A company buys a start-up with no revenue yet, but with a team of skilled engineers developing a product, a lab and equipment of similar value.","a":"A business combination: inputs plus a substantive process (the skilled team)","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: outputs aren't required; without outputs, a process is substantive if it's critical and an organised skilled workforce comes with it. Applied: the engineers can turn the inputs into products, and the concentration test fails (assets of similar value). P/L category: Goodwill isn't amortised but tested for impairment (any loss in Operating). Users: Goodwill reflects the team's value; impairment risk is high if the product fails. Common mistake: 'no revenue, so no business'.","i":"3bc3560c22"}],"i":"6c3b0f9f33"}],"i":"3190a70a88"},{"x":"group","t":"Acquisition accounting (IFRS 3)","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#0","x":"card","t":"The acquisition method: identify the acquirer, set the acquisition date, measure net assets, then recognise goodwill (or a bargain gain) and NCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Applied only at the date control is gained.","i":"1ac19e7f8c"},{"x":"group","t":"The acquirer","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#1","x":"card","t":"The acquirer is usually the one paying cash or assets, or else the one issuing shares, and typically has the dominant management, the largest voting share and the bigger size","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Abacus/Calculator via Phone: Abacus owners 60%, 4 of 6 directors → Abacus is the acquirer.","i":"6c89d7a862"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"The company that issues the new shares is always the acquirer.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Look at substance: whose owners hold most votes, whose management dominates, which is bigger.","i":"17202f5827"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-1","c":"sbr-18-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"NewCo is set up to combine Alder and Birch. It issues shares to both sets of owners: Alder's owners get 65% of the votes and 5 of 7 board seats; Alder is the larger company. Who is the acquirer?","a":"Alder","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: when no cash is paid, the share issuer is usually the acquirer, but look at the substance: who holds most votes, whose management dominates, which is larger. Applied: all point to Alder; NewCo is just a vehicle. P/L category: No P/L effect (identifies whose assets are remeasured to fair value). Users: Birch's net assets are fair-valued and goodwill arises on Birch, not Alder: this shapes the group's asset base. Common mistake: choosing NewCo because it issued the shares.","i":"09bb73414c"}],"i":"68f965f549"},{"x":"group","t":"The acquisition date","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#2","x":"card","t":"The acquisition date is when control is obtained","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Goodwill is measured and consolidation starts then.","i":"f6b26dd076"}],"i":"b6deb7a94d"},{"x":"group","t":"Identifiable net assets at fair value","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#3","x":"card","t":"Identifiable assets and liabilities are measured at fair value at the acquisition date","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. They must meet the CF definitions and be part of the exchange.","i":"d3b232e04c"},{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#4","x":"card","t":"An acquired contingent liability is recognised at fair value, even if an outflow isn't probable","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. P/S example: $1m claim, unlikely → recognised at its $100k fair value.","i":"3a0ff98f5d"},{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#5","x":"card","t":"The acquirer's planned restructuring or future losses are not liabilities at acquisition","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. P/S: $200k redundancy plan is post-acquisition.","i":"3b161d3c31"},{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#6","x":"card","t":"The subsidiary's internally generated brand is recognised at fair value in the group","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Separable or legal intangibles are recognised even if the sub couldn't.","i":"e3902b8faa"},{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#7","x":"card","t":"Goodwill already in the subsidiary's own books is excluded (it isn't identifiable)","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. New goodwill is calculated from scratch.","i":"fd665954df"},{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#8","x":"card","t":"Exceptions to fair value: items under IAS 12, IAS 19, IFRS 2 and IFRS 5","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. E.g. deferred tax is measured under IAS 12.","i":"12660357e5"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"In an acquisition: a present obligation of the subsidiary whose outflow is only possible","a":"Recognised at fair value (IFRS 3)","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. In a business combination it is recognised at fair value even if an outflow isn't probable.","i":"7d0e801236"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"A present obligation whose outflow is only possible, in the subsidiary's own statements","a":"Only disclosed (IAS 37)","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. In single-entity statements IAS 37 applies.","i":"70ee2f1dd7"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#0","x":"trap","t":"The subsidiary's own goodwill is left out of its net assets at acquisition.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It isn't identifiable; goodwill is worked out afresh.","i":"a9bb360642"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#1","x":"trap","t":"A restructuring the buyer plans after acquisition is a liability at acquisition.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. No obligation exists at the acquisition date; it's a post-acquisition matter.","i":"4695ddf322"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-2","c":"sbr-18-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"At acquisition a subsidiary's book net assets are 500, including its own goodwill of 40. Land is worth 60 more than book; an unrecognised customer list is worth 80; a lawsuit (outflow not probable) has a fair value of 30; the buyer plans a 50 restructuring. What are the identifiable net assets?","a":"570","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: identifiable net assets at fair value; exclude the subsidiary's goodwill; include intangibles and contingent liabilities at fair value; exclude the buyer's plans. Applied: 500 − 40 + 60 + 80 − 30 = 570. P/L category: No P/L effect at acquisition; the fair value uplifts later bring extra depreciation or amortisation in Operating. Users: Higher net assets mean lower goodwill and higher future charges. Common mistakes: 610 (keeping its goodwill), 520 (deducting the restructuring), 600 (ignoring the contingent liability).","i":"aec3db91e5"}],"i":"1c2eebfc08"},{"x":"group","t":"Consideration","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-8","c":"sbr-18-8#0","x":"card","t":"Consideration is measured at fair value: cash, deferred cash at PV, shares at fair value, and contingent consideration at fair value even if payment isn't probable","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. P/S: 250k × 50% / 1.1 for the contingent part.","i":"34f6f99ae0"},{"s":"sbr-18-8","c":"sbr-18-8#1","x":"card","t":"Acquisition-related legal and professional fees are expensed, not part of consideration","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Issue costs of debt or shares follow IFRS 9 / IAS 32.","i":"b3036b7fa4"},{"s":"sbr-18-8","c":"sbr-18-8#2","x":"card","t":"When the acquirer replaces the sub's share options, the part counted as consideration is capped at the value of the original award; the rest is post-acquisition expense","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. P/S: 400k in consideration, 100k expensed.","i":"7112a815ed"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Legal fees for an acquisition","a":"Expensed","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Acquisition-related costs are expensed (debt or equity issue costs follow IFRS 9 / IAS 32).","i":"474d7a14ce"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Contingent consideration","a":"Part of consideration","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. At fair value, even if payment isn't probable.","i":"3c0b2a7d76"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#2","x":"trap","t":"Deferred consideration is included in goodwill at the amount to be paid.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. At fair value: the present value at the acquisition date.","i":"1ee52e07c8"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#9","x":"trap","t":"Legal fees for issuing the shares used as consideration are expensed like other acquisition costs.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Share issue costs are deducted from equity (IAS 32); only the other acquisition costs are expensed.","i":"5566de022e"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-2","c":"sbr-18-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"Consideration: 400 cash now; 220 payable in one year (discount rate 10%); 50,000 shares worth $4 each; contingent consideration with a fair value of 90; legal fees 25. What is the consideration for goodwill?","a":"890","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Rule: consideration at fair value; deferred cash at present value; legal fees expensed. Applied: 400 + 200 (220 / 1.1) + 200 + 90 = 890. P/L category: Legal fees 25 go to Operating; the discount on the deferred cash unwinds in Financing. Users: Debt-like liabilities (deferred and contingent consideration) raise gearing. Common mistakes: 915 (adding fees), 910 (deferred at 220), 800 (leaving out contingent consideration).","i":"77b60367bc"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-2","c":"sbr-18-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"The buyer must replace the target's share options. The target's award is fully vested and worth 300 at acquisition; the replacement award, with no further conditions, is worth 360.","a":"300 in consideration; 60 expensed immediately","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 2 Share-based Payment. Rule: the part of a replacement award for pre-acquisition service is consideration, capped at the value of the original award; the rest is post-acquisition remuneration. Applied: 300 consideration; the extra 60 has no future service condition, so it's expensed at once. P/L category: 60 in Operating (employee costs). Users: Part of the deal's cost appears as an expense, reducing first-year profit. Common mistake: putting all 360 in goodwill.","i":"0d67b00eac"}],"i":"e29a863bb1"},{"x":"group","t":"Goodwill and the NCI","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-8","c":"sbr-18-8#3","x":"card","t":"The NCI measurement method (proportionate or fair value) is chosen acquisition by acquisition","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. So one group can use both.","i":"3af48bd0f9"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill includes the NCI's share","a":"NCI at fair value","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. The 'full goodwill' method.","i":"cfdd9c5606"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#10","x":"trap","t":"The NCI method (fair value or proportionate) can differ from one acquisition to the next.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It's chosen acquisition by acquisition.","i":"5722b44bec"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-2","c":"sbr-18-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"Consideration 890 for 80%; identifiable net assets 570; fair value of the 20% NCI 150. Goodwill under the fair value method and the proportionate method?","a":"470 and 434","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: goodwill = consideration + NCI − net assets. Applied: fair value: 890 + 150 − 570 = 470; proportionate: NCI = 20% × 570 = 114, so 890 + 114 − 570 = 434. The difference (36) is the NCI's goodwill. P/L category: No P/L effect at acquisition. Users: The fair value method shows higher goodwill and NCI; impairments later are larger and shared with NCI. Common mistake: leaving the NCI out (890 − 570 = 320).","i":"1138e43b44"},{"x":"group","t":"A bargain purchase","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-8","c":"sbr-18-8#4","x":"card","t":"A bargain purchase means the net assets exceed the consideration; after re-checking the figures, the gain goes to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. IFRS 3 says it's rare and may signal errors.","i":"8c492e519f"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#5","x":"trap","t":"A gain on a bargain purchase is credited to P/L after the figures have been re-checked.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. IFRS 3 asks for a review first, because negative goodwill is rare and may signal errors.","i":"a820924a51"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-2","c":"sbr-18-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"Consideration 400; NCI (proportionate) 100; identifiable net assets 560. What happens?","a":"Re-check the figures; if correct, a 60 bargain gain in P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: a bargain purchase is rare; IFRS 3 asks you to review every figure, then recognise the gain in P/L. Applied: 400 + 100 − 560 = −60. P/L category: A bargain purchase gain in P/L (Operating). Users: A one-off gain flatters profit; users should strip it out when judging sustainable earnings. Common mistake: carrying negative goodwill on the balance sheet.","i":"5814dcd069"}],"i":"86c7092866"}],"i":"89cede6f01"},{"x":"group","t":"After the acquisition date","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-7","c":"sbr-18-7#9","x":"card","t":"During the measurement period, provisional amounts are adjusted retrospectively for new facts about acquisition-date conditions, for up to 12 months","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Goodwill is recalculated.","i":"9a03ddc1dd"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#10","x":"sort","t":"A fair value change in contingent consideration after acquisition","a":"Goes to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Unless it reflects facts at acquisition found within the measurement period.","i":"e7383cc7a9"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#11","x":"sort","t":"New information within 12 months about facts at the acquisition date","a":"Adjusts goodwill","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. A measurement-period adjustment, made retrospectively.","i":"01ac105c26"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#3","x":"trap","t":"A change in the fair value of contingent consideration a year later normally goes to P/L, not goodwill.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It goes to P/L, unless it's a measurement-period adjustment for facts existing at acquisition.","i":"e72cf38b5f"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#11","x":"trap","t":"Measurement-period adjustments can be made at any time.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Only within 12 months of the acquisition date, and only for facts that existed then.","i":"95e7a14cae"}],"i":"6c8951f642"},{"x":"group","t":"Correcting a draft","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-8","c":"sbr-18-8#5","x":"card","t":"Correcting missed contingent consideration increases goodwill and creates a liability that unwinds with interest (financing)","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Fish/Lobster: 4m added to goodwill; later fair value changes go to P/L.","i":"35006ab309"},{"s":"sbr-18-8","c":"sbr-18-8#6","x":"card","t":"Missed fair value uplifts on land reduce goodwill and increase PPE","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Fish/Lobster: land +1m → goodwill −1m.","i":"b673b1846a"},{"s":"sbr-18-8","c":"sbr-18-8#7","x":"card","t":"A recognised acquired brand reduces goodwill and is then amortised over its useful life","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Fish/Lobster: 5m brand, 1m amortisation a year (shared with NCI under FV method).","i":"e37732ea53"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-1","c":"sbr-18-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"A fair value uplift on the subsidiary's machinery was missed at acquisition","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. An uplift changes goodwill at acquisition and, through depreciation, the post-acquisition profit that the NCI shares.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Dr PPE, Cr goodwill (the uplift raises net assets, so goodwill falls)","i":"b4137ddc52"},{"x":"step","t":"Work out the extra depreciation since acquisition","i":"c09ac88339"},{"x":"step","t":"Dr P/L (operating), Cr PPE for the extra depreciation","i":"98ab70ac34"},{"x":"step","t":"Share that depreciation between group reserves and NCI (it is post-acquisition in W2)","i":"f84b5d7ef9"}],"i":"2aed7fd320"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-1","c":"sbr-18-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Contingent consideration was left out of goodwill","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Goodwill is fixed at the acquisition date (apart from measurement-period adjustments); later remeasurements are P/L.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Measure it at fair value at the acquisition date (probability and discounting built in)","i":"c608807144"},{"x":"step","t":"Dr goodwill, Cr liability","i":"6e0d5eb653"},{"x":"step","t":"Unwind the discount each year: Dr finance cost (financing), Cr liability","i":"2d93b70019"},{"x":"step","t":"Later changes in its fair value: to P/L, not to goodwill","i":"8eb2097ef0"}],"i":"b263e3fa8e"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-1","c":"sbr-18-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Deferred cash consideration was recorded at its full future amount","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Consideration is measured at fair value; for deferred cash that is its present value.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Remeasure it at present value at the acquisition date","i":"759e1c1972"},{"x":"step","t":"Reduce goodwill and the liability by the discount","i":"0daaa81853"},{"x":"step","t":"Unwind the discount each year: Dr finance cost (financing), Cr liability","i":"b3bcf95b87"}],"i":"fba2a420c8"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-1","c":"sbr-18-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Acquisition legal fees were added to goodwill","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. IFRS 3: acquisition-related costs are expensed; they aren't part of consideration.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Remove them from goodwill: Cr goodwill","i":"01b9ef5771"},{"x":"step","t":"Expense them: Dr P/L (operating)","i":"546b586e3f"},{"x":"step","t":"Reduce group retained earnings (the parent paid them)","i":"615a4f669a"}],"i":"01b94824b6"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-1","c":"sbr-18-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"The subsidiary's internally built brand was not recognised at acquisition","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Identifiable intangibles are recognised in the group even if the subsidiary couldn't recognise them.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Dr intangible assets, Cr goodwill at fair value","i":"731f9e0155"},{"x":"step","t":"Amortise it over its remaining life: Dr P/L (operating), Cr intangible","i":"fd2cef454c"},{"x":"step","t":"Share the amortisation with the NCI (post-acquisition, in W2)","i":"6e6bdedfde"}],"i":"32b8d69166"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-1","c":"sbr-18-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"The subsidiary sold goods to the parent at a profit; some are still in the parent's stock","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. When the subsidiary is the seller, the PURP reduces its post-acquisition profit, shared with the NCI.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Cancel the sale: Dr revenue, Cr cost of sales","i":"cf0ad7821f"},{"x":"step","t":"Work out the unrealised profit in closing inventory","i":"44dbc6d58b"},{"x":"step","t":"Dr cost of sales, Cr inventory (PURP)","i":"59c2399d1c"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct the PURP in W2, so the NCI bears its share","i":"11b017059b"}],"i":"25ba008da7"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-1","c":"sbr-18-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"Intra-group balances don't agree because the subsidiary sent cash just before year end","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). Adjust the item in transit first, then eliminate.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Bring the cash in transit into group cash: Dr cash, Cr receivable","i":"6152ef11d3"},{"x":"step","t":"Now the balances agree","i":"98e33be48e"},{"x":"step","t":"Cancel them: Dr payable, Cr receivable","i":"4381d47efd"}],"i":"b4dc477529"},{"s":"sbr-18-structure-1","c":"sbr-18-structure-1#7","x":"steps","t":"Goodwill is impaired and the NCI was measured at fair value","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Full goodwill includes the NCI's share, so the NCI bears part of the impairment.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Compare goodwill plus net assets with the recoverable amount","i":"5557833026"},{"x":"step","t":"Dr P/L (operating), Cr goodwill for the loss","i":"011f917c63"},{"x":"step","t":"Split the loss between group reserves and NCI by shareholding","i":"f187c68396"}],"i":"82ae8667ec"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-3","c":"sbr-18-apply-3#3","x":"example","t":"A draft group statement left out contingent consideration with a fair value of 5 at acquisition (10% discount rate). A year has passed. What corrections are needed?","a":"Dr goodwill 5, Cr liability 5; Dr finance cost 0.5, Cr liability 0.5","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: contingent consideration is part of consideration at fair value; its discount unwinds as a finance cost. Applied: goodwill +5 at acquisition; one year's unwinding 5 × 10% = 0.5 in financing, liability 5.5. P/L category: The unwinding (0.5) goes to Financing. Users: Higher liabilities raise gearing; a later rise in the payout would hit profit. Common mistake: putting the unwinding into goodwill.","i":"ae590e0a9e"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-3","c":"sbr-18-apply-3#4","x":"example","t":"Acquisition legal fees of 0.6 were added to goodwill in the draft. Correction?","a":"Dr P/L (operating) 0.6, Cr goodwill 0.6","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Rule: IFRS 3 requires acquisition-related costs to be expensed. Applied: remove from goodwill and charge to P/L, reducing group retained earnings. P/L category: Operating. Users: Profit falls in the acquisition year; goodwill isn't overstated. Common mistake: treating them as share issue costs (only costs of issuing shares go to equity).","i":"a95470080f"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-3","c":"sbr-18-apply-3#5","x":"example","t":"A 75%-owned subsidiary sold goods to the parent for 100 at a 25% margin; 40% are still in the parent's stock. Correction for unrealised profit?","a":"Dr cost of sales 10, Cr inventory 10; the NCI bears 2.5","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements (consolidation procedures). Rule: remove unrealised profit on goods still in the group; if the subsidiary sold, the NCI shares it. Applied: profit 25 × 40% unsold = 10; the subsidiary's post-acquisition profit falls by 10, so NCI (25%) bears 2.5. P/L category: Cost of sales rises (Operating), lowering gross profit. Users: Group profit and inventory aren't inflated by trading within the group. Common mistake: charging it all to the group (that's when the parent sells).","i":"3c35ce1843"}],"i":"bec00c8d76"}],"i":"a3268e4e47"},{"x":"group","t":"Goodwill impairment","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-9","c":"sbr-18-9#0","x":"card","t":"Goodwill is tested every year as part of a cash-generating unit (often the whole subsidiary)","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Losses go first to goodwill, then other assets pro rata.","i":"055709fbf7"},{"s":"sbr-18-9","c":"sbr-18-9#1","x":"card","t":"With NCI at fair value, compare the full goodwill and net assets with recoverable amount, then split the loss by ownership","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Tudor: impairment 300 shared 80/20.","i":"25b96e1f3a"},{"s":"sbr-18-9","c":"sbr-18-9#2","x":"card","t":"With proportionate NCI, before testing, goodwill is grossed up to a notional 100%; only the parent's share of loss is recognised","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. So the comparison is like for like.","i":"4a91f8d3f0"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill must be grossed up before an impairment test","a":"NCI at share of net assets","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Only the parent's goodwill is recognised, so a notional 100% is tested.","i":"56c1424cc6"},{"s":"sbr-18-compare-2","c":"sbr-18-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Impairment of goodwill is shared with the NCI","a":"NCI at fair value","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Because goodwill includes the NCI's part.","i":"c0c0ca4b92"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-2","c":"sbr-18-watch-2#4","x":"trap","t":"Under the proportionate method the NCI bears part of any goodwill impairment.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Only the parent's goodwill is recognised, so only the parent bears it.","i":"6da338e790"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-3","c":"sbr-18-apply-3#0","x":"example","t":"NCI at fair value (25%). Goodwill 120; subsidiary's net assets 800; recoverable amount 860. How is the impairment shared?","a":"60 loss: 45 group, 15 NCI","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Rule: with full goodwill, compare goodwill + net assets with recoverable amount and share the loss by holding. Applied: 120 + 800 = 920 vs 860: loss 60 against goodwill; 75% = 45 group, 25% = 15 NCI. P/L category: Impairment in Operating; 15 of it attributed to the NCI. Users: Lower assets and profit; impairment signals the acquisition is earning less than expected. Common mistake: charging it all to the group.","i":"0f25fa02cb"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-3","c":"sbr-18-apply-3#1","x":"example","t":"NCI at share of net assets (group owns 75%). Recognised goodwill 90; net assets 800; recoverable amount 860. What impairment is recognised?","a":"45, all against group","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Rule: gross up goodwill to a notional 100% before testing, then recognise only the parent's share. Applied: notional goodwill 90 / 75% = 120; 120 + 800 = 920 vs 860: notional loss 60; recognise 75% × 60 = 45, all group. P/L category: Impairment of 45 in Operating, all attributable to owners of the parent. Users: Profit attributable to the parent falls by the whole loss. Common mistake: testing 90 + 800 = 890 vs 860 (30).","i":"dd4f814e24"}],"i":"ba019fc507"},{"x":"group","t":"Exemptions from consolidation","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-9","c":"sbr-18-9#3","x":"card","t":"An intermediate parent need not consolidate if NCI don't object, its shares aren't traded or about to be, and the ultimate parent publishes IFRS consolidated statements","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. It must disclose that fact and list its significant investments.","i":"183d23dfde"},{"s":"sbr-18-9","c":"sbr-18-9#4","x":"card","t":"Investment entities don't consolidate subsidiaries; they measure them at fair value through P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Investment entity: gets funds, invests for returns, measures on fair value.","i":"99f308ddf5"},{"s":"sbr-18-9","c":"sbr-18-9#5","x":"card","t":"Invalid reasons to exclude a subsidiary include fund restrictions, different activities, losses, hiding ownership or hiding the group's true size","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Often motivated by bonuses or avoiding regulation.","i":"1efb00e563"},{"s":"sbr-18-9","c":"sbr-18-9#6","x":"card","t":"A special purpose entity set up to hold debt or risky projects must be consolidated if controlled","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Not consolidating SPEs was behind past scandals.","i":"63c9e0c3f9"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A subsidiary with very different activities may be left out of the consolidation.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Different activities, losses, fund restrictions or a wish to look smaller are not valid reasons.","i":"1414c618a5"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"A special purpose entity set up by the parent to hold debt must be consolidated if the parent controls it.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Substance over form.","i":"5c56decb3c"},{"s":"sbr-18-watch-1","c":"sbr-18-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"An investment entity consolidates the companies it controls.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. It measures them at fair value through P/L instead.","i":"8202dd15a2"}],"i":"5c4ce3820d"},{"x":"group","t":"IFRS 19: reduced disclosures for subsidiaries","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-5","c":"sbr-18-5#5","x":"card","t":"IFRS 19 lets a subsidiary without public accountability, whose parent publishes IFRS consolidated statements, use reduced disclosures (with full IFRS recognition and measurement)","d":"Reference: IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements; IAS 27 Separate Financial Statements; IFRS 12 Disclosure of Interests in Other Entities; IFRS 19 Subsidiaries without Public Accountability: Disclosures. Except IFRS 8, IFRS 17 and IAS 33, whose disclosures stay in full if applied.","i":"0a8c822343"}],"i":"9b06f451fa"},{"x":"group","t":"Joint arrangements (IFRS 11)","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-5","c":"sbr-18-5#0","x":"card","t":"A joint arrangement exists when two or more parties have joint control, which requires unanimous consent on relevant activities","d":"Reference: IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements. IFRS 11.","i":"8f6d603805"},{"s":"sbr-18-5","c":"sbr-18-5#1","x":"card","t":"In a joint operation, the parties have rights to the assets and obligations for the liabilities, so each recognises its own share of assets, liabilities, income and expenses","d":"Reference: IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements. Usually no separate entity.","i":"ce71a82e8f"},{"s":"sbr-18-5","c":"sbr-18-5#2","x":"card","t":"In a joint venture, the parties have rights to the net assets, so they use the equity method","d":"Reference: IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements. Usually a separate entity.","i":"83f4bb0be6"},{"s":"sbr-18-apply-1","c":"sbr-18-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"Two companies set up a separate company to run a pipeline; all key decisions need both their votes and each shares in the company's net profit. What is it for each investor?","a":"A joint venture, using the equity method","d":"Reference: IFRS 11 Joint Arrangements. Rule: joint control (unanimous consent) over a separate vehicle where the parties have rights to net assets = joint venture. Applied: separate company, shared decisions, shared profit. P/L category: Share of the joint venture's profit goes to Investing. Users: The pipeline's assets and debt stay off the investors' balance sheets; only the net investment shows. Common mistake: proportionately booking assets as for a joint operation.","i":"83bc393e4f"}],"i":"a23d963ee5"},{"x":"group","t":"Other issues","k":[{"x":"group","t":"IFRS 12 disclosures","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-5","c":"sbr-18-5#4","x":"card","t":"IFRS 12 requires disclosure of judgements on control, joint control and significant influence, and material NCI, JVs, associates and restrictions on group assets","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. It is the single source of disclosures about interests in other entities.","i":"fc637b3f9d"}],"i":"9936bf0c9f"},{"x":"group","t":"IAS 27: the parent's own statements","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-5","c":"sbr-18-5#3","x":"card","t":"In separate (parent-only) statements, investments in subsidiaries, associates and JVs are at cost, under IFRS 9, or using the equity method","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. IAS 27. Dividends go to P/L unless the equity method is used.","i":"980adb4f0a"}],"i":"64f2d318cd"},{"x":"group","t":"Criticisms of IFRS 3","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-10","c":"sbr-18-10#0","x":"card","t":"Fair valuing acquired assets makes acquisitive groups hard to compare with groups that grow organically","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Inventory at fair value also squeezes next year's margin.","i":"f161aa89de"},{"s":"sbr-18-10","c":"sbr-18-10#1","x":"card","t":"Valuing acquired intangibles and contingent consideration is criticised as subjective, raising the risk of bias","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. No active market for most intangibles.","i":"2c709dccaa"},{"s":"sbr-18-10","c":"sbr-18-10#2","x":"card","t":"Some argue a bargain purchase gain should go to OCI, not P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. It distorts the performance picture.","i":"e77869086e"},{"s":"sbr-18-10","c":"sbr-18-10#3","x":"card","t":"Annual goodwill impairment tests are criticised as complex and volatile, and some prefer amortising goodwill","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Purchased goodwill is gradually replaced by internal goodwill, which isn't allowed on the SoFP.","i":"66b6bfda93"},{"s":"sbr-18-10","c":"sbr-18-10#4","x":"card","t":"The choice of NCI measurement method is criticised because it reduces comparability (and fair value is hard for unlisted subs)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Same deal, different goodwill.","i":"064c39cec5"}],"i":"ad19d0379f"}],"i":"22426fc9d4"},{"x":"group","t":"Exam focus","k":[{"s":"sbr-18-1","c":"sbr-18-1#5","x":"card","t":"SBR Question 1 always tests groups, asking you to adjust draft consolidated statements and explain the numbers","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. You won't prepare full statements from scratch.","i":"639631853e"}],"i":"4d16e3598e"}],"i":"a6489510a9"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 19: Change in a group structure","ch":19,"d":"Study Text pages 473–514.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"19.1 Mid-year acquisitions","s":"sbr-19-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-1","c":"sbr-19-1#0","x":"card","t":"A subsidiary acquired mid-year is consolidated from the date control is gained","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Only post-acquisition income and expenses enter group P/L.","i":"5056224757"},{"s":"sbr-19-1","c":"sbr-19-1#1","x":"card","t":"Revenue and expenses of a mid-year subsidiary are time-apportioned (e.g. 9/12)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Tudor: 9/12 × 24,000 of Windsor's revenue.","i":"965af4d025"},{"s":"sbr-19-1","c":"sbr-19-1#2","x":"card","t":"Intra-group sales are eliminated only for the post-acquisition period","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Tudor: the 12m sold after acquisition is removed.","i":"dc39c5f9c8"},{"s":"sbr-19-1","c":"sbr-19-1#3","x":"card","t":"Extra depreciation on fair value uplifts is charged for the post-acquisition months only","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Tudor: 3,200 / 4 × 9/12 = 600.","i":"80388b1f94"},{"s":"sbr-19-1","c":"sbr-19-1#4","x":"card","t":"Net assets at acquisition = equity at the start of the year + profit up to the acquisition date + fair value adjustments","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Tudor: 5,400 b/f + 3/12 × 3,000 = 6,150 retained earnings at acquisition.","i":"dd88279e5d"},{"s":"sbr-19-1","c":"sbr-19-1#5","x":"card","t":"The NCI's share of profit in a mid-year acquisition uses post-acquisition profit less adjustments, times NCI %","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Tudor: (2,250 − 600 − 300) × 20% = 270.","i":"663ed42336"}],"i":"5ed22a0a38"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.2 Step acquisitions","s":"sbr-19-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-2","c":"sbr-19-2#0","x":"card","t":"A step acquisition is when control is achieved by buying shares in stages","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Acquisition accounting applies only at the date control is gained.","i":"62cb861cc8"},{"s":"sbr-19-2","c":"sbr-19-2#1","x":"card","t":"Before control, an earlier holding is accounted for under IFRS 9 (investment), IAS 28 (associate) or IFRS 11","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Major/Tom: 40% held as an associate first.","i":"57e39ae52c"},{"s":"sbr-19-2","c":"sbr-19-2#2","x":"card","t":"When control is gained, the previously held interest is remeasured to fair value","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Major/Tom: the 40% stake is remeasured to 105,000.","i":"f1018a68ad"},{"s":"sbr-19-2","c":"sbr-19-2#3","x":"card","t":"The gain on remeasuring the old holding goes to P/L (investing), or OCI if it was FVOCI","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. FVOCI gains stay in equity and are never recycled.","i":"f256d6c99b"},{"s":"sbr-19-2","c":"sbr-19-2#4","x":"card","t":"Goodwill in a step acquisition = FV of old interest + FV of new consideration + NCI − fair value of net assets at the date control is gained","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Major/Tom: 105,000 + 70,000 + NCI − net assets.","i":"732fe7ba96"},{"s":"sbr-19-2","c":"sbr-19-2#5","x":"card","t":"Buying more shares after control is gained (e.g. 60% → 75%) doesn't change goodwill; it's a deal between owners","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. See control-to-control.","i":"f9fed83b74"}],"i":"e091b036ea"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.3 Losing control: disposals","s":"sbr-19-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-3","c":"sbr-19-3#0","x":"card","t":"In the parent's own statements, the gain on selling shares = proceeds − carrying amount of the shares sold","d":"Reference: IAS 27 Separate Financial Statements; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Shown in investing; tax may be payable on it.","i":"492179c865"},{"s":"sbr-19-3","c":"sbr-19-3#1","x":"card","t":"When control is lost, the group consolidates the subsidiary's results up to the disposal date","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Then removes its assets, liabilities, goodwill and NCI.","i":"d94b5d1ad1"},{"s":"sbr-19-3","c":"sbr-19-3#2","x":"card","t":"The group gain on losing control = proceeds + FV of retained interest − (net assets + goodwill − NCI) at the disposal date","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Shown in operating (investing if the sub only held investment property).","i":"e42761b0c4"},{"s":"sbr-19-3","c":"sbr-19-3#3","x":"card","t":"A retained interest after losing control is recognised at fair value at the disposal date","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Liesel/Hans: the 30% kept is recorded at 51m.","i":"1ce5c86f10"},{"s":"sbr-19-3","c":"sbr-19-3#4","x":"card","t":"A retained 20–50% holding is then accounted for as an associate (equity method)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Under 20%: IFRS 9 investment.","i":"93f1e95134"},{"s":"sbr-19-3","c":"sbr-19-3#5","x":"card","t":"Posting disposal cash to a suspense account instead of calculating the group gain is wrong: derecognise the sub and recognise the gain","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Liesel/Hans: compute the full disposal using the template.","i":"36fcd21ae0"},{"s":"sbr-19-3","c":"sbr-19-3#6","x":"card","t":"Translation reserve for a foreign subsidiary sold is recycled to P/L as part of the disposal","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. See chapter 20.","i":"2a5f78fcd9"}],"i":"daa4d59da3"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.4 Control to control","s":"sbr-19-4","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-4","c":"sbr-19-4#0","x":"card","t":"Buying more of a subsidiary (80% → 85%) is a transaction between owners: no gain or loss, goodwill unchanged","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. The NCI shrinks; the difference goes to equity.","i":"716c44467d"},{"s":"sbr-19-4","c":"sbr-19-4#1","x":"card","t":"When buying out NCI, the NCI is reduced by the proportion of its carrying amount bought","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. 30% → 20% means the NCI falls by one-third.","i":"6eb7f8c515"},{"s":"sbr-19-4","c":"sbr-19-4#2","x":"card","t":"The difference between cash paid and the decrease in NCI goes to other components of equity","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Gordon: pay 25,000 for all 20% (NCI 20,000) → 5,000 debit to equity.","i":"bc12e98526"},{"s":"sbr-19-4","c":"sbr-19-4#3","x":"card","t":"Selling some shares but keeping control (80% → 75%) is also an equity transaction: no P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Goodwill is not recalculated.","i":"ca99492682"},{"s":"sbr-19-4","c":"sbr-19-4#4","x":"card","t":"When selling to NCI, the NCI rises by its new share of net assets and goodwill (fair value method only)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. 20% → 30% means +10% of net assets (and goodwill).","i":"08a8c551d0"},{"s":"sbr-19-4","c":"sbr-19-4#5","x":"card","t":"The difference between proceeds and the increase in NCI goes to other components of equity","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Liesel/Rosa: crediting 9m to P/L is wrong; NCI up by 5% × (120 + 8) = 6.4m, equity up 2.6m.","i":"7c7b42ed9d"}],"i":"6dd150d972"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.5 Subs bought for resale and associate disposals","s":"sbr-19-5","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-5","c":"sbr-19-5#0","x":"card","t":"A subsidiary bought only to resell is still consolidated (no exemption)","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. But if IFRS 5 criteria are met, it's presented differently.","i":"2b410258f6"},{"s":"sbr-19-5","c":"sbr-19-5#1","x":"card","t":"If it meets the held-for-sale criteria, it's shown as one line of assets and one of liabilities, at FV less costs to sell","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. It also counts as a discontinued operation.","i":"ec1fb7f014"},{"s":"sbr-19-5","c":"sbr-19-5#2","x":"card","t":"When significant influence over an associate is lost, the gain = proceeds + FV of retained interest − the associate's carrying amount","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Shown in investing. Raven: 2m + 0.5m − carrying amount.","i":"87da6991d2"},{"s":"sbr-19-5","c":"sbr-19-5#3","x":"card","t":"A small stake kept after selling an associate is an IFRS 9 investment at fair value","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Raven: 5% at 0.5m, then 0.6m at the year end.","i":"8a61c1d5df"}],"i":"de956b6a85"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"19.S1 Changes in holding: the entries","s":"sbr-19-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-structure-1","c":"sbr-19-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Control is gained in stages (a 15% investment grows to 60%)","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Crossing the control line is treated as selling the old holding and buying the whole subsidiary.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Remeasure the old holding to fair value at the control date","i":"4a77568985"},{"x":"step","t":"Gain or loss to P/L (investing), or OCI if it was designated FVOCI","i":"ff414b3b96"},{"x":"step","t":"Goodwill = FV of old holding + new consideration + NCI − net assets","i":"c363bada8f"},{"x":"step","t":"Consolidate the subsidiary's results only from the control date","i":"85003e565e"}],"i":"78cd790e8b"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-1","c":"sbr-19-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Control is lost (subsidiary sold, a 25% stake kept)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Losing control ends one relationship and starts another at fair value.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Consolidate the subsidiary's results up to the disposal date","i":"fd8fb5bd23"},{"x":"step","t":"Remove its net assets, goodwill and NCI","i":"4822ae8e96"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise the stake kept at fair value","i":"21f0eccd8d"},{"x":"step","t":"Gain or loss = proceeds + FV kept − (net assets + goodwill − NCI), in P/L (operating)","i":"4d50a8c2c2"},{"x":"step","t":"From then on: equity method (associate) or IFRS 9 (investment)","i":"ab55a86262"}],"i":"0ef0675240"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-1","c":"sbr-19-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"The parent buys more shares in an existing subsidiary (70% to 80%)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Within control, it's a deal between owners: equity, not profit.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Cr cash for the price paid","i":"e3b4b1babb"},{"x":"step","t":"Dr NCI for the proportion of NCI given up (10/30 of its carrying amount)","i":"3f851821de"},{"x":"step","t":"Put the difference in equity (other components): no P/L","i":"4d11e729f5"},{"x":"step","t":"Leave goodwill unchanged","i":"8a450d6353"}],"i":"6c81628ccc"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-1","c":"sbr-19-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"The parent sells shares but keeps control (90% to 80%)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Control stays, so no gain is recognised and the subsidiary is still fully consolidated.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Dr cash for the proceeds","i":"e869acb638"},{"x":"step","t":"Cr NCI for the share passed on: 10% of net assets, plus 10% of goodwill if NCI is at fair value","i":"d825ab70c4"},{"x":"step","t":"Put the difference in equity (other components): no P/L","i":"b6215b40b9"},{"x":"step","t":"Leave goodwill unchanged","i":"346cedcfc7"}],"i":"753166c2b4"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-1","c":"sbr-19-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"An associate is sold and a small stake kept","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Losing significant influence is also a 'crossing the line' event.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Equity-account up to the disposal date","i":"37c15d2589"},{"x":"step","t":"Remove the associate's carrying amount","i":"d317c2c992"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise the stake kept at fair value under IFRS 9","i":"55775bf87e"},{"x":"step","t":"Gain or loss = proceeds + FV kept − carrying amount, in P/L (investing)","i":"392b92d27d"}],"i":"ab65ae7127"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-1","c":"sbr-19-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"A subsidiary is bought only to be sold, and meets the held-for-sale criteria","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. It is still consolidated, but in a short form under IFRS 5.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Measure it at fair value less costs to sell at acquisition","i":"3b08120c9e"},{"x":"step","t":"Show its assets in one line and its liabilities in another","i":"dab5c2136e"},{"x":"step","t":"Remeasure at the reporting date","i":"55d505370a"},{"x":"step","t":"Show the change and its results as one discontinued line","i":"16010ff3f9"}],"i":"e8cdee380a"}],"i":"3d23e973be"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.S2 The pro-formas","s":"sbr-19-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-structure-2","c":"sbr-19-structure-2#0","x":"steps","t":"Group gain or loss when control is lost","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. The NCI is added back because the group never owned that part of the net assets and goodwill.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Disposal proceeds","i":"5f0ce79b32"},{"x":"step","t":"Add fair value of the interest kept","i":"41379aa964"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct net assets at the disposal date","i":"2ef930ddf0"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct goodwill at the disposal date","i":"eca0ea75d8"},{"x":"step","t":"Add back the NCI at the disposal date","i":"794df4b917"},{"x":"step","t":"= gain or loss to the group","i":"bc6a33dbae"}],"i":"1c3d1b5310"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-2","c":"sbr-19-structure-2#1","x":"steps","t":"Goodwill in a step acquisition","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. All measured at the date control is gained, not when the first shares were bought.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Fair value of the interest held before","i":"9ad86e8cbe"},{"x":"step","t":"Add consideration for the new shares","i":"59b20c4467"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI at the control date","i":"7aaac4ed0e"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct fair value of net assets at the control date","i":"7a4106811a"},{"x":"step","t":"= goodwill","i":"0dcdb0c9e3"}],"i":"12967f9c71"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-2","c":"sbr-19-structure-2#2","x":"steps","t":"NCI at the disposal date (fair value method)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. With proportionate NCI there is no impairment line.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"NCI at acquisition","i":"ab69a8e5c5"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI share of the post-acquisition change in net assets","i":"4d2ac7507a"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct NCI share of goodwill impairment","i":"824aa97a75"},{"x":"step","t":"= NCI removed on disposal","i":"b7f1b3373f"}],"i":"0a45b52724"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-2","c":"sbr-19-structure-2#3","x":"steps","t":"Buying more shares in a subsidiary","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. A credit balance increases equity; a debit decreases it.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Cash paid (credit)","i":"8d62e5f7c3"},{"x":"step","t":"Decrease in NCI (debit)","i":"9aa1a6a2ae"},{"x":"step","t":"Balance: equity adjustment","i":"be88d4fd6f"}],"i":"dd271cc995"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-2","c":"sbr-19-structure-2#4","x":"steps","t":"Gain or loss when an associate is sold","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. There is no NCI or goodwill line: an associate isn't consolidated.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Disposal proceeds","i":"9b5579beaf"},{"x":"step","t":"Add fair value of the stake kept","i":"859cd45dc3"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct carrying amount of the associate","i":"83bb683721"},{"x":"step","t":"= gain or loss (investing)","i":"6903a295fa"}],"i":"fd12b08b86"}],"i":"d6ca145c30"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.S3 Where does each effect land?","s":"sbr-19-structure-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#0","x":"sort","t":"Gain on remeasuring a 15% FVTPL holding when control is gained","a":"P/L: investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It is the old investment's fair value gain: investing.","i":"25db2d81aa"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#1","x":"sort","t":"Gain on remeasuring an old holding that was designated FVOCI","a":"OCI, never recycled","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. It stays in OCI and is never recycled to P/L.","i":"ea212802ec"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#2","x":"sort","t":"Group gain on selling a trading subsidiary (control lost)","a":"P/L: operating","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Kaplan follows IFRS 18: in operating, as the subsidiary's activities were part of operations.","i":"9c52f42fa3"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#3","x":"sort","t":"Group gain on selling a subsidiary whose only assets are investment properties","a":"P/L: investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. The underlying assets are investments, so investing.","i":"1c764e5f9a"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#4","x":"sort","t":"Gain on selling an associate","a":"P/L: investing","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Associates' results are investing, so is the disposal gain.","i":"2c273c7e85"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#5","x":"sort","t":"Difference when the parent sells 5% of a subsidiary and keeps control","a":"Equity (no P/L)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. A deal between owners: straight to equity.","i":"80987a9022"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#6","x":"sort","t":"Difference when the parent buys out part of the NCI","a":"Equity (no P/L)","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Also a deal between owners: equity.","i":"98b71f918f"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#7","x":"sort","t":"Results and gain of a sold subsidiary that is a discontinued operation","a":"P/L: one discontinued line","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. IFRS 5: one line after profit from continuing operations.","i":"82cf7004bb"},{"s":"sbr-19-structure-3","c":"sbr-19-structure-3#8","x":"sort","t":"Fall in fair value less costs to sell of a subsidiary bought for resale","a":"P/L: one discontinued line","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Shown in the discontinued line with its post-acquisition results.","i":"082661aef1"}],"i":"8dbc4e0933"}],"i":"ee6e179a60"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"19.C1 Crossing a line vs moving within it","s":"sbr-19-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-compare-1","c":"sbr-19-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"15% investment to 60% subsidiary","a":"Crossing a line: remeasure and P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Control is gained.","i":"7486d84f6e"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-1","c":"sbr-19-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"30% associate to 70% subsidiary","a":"Crossing a line: remeasure and P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Influence becomes control.","i":"c851fccba4"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-1","c":"sbr-19-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"80% to 90% of a subsidiary","a":"Within control: equity","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It's a subsidiary before and after.","i":"2b6b1afc98"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-1","c":"sbr-19-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"90% to 60% of a subsidiary","a":"Within control: equity","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Still over 50% and nothing else changes control.","i":"5884e57cab"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-1","c":"sbr-19-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"60% subsidiary to 40% associate","a":"Crossing a line: remeasure and P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Control is lost.","i":"9879586f81"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-1","c":"sbr-19-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"25% associate to 5% investment","a":"Crossing a line: remeasure and P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Significant influence is lost.","i":"f7577dd5bf"}],"i":"7746089393"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.C2 Side by side","s":"sbr-19-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Profit = proceeds − cost of the shares","a":"Parent's own statements","d":"Reference: IAS 27 Separate Financial Statements; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. The parent sees only an investment at cost.","i":"ad8a696fb4"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Profit = proceeds + FV kept − (net assets + goodwill − NCI)","a":"Group statements","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. The group held the underlying net assets and goodwill.","i":"a14267f3ac"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Each line of the sold subsidiary's results is time-apportioned","a":"Line by line","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Used when it isn't a discontinued operation.","i":"96bdc10ecf"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Results to disposal and the disposal gain in one line after tax","a":"Discontinued operation","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. IFRS 5 presentation.","i":"94728d9b1e"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Increase in NCI on a sale within control includes goodwill","a":"NCI at fair value","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Only full goodwill has an NCI share.","i":"dba006f426"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Increase in NCI on a sale within control is just its share of net assets","a":"NCI at share of net assets","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. No NCI goodwill exists.","i":"e75c9ab01e"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"The interest kept is recognised at its fair value","a":"When control is lost","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. The fair value becomes its new cost.","i":"53876201a3"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill stays exactly as it was","a":"When control is kept","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It's only recalculated when control is gained.","i":"49b31a188a"},{"s":"sbr-19-compare-2","c":"sbr-19-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"NCI is derecognised","a":"Loss of control of a subsidiary","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. An associate has no NCI in the group statements.","i":"788f6801c8"}],"i":"25697e6993"}],"i":"f3d6395526"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"19.W Change-in-group traps","s":"sbr-19-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"When control is gained in stages, goodwill is worked out at the date the first shares were bought.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Only at the date control is gained, using the old holding's fair value then.","i":"f538b3b41e"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"The gain on remeasuring a previously held FVOCI investment is recycled to P/L when control is gained.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. OCI gains on equity investments are never recycled.","i":"1ea20e4688"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Buying more shares in a subsidiary changes goodwill.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Goodwill is fixed when control is gained; later deals with NCI go to equity.","i":"87612e6e81"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Selling 5% of a 90% subsidiary gives a profit in the group P/L.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Control is kept, so the difference goes to equity.","i":"12cdeeac42"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"The group gain on disposal usually differs from the parent's own gain.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 27 Separate Financial Statements; IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. The parent compares with cost; the group compares with net assets, goodwill and NCI.","i":"2ea95af14b"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"The fair value of the stake kept is part of the group disposal gain calculation.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It's treated as if sold and bought back at fair value.","i":"76ef1f9422"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"A subsidiary sold during the year is left out of the group P/L completely.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Its results are consolidated up to the disposal date.","i":"fa82152075"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"A dividend the subsidiary paid before disposal reduces its net assets at disposal.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. It's cash leaving the subsidiary before the sale date.","i":"4b9f32022d"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"A subsidiary bought only to be resold is exempt from consolidation.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. It's consolidated, but presented as held for sale if IFRS 5's criteria are met.","i":"a21138c2d6"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"A newly bought subsidiary held for sale is automatically a discontinued operation.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. IFRS 5 treats it as a discontinued operation.","i":"7aa8457b71"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"The NCI's share of profit for a subsidiary bought mid-year is based on its whole year's profit.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Only on the post-acquisition period, after fair value depreciation and (full goodwill) impairment.","i":"296d64e999"},{"s":"sbr-19-watch-1","c":"sbr-19-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"On loss of control, goodwill already impaired is removed at its impaired amount.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Carrying amount at the disposal date.","i":"92aabf9be4"}],"i":"1da9f016a9"}],"i":"b49c522eb4"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"19.A1 Gaining control","s":"sbr-19-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-apply-1","c":"sbr-19-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Owl buys 75% of Robin on 1 April; the year ends 31 December. Robin earns 400 evenly over the year. A fair value uplift of 800 is depreciated over 4 years; goodwill (NCI at fair value) is impaired by 50 this year. What profit goes to the NCI?","a":"25","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 3 Business Combinations. Rule: consolidate from the control date; the NCI shares post-acquisition profit after fair value depreciation and, with full goodwill, impairment. Applied: 400 × 9/12 = 300; depreciation 800 / 4 × 9/12 = 150; 300 − 150 − 50 = 100; 25% = 25. P/L category: Robin's results in their usual categories (mostly Operating); impairment and extra depreciation in Operating. Users: Only nine months of Robin's revenue and profit, so growth looks bigger next year when a full year is included. Common mistakes: 37.5 (forgetting impairment), 75 (no adjustments), 100 (whole year).","i":"bcd5d9c0ec"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-1","c":"sbr-19-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Hawk holds 15% of Wren (FVTPL, carried at 30). It buys a further 45% for 120, gaining control. At that date the 15% is worth 36, the 40% NCI's fair value is 95 and Wren's net assets are 200. Treatment?","a":"Gain 6 in P/L; goodwill 51","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: remeasure the old holding at the control date, gain to P/L, and use its fair value in goodwill. Applied: 36 − 30 = 6 (investing); goodwill 36 + 120 + 95 − 200 = 51. P/L category: Gain of 6 in Investing. Users: A one-off remeasurement gain: users should exclude it from sustainable profit. Common mistakes: 45 (using the carrying amount 30), 15 (leaving the old holding out).","i":"e6b67554d6"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-1","c":"sbr-19-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Kite has held 30% of Lark as an associate, carried at 84. It buys another 30% for 100, gaining control. The old 30% is then worth 90; NCI is measured at its 40% share of net assets of 250. What is the gain and the goodwill?","a":"Gain 6; goodwill 40","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: an associate stake is remeasured to fair value when control is gained, and that fair value goes into goodwill. Applied: gain 90 − 84 = 6 (investing); goodwill 90 + 100 + 100 (40% × 250) − 250 = 40. P/L category: Gain of 6 in Investing; from then on Lark is consolidated line by line. Users: The group's revenue, assets and debt jump as Lark moves from one line to full consolidation. Common mistakes: 34 (using 84), 140 (forgetting to deduct net assets).","i":"857e204acc"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-1","c":"sbr-19-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Hawk held 15% of Wren, designated at fair value through OCI and carried at 30. It buys a further 45%, gaining control, when the 15% is worth 36. Where does the 6 remeasurement gain go?","a":"OCI, and it is never recycled to P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 3 Business Combinations; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: equity investments designated FVOCI keep their gains in OCI permanently. Applied: the 6 goes to OCI; goodwill still uses the fair value of 36. P/L category: OCI, not recycled. Users: No effect on EPS; total comprehensive income shows the gain. Common mistake: recycling it on the change of status.","i":"9fe9d91e90"}],"i":"0025e59fdb"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.A2 Losing control","s":"sbr-19-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-apply-2","c":"sbr-19-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"Fox bought 80% of Hare for 500; NCI fair value at acquisition 110; Hare's net assets were 450 then and 600 now; goodwill is impaired by 40 to date. Fox sells all its shares for 800. Group gain?","a":"212","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: group gain = proceeds − (net assets + goodwill − NCI) at the disposal date. Applied: goodwill 500 + 110 − 450 = 160, less 40 = 120; NCI 110 + 20% × 150 − 20% × 40 = 132; 800 − (600 + 120 − 132) = 212. P/L category: Gain of 212 in Operating (a separate line may be needed). Users: A large one-off gain: investors strip it out; cash inflow of 800 in investing activities. Common mistakes: 300 (the parent's own gain, 800 − 500), 180 (ignoring the impairment), 220 (cutting goodwill but not the NCI's share of the impairment).","i":"ce333acdc1"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-2","c":"sbr-19-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"Fox bought 80% of Hare for 500 and sells all its shares for 800, a group gain of 212. What gain does Fox show in its own (separate) statements?","a":"300","d":"Reference: IAS 27 Separate Financial Statements. Rule: the parent holds the shares at cost, so its gain = proceeds − cost. Applied: 800 − 500 = 300 (investing in its own P/L), possibly with tax. P/L category: Gain of 212 in Operating (a separate line may be needed). Users: A large one-off gain: investors strip it out; cash inflow of 800 in investing activities. Common mistake: using the group calculation in the parent's own statements.","i":"2abfd9b8c0"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-2","c":"sbr-19-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"Bear owned 70% of Moose (net assets 500, goodwill 90, NCI 140). It sells 45% for 300, keeping 25% worth 160, which gives significant influence. What happens?","a":"Gain 10 in P/L; Moose becomes an associate at 160","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: losing control means remeasuring the stake kept to fair value and recognising a gain in P/L. Applied: 300 + 160 − (500 + 90 − 140) = 10; the 25% is equity-accounted from a cost of 160. P/L category: Gain of 10 in Operating; later share of Moose's profit in Investing. Users: Revenue and debt fall as Moose leaves the group; gearing may improve. Common mistake: keeping it at its share of the old net assets.","i":"970d1b8bfb"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-2","c":"sbr-19-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"Lynx sold 50% of a 60% subsidiary in the year, keeping 10% (no influence). How are the retained shares and the year's results shown?","a":"Results consolidated to the disposal date; 10% at fair value under IFRS 9","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: consolidate up to the disposal date; the stake kept is an IFRS 9 financial asset initially at fair value. Applied: later fair value changes go to P/L (or OCI if designated). P/L category: Disposal gain in Operating; later IFRS 9 changes in Investing (or OCI if designated). Users: Results stop being consolidated, so year-on-year comparisons need care. Common mistake: dropping the subsidiary's pre-disposal results.","i":"1d0fbb6dfb"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-2","c":"sbr-19-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"A subsidiary sold on 30 June is a discontinued operation. Its six-month post-tax profit is 40 and the group gain is 70. How is it shown?","a":"One line: profit from discontinued operations 110, after continuing profit","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: IFRS 5 presents a discontinued operation's post-tax results and the gain on disposal as a single amount. Applied: 40 + 70 = 110 below continuing profit; comparatives are re-presented. P/L category: Discontinued. Users: Users can separate the continuing business, which forecasts future results. Common mistake: mixing the line-by-line and one-line methods.","i":"9d6e75a41b"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-2","c":"sbr-19-apply-2#5","x":"example","t":"A subsidiary's only assets are investment properties. The group sells it at a gain. Where is the gain in the group P/L?","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: under IFRS 18, the category follows the nature of what was held; selling a subsidiary that only holds investments is an investing activity. Applied: investing. P/L category: Investing. Users: Operating profit isn't flattered by the sale of investments. Common mistake: putting every subsidiary disposal gain in operating.","i":"49947f9e1f"}],"i":"a106e434c5"},{"x":"stage","t":"19.A3 Within control and associates","s":"sbr-19-apply-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-19-apply-3","c":"sbr-19-apply-3#0","x":"example","t":"Puma owns 70% of Jag; the NCI is carried at 240. Puma buys another 10% for 70. Entry?","a":"Cr cash 70, Dr NCI 80, Cr equity 10","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: within control, the NCI falls in proportion and the difference goes to equity. Applied: NCI falls by 10/30 × 240 = 80; paid 70, so equity rises 10. P/L category: No P/L effect. Users: EPS isn't affected by the deal; future profit attributable to the parent rises with the larger share. Common mistakes: changing goodwill, or putting the 10 in P/L.","i":"69b07f4bf0"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-3","c":"sbr-19-apply-3#1","x":"example","t":"Lion owns 90% of Tiger; NCI is at fair value. Tiger's net assets are 260 and goodwill 40. Lion sells 15% for 50 and keeps control. Entry?","a":"Dr cash 50, Cr NCI 45, Cr equity 5","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: when control is kept, NCI rises by the share passed on of net assets plus goodwill (fair value method). Applied: 15% × (260 + 40) = 45; 50 − 45 = 5 to equity. P/L category: No P/L effect. Users: Cash comes in without a profit; equity attributable to the parent rises slightly. Common mistakes: 39 (net assets only, the proportionate method), or a P/L gain.","i":"399a9534ed"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-3","c":"sbr-19-apply-3#2","x":"example","t":"The draft group statements booked 'Dr cash 30, Cr P/L 30' when 10% of a 90% subsidiary was sold (control kept). Net assets 200, goodwill 40, NCI at fair value. Correction?","a":"Dr P/L 30, Cr NCI 24, Cr equity 6","d":"Reference: IFRS 10 Consolidated Financial Statements. Rule: no gain arises while control stays. Applied: NCI increase 10% × 240 = 24; balance 6 to equity; remove the 30 from P/L. P/L category: Removes 30 of income from P/L (the draft overstated it). Users: The draft overstated profit and EPS; equity in total is unchanged. Common mistake: keeping a profit on a deal between owners.","i":"f4bb4439f7"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-3","c":"sbr-19-apply-3#3","x":"example","t":"Seal held 30% of Otter (cost 400; share of post-acquisition profit to date 60). It sells 20% for 380, keeping 10% worth 190 with no influence. Gain?","a":"110 in P/L (investing)","d":"Reference: IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures; IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: losing significant influence: gain = proceeds + FV kept − carrying amount. Applied: carrying amount 460; 380 + 190 − 460 = 110; the 10% is then an IFRS 9 asset at 190. P/L category: Gain of 110 in Investing; later fair value changes on the 10% in Investing (or OCI). Users: A one-off gain; the investment is now volatile at fair value. Common mistake: 170 (using cost 400 instead of the equity-accounted 460).","i":"9dee33eba7"},{"s":"sbr-19-apply-3","c":"sbr-19-apply-3#4","x":"example","t":"A group buys a subsidiary only to resell it within a year (criteria met). At acquisition its assets are worth 300, liabilities 90, selling costs 10. At year end: assets 280, liabilities 85, costs 10. How is it shown?","a":"Assets 270 and liabilities 85 as held for sale; 15 fall in the discontinued line","d":"Reference: IFRS 5 Non-current Assets Held for Sale and Discontinued Operations. Rule: measured at fair value less costs to sell and presented in two lines. Applied: 300 − 10 − 90 = 200 at acquisition; 280 − 10 − 85 = 185 at year end; the 15 fall and its results are in discontinued operations. P/L category: Discontinued (the 15 fall and the subsidiary's results). Users: Users see it isn't part of the continuing group; its assets and liabilities are kept apart. Common mistake: consolidating it line by line.","i":"ae777feca9"}],"i":"cd62dbce0b"}],"i":"562056268e"}],"i":"17af68bd3f"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 20: Group accounting – foreign currency","ch":20,"d":"Study Text pages 515–536.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"20.1 Consolidating a foreign operation","s":"sbr-20-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#0","x":"card","t":"Before consolidating a foreign subsidiary, its statements are translated into the group's presentation currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Its own records stay in its functional currency.","i":"d3b628a1b1"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#1","x":"card","t":"Income and expenses are translated at the rate on each transaction date (average rate as an approximation)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Dragon/Tattoo: revenue 600 / 2.6.","i":"87f94a2ea6"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#2","x":"card","t":"Assets and liabilities are translated at the closing rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Dragon/Tattoo: PPE 270 / 2.0.","i":"9c764d4910"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#3","x":"card","t":"Share capital and pre-acquisition reserves are translated at the historical rate at acquisition","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Used in the goodwill and reserves workings.","i":"7afce0f274"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#4","x":"card","t":"Goodwill on a foreign subsidiary is calculated in the sub's currency and retranslated at the closing rate every year","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It's treated as an asset of the foreign operation.","i":"5984852933"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#5","x":"card","t":"Exchange differences arise on the opening net assets, the year's profit and goodwill","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Opening net assets: opening vs closing rate; profit: average vs closing rate.","i":"212fe4ce93"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#6","x":"card","t":"Translation differences go to OCI, building up a translation reserve","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. They are unrealised until the operation is sold.","i":"7a124fe04b"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#7","x":"card","t":"The NCI takes its share of exchange differences on net assets and, under the fair value method, on goodwill too","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Under the proportionate method all goodwill differences belong to the group.","i":"6aaca2f6c2"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#8","x":"card","t":"A loan from the parent in the parent's currency is a monetary item for the sub, so the sub retranslates it at the closing rate with the difference in its P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Saint/Albans: retranslate before cancelling the intra-group balances.","i":"4e9f53490c"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#9","x":"card","t":"Foreign associates and joint ventures follow the same rules: share of profit at the average rate, the investment at the closing rate, differences to OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Parrot/Anaconda example.","i":"8b9ef3173e"},{"s":"sbr-20-1","c":"sbr-20-1#10","x":"card","t":"When a foreign operation is sold, the group's share of the translation reserve is recycled to P/L (operating)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. LUMS: the 5,000 gain is added to the profit on disposal.","i":"ed26a18df2"}],"i":"01504d6393"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"20.S1 Consolidating a foreign subsidiary","s":"sbr-20-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-20-structure-1","c":"sbr-20-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Bringing a foreign subsidiary into the group","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Two different rates for P/L and balance sheet are what create the exchange differences.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Translate its income and expenses at the average rate","i":"db4e16ae9b"},{"x":"step","t":"Translate its assets and liabilities at the closing rate","i":"86975d6b53"},{"x":"step","t":"Work out goodwill in its own currency and translate it at the closing rate","i":"a000a1ed54"},{"x":"step","t":"Work out the exchange differences on goodwill and on net assets","i":"2976f56c8f"},{"x":"step","t":"Split them between the group (translation reserve) and the NCI","i":"5e43f220cf"}],"i":"65f79eee0d"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-1","c":"sbr-20-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Exchange difference on goodwill (the pro-forma)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Goodwill is treated as the foreign operation's own asset, so it moves with the currency.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Opening goodwill (or goodwill at acquisition) at the opening (acquisition) rate","i":"56b389ce29"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct this year's impairment at the average rate","i":"466b2b60e6"},{"x":"step","t":"Balancing figure: the exchange difference","i":"8932d3c2ba"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing goodwill at the closing rate","i":"ff74918506"}],"i":"d1f573d6cc"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-1","c":"sbr-20-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Exchange difference on net assets (the pro-forma)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. The two sources: old net assets retranslated, and profit earned at the average rate but held at the closing rate.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Opening net assets (or at acquisition) at the opening (acquisition) rate","i":"b68901cf17"},{"x":"step","t":"Add the year's profit at the average rate","i":"3214530c17"},{"x":"step","t":"Balancing figure: the exchange difference","i":"0564fee307"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing net assets at the closing rate","i":"c9d18aefb4"}],"i":"27d43df881"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-1","c":"sbr-20-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"NCI for a foreign subsidiary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Every exchange difference the NCI shares sits inside the NCI, not the translation reserve.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"NCI at acquisition, at the acquisition rate","i":"16606e9403"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI share of post-acquisition profit, at the average rate","i":"628500d0b8"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI share of the net assets exchange difference","i":"df9f3b688a"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI share of the goodwill exchange difference (fair value method only)","i":"a952a21189"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct NCI share of goodwill impairment (fair value method only)","i":"6ce02e9fed"}],"i":"1333109da9"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-1","c":"sbr-20-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"A parent's loan to a foreign subsidiary in the parent's currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Fix the subsidiary's books first, then eliminate.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"The subsidiary records it at the rate on the loan date","i":"03024a622e"},{"x":"step","t":"At year end the subsidiary retranslates it at the closing rate","i":"7eb22116a0"},{"x":"step","t":"The difference goes to the subsidiary's P/L (and so into W2)","i":"62a74cd95f"},{"x":"step","t":"Now both sides agree in dollars, so cancel them","i":"4f5af1df4d"}],"i":"6bbfdd1793"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-1","c":"sbr-20-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"Selling a foreign subsidiary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. The exchange differences become realised when the operation is sold.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Consolidate its results up to the disposal date","i":"bfd15e51ac"},{"x":"step","t":"Remove its net assets, goodwill and NCI","i":"31e6e073c9"},{"x":"step","t":"Recycle the group's share of the translation reserve to P/L","i":"001923f7bb"},{"x":"step","t":"Show the total gain or loss in operating","i":"772b44d742"}],"i":"2ccf3018a7"}],"i":"77761a5ebc"},{"x":"stage","t":"20.S2 Which rate?","s":"sbr-20-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"The subsidiary's revenue and expenses","a":"Average rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Approximation of the rates on each transaction date.","i":"4f8c50e138"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"The subsidiary's property, plant and equipment in the group balance sheet","a":"Closing rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. All assets and liabilities use the closing rate.","i":"795a5356be"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill in the group balance sheet","a":"Closing rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It's treated as the subsidiary's asset.","i":"534208f590"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Goodwill impairment for the year","a":"Average rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. An expense of the year.","i":"fe999224a1"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"NCI at acquisition, as the starting point of the NCI working","a":"Rate at acquisition","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It's a historical figure; changes since then come through profit and exchange differences.","i":"a5fc33a198"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"The subsidiary's net assets at acquisition in the exchange difference working","a":"Rate at acquisition","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. The starting point (or opening rate in later years).","i":"2e11890971"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"The group's share of the subsidiary's profit in retained earnings","a":"Average rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Profit is translated at the average rate.","i":"d5f3160e58"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"The subsidiary's inventory at the year end","a":"Closing rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. An asset in the group balance sheet.","i":"e90320b96a"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"A foreign associate's share of profit","a":"Average rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Same rules as a subsidiary.","i":"270687a6fa"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-2","c":"sbr-20-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"A foreign associate's carrying amount at the year end","a":"Closing rate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. The investment is retranslated.","i":"3e1b89cdfb"}],"i":"660e719dc1"},{"x":"stage","t":"20.S3 Where does it go?","s":"sbr-20-structure-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-20-structure-3","c":"sbr-20-structure-3#0","x":"sort","t":"Exchange difference on translating a foreign subsidiary's net assets","a":"OCI (translation reserve and NCI)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Translation differences go to OCI.","i":"1207a8fc99"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-3","c":"sbr-20-structure-3#1","x":"sort","t":"Exchange difference on goodwill of a foreign subsidiary","a":"OCI (translation reserve and NCI)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Also OCI, shared with NCI under the fair value method.","i":"1a5597d553"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-3","c":"sbr-20-structure-3#2","x":"sort","t":"The subsidiary's exchange loss on retranslating a loan owed to the parent","a":"P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It's a monetary item in the subsidiary's own books, so the loss goes through its P/L.","i":"c063ae8e80"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-3","c":"sbr-20-structure-3#3","x":"sort","t":"The parent's exchange gain on a foreign currency receivable from a customer","a":"P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. An individual company transaction (IAS 21 in its own books).","i":"cbf0459670"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-3","c":"sbr-20-structure-3#4","x":"sort","t":"The group's share of a foreign subsidiary's profit, translated at the average rate","a":"Group retained earnings","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Profit belongs in retained earnings; only the translation differences go to OCI.","i":"85e13c7296"},{"s":"sbr-20-structure-3","c":"sbr-20-structure-3#5","x":"sort","t":"Translation reserve when the foreign subsidiary is sold","a":"P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It's recycled to P/L as part of the gain or loss on disposal.","i":"281d74bc1a"}],"i":"291962c3f2"}],"i":"5bc2513d63"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"20.C1 Side by side","s":"sbr-20-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Exchange differences go to P/L","a":"Foreign transactions in a company's own books","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Monetary items retranslated in a company's own books affect P/L.","i":"5064bbc36c"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Exchange differences go to OCI","a":"Translating a foreign operation","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Translation of a whole operation doesn't change its cash flows in its own currency.","i":"e2212b2dab"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Only monetary items are retranslated at the closing rate","a":"Foreign transactions in a company's own books","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Non-monetary items stay at historical rates.","i":"cfe05ec454"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"All assets and liabilities are translated at the closing rate","a":"Translating a foreign operation","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Including non-monetary items such as PPE and goodwill.","i":"21af22093c"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Exchange difference on goodwill is shared with the NCI","a":"NCI at fair value","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Goodwill includes the NCI's part.","i":"322e4f88df"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Exchange difference on goodwill belongs entirely to the group","a":"NCI at share of net assets","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Only the parent's goodwill is recognised.","i":"048fb4d4bc"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"The currency the entity's own records are kept in","a":"Functional currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Set by its primary economic environment.","i":"3f4e146b0d"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"The currency the group statements are shown in","a":"Presentation currency","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. A choice.","i":"8d51e0dff3"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Line-by-line translation of each income, expense, asset and liability","a":"Foreign subsidiary","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It's consolidated.","i":"646361454e"},{"s":"sbr-20-compare-1","c":"sbr-20-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"One investment line and one share of profit, translated, with differences to OCI","a":"Foreign associate","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Equity method.","i":"d42ac64b68"}],"i":"5afde5f4f3"}],"i":"3acd5d97ef"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"20.W Foreign group traps","s":"sbr-20-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"Goodwill on a foreign subsidiary is fixed at the acquisition-date rate.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It's an asset of the foreign operation, so it's retranslated at the closing rate every year.","i":"edf2e0b1c0"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Translation differences on a foreign subsidiary go to P/L.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. They go to OCI and build up a translation reserve (the NCI's share goes to NCI).","i":"d83f452b68"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"The translation reserve is recycled to P/L when the foreign operation is sold.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. IAS 21 treats it as realised on disposal.","i":"8a4598ebc1"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"The whole translation difference, including the NCI's share, sits in the translation reserve.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. The group's share goes to the reserve; the NCI's share is part of the NCI.","i":"6c25275e96"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"Under the proportionate method the NCI shares the exchange difference on goodwill.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. No NCI goodwill is recognised, so the whole difference belongs to the group.","i":"3fe7dfa027"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"An exchange difference arises even if the subsidiary makes no profit, if the rate moves.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Opening net assets are retranslated at a new closing rate.","i":"c40505631d"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"A profitable foreign subsidiary always creates an exchange gain.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. If its currency weakens against the group's, the difference can be a loss.","i":"9732d48a43"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"An intra-group loan in the parent's currency can be cancelled without adjusting the subsidiary's books.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. The subsidiary must first retranslate it at the closing rate, with the difference in its own P/L.","i":"ef63bc017c"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Goodwill impairment for the year is translated at the closing rate.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. At the average rate, like other expenses.","i":"abad71c100"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Foreign associates are translated using the same rules as subsidiaries.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Share of profit at average, investment at closing, differences to OCI.","i":"93956f6454"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"Translation differences on a foreign operation are shown in OCI as items that may be reclassified.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Because they are recycled on disposal.","i":"2692167d04"},{"s":"sbr-20-watch-1","c":"sbr-20-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"On disposal, the gain is worked out first, and the translation reserve then goes straight to retained earnings.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. The group's share of the reserve is recycled through P/L as part of the gain.","i":"c300d3186a"}],"i":"035cd044c2"}],"i":"8046fc2528"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"20.A1 Translating and goodwill","s":"sbr-20-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-20-apply-1","c":"sbr-20-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Maple's foreign subsidiary Kiwi has revenue of K600 and PPE of K250. Rates (K per $): acquisition 4.0, average 3.0, closing 2.5. What goes into the group figures?","a":"Revenue $200; PPE $100","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: income and expenses at the average rate; assets and liabilities at the closing rate. Applied: 600 / 3.0 = 200; 250 / 2.5 = 100. P/L category: Kiwi's revenue and costs fall in their usual categories (mostly Operating). Users: Group revenue now moves with the currency as well as with sales; investors look at constant-currency growth. Common mistakes: 240 (closing rate for revenue), 62.5 (historical rate for PPE).","i":"d41444019a"},{"s":"sbr-20-apply-1","c":"sbr-20-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Maple bought 80% of Kiwi at the start of the year for K400; NCI at fair value K100; Kiwi's net assets K300. Rates: acquisition 4.0, closing 2.5. What is goodwill at the year end and the exchange difference on it?","a":"$80; gain $30 in OCI, $24 group and $6 NCI","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: goodwill is worked out in the subsidiary's currency and retranslated at the closing rate; with full goodwill the difference is shared. Applied: K400 + K100 − K300 = K200; K200 / 4.0 = $50 at acquisition, K200 / 2.5 = $80 now: gain $30, 80% / 20%. P/L category: No P/L effect: the difference goes to OCI (may be reclassified). Users: Equity moves with the currency; total comprehensive income is more volatile than profit. Common mistake: keeping goodwill at $50.","i":"cba92f862c"},{"s":"sbr-20-apply-1","c":"sbr-20-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Kiwi's net assets were K300 at acquisition; it earned K120 in the year (average rate 3.0); closing net assets are K420. Rates: acquisition 4.0, closing 2.5. What is the exchange difference on net assets?","a":"$53 gain","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: compare closing net assets at the closing rate with opening net assets at the opening rate plus profit at the average rate. Applied: 420 / 2.5 = 168; 300 / 4.0 = 75; 120 / 3.0 = 40; 168 − 75 − 40 = 53. P/L category: No P/L effect: OCI. Users: A weaker group currency raises reported net assets without any change in the subsidiary's business. Common mistakes: 93 (forgetting profit), 45 (translating profit at the closing rate).","i":"9842626240"},{"s":"sbr-20-apply-1","c":"sbr-20-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Goodwill of K200 was $50 at the opening rate (4.0). This year it is impaired by K30 (average rate 3.0); the closing rate is 2.5. What is the exchange difference on goodwill?","a":"$28 gain","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: opening goodwill at opening rate, less impairment at average rate, balance to closing goodwill at closing rate. Applied: closing K170 / 2.5 = 68; impairment K30 / 3.0 = 10; 68 − (50 − 10) = 28. P/L category: Impairment of $10 in Operating; the exchange gain in OCI. Users: Profit falls by the impairment; the currency gain lifts only OCI. Common mistake: 18 (68 − 50, ignoring the impairment).","i":"d79ad922b6"},{"s":"sbr-20-apply-1","c":"sbr-20-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Maple owns 80% of Kiwi, which earned K120 this year. Rates (K per $): 4.0 at acquisition, 3.0 average, 2.5 closing. How much goes into group retained earnings?","a":"$32","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: the group's share of post-acquisition profit is at the average rate; exchange differences go separately to the translation reserve. Applied: 120 / 3.0 = 40; 80% = 32. Common mistakes: 38.4 (closing rate), 24 (acquisition rate), 40 (forgetting NCI).","i":"1ad7e44421"},{"s":"sbr-20-apply-1","c":"sbr-20-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"Kiwi's NCI was measured at the proportionate share of net assets instead. Goodwill rises by $30 on translation. How is the $30 split?","a":"All to the group's translation reserve","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: under the proportionate method only the parent's goodwill is recognised. Applied: the whole $30 belongs to the group. P/L category: No P/L effect: OCI. Users: The whole currency effect on goodwill sits with the parent's owners. Common mistake: splitting it as for full goodwill.","i":"91daa5155b"}],"i":"48baa5af09"},{"x":"stage","t":"20.A2 Loans, associates and disposal","s":"sbr-20-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-20-apply-2","c":"sbr-20-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"Maple lends Kiwi $500 when the rate is 3.0 (Kiwi records K1,500). At the year end the rate is 2.5. What happens before the balances are cancelled?","a":"Kiwi retranslates the loan to K1,250 and records a K250 gain in its P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: in Kiwi's books the loan is a foreign currency monetary item, so it is retranslated at the closing rate with the difference in P/L. Applied: $500 × 2.5 = K1,250; gain K250; then $500 cancels against $500. P/L category: Kiwi's gain is in Financing (it's on a loan). Users: The group's profit includes an exchange gain on an internal loan; users may see volatility from internal financing. Common mistake: cancelling without the retranslation, leaving a difference.","i":"91d19ca999"},{"s":"sbr-20-apply-2","c":"sbr-20-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"Pine buys 25% of a foreign associate for D800 (rate 4.0). The associate earns D160 (average rate 3.2). Closing rate 2.8. What does the group show?","a":"Investment $300; share of profit $12.5; exchange gain $87.5 in OCI","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Rule: equity method plus translation: share of profit at the average rate, investment at the closing rate, the difference to OCI. Applied: cost 800 / 4.0 = 200; profit 25% × 160 = D40 / 3.2 = 12.5; closing D840 / 2.8 = 300; gain 300 − 200 − 12.5 = 87.5. P/L category: Share of profit in Investing; the exchange gain in OCI. Users: The investment's carrying amount moves with the currency; profit isn't affected by the translation. Common mistake: leaving the investment at historical rates.","i":"1b58014cf0"},{"s":"sbr-20-apply-2","c":"sbr-20-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"Cedar sells its 100% foreign subsidiary for $900. Net assets $500, goodwill $150; the translation reserve holds a $60 gain. What is the gain on disposal?","a":"$310 in P/L (operating)","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: on disposal, the group's share of cumulative translation differences is recycled to P/L. Applied: 900 − (500 + 150) = 250, plus the 60 recycled = 310. P/L category: Operating (the gain including the recycled 60). Users: Part of the gain is old currency movements finally passing through profit: a one-off. Common mistake: transferring the reserve to retained earnings.","i":"600c3d39a9"},{"s":"sbr-20-apply-2","c":"sbr-20-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"Cedar's foreign subsidiary had a translation reserve showing a $40 loss when it was sold. Proceeds $700; net assets plus goodwill $600. Gain?","a":"$60","d":"Reference: IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: a cumulative loss is recycled too. Applied: 700 − 600 = 100, less the 40 recycled loss = 60. P/L category: Operating (the gain is reduced by the recycled loss). Users: Past currency losses, hidden in OCI, now reduce profit. Common mistake: 140 (adding the loss).","i":"2e08e5bbaa"}],"i":"0ff874576d"}],"i":"92b86b0385"}],"i":"d835314898"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 21: Group statement of cash flows","ch":21,"d":"Study Text pages 537–580.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"21.1 IAS 7: basics and classification","s":"sbr-21-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#0","x":"card","t":"IFRS 18 changed IAS 7 so the indirect method now starts at operating profit, not profit before tax","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. The subtotal is now 'cash from operating activities before income taxes'.","i":"e7fc1b8464"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#1","x":"card","t":"After IFRS 18, interest paid is always a financing cash flow","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Interest and dividends received are investing; dividends paid are financing.","i":"fb4e69661b"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#2","x":"card","t":"The cash flow statement helps users judge liquidity, solvency and financial adaptability","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Profit can look healthy while cash runs out.","i":"f4d367eab4"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#3","x":"card","t":"Operating activities are the main revenue activities plus anything not investing or financing, including tax paid","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Tax paid sits in operating activities.","i":"b6f7e223de"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#4","x":"card","t":"Investing activities are buying and selling long-term assets and investments, plus interest and dividends received","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Loans to others (except by banks) are investing too.","i":"eea203b776"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#5","x":"card","t":"Financing activities are flows that change the size and make-up of equity and borrowings","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Share issues, loans and repayments, lease principal, interest paid, dividends paid.","i":"a3a82d5741"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#6","x":"card","t":"The direct method shows receipts from customers and payments to suppliers and staff; the indirect method adjusts operating profit for non-cash items and working capital","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. IAS 7 encourages direct; most use indirect. Users find direct easier to understand.","i":"81b658905f"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#7","x":"card","t":"In the indirect method, an increase in inventory or receivables reduces cash; an increase in payables increases it","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Add back depreciation, impairment and losses; deduct gains.","i":"8a967e3d07"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#8","x":"card","t":"Cash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid, readily convertible investments with an insignificant risk of value change (usually ≤3 months from acquisition)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Overdrafts repayable on demand are usually included.","i":"ee70050011"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#9","x":"card","t":"Listed equity shares are not cash equivalents (value risk too high)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Minted: overdraft and 28-day deposit included; shares excluded.","i":"73b0eee3f9"},{"s":"sbr-21-1","c":"sbr-21-1#10","x":"card","t":"Moving PPE sale proceeds into operating cash flows is misleading and an ethical breach","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Plaster: users would overestimate sustainable operating cash.","i":"db3662d3a2"}],"i":"4508304de9"},{"x":"stage","t":"21.2 Working out the cash figures","s":"sbr-21-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-2","c":"sbr-21-2#0","x":"card","t":"Cash paid for PPE is the balancing figure after opening balance, depreciation, disposals, revaluations and non-cash additions like new leases","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Example: 250 − 100 + 20 + 15 − 60 − 30 = 75 cash spent.","i":"badd6f97a5"},{"s":"sbr-21-2","c":"sbr-21-2#1","x":"card","t":"Tax paid is the balancing figure on current and deferred tax together (opening + charge − closing)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Example: (50 + 100) + 180 − (100 + 120) = 110.","i":"8b904c82db"},{"s":"sbr-21-2","c":"sbr-21-2#2","x":"card","t":"Dividends paid are found from retained earnings: opening + profit − closing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Single: 35 + 34 − 60 = 9.","i":"216cf88c91"},{"s":"sbr-21-2","c":"sbr-21-2#3","x":"card","t":"Interest paid is found from the interest accrual: opening + charge − closing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Single: 5 + 3 − 4 = 4.","i":"563c757d88"},{"s":"sbr-21-2","c":"sbr-21-2#4","x":"card","t":"The profit or loss on disposal of PPE is removed from operating profit; the proceeds go to investing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Single: profit 4 deducted, proceeds 13 in investing.","i":"dabbd71b5e"}],"i":"48af322afb"},{"x":"stage","t":"21.3 Group cash flows","s":"sbr-21-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#0","x":"card","t":"The group cash flow statement is built from the consolidated statements, so intra-group flows are already eliminated","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Only cash with outside parties appears.","i":"c5bb2aa300"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#1","x":"card","t":"Buying a subsidiary is shown in investing as cash paid less the subsidiary's cash acquired","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Sparkling: 475,000 outflow.","i":"09dd1546be"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#2","x":"card","t":"In workings, a subsidiary acquired in the year adds its assets and liabilities without any cash flow","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. AH: CJ's inventory and receivables are added before finding working capital changes.","i":"cd3cdc650d"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#3","x":"card","t":"Selling a subsidiary is shown in investing as proceeds less the cash the subsidiary takes with it","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Its balances are removed in the workings.","i":"87ba4498d3"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#4","x":"card","t":"A gain on disposal of a subsidiary is deducted from operating profit in the indirect method","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. The cash is in investing.","i":"6eb652b747"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#5","x":"card","t":"Goodwill impairment is added back to operating profit","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Find it from the goodwill working including new acquisitions.","i":"e42d6e94da"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#6","x":"card","t":"Dividends paid to NCI are financing and are the balancing figure in the NCI working (opening + TCI to NCI + acquisitions − disposals − closing)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Example: 440 + 500 − 840 = 100.","i":"bbb5993a00"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#7","x":"card","t":"Dividends received from associates are investing and are the balancing figure in the associate working (opening + share of profit and OCI − closing)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Example: 456 + 48 − 466 = 38.","i":"7abbe60185"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#8","x":"card","t":"Loans to and payments for associates are investing cash flows","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. The associate isn't in the group, so these flows appear.","i":"017cfe3fe2"},{"s":"sbr-21-3","c":"sbr-21-3#9","x":"card","t":"IAS 7 is criticised for allowing two methods, unclear classifications (leases, research) and weak disclosure of restrictions on cash","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 16 Leases. E.g. cash stuck overseas.","i":"2915de3770"}],"i":"6d640c398c"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"21.S1 Building the statement","s":"sbr-21-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-structure-1","c":"sbr-21-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Operating section, indirect method (after the IFRS 18 changes to IAS 7)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It now starts at operating profit, not profit before tax, and interest paid is no longer here.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Operating profit","i":"4e0f2a23b0"},{"x":"step","t":"Add back non-cash charges (depreciation, impairment, losses on disposal, provision increases)","i":"30fa74a0f3"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct non-cash gains (gains on disposal of assets or subsidiaries)","i":"99df824cd6"},{"x":"step","t":"Adjust for working capital changes (inventory, receivables, payables)","i":"b9a816f496"},{"x":"step","t":"= Cash from operating activities before income taxes","i":"0ab05e06ad"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct tax paid = net cash from operating activities","i":"81cc10ebc2"}],"i":"98876907b0"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-1","c":"sbr-21-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"The statement as a whole","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. The exchange-rate effect on cash held in foreign currency is a separate reconciling line.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Operating activities","i":"c41fcb0e67"},{"x":"step","t":"Investing activities","i":"0898b1dbe4"},{"x":"step","t":"Financing activities","i":"1fc6073a91"},{"x":"step","t":"Net change in cash and cash equivalents","i":"bd8031be58"},{"x":"step","t":"Add opening cash and cash equivalents (and the effect of exchange rates)","i":"71cbc939bc"},{"x":"step","t":"= Closing cash and cash equivalents","i":"126c56380e"}],"i":"e4fe4104f4"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-1","c":"sbr-21-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Working capital with a subsidiary bought and another sold","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Balances arriving or leaving with subsidiaries are not trading cash flows.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Closing balance − opening balance","i":"6910709419"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct the balance the bought subsidiary brought in","i":"60c859e536"},{"x":"step","t":"Add back the balance the sold subsidiary took out","i":"874995c364"},{"x":"step","t":"= the movement that came from trading (cash effect)","i":"f28c2288d1"}],"i":"8fb4cec2bd"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-1","c":"sbr-21-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Cash paid for PPE (a balancing working)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Every non-cash movement must be taken out so that the balance is pure cash.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Opening PPE","i":"12d257f4c1"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct depreciation and the carrying amount of disposals","i":"f440223a30"},{"x":"step","t":"Add revaluations, PPE from subsidiaries acquired, new leased assets and exchange gains","i":"f59cd79961"},{"x":"step","t":"Balancing figure = cash paid","i":"44c0dcab8a"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing PPE","i":"6bcea26ac2"}],"i":"f70f23c981"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-1","c":"sbr-21-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"Dividend paid to the NCI (a balancing working)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Use the NCI share of total comprehensive income if OCI is shared.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Opening NCI","i":"edb3ffc4b8"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI from subsidiaries acquired","i":"813927a0eb"},{"x":"step","t":"Add NCI share of total comprehensive income","i":"f42600dbb6"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct NCI removed on disposals","i":"105b974f0b"},{"x":"step","t":"Balancing figure = dividend paid (financing)","i":"93dfd27f65"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing NCI","i":"bbd66ae33b"}],"i":"e7c4c75e41"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-1","c":"sbr-21-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"Dividend received from an associate (a balancing working)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. The associate is outside the group, so its dividends are real cash inflows.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Opening investment in associate","i":"c698b247a9"},{"x":"step","t":"Add share of profit and share of OCI","i":"154967316a"},{"x":"step","t":"Add any new investment (cash paid separately in investing)","i":"badf6c97f7"},{"x":"step","t":"Balancing figure = dividend received (investing)","i":"5574e3c98c"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing investment","i":"7653720311"}],"i":"b4bcce974b"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-1","c":"sbr-21-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"Tax paid (a balancing working)","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Including deferred tax in both balances avoids a mismatch with the total charge.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Opening current tax and deferred tax","i":"e2d42485ab"},{"x":"step","t":"Add the tax charge (P/L and OCI)","i":"ffbf092191"},{"x":"step","t":"Add tax balances of subsidiaries acquired (deduct those of subsidiaries sold)","i":"be873bbc12"},{"x":"step","t":"Balancing figure = tax paid","i":"e189d52c28"},{"x":"step","t":"Closing current tax and deferred tax","i":"26fad060c3"}],"i":"67a453dc63"}],"i":"a1bbf6dc27"},{"x":"stage","t":"21.S2 Which section?","s":"sbr-21-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Interest paid on a bank loan","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. After the IFRS 18 changes, IAS 7 puts interest paid in financing.","i":"390c66eae1"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Interest received on a deposit","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Interest and dividends received are investing.","i":"8cd6a8d9f3"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Dividends received from an associate","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Investing.","i":"54d9e3c266"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Dividend paid to the NCI","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. A payment to owners of the group's equity: financing.","i":"0dc48405c1"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Cash paid for a subsidiary, less the cash it held","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Shown as one investing line, net of cash acquired.","i":"d6f3a3b4ff"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Shares issued to the seller as consideration for a subsidiary","a":"Not a cash flow","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. No cash moves, so it's left out (disclosed as a non-cash transaction).","i":"5f33490f3d"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Capital part of lease payments","a":"Financing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 16 Leases. Repaying the lease liability is financing.","i":"87c6df9dfc"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Income tax paid","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Operating unless specifically linked to investing or financing.","i":"3ff3f88084"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"A new right-of-use asset recognised under a lease","a":"Not a cash flow","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 16 Leases. No cash moves when the asset is recognised.","i":"ea3835e923"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Cash spent on research","a":"Operating","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Research is expensed, so it's operating (some argue it should be investing).","i":"649a261734"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#10","x":"sort","t":"Exchange gain on translating a foreign subsidiary's PPE","a":"Not a cash flow","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. A non-cash movement removed in the PPE working.","i":"5913a25a5f"},{"s":"sbr-21-structure-2","c":"sbr-21-structure-2#11","x":"sort","t":"Proceeds from selling a subsidiary, net of its cash","a":"Investing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Investing; the gain on sale is deducted in the operating section.","i":"b5889d369e"}],"i":"b1cd554a65"}],"i":"ef03cecadf"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"21.C1 Side by side","s":"sbr-21-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Shows cash received from customers and paid to suppliers","a":"Direct method","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Clearer for users; IAS 7 encourages it.","i":"40ef20d374"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Starts from operating profit and adjusts for non-cash items","a":"Indirect method","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Most companies use it.","i":"57d499c7b7"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Harder for users to understand; adjustments don't always match the balance sheet","a":"Indirect method","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. A key criticism in the investor perspective.","i":"d75b11bd3a"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Starting point of the indirect method","a":"After IFRS 18's IAS 7 changes","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Now operating profit (previously profit before tax).","i":"1a3c8688e6"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Interest paid could be in operating or financing","a":"Before IFRS 18's IAS 7 changes","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Now it must be financing.","i":"025da1517e"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"A bank overdraft repayable on demand and used in daily cash management","a":"Cash and cash equivalents","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. It's part of cash management.","i":"6ec2dd8b4d"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Money in an account needing 28 days' notice, kept for short-term needs, no significant penalty","a":"Cash and cash equivalents","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Readily convertible with insignificant risk of value change.","i":"fe6b149625"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Listed ordinary shares that could be sold today","a":"Not cash or cash equivalents","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Their value can change significantly.","i":"8bde8989a7"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"A six-month deposit made four months ago","a":"Not cash or cash equivalents","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Maturity is measured from the date of acquisition: six months is over three.","i":"f5c43a4373"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Preference shares bought within three months of a fixed redemption date","a":"Cash and cash equivalents","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. IAS 7's exception for equity close to redemption.","i":"272f6e30a3"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Cash flows with an associate","a":"Shown in the group cash flow","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. The associate is outside the group.","i":"48726821d6"},{"s":"sbr-21-compare-1","c":"sbr-21-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Cash flows between parent and subsidiary","a":"Eliminated","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. They're intra-group.","i":"919888e042"}],"i":"bda53a47a4"}],"i":"f132b7524a"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"21.W Cash flow traps","s":"sbr-21-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"The full price paid for a subsidiary is shown as an investing outflow.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Only the cash paid, net of the subsidiary's cash at acquisition; share consideration isn't a cash flow.","i":"b574aad4d5"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"The gain on disposal of a subsidiary is deducted in the operating section.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. It's non-cash; the proceeds appear in investing.","i":"fbff962f24"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"A subsidiary's inventory at acquisition increases the group's inventory cash outflow.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. It arrived through the acquisition, so it's taken out of the working capital movement.","i":"7b47e2cb65"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Under IAS 7 (as changed by IFRS 18) interest paid can still be operating.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. It must now be financing for most entities.","i":"389459592c"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A share of an associate's profit has to be deducted from operating profit in the indirect method.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Under IFRS 18 it's already below operating profit (investing category).","i":"007b6c152b"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"Exchange differences on cash held abroad are shown as a separate reconciling line.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. They aren't operating, investing or financing flows.","i":"cd478f5897"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"An exchange loss on a foreign subsidiary's loan increases the cash raised from loans.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. It's non-cash; it must be taken out of the loan working.","i":"4a28080263"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Classifying asset sale proceeds as operating cash flow is just a presentation choice.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. It misleads users about sustainable cash generation; it breaches IAS 7 and integrity.","i":"ba192b090a"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Cash equivalents are investments with no more than three months to maturity from the date they were bought.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Not from the reporting date.","i":"f19c35a25b"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Dividends paid to the NCI are presented separately in financing.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. They're a payment to owners of the group.","i":"1a4a6d6ad1"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"A revaluation gain on PPE is a cash inflow.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Non-cash: removed in the PPE working.","i":"22216d4693"},{"s":"sbr-21-watch-1","c":"sbr-21-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"The group statement of cash flows is prepared from the consolidated statements, so intra-group flows are already gone.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. That's why it's based on consolidated figures.","i":"c17e72aa77"}],"i":"2b5ecaba5a"}],"i":"626d2c1d7f"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"21.A1 The balancing workings","s":"sbr-21-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-apply-1","c":"sbr-21-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Group PPE: opening 400, closing 620. Depreciation 60; disposals at carrying amount 30; revaluation gain 50; PPE from a subsidiary acquired (fair value) 90; new right-of-use assets 40. Cash paid for PPE?","a":"130","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 16 Leases. Rule: take every non-cash movement out of PPE; the balance is cash. Applied: 620 − 400 + 60 + 30 − 50 − 90 − 40 = 130. Users: Capital spending shows whether the group is investing to sustain future cash flows. Common mistakes: 310 (only depreciation and disposals), 220 (keeping the subsidiary's PPE), 170 (forgetting leased assets).","i":"31ae1797b2"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-1","c":"sbr-21-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Tax: opening current 80 and deferred 40; closing current 90 and deferred 55; the tax charge is 150; a subsidiary bought in the year had a tax liability of 15. Tax paid?","a":"140","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 12 Income Taxes. Rule: include current and deferred balances and the acquired subsidiary's tax. Applied: 120 + 15 + 150 − 145 = 140. Users: Tax paid is a real outflow that can differ greatly from the charge. Common mistakes: 125 (forgetting the subsidiary), 155 (leaving out deferred tax), 150 (assuming the charge equals cash).","i":"793560a272"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-1","c":"sbr-21-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"NCI: opening 300, closing 360. NCI share of total comprehensive income 90; NCI from a subsidiary bought 70; NCI removed on a subsidiary sold 50. Dividend paid to the NCI?","a":"50","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Rule: reconcile the NCI; the balance is the dividend. Applied: 300 + 70 + 90 − 50 − 360 = 50 (financing). Users: Cash leaving the group to outside shareholders, not available to the parent's owners. Common mistakes: 30 (ignoring both changes in the group), 100 (ignoring the disposal).","i":"d6f1816777"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-1","c":"sbr-21-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Investment in associate: opening 500, closing 540. Share of profit 60; share of OCI 10. Dividend received?","a":"30","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 28 Investments in Associates and Joint Ventures. Rule: reconcile the associate balance; the balance is cash received. Applied: 500 + 60 + 10 − 540 = 30 (investing). Users: Shows whether associates actually return cash, not just profit. Common mistake: 40 (forgetting the share of OCI).","i":"d93145f565"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-1","c":"sbr-21-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Inventory: opening 700, closing 900. A subsidiary bought in the year had inventory of 120; a subsidiary sold had inventory of 60. Cash effect of the inventory movement?","a":"140 outflow","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Rule: remove balances that arrived or left with subsidiaries. Applied: 900 − 700 − 120 + 60 = 140 increase: an outflow. Users: Working capital growth from trading consumes cash; growth by acquisition doesn't. Common mistakes: 200 (no adjustment), 260 (adding the acquisition), 80 (wrong signs on the disposal).","i":"3e8944a356"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-1","c":"sbr-21-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"Loans: opening 100, closing 180. A foreign subsidiary's loans had an exchange loss of 15 on translation; no repayments. Cash raised from new loans?","a":"65","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: exchange movements on liabilities are non-cash. Applied: 180 − 100 − 15 = 65 (financing). Users: Lenders see how much new debt was really raised. Common mistakes: 80 (ignoring the exchange loss), 95 (adding it).","i":"eb95da8864"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-1","c":"sbr-21-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"Interest: opening accrual 5, closing accrual 8; interest expense 30. Interest paid and where?","a":"27, financing","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements. Rule: reconcile the accrual; interest paid is financing after the IFRS 18 changes to IAS 7. Applied: 5 + 30 − 8 = 27. Users: With interest in financing, operating cash flow is no longer reduced by it; comparisons with older years need care. Common mistake: 33 (wrong direction on the accrual).","i":"364f37c18d"}],"i":"8c4bed1f1f"},{"x":"stage","t":"21.A2 Subsidiaries and judgement","s":"sbr-21-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-21-apply-2","c":"sbr-21-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"Birch buys 75% of Elm, paying 800 in cash and 200 in its own shares. Elm had cash of 50 at acquisition. What appears in investing?","a":"Outflow 750","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Rule: show cash paid net of cash acquired; share consideration isn't a cash flow. Applied: 800 − 50 = 750. Users: Shows the real cash cost of growth by acquisition. Common mistakes: 950 (including shares), 800 (ignoring Elm's cash).","i":"08f6b861b7"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-2","c":"sbr-21-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"Birch sells a subsidiary for 600 cash and records a group gain of 90. The subsidiary had cash of 40. How is it shown?","a":"Investing inflow 560; 90 deducted in the operating section","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Rule: show proceeds net of cash given up; remove the non-cash gain from operating profit. Applied: 600 − 40 = 560. P/L category: The 90 gain is in Operating in the P/L. Users: The cash from the sale is a one-off, not sustainable operating cash. Common mistake: leaving the gain in operating profit, so it's counted twice.","i":"56b251bd0c"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-2","c":"sbr-21-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"A subsidiary was bought for shares only; it held cash of 30. What appears in the statement of cash flows?","a":"An investing inflow of 30","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Rule: cash acquired with a subsidiary is a group cash inflow, net of any cash paid (nil here). Applied: inflow 30; the share issue is a non-cash transaction, disclosed. Users: Non-cash deals are disclosed so users see the full investing activity. Common mistake: ignoring the cash acquired.","i":"8c6816c70a"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-2","c":"sbr-21-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"Directors want to show the proceeds of selling surplus buildings as operating cash flow, to look stronger before a loan application. What is your advice?","a":"Refuse: they're investing; the change would mislead lenders and breach integrity and objectivity","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; ACCA Code of Ethics and Conduct. Rule: IAS 7 classifies disposals of long-term assets as investing; ethics require faithful representation. Applied: asset sales can't recur, so showing them as operating overstates sustainable cash generation; the motive (raising finance) threatens objectivity. Users: Lenders rely on operating cash flow to judge repayment capacity; misclassification would hide weak trading. Common mistake: treating classification as a free choice.","i":"173544df94"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-2","c":"sbr-21-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"A group's foreign subsidiary holds cash. Its translation raises the group's closing cash by 12. How is the 12 shown?","a":"A separate reconciling line: effect of exchange rates on cash","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 21 The Effects of Changes in Foreign Exchange Rates. Rule: IAS 7 shows the effect of exchange rate changes on cash separately from the three activities. Applied: a separate line after the net change in cash. Users: Users can separate real cash generation from currency effects. Common mistake: hiding it in operating.","i":"81b494ca28"},{"s":"sbr-21-apply-2","c":"sbr-21-apply-2#5","x":"example","t":"Operating profit is 500 and includes a 40 impairment of goodwill, a 25 gain on selling equipment and a 90 gain on disposing of a subsidiary. Adjusted figure before working capital (ignoring depreciation)?","a":"425","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows. Rule: add back non-cash charges; deduct non-cash gains. Applied: 500 + 40 − 25 − 90 = 425. Users: Users see that non-cash items and one-off gains explain much of the profit. Common mistakes: 655 (adding the gains), 575 (forgetting the subsidiary gain).","i":"498c0b370f"}],"i":"0278821883"}],"i":"983a91f44b"}],"i":"d41dc8585d"}],"i":"6260e6375c"},{"x":"area","a":"E","t":"E · Interpreting information for stakeholders","k":[{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 22: Analysis and interpretation","ch":22,"d":"Study Text pages 581–632.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"22.1 Ratios and EPS","s":"sbr-22-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#0","x":"card","t":"Gross margin can rise because of higher selling prices, lower purchase prices or a better sales mix","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Always explain the cause, not just the number.","i":"a9e05d0dcb"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#1","x":"card","t":"Operating margin is more volatile than gross margin because of fixed costs and judgemental items like depreciation and impairment","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Judgement also makes it harder to compare companies.","i":"e7c86599dd"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#2","x":"card","t":"ROCE = profit before financing and income taxes / capital employed, where capital employed is equity plus interest-bearing debt","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. It measures how efficiently resources are used. Operating profit can be used instead only if the entity has no investment income, because the two figures are then the same.","i":"348173f1d2"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#3","x":"card","t":"Basic EPS = profit attributable to ordinary shareholders (after irredeemable preference dividends) / weighted average number of ordinary shares","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Group EPS uses the parent's share of consolidated profit.","i":"58ee149522"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#4","x":"card","t":"Shares issued at full price during the year are weighted for the time they were in issue","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. 800,000 × 6/12 + 1,000,000 × 6/12 = 900,000.","i":"1409cc4482"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#5","x":"card","t":"A bonus issue is treated as if the shares had always been in issue, and the comparative EPS is restated","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Restate last year's EPS by the inverse of the bonus fraction, e.g. 46.0 × 5/6.","i":"cdfce3939d"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#6","x":"card","t":"The bonus fraction for a rights issue is price before the issue / theoretical ex-rights price","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Applied to shares before the rights issue; comparatives are restated too.","i":"00a24d9122"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#7","x":"card","t":"Diluted EPS adjusts earnings for costs saved (interest, preference dividends) and shares for potential ordinary shares (convertibles, options)","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Diluted EPS is disclosed when dilutive; SBR tests it only lightly.","i":"1d9ccc181e"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#8","x":"card","t":"EPS is limited because it depends on policies, ignores inflation, has little predictive value, and ignores cash flow and stewardship","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. The P/E ratio built on it shows market confidence, which can be wrong.","i":"f4e1970683"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#9","x":"card","t":"Accounting choices and estimates can change ratios without changing cash","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. E.g. cost vs fair value models, depreciation, provisions.","i":"34c9453452"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#10","x":"card","t":"Interest cover = profit before financing and income taxes / interest expense","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Low cover means trouble servicing debt if profits fall. Operating profit can be used only if there is no investment income.","i":"0ad9080cdc"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#11","x":"card","t":"Gearing, as debt / equity or debt / (debt + equity), shows the risk attached to how the entity is financed","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Highly geared entities have a greater risk of insolvency.","i":"c8c21b3216"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#12","x":"card","t":"A rising receivables collection period may point to weak credit control, and so irrecoverable debts","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Payables are the other side: a long credit period is free finance until suppliers cut it off.","i":"44e24f9760"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#13","x":"card","t":"P/E = share price / EPS, and a high figure suggests the market expects growth","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. It is the market's view of prospects, and the market can be wrong.","i":"60ec8da4b9"},{"s":"sbr-22-1","c":"sbr-22-1#14","x":"card","t":"A current ratio that is too high may suggest inefficient management of working capital","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Higher is safer only up to a point.","i":"bb6c6c52fd"}],"i":"95a1625b5b"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.2 Cash flows, APMs and tech companies","s":"sbr-22-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#0","x":"card","t":"Cash flows help analysis because they are objective, show the quality of profit, show adaptability, and help predict future cash flows","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. Good-quality profit turns into cash.","i":"9b587a7016"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#1","x":"card","t":"Warning signs in a cash flow statement include weak operating cash, working capital build-up, selling PPE to fund spending, and issuing shares to repay loans","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. Tuyet: positive overall but propped up by share issues and disposals, with loans due.","i":"0ebaad52da"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#2","x":"card","t":"Common APMs include EBIT, EBITDA, net debt and free cash flow","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. APMs show management's view and can strip out one-offs.","i":"bec833215c"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#3","x":"card","t":"APMs can mislead because they may be calculated inconsistently, are hard to compare, poorly reconciled and chosen to look optimistic","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. APMs that are MPMs get IFRS 18's reconciliation rules.","i":"091e77ca0d"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#4","x":"card","t":"Tech companies' net assets are far below market value because of unrecognised brands, assets owned by others and network effects","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Each new user makes the platform more valuable.","i":"0dc11b4792"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#5","x":"card","t":"Tech companies often show years of losses then high margins, so the P/L matches revenue and costs poorly","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. Investors look at revenue growth and user numbers instead.","i":"94beca0dfd"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#6","x":"card","t":"Tech companies can give better information through integrated reporting, sustainability reporting and real-time KPIs like users and spend per user","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. Financial statements alone underreport their value.","i":"9e4c9b9dcb"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#7","x":"card","t":"An additional performance measure (APM) isn't defined by a reporting framework, so no standard says how to calculate it","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. Those that are also MPMs do get IFRS 18's disclosure rules.","i":"bdf99c2dda"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#8","x":"card","t":"Free cash flow = cash flows from operating activities less capital expenditure","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. Net financial debt = gross debt less cash, cash equivalents and other financial assets.","i":"9a863fdf9f"},{"s":"sbr-22-2","c":"sbr-22-2#9","x":"card","t":"Giving an APM undue prominence can lead users to believe it is a requirement of IFRS Accounting Standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, statements of cash flows and APMs. Too many measures can also confuse users.","i":"f571673d42"}],"i":"365da0ed96"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.3 Non-financial measures and ethics","s":"sbr-22-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-3","c":"sbr-22-3#0","x":"card","t":"Financial KPIs are limited because they are historical, ignore long-term drivers, suit tech companies poorly and can be manipulated","d":"Reference: Kaplan, non-financial measures and ethics. Non-financial measures fill the gaps.","i":"116f41fd6e"},{"s":"sbr-22-3","c":"sbr-22-3#1","x":"card","t":"Examples of non-financial measures are staff turnover, absence, staff and customer satisfaction, delivery times and brand awareness and loyalty","d":"Reference: Kaplan, non-financial measures and ethics. Lorenzo: fewer faults, shorter waits, lower turnover support future revenue despite lower profit.","i":"45bc60895d"},{"s":"sbr-22-3","c":"sbr-22-3#2","x":"card","t":"The UN defines sustainability as meeting present needs without compromising future generations' ability to meet theirs","d":"Reference: Kaplan, non-financial measures and ethics. Investors increasingly care about it.","i":"ae24c6af84"},{"s":"sbr-22-3","c":"sbr-22-3#3","x":"card","t":"Ethical factors investors check include animal welfare, arms, emissions and net zero, energy, marketing, pay gaps, supply chains, tax avoidance, transparency and the treatment of workers","d":"Reference: Kaplan, non-financial measures and ethics. Investors weigh these differently, but still want returns.","i":"fb47b15747"},{"s":"sbr-22-3","c":"sbr-22-3#4","x":"card","t":"Ethical behaviour may raise costs and prices, but it can also attract ethical investors and customers and avoid boycotts","d":"Reference: Kaplan, non-financial measures and ethics. The link between ethics and profit isn't straightforward.","i":"f288aab562"},{"s":"sbr-22-3","c":"sbr-22-3#5","x":"card","t":"Entities set sustainable goals because it's ethical, attracts government funding, lowers risk, opens growth markets and fits investors' long-term focus","d":"Reference: Kaplan, non-financial measures and ethics. The 17 UN SDGs include no poverty, zero hunger and responsible consumption.","i":"40464a0810"}],"i":"85be5181a5"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.4 Sustainability: why and who","s":"sbr-22-4","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#0","x":"card","t":"Unmanaged sustainability risks can bring regulatory fines, disruption, reputational damage and higher costs of capital","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. That's why investors need decision-useful information about long-term viability.","i":"116e54f7ed"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#1","x":"card","t":"An entity's sustainability impacts are how its operations affect people and the planet (the 'inside-out' view)","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. E.g. pollution from a factory.","i":"2b6c5ae680"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#2","x":"card","t":"An entity's sustainability dependencies are the natural and social resources it relies on to operate","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. E.g. a stable climate or fresh water. Impacts and dependencies are often the source of financial risks.","i":"aed85336b7"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#3","x":"card","t":"The UN Global Compact asks entities to align with ten principles and report through an annual Communication on Progress","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. Human rights, labour, environment and anti-corruption. It's voluntary.","i":"30c154b77e"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#4","x":"card","t":"The GRI standards, the most widely used, focus on an entity's impacts on the economy, environment and society","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. They aim for balanced reports and stakeholder engagement.","i":"773e2146c1"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#5","x":"card","t":"The Integrated Reporting Framework explains how an entity creates value using six capitals over the short, medium and long term","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. Now part of the IFRS Foundation through the Value Reporting Foundation.","i":"16f1d94e54"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#6","x":"card","t":"The ISSB was formed by the IFRS Foundation in 2021 so that one body develops both financial reporting and sustainability disclosure requirements","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. It delivers a global baseline focused on information material to investors.","i":"fb6d92f81c"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#7","x":"card","t":"The GRI and the ISSB differ mainly in their users: the ISSB serves capital markets, the GRI transparency about impacts on people and the environment","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. They've agreed to work towards compatible standards.","i":"4c945edcde"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#8","x":"card","t":"The EU's CSRD requires large companies and certain SMEs to apply the European Sustainability Reporting Standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. Singapore's exchange requires listed companies to report on a 'comply or explain' basis. Only ESRS's differences from ISSB standards are examinable.","i":"16ff553ae9"},{"s":"sbr-22-4","c":"sbr-22-4#9","x":"card","t":"IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards apply voluntarily, unless a jurisdiction requires them","d":"Reference: Kaplan, sustainability reporting. There's no ISSB mandate to apply them.","i":"a9d5d444bf"}],"i":"1c4ae25dae"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.5 IFRS S1","s":"sbr-22-5","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#0","x":"card","t":"IFRS S1's objective is disclosure of sustainability risks and opportunities useful to primary users in deciding on providing resources to the entity","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. Risks that wouldn't affect the entity's prospects, cash flows, access to finance or cost of capital are out of scope.","i":"fa5eb41329"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#1","x":"card","t":"IFRS S1 disclosures must be part of the general purpose financial report, cover the same period, be released at the same time and include comparatives for all amounts","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. The standard is a global baseline: entities can go further.","i":"886d3aa9da"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#2","x":"card","t":"IFRS S1's qualitative characteristics mirror the Conceptual Framework: relevance and faithful representation, plus comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. Fundamental and enhancing, as in the Framework.","i":"55c639fd32"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#3","x":"card","t":"Under IFRS S1, sustainability information is relevant only if it is material","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. E.g. water scarcity for a food group is relevant; $50,000 of office paper isn't.","i":"f6b341437e"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#4","x":"card","t":"IFRS S1's connectivity means explaining the links between risks and opportunities, the core content areas, and the figures in the financial statements","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. E.g. how climate assumptions affect impairment or useful lives.","i":"63b046cd57"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#5","x":"card","t":"IFRS S1 disclosures are organised under governance, strategy, risk management and metrics and targets","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. The structure comes from the TCFD recommendations.","i":"15033854bd"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#6","x":"card","t":"The governance disclosures cover the processes and controls used to oversee sustainability risks and opportunities","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. E.g. who is responsible and how often they meet.","i":"9797f0af7b"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#7","x":"card","t":"The strategy disclosures cover how the entity manages these risks and opportunities and how they affect its business model, strategy and financial planning","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. Includes the financial effects and the entity's resilience.","i":"9cc9a443c7"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#8","x":"card","t":"The risk management disclosures cover how risks are identified, assessed, prioritised and monitored","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. E.g. data sources and scenario analysis.","i":"05fae43920"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#9","x":"card","t":"For amounts with high measurement uncertainty, IFRS S1 requires disclosure of the sources of uncertainty and the assumptions, approximations and judgements used","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. E.g. estimating Scope 3 emissions. Use data consistent with the financial statements where possible.","i":"5d3eb8321f"},{"s":"sbr-22-5","c":"sbr-22-5#10","x":"card","t":"Material prior period errors in sustainability disclosures are corrected by restating the comparatives, unless that isn't possible","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. The same idea as in financial reporting.","i":"a133277775"}],"i":"23fb87468d"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.6 IFRS S2 and ESRS","s":"sbr-22-6","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#0","x":"card","t":"IFRS S2 covers climate-related physical risks, transition risks and climate-related opportunities","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Those not expected to affect prospects are out of scope.","i":"3ed9a8e8c8"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#1","x":"card","t":"Physical climate risks are acute, from one-off events, or chronic, from permanent changes such as rising seas or drought","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Acute: floods, wildfires.","i":"3cb4bae311"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#2","x":"card","t":"Transition risks arise from the move to a lower-carbon economy and include policy, legal, technological, market and reputational risks","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. E.g. new emissions rules or customers moving away.","i":"b222fbbd60"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#3","x":"card","t":"IFRS S2 expects the strategy disclosures to show resilience, often through climate scenario analysis","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Without it, users can't judge whether the strategy would survive.","i":"c7e1479395"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#4","x":"card","t":"Scope 1 emissions are direct emissions from sources the entity owns or controls","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Mandatory. Reported gross in tonnes of CO2 equivalent.","i":"5cfd5d405a"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#5","x":"card","t":"Scope 2 emissions are indirect emissions from purchased electricity, steam, heating or cooling","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Mandatory.","i":"7429d49dba"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#6","x":"card","t":"Scope 3 emissions are all other indirect emissions in the value chain, and must be disclosed if material to the entity's carbon footprint","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Purchased goods, business travel, use of sold products. They can be omitted in the first year of applying S2.","i":"e99f05dc08"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#7","x":"card","t":"Under IFRS S2 emissions must be measured using the Greenhouse Gas Protocol","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. The Protocol itself isn't examinable.","i":"c8d2a95b0a"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#8","x":"card","t":"A carbon credit is a permit representing a reduction or removal of emissions somewhere else","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. E.g. projects that plant trees.","i":"30f7f49ef5"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#9","x":"card","t":"An entity disclosing a net emissions target must also disclose its gross target, and how far it relies on credits","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Plus enough detail to judge the credits: verification scheme and type. This prevents greenwashing.","i":"ab519b1067"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#10","x":"card","t":"IFRS S1 and S2 improve comparability, give a common language and may improve access to capital, but they are costly and need no statutory assurance","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. So there's room for omission, manipulation or bias.","i":"bb87a7f40d"},{"s":"sbr-22-6","c":"sbr-22-6#11","x":"card","t":"Unlike the financial materiality of ISSB standards, ESRS use double materiality, on both a financial and an impact basis","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. ESRS are mandatory for companies already reporting under the NFRD; there are 12 standards.","i":"8ed3691d62"}],"i":"a65bfb2cfb"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.7 Management commentary and not-for-profits","s":"sbr-22-7","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-7","c":"sbr-22-7#0","x":"card","t":"Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary was revised in 2025, and applying it is not mandatory","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. It can be part of a larger report or stand alone, and may have another name, such as a strategic report.","i":"24a32bb088"},{"s":"sbr-22-7","c":"sbr-22-7#1","x":"card","t":"Management commentary aims to deepen understanding of performance and position and to give management's insight into factors affecting cash flows over the short, medium and long term","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. Those factors include sustainability, so the information must be forward-looking.","i":"ba283a6c72"},{"s":"sbr-22-7","c":"sbr-22-7#2","x":"card","t":"Its six areas are business model, strategy, resources and relationships, risks, external environment and financial performance and position, including future impacts","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. Financial statements are often criticised for saying too little about risk.","i":"ee97585224"},{"s":"sbr-22-7","c":"sbr-22-7#3","x":"card","t":"Management commentary should have the Conceptual Framework's qualities and include only material information tailored to the entity","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. Generic content adds clutter and makes relevant information harder to find.","i":"61e940cc92"},{"s":"sbr-22-7","c":"sbr-22-7#4","x":"card","t":"Performance measures in management commentary should be consistent over time, widely used in the industry and reconciled to the financial statements when adjusted","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. Non-financial measures are encouraged.","i":"d7cb843105"},{"s":"sbr-22-7","c":"sbr-22-7#5","x":"card","t":"A not-for-profit entity aims to deliver services, judged on value for money: economy, efficiency and effectiveness","d":"Reference: Kaplan, not-for-profit entities. Public sector bodies normally aim to break even. Success is measured against the mission, through outputs, outcomes and impact.","i":"14cebda600"}],"i":"ef591384a9"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"22.S1 Calculations in order","s":"sbr-22-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-structure-1","c":"sbr-22-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Basic EPS after a share issue at full price","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. A full-price issue brought in real money, so only time-weighting is needed.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Take profit attributable to the parent's ordinary shareholders","i":"279a8fbf10"},{"x":"step","t":"Deduct irredeemable preference dividends","i":"7176910355"},{"x":"step","t":"Time-weight the shares for the months each number was in issue","i":"784544d7af"},{"x":"step","t":"Divide earnings by the weighted average","i":"7cc42657f4"},{"x":"step","t":"Leave the comparative unchanged","i":"5620d35eff"}],"i":"09a3ca1ab8"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-1","c":"sbr-22-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Basic EPS after a bonus issue","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Bonus shares are treated as if they had always been in issue.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find the bonus fraction (shares after / shares before)","i":"e18756faf7"},{"x":"step","t":"Multiply the shares before the issue by the fraction","i":"36be0f2069"},{"x":"step","t":"Time-weight the shares over the year","i":"67e8ffdc6a"},{"x":"step","t":"Divide earnings by the weighted average","i":"9abf9c120a"},{"x":"step","t":"Restate the comparative by the inverse of the fraction","i":"9713caf578"}],"i":"c27d64913b"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-1","c":"sbr-22-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Basic EPS after a rights issue below market price","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. A rights issue is part full-price issue and part bonus issue.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Work out the theoretical price after the issue","i":"3c071e8d2b"},{"x":"step","t":"Bonus fraction = market price before / theoretical price after","i":"f1a4961342"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply the fraction to the shares before the issue, for the months before it","i":"ab9ef65605"},{"x":"step","t":"Add the shares after the issue for the months after it","i":"d5567973d6"},{"x":"step","t":"Divide earnings by the weighted average","i":"fa23ee5718"},{"x":"step","t":"Restate the comparative by the inverse of the fraction","i":"67f4a03020"}],"i":"b137ada105"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-1","c":"sbr-22-structure-1#3","x":"steps","t":"Theoretical price after a rights issue","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. E.g. 4 at $4 plus 1 at $2 is $18 for 5 shares: $3.60.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Value of existing shares at the market price","i":"4688cfcb33"},{"x":"step","t":"Add cash from the new shares","i":"a43c175bcb"},{"x":"step","t":"Total value","i":"c6c1a007f2"},{"x":"step","t":"Divide by the total number of shares after the issue","i":"75b9c15ab1"}],"i":"4c66e58c3f"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-1","c":"sbr-22-structure-1#4","x":"steps","t":"Analysing a statement of cash flows","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; Kaplan, interpreting statements. Link each comment to a figure and say what it means for the user.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Overall cash position and the movement in the year","i":"c093314e80"},{"x":"step","t":"Operating: does profit turn into cash? Why not?","i":"5e6d16ef2d"},{"x":"step","t":"Investing: is capacity maintained, and are disposals one-offs?","i":"4ff8f1d9d2"},{"x":"step","t":"Financing: where did the money come from, and what is due?","i":"34d7a3b059"},{"x":"step","t":"Conclude on sustainability, for the user asking","i":"c490ceb97e"}],"i":"dc31682c3e"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-1","c":"sbr-22-structure-1#5","x":"steps","t":"Judging an APM","d":"Reference: Kaplan, APMs. Recurring costs like depreciation are a warning sign when added back. Not part of it: Accept it because the auditor has read the annual report; Prefer it to the IFRS figure whenever it's higher.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"See what has been adjusted and whether a loss became a profit","i":"d7dd7c018e"},{"x":"step","t":"Ask whether each item really is one-off or non-core","i":"e9d2d0d8c8"},{"x":"step","t":"Check the reconciliation to the IFRS figure","i":"b2ff07b41b"},{"x":"step","t":"Check consistency with last year and other entities","i":"52b908b5c8"},{"x":"step","t":"Check how prominent it is","i":"6f44ee9d86"},{"x":"step","t":"Conclude whether it helps or misleads","i":"f05cfa5224"}],"any":true,"i":"db1e275cc8"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-1","c":"sbr-22-structure-1#6","x":"steps","t":"Judging a sustainability report under IFRS S2","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Say what each gap stops an investor from judging.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Is each of the four core areas covered?","i":"f36b9bde45"},{"x":"step","t":"Governance: who oversees climate risk, and how often?","i":"91f34e6a5d"},{"x":"step","t":"Strategy: which risks and opportunities, what scenario analysis, what financial effect?","i":"b316313418"},{"x":"step","t":"Risk management: how are risks assessed and prioritised?","i":"863e8d7045"},{"x":"step","t":"Metrics and targets: Scopes 1, 2 and 3, comparatives and progress","i":"2e437be85e"},{"x":"step","t":"Conclude on usefulness to investors","i":"46adada7b3"}],"i":"e64de46ee2"}],"i":"a5c8f8e86e"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.S2 Which ratio group?","s":"sbr-22-structure-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Gross profit / revenue","a":"Profitability","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. It rises with higher prices, lower purchase costs or a better sales mix.","i":"cc1322b992"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Profit before financing and income taxes / capital employed","a":"Profitability","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. ROCE. Compare it with last year, the target, competitors and the cost of borrowing.","i":"dd74345f6b"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Current assets / current liabilities","a":"Liquidity and working capital","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Can short-term obligations be met?","i":"109466d16f"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Inventories / cost of sales × 365","a":"Liquidity and working capital","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. A long period may mean weak demand or poor inventory control.","i":"cec9cd1f4b"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Trade payables / credit purchases × 365","a":"Liquidity and working capital","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Free finance, until suppliers lose patience.","i":"32f82acec6"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Debt / (debt + equity)","a":"Long-term stability","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Gearing: the risk in how the entity is financed.","i":"4b76a1c678"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Profit before financing and income taxes / interest expense","a":"Long-term stability","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Interest cover.","i":"9b069e261c"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Share price / EPS","a":"Investor","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. A high P/E suggests growth is expected.","i":"2cc75c6193"},{"s":"sbr-22-structure-2","c":"sbr-22-structure-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Profit attributable to ordinary shareholders / weighted average shares","a":"Investor","d":"Reference: Kaplan, assessing financial performance and position. Basic EPS, standardised by IAS 33.","i":"819094fbd7"}],"i":"3fd1baa24d"}],"i":"ccc898213b"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"22.C1 Measures side by side","s":"sbr-22-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Adjusted operating profit used in the results announcement","a":"An MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. A subtotal of income and expenses in public communications showing management's view.","i":"1baf2c09b9"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Underlying profit before tax in the annual report","a":"An MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. Same reasoning; IFRS 18's note and reconciliation apply.","i":"ae937345fa"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Free cash flow","a":"An APM but not an MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. It's a cash measure, not a subtotal of income and expenses.","i":"ae906a284a"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Gearing","a":"An APM but not an MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. Financial ratios aren't MPMs.","i":"fa40d2d1c5"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Net financial debt","a":"An APM but not an MPM","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. It's based on assets and liabilities.","i":"b484ab1a0d"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Operating profit","a":"A required or common subtotal","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. IFRS 18 requires it.","i":"b7f08be79c"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Gross profit","a":"A required or common subtotal","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. Commonly used, so not an MPM.","i":"597231fe46"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Lets users see the business through management's eyes","a":"A benefit of APMs","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. They add insight beyond the required figures.","i":"52717d6942"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Strips out one-off items to show the underlying trend","a":"A benefit of APMs","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. Provided the items really are one-off.","i":"af6791186a"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Two entities use the same name for different calculations","a":"A drawback of APMs","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. It harms comparability.","i":"11c77a9d0b"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Chosen to give an overly optimistic picture","a":"A drawback of APMs","d":"Reference: IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements; Kaplan, APMs. Common when costs are removed but gains kept.","i":"636c9b5176"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Issue at full price during the year","a":"Time-weight only","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. New money arrived, so comparatives don't change.","i":"cc14434635"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Issue of new shares for cash, below market price, to existing holders","a":"Time-weight and apply a bonus fraction","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. A rights issue contains a bonus element.","i":"b89053dc5e"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-1","c":"sbr-22-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"One new share free for every five held","a":"Treat as always in issue","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. A bonus issue brings no cash.","i":"457197bba6"}],"i":"9feb30f86c"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.C2 IFRS Sustainability Standards or ESRS?","s":"sbr-22-compare-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#0","x":"sort","t":"Voluntary unless a jurisdiction requires it","a":"ISSB standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. There's no ISSB mandate.","i":"64e1527716"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#1","x":"sort","t":"Mandatory for companies already reporting under the NFRD","a":"ESRS","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. Required by EU law through the CSRD.","i":"972abae516"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#2","x":"sort","t":"Suitable for all profit-orientated and public sector entities","a":"ISSB standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. A deliberately wide scope.","i":"b223926309"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#3","x":"sort","t":"Target users are investors, lenders and creditors","a":"ISSB standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. Same primary users as IFRS Accounting Standards.","i":"e5a082c5fa"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#4","x":"sort","t":"Target users are a wider range of stakeholders","a":"ESRS","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. Follows from also looking at impact.","i":"7cfff31a82"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#5","x":"sort","t":"Two standards in total","a":"ISSB standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. IFRS S1 and IFRS S2.","i":"c6b0c2d0eb"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#6","x":"sort","t":"Twelve standards: two cross-cutting and ten topical","a":"ESRS","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. ESRS 1, ESRS 2 and ten topics.","i":"fbe0823a6f"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#7","x":"sort","t":"Financial materiality","a":"ISSB standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. What matters to the entity's prospects.","i":"08076f1b19"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#8","x":"sort","t":"Double materiality","a":"ESRS","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. Both financial and impact.","i":"aa4f264d7c"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#9","x":"sort","t":"Some disclosures always apply, whatever the materiality assessment","a":"ESRS","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. Others depend on the assessment.","i":"db824228d9"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#10","x":"sort","t":"No prescribed location in the corporate report","a":"ISSB standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. ESRS prescribe the structure.","i":"a8bfccf17a"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-2","c":"sbr-22-compare-2#11","x":"sort","t":"Sector-specific disclosures included","a":"ISSB standards","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. ESRS's are still to be developed.","i":"67ffafdf56"}],"i":"2b00773a4b"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.C3 Sustainability terms","s":"sbr-22-compare-3","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#0","x":"sort","t":"Fuel burned in the company's delivery vans","a":"Scope 1","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Direct, from a source it controls.","i":"4b0ffa6195"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#1","x":"sort","t":"Electricity bought for the head office","a":"Scope 2","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Purchased energy.","i":"f324e6266c"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#2","x":"sort","t":"Staff flights on commercial airlines","a":"Scope 3","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Business travel, in the value chain.","i":"4e77718e27"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#3","x":"sort","t":"Emissions when customers use the products sold","a":"Scope 3","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Downstream in the value chain.","i":"a51c43f267"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#4","x":"sort","t":"A flood closes a distribution centre","a":"Physical: acute","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. A one-off event.","i":"37069e69f2"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#5","x":"sort","t":"Years of drought cut harvests","a":"Physical: chronic","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. A lasting change.","i":"cfe66fc04b"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#6","x":"sort","t":"A new carbon tax raises costs","a":"Transition","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Policy risk from moving to a lower-carbon economy.","i":"f71dffb22d"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#7","x":"sort","t":"Customers switch to brands seen as greener","a":"Transition","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Market and reputational risk.","i":"77a2106620"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#8","x":"sort","t":"A company's waste harms a river","a":"Impact","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. How it affects the planet: inside-out.","i":"bbed446aa2"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#9","x":"sort","t":"A brewery needs a reliable water supply","a":"Dependency","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. A resource it relies on.","i":"a30fe97065"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#10","x":"sort","t":"The board's committee reviews climate risk each quarter","a":"Governance","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Who oversees it, and how.","i":"9588e9b929"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#11","x":"sort","t":"Testing the business plan against climate scenarios","a":"Strategy","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Resilience of the strategy.","i":"d1f0f2965e"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#12","x":"sort","t":"How risks are rated and prioritised","a":"Risk management","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. The process.","i":"b8472de73a"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#13","x":"sort","t":"Percentage of energy from renewable sources","a":"Metrics and targets","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Measured performance.","i":"a2e55e4d68"},{"s":"sbr-22-compare-3","c":"sbr-22-compare-3#14","x":"sort","t":"A report shows a good plant and a fined plant with equal prominence","a":"Neutral","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Balance prevents greenwashing.","i":"3956334bf9"}],"i":"487d60912c"}],"i":"690bef4649"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"22.W1 Ratios, EPS and APMs","s":"sbr-22-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"SBR places less emphasis on calculating ratios than Financial Reporting does.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. Explain what the figures mean; the calculations are a tool.","i":"d7a8a2c277"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"A private company that chooses to disclose EPS must calculate it under IAS 33.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. IAS 33 applies to listed entities and to anyone choosing to disclose EPS.","i":"d1691d1a8e"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"A bonus issue raises EPS because share capital goes up.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. No cash comes in and earnings don't change, so more shares means lower EPS.","i":"c228c3eb05"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"A share issue at full price means the comparative EPS must be restated.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Only bonus elements restate comparatives; a full-price issue is just time-weighted.","i":"eac167a1fc"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A rights issue below market price has a bonus element.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. So a bonus fraction applies to the shares before the issue.","i":"ef584507ca"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"Operating profit can replace profit before financing and income taxes in ROCE only if there is no investment income.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. With no investment income the two figures are the same.","i":"a6cda8be0a"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"A long payables period is always a bad sign.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. It's free finance, though taking too long can mean credit being reduced or withdrawn.","i":"d8d34c4b47"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"An upward revaluation can make ROCE worse and gearing better at once.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Higher depreciation cuts profit and higher equity raises capital employed, while gearing falls.","i":"bee83fcbab"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#8","x":"trap","t":"Accounting policy choices change the ratios and also change cash generation.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. Choices and estimates alter the picture, not the cash.","i":"3441a8b59e"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#9","x":"trap","t":"Depreciation is safe to add back in an APM because it is non-cash.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. The assets still have to be replaced, so it's a real cost over time.","i":"1267e86fb1"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#10","x":"trap","t":"Excluding an impairment from an APM can hide a poor past decision.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. An impairment may show management overpaid or that prospects are weaker.","i":"869e33c959"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-1","c":"sbr-22-watch-1#11","x":"trap","t":"Gains on financial assets at FVPL are always one-off.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. Entities trading such assets repeat them, so excluding them isn't automatically right.","i":"95fa22df10"}],"i":"ae3040a2df"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.W2 Sustainability and commentary","s":"sbr-22-watch-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#0","x":"trap","t":"The ISSB requires every listed company to apply IFRS S1 and S2.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. They're voluntary unless a jurisdiction requires them.","i":"6adea8cbf6"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#1","x":"trap","t":"IFRS S1 disclosures must cover the same period as the financial statements.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. They're also released at the same time, with comparatives.","i":"4b394ef861"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#2","x":"trap","t":"Sustainability disclosures may leave out comparatives in any year.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information; IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Comparatives are required for all amounts disclosed.","i":"a5e6e8e23d"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#3","x":"trap","t":"A climate risk that wouldn't affect the entity's prospects is outside IFRS S2.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. The standards focus on what matters to primary users.","i":"7643f071a3"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#4","x":"trap","t":"IFRS S2 requires Scope 3 emissions whether or not they are material.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Scope 3 is mandatory if material to the entity's carbon footprint.","i":"72d06e5752"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#5","x":"trap","t":"An entity may omit Scope 3 emissions in its first year of applying IFRS S2.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. A transition relief, given how hard they are to calculate.","i":"dc1539bc5d"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#6","x":"trap","t":"Being hard to collect is a valid reason to omit Scope 3 after the first year.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. If material, they're required; omission makes the report incomplete.","i":"c9e91df5f3"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#7","x":"trap","t":"A net emissions target can be disclosed without the gross target.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. The gross target and the reliance on credits must also be disclosed.","i":"a886b70d3e"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#8","x":"trap","t":"IFRS S1 and S2 disclosures must have statutory assurance.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. None is required, which is a key criticism.","i":"5fa63a08d7"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#9","x":"trap","t":"The Greenhouse Gas Protocol is an examinable document in SBR.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information; IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. It's named for context only.","i":"b384a04958"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#10","x":"trap","t":"Management commentary is mandatory under IFRS Accounting Standards.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. The Practice Statement isn't mandatory, though entities are encouraged to use it.","i":"0e8123c6dc"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#11","x":"trap","t":"Management commentary can comment on the sustainability disclosures too.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. The 2025 revision links it to sustainability reporting.","i":"241f4c2173"},{"s":"sbr-22-watch-2","c":"sbr-22-watch-2#12","x":"trap","t":"Non-financial measures are encouraged in management commentary.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. They're typically absent from traditional financial reporting.","i":"21a23e42d5"}],"i":"5f64a7d280"}],"i":"04c9a4d19c"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"22.A1 Interpreting the numbers","s":"sbr-22-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Holt has 1,000,000 shares. On 1 April it makes a 1 for 4 rights issue at $2.00, when the market price is $4.00. Profit for the year to 31 December is $900,000. What is basic EPS?","a":"74.1c","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Step 1 (theoretical price): (4 × $4.00 + $2.00) / 5 = $3.60. Step 2 (bonus fraction): 4.00 / 3.60. Step 3 (shares): 1,000,000 × 3/12 × 4.00/3.60 = 277,778; 1,250,000 × 9/12 = 937,500; total 1,215,278. Step 4: $900,000 / 1,215,278 = 74.1c. Users: EPS is comparable with other companies only if the bonus element is handled properly. Common mistake: leaving out the bonus fraction gives 75.8c; ignoring time gives 72.0c.","i":"8a07f3274c"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Tarn has 2,000,000 shares and makes a 1 for 4 bonus issue on 1 October. Profit is $1,000,000. Last year's reported EPS was 40.0c. What are this year's EPS and the restated comparative?","a":"40.0c and 32.0c","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Rule: bonus shares count as always in issue, so no time-weighting of the new shares and the comparative is restated. Applied: $1,000,000 / 2,500,000 = 40.0c; comparative 40.0c × 4/5 = 32.0c. Users: restating keeps the trend comparable: EPS actually grew. Common mistake: time-weighting the bonus shares as if they brought new money.","i":"c8957c9af7"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Bexley's operating profit is $80m, investment income $10m, equity $300m, loans $150m and interest expense $15m. What are ROCE and interest cover?","a":"20.0% and 6.0 times","d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. Rule: use profit before financing and income taxes when there is investment income. Applied: profit is $80m + $10m = $90m. ROCE = 90 / (300 + 150) = 20.0%. Interest cover = 90 / 15 = 6.0 times. Users: Lenders use interest cover to judge how safely profit covers interest. Common mistake: using operating profit, which gives 17.8% and 5.3 times.","i":"dea47b185f"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Cole and Dane are identical except that Cole uses the cost model and Dane has revalued its buildings. Dane's depreciation is higher and its equity much larger. What will a user see?","a":"Dane has lower ROCE but lower gearing, with no real difference between them","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment. Rule: policy choices change ratios without changing cash. Why: extra depreciation cuts profit, while the surplus raises capital employed and equity. Applied: ROCE falls and gearing falls. Users: compare like with like, or adjust, before concluding one is less efficient or less risky. Common mistake: judging performance from unadjusted ratios.","i":"132e59dbeb"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Ferro has recorded shares it must redeem in cash in three years as equity. What does correcting the error do?","a":"Gearing rises, and profit and EPS fall","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation. Rule: an obligation to pay cash makes the shares a financial liability. Why: their 'dividends' become interest in P/L. Applied: liabilities up and equity down, so gearing rises; interest reduces profit and EPS. Users: the error understated financial risk. Common mistake: looking only at the balance sheet.","i":"dcf8ef9573"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"A 75% subsidiary wrongly recognised an interest-free loan at face value, overstating group profit by $0.4m. How much do the group's EPS earnings fall?","a":"$0.3m","d":"Reference: IAS 33 Earnings per Share. Rule: EPS uses profit attributable to the parent's shareholders. Applied: $0.4m × 75% = $0.3m; the other $0.1m falls on the NCI. P/L category: A write-down of a financial asset goes to Investing. Common mistake: taking the whole adjustment to EPS.","i":"9bd4d1bc43"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#6","x":"example","t":"Operating profit is $50m but cash from operations before tax is only $12m, with inventories and receivables up $40m. The entity also sold PPE for $25m and issued shares for $30m to repay loans. What should an investor conclude?","a":"Low-quality profit; one-off sources are funding it","d":"Reference: IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; Kaplan, interpreting statements. Rule: good-quality profit turns into cash. Why: disposals and share issues can't recur for ever. Applied: only $12m of $50m became cash, and working capital absorbed the rest; loan repayment relied on new shares. Users: ask whether future repayments can be met from trading. Common mistake: being reassured by a rise in cash.","i":"6d23ef9a66"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#7","x":"example","t":"Lark's cash from operating activities is $120m, capital expenditure $45m, and dividends paid $20m. What is free cash flow, as described in the text?","a":"$75m","d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. Rule: free cash flow is operating cash less capital expenditure. Applied: 120 − 45 = $75m. Users: shows what's left for dividends and debt, but it's an APM, so definitions vary. Common mistake: deducting dividends too.","i":"659d7e2105"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#8","x":"example","t":"Mere reports 'underlying profit' of $30m after turning a $5m loss into a profit by adding back interest, depreciation, amortisation, a brand impairment and a foreign exchange loss on receivables. What's the best comment?","a":"It may mislead: those costs are real or near to being realised","d":"Reference: Kaplan, APMs. Rule: judge each adjustment. Why: assets must be replaced, an impairment signals weaker prospects or overpaying, and receivables losses will soon be realised. Applied: a reconciliation helps users, but the prominence of a loss-to-profit measure is a warning sign. Users: they should start from the IFRS figures. Common mistake: saying all APMs are bad, or accepting all of them.","i":"80c26a72c0"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#9","x":"example","t":"A social media company's net assets are a small fraction of its market value. Why, under IFRS Accounting Standards?","a":"Internally generated brands aren't recognised and platforms gain value with use","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets. Rule: IAS 38 stops internally generated brands being recognised. Why: assets used may belong to others, and platform value grows with users even while amortised. Users: they look to user numbers and revenue growth, and to other reports. Common mistake: concluding the shares must be overvalued.","i":"19d4b6055f"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-1","c":"sbr-22-apply-1#10","x":"example","t":"Lorne's profit fell, but product faults, helpline waits and staff turnover also fell. How should an investor read this?","a":"Future prospects may be better than profit suggests, if the data is reliable","d":"Reference: Kaplan, analysis and interpretation. Rule: non-financial measures show long-term drivers. Why: fewer faults cut repair costs and build loyalty; retained staff keep their experience. Applied: compare with the sector and ask about assurance. Common mistake: treating these indicators as guarantees.","i":"4879da73e3"}],"i":"edef8da48b"},{"x":"stage","t":"22.A2 Judging sustainability reports","s":"sbr-22-apply-2","k":[{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#0","x":"example","t":"A food group is deciding what to disclose under IFRS S1. Water scarcity in its main sourcing region could disrupt production; its office paper costs $50,000 (0.001% of revenue). What should be disclosed?","a":"The water risk only","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. Rule: relevant information is material and has predictive or confirmatory value. Applied: water scarcity affects future cash flows and the cost of capital; paper doesn't. Users: too much trivia hides what matters. Common mistake: thinking all sustainability data belongs in the report.","i":"c4c5b6b31c"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#1","x":"example","t":"A retailer's report shows its best plant's 95% renewable energy but leaves out a large fine at another plant. Which quality is breached?","a":"Neutrality, and it risks greenwashing","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information; IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Rule: faithful representation needs neutral information, without bias. Applied: showing positive and negative results with equal prominence avoids greenwashing. Users: they would underestimate regulatory risk. Common mistake: thinking accuracy of the figures shown is enough.","i":"bf4b04782d"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#2","x":"example","t":"A manufacturer in its third year of applying IFRS S2 reports Scopes 1 and 2 but omits Scope 3 because supplier data is hard to collect. Scope 3 is likely to be its largest source. Is this acceptable?","a":"No: material Scope 3 is required","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Rule: Scope 3 is mandatory if material; only the first year has relief. Applied: this is the third year, and for a manufacturer Scope 3 is likely to be material. Users: the report is incomplete and could mislead about transition risk. Common mistake: extending the first-year relief.","i":"703699e5d9"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#3","x":"example","t":"In its first year of applying IFRS S2, an entity leaves out Scope 3 emissions. Is that allowed?","a":"Yes: this is the transition relief","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Rule: IFRS S2 permits omitting Scope 3 in the first annual reporting period. Why: it recognises how complex the calculation is. Common mistake: forgetting that the relief ends after that year.","i":"bb95f3e28a"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#4","x":"example","t":"An airline announces 'net zero by 2035', reached mostly through bought carbon credits, and discloses only the net target. What is missing?","a":"The gross target, the reliance on credits and details of the credits","d":"Reference: IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Rule: IFRS S2 requires the gross target alongside a net target, the extent of reliance on credits, and credit details such as verification scheme and type. Why: investors must see offsets separately from real reductions. Users: heavy reliance leaves transition risk in place. Common mistake: treating a net target as proof that emissions fell.","i":"a9c0cafa1b"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#5","x":"example","t":"A company's report says risks 'may affect the business' and rates them all 'medium', with no explanation. It performs no scenario analysis because it is 'too speculative'. How useful is this?","a":"Limited: users can't assess the ratings or resilience","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information; IFRS S2 Climate-related Disclosures. Rule: disclosure needs substance in each core area. Applied: with no basis for the ratings, faithful representation suffers; with no scenario analysis, users can't test the strategy; no financial effects are given. Users: they can't link climate to future cash flows. Common mistake: ticking off headings rather than judging content.","i":"0f21f01459"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#6","x":"example","t":"Flooding risk may shorten the useful life of a factory, yet the sustainability report and the accounts use unconnected assumptions. Which IFRS S1 idea is this about?","a":"Connectivity","d":"Reference: IFRS S1 General Requirements for Disclosure of Sustainability-related Financial Information. Rule: IFRS S1 requires links between sustainability risks and the financial statements, with consistent data where possible. Applied: flood assumptions should feed useful lives and impairment tests. Common mistake: treating the two reports as separate exercises.","i":"1f9ee49d1e"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#7","x":"example","t":"An EU-listed company asks whether meeting IFRS S1 and S2 alone would give it the same materiality view as ESRS. What's the answer?","a":"No: ESRS also assess impact on people and the environment","d":"Reference: Kaplan, differences between ISSB standards and ESRS. Rule: ISSB standards use financial materiality; ESRS use double materiality. Users: ESRS serve a wider range of users. Common mistake: assuming interoperability means the two are identical.","i":"902fccaf6f"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#8","x":"example","t":"A company's management commentary repeats standard phrases used by its competitors and gives only historical figures. How does this fall short?","a":"It isn't tailored or forward-looking","d":"Reference: IFRS Practice Statement 1 Management Commentary. Rule: commentary should be entity-specific and forward-looking, covering six areas. Why: generic text adds clutter and isn't relevant. Applied: rewrite it around this entity's business model, risks and outlook. Common mistake: thinking it is mandatory or audited.","i":"b58234b61e"},{"s":"sbr-22-apply-2","c":"sbr-22-apply-2#9","x":"example","t":"A charity's surplus fell, but it treated far more patients for the same cost per patient. How should its performance be assessed?","a":"Against its mission, through outputs and outcomes","d":"Reference: Kaplan, not-for-profit entities. Rule: not-for-profit entities are judged on their goals and on value for money (economy, efficiency, effectiveness). Applied: more patients at the same unit cost is good performance. Common mistake: applying commercial profit measures.","i":"e42c496571"}],"i":"050dfc9434"}],"i":"1ae9936783"}],"i":"0e25481fd5"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 14: Segment reporting","ch":14,"d":"Study Text pages 373–382.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"14.1 IFRS 8 Operating segments","s":"sbr-14-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#0","x":"card","t":"IFRS 8 applies to entities whose debt or equity is traded in a public market","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Its aim: let users see the nature and effects of the different business activities.","i":"d7e643360d"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#1","x":"card","t":"An operating segment earns revenue and incurs expenses, has discrete information, and its results are reviewed regularly by the chief operating decision maker","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Segments follow internal management reports.","i":"3bcd512744"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#2","x":"card","t":"Head office and pension plans are not operating segments (they don't earn revenue)","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. They appear in reconciliations.","i":"960f8150aa"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#3","x":"card","t":"If management uses several sets of information (products and regions), the entity picks the one that best helps users understand the business","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Only one basis is reported.","i":"8bedb6731f"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#4","x":"card","t":"Segments can be combined if they have similar economics and are similar in products, processes, customers and distribution methods","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. E-Games: online at $30 vs stores at $40 → different margins, don't combine.","i":"90330f6e0b"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#5","x":"card","t":"A segment is reportable if its revenue, profit or loss, or assets are 10% or more of the total","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. For profit: 10% of the greater of total profits and total losses.","i":"3eb6adb689"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#6","x":"card","t":"Reportable segments must cover at least 75% of external revenue","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Add segments until reached; the rest go into 'all other segments'.","i":"0f394b334c"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#7","x":"card","t":"For each reportable segment, disclose a measure of profit or loss and total assets (and other items if reviewed by the CODM)","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Also explain how they are measured, since internal measures may differ from IFRS.","i":"1d18655510"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#8","x":"card","t":"Segment reports help investors because they show management's view and different risks and growth rates across the business","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Cheap to prepare, since the information already exists.","i":"8277f5193d"},{"s":"sbr-14-1","c":"sbr-14-1#9","x":"card","t":"Segment reports are limited because of inter-segment pricing, poor comparability, hidden loss-makers, over-aggregation and arbitrary allocation of common costs","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Users must trust management's choices.","i":"2d708783fc"}],"i":"23acb5eda2"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"14.S Finding reportable segments","s":"sbr-14-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-14-structure-1","c":"sbr-14-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"From operating segments to what is reported","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Management's own view drives the segments.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Identify components reviewed by the chief operating decision maker","i":"b0c451f4ae"},{"x":"step","t":"Consider combining those with similar economic characteristics","i":"bead3f32b7"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply the 10% tests for revenue, profit or loss, and assets","i":"9e94157125"},{"x":"step","t":"Check that reportable segments cover at least 75% of external revenue","i":"49e6551590"},{"x":"step","t":"Add segments until they do","i":"0d90b7ce2a"},{"x":"step","t":"Combine the rest as 'all other segments'","i":"ef55082ff6"}],"i":"c3ebb4e601"},{"s":"sbr-14-structure-1","c":"sbr-14-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"The profit or loss test","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. A loss-making segment can pass this test.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Add up the profits of all segments that made a profit","i":"3cab057f6b"},{"x":"step","t":"Add up the losses of all segments that made a loss","i":"2c64a7fe45"},{"x":"step","t":"Take the greater in absolute terms","i":"ecc14eeca5"},{"x":"step","t":"10% of that is the threshold","i":"d235bf06fd"},{"x":"step","t":"Compare each segment's profit or loss with it","i":"96bc4c3639"}],"i":"d337140ec6"},{"s":"sbr-14-structure-1","c":"sbr-14-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"Is a part of the business an operating segment?","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Corporate headquarters and pension plans fail the first test.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Does it earn, or may it earn, revenue and incur expenses?","i":"a238bb5219"},{"x":"step","t":"Are its results regularly reviewed by the chief operating decision maker?","i":"5b78fed79e"},{"x":"step","t":"Is discrete financial information available?","i":"ae7a13e55f"},{"x":"step","t":"If all yes, it's an operating segment","i":"4bd0576db1"}],"i":"0973063748"}],"i":"000d7deb04"}],"i":"f1151c02c7"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"14.C Side by side","s":"sbr-14-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"A division earning revenue, with results reviewed monthly by the CEO","a":"Operating segment","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. It meets the definition.","i":"af60dd7ed6"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Corporate headquarters","a":"Not an operating segment","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Earns no revenue.","i":"1737e45ec0"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"The entity's pension plan","a":"Not an operating segment","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Stated in IFRS 8.","i":"37301b78b3"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"A start-up division with no revenue yet, but reviewed regularly","a":"Operating segment","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. It may earn revenue.","i":"47b222dedd"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Revenue includes inter-segment sales for the test","a":"10% revenue test","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Total revenue.","i":"4f13389e3c"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Only sales to outside customers count","a":"75% coverage test","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. External revenue.","i":"3230c52999"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"Similar products, customers and distribution, and similar margins","a":"Can be aggregated","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. All the criteria are met.","i":"64de00d094"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Same products sold at very different prices in stores and online","a":"Can't be aggregated","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Different economic characteristics.","i":"246be5222c"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Shows how management sees the business","a":"Benefit of IFRS 8","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Investors want this view.","i":"9fdba8003c"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Cheap, as the information already exists internally","a":"Benefit of IFRS 8","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Benefits exceed cost.","i":"51b35cb980"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Helps predict growth and risk in different areas","a":"Benefit of IFRS 8","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Better forecasting.","i":"2a111cdfe6"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"Different entities define segments differently","a":"Criticism of IFRS 8","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Poor comparability.","i":"ef9ca7796f"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#12","x":"sort","t":"Common costs can be allocated arbitrarily","a":"Criticism of IFRS 8","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Results can be distorted.","i":"aa4018c0dd"},{"s":"sbr-14-compare-1","c":"sbr-14-compare-1#13","x":"sort","t":"Loss-making areas can be hidden inside larger segments","a":"Criticism of IFRS 8","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Trust in management.","i":"5661b6c7d9"}],"i":"768a326546"}],"i":"70a949aec2"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"14.W Segment traps","s":"sbr-14-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-14-watch-1","c":"sbr-14-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"IFRS 8 applies to all entities.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Those that trade debt or equity in a public market.","i":"7ba95ce8f4"},{"s":"sbr-14-watch-1","c":"sbr-14-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Segments follow internal management reports.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Even if on a different basis from the statements.","i":"481d3db1c0"},{"s":"sbr-14-watch-1","c":"sbr-14-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Segment information must follow IFRS measurement rules.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. It follows what management uses, with explanations.","i":"79b008c29c"},{"s":"sbr-14-watch-1","c":"sbr-14-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"A segment passing any one 10% test must be reported.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. One is enough.","i":"b5fbdc3dda"},{"s":"sbr-14-watch-1","c":"sbr-14-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A loss-making segment can't be reportable on the profit test.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. The test uses absolute amounts.","i":"bc2d6f76f6"},{"s":"sbr-14-watch-1","c":"sbr-14-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"If reportable segments cover 70% of external revenue, more must be identified.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. At least 75%.","i":"fd2db8e831"},{"s":"sbr-14-watch-1","c":"sbr-14-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"Management can combine segments simply because it finds this simpler.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. They need similar economic characteristics and other similarities.","i":"8922b9cf70"},{"s":"sbr-14-watch-1","c":"sbr-14-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Trading between segments can distort segment results.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Especially if not at fair value.","i":"44092831ba"}],"i":"8e5e76dc80"}],"i":"87471c2d42"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"14.A Segment scenarios","s":"sbr-14-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-14-apply-1","c":"sbr-14-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Total revenue (with internal), profit or (loss), assets and external revenue: N 400, 90, 3,000, 300; S 300, 60, 2,500, 200; E 90, 8, 400, 80; W 110, (45), 600, 100; C 60, 12, 1,200, 60; X 40, (5), 300, 40. Which are reportable?","a":"N, S, W and C","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Step 1 (revenue): 10% of 1,000 is 100: N, S, W. Step 2 (profit): profits 170 exceed losses 50, so the limit is 17: N, S, W. Step 3 (assets): 10% of 8,000 is 800: N, S, C. Step 4 (75%): those four have 660 of 780 external revenue, above 585. Common mistake: forgetting that any one test is enough.","i":"359afefcaa"},{"s":"sbr-14-apply-1","c":"sbr-14-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"A retailer sells the same goods online at lower prices than in its stores. Internal reports show them apart. It wants to report one segment. Is that allowed?","a":"No: economic characteristics differ","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Rule: aggregation needs similar economic characteristics and similar products, processes, customers and distribution. Applied: prices and likely margins differ. Users: they lose sight of different growth and risk. Common mistake: looking only at the products.","i":"656a26276a"},{"s":"sbr-14-apply-1","c":"sbr-14-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A company merges a loss-making division into a large profitable segment, though the CEO reviews it separately. What's the concern?","a":"It hides losses, and may breach IFRS 8","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Rule: segments follow what is reviewed, and aggregation has criteria. Ethics: hiding the loss would mislead investors. Common mistake: thinking that total profit is all that matters.","i":"becee79d34"},{"s":"sbr-14-apply-1","c":"sbr-14-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"An investor compares two retailers' segment notes and finds different segment structures. What's the best comment?","a":"Segments reflect each management's view, so comparing is hard","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Rule: segments follow internal reporting. Users: it's good for insight into one entity, and weaker for comparing entities. Common mistake: expecting uniform segments.","i":"af570cad5d"},{"s":"sbr-14-apply-1","c":"sbr-14-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Reportable segments so far have 70% of external revenue. There are two other small segments, each below the 10% tests. What's done?","a":"Treat more as reportable until 75% is reached","d":"Reference: IFRS 8 Operating Segments. Rule: at least 75% of external revenue must be in reportable segments. Applied: add segments, even if they fail the tests. Common mistake: thinking that passing the tests is the only route.","i":"026326789c"}],"i":"f564471794"}],"i":"22b8ceda12"}],"i":"1f265fe549"}],"i":"275f017593"},{"x":"area","a":"F","t":"F · Changes in accounting regulation","k":[{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 16: Adopting new accounting standards","ch":16,"d":"Study Text pages 397–404.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"16.1 IFRS 1 and adopting new standards","s":"sbr-16-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#0","x":"card","t":"A first-time adopter is an entity making its first explicit and unreserved statement of IFRS compliance","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. IFRS 1 sets out how to switch from local GAAP.","i":"22c79bd436"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#1","x":"card","t":"The date of transition is the start of the earliest period with full IFRS comparatives","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. First IFRS year to 31.12.20X8 with one comparative → 1.1.20X7.","i":"baef4b3d94"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#2","x":"card","t":"An opening IFRS SoFP is prepared at the transition date; it need not be published","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. It recognises all IFRS assets and liabilities, removes others, reclassifies and remeasures.","i":"4ad89e6de5"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#3","x":"card","t":"All periods presented use policies based on the IFRSs in force at the reporting date","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Early application of new standards is allowed if permitted.","i":"f6026a52c4"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#4","x":"card","t":"Estimates at transition must match those made under old GAAP unless there's evidence they were wrong","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Nat (iii): understated but reasonable estimates → not errors; adjust prospectively.","i":"980d696720"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#5","x":"card","t":"Adjustments on adopting IFRS go to retained earnings, not P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Nat: $5m advance orders become a contract liability; an invalid $1m provision is removed.","i":"c88b2ede95"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#6","x":"card","t":"In the first IFRS statements, entities explain how the switch affects position, performance and cash flows","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Errors found must be shown separately.","i":"b80fea5584"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#7","x":"card","t":"Fair value at transition can be used as deemed cost for PPE, intangibles and investment property","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Disclose the fair values and the adjustments.","i":"56c7e5aa4c"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#8","x":"card","t":"IFRS 1 exemptions include not restating past business combinations, deeming translation differences nil and keeping borrowing costs capitalised under old GAAP","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Given where cost would outweigh benefit.","i":"4811fd0f63"},{"s":"sbr-16-1","c":"sbr-16-1#9","x":"card","t":"Before adopting any new standard, consider the transition rules, bonuses, IT systems, loan covenants, EPS, perception and staff knowledge and training","d":"Reference: Kaplan, implications of adopting new standards. E.g. IFRS 16 put leases on balance sheets and broke some covenants.","i":"e8432f2147"}],"i":"7768aba9c8"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"16.S In order","s":"sbr-16-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-16-structure-1","c":"sbr-16-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Adopting IFRS for the first time","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. The opening statement needn't be published.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Find the date of transition: the start of the earliest comparative period","i":"ee86eeefcf"},{"x":"step","t":"Prepare an opening statement of financial position at that date","i":"2614c542c1"},{"x":"step","t":"Recognise, derecognise, reclassify and measure items under IFRS","i":"e8a36559e3"},{"x":"step","t":"Take adjustments to retained earnings","i":"e77189833f"},{"x":"step","t":"Apply any exemptions","i":"70071bde4d"},{"x":"step","t":"Explain the effect on performance, position and cash flows","i":"1f5b9d2f8d"}],"i":"b65c17aa53"},{"s":"sbr-16-structure-1","c":"sbr-16-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Preparing for a new standard","d":"Reference: Kaplan, implications of adopting new standards. Ratios can deteriorate even if nothing has changed in the business. Not part of it: Delay applying it until loan covenants are renegotiated; Restate prior years only if the effect is favourable.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Read the transitional guidance","i":"09b382c511"},{"x":"step","t":"If there's none, apply IAS 8","i":"b7383e7117"},{"x":"step","t":"Estimate the effect on profit, EPS and bonuses","i":"c9c816a6d8"},{"x":"step","t":"Check loan covenants, and talk to lenders if needed","i":"9da4829c2b"},{"x":"step","t":"Check that IT systems and staff knowledge are adequate","i":"d19a4a94f4"}],"any":true,"i":"f44b684ee3"}],"i":"9d53be7a02"}],"i":"55135b1958"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"16.C Side by side","s":"sbr-16-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"A gain from recognising a derivative at the date of transition","a":"Retained earnings","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Adjustments on adoption.","i":"14a56b4c70"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"A provision removed because it doesn't meet IAS 37","a":"Retained earnings","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Adjustments on adoption.","i":"ceb207add6"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Extra expense as a reasonable estimate later proves low","a":"Profit or loss","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. A change in estimate, prospectively.","i":"5c9e468784"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Previous business combinations","a":"Exemption available","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Needn't be restated.","i":"ec4da922f2"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"Past translation differences on an overseas subsidiary","a":"Exemption available","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Can be deemed nil.","i":"1822d4f8c2"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Borrowing costs capitalised before transition","a":"Exemption available","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Needn't be restated.","i":"99b0543fb6"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"A derivative not previously recognised","a":"Must apply IFRS in full","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. It must be recognised.","i":"2c58685bd3"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"Fair value of PPE used as deemed cost","a":"Allowed, with disclosure","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Aggregate and adjustments are disclosed.","i":"eaf49acda9"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"Different policies in the comparative year","a":"Not allowed","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Same policies for all periods presented.","i":"50620dd5c8"},{"s":"sbr-16-compare-1","c":"sbr-16-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Applying a new standard early, where it permits this","a":"Allowed, with disclosure","d":"Reference: Kaplan, implications of adopting new standards. It helps entities plan the change.","i":"a8641928be"}],"i":"d0d4fd6be8"}],"i":"c9c375ccc9"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"16.W Traps","s":"sbr-16-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-16-watch-1","c":"sbr-16-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"With one comparative, first IFRS statements for 20X8 mean a transition date of 1 January 20X7.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. The first day of the comparative period.","i":"e840703943"},{"s":"sbr-16-watch-1","c":"sbr-16-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"The opening IFRS statement of financial position must be published.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. It must be prepared, but needn't be published.","i":"361cd2930d"},{"s":"sbr-16-watch-1","c":"sbr-16-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Adjustments on first-time adoption go through profit or loss.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. They go directly to retained earnings.","i":"2b8dc04e35"},{"s":"sbr-16-watch-1","c":"sbr-16-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"Transition estimates can be changed with the benefit of hindsight.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Unless evidence shows they were wrong.","i":"de61051836"},{"s":"sbr-16-watch-1","c":"sbr-16-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"Errors found on transition must be disclosed separately.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. So they aren't confused with policy changes.","i":"6f05d67eb7"},{"s":"sbr-16-watch-1","c":"sbr-16-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"Adopting a new standard can affect bonuses.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, implications of adopting new standards. They're often linked to profit.","i":"9e0a19f4ce"},{"s":"sbr-16-watch-1","c":"sbr-16-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"Loan covenants can be affected by a new standard.","v":true,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, implications of adopting new standards. Talk to the bank before ratios deteriorate.","i":"b69595463a"},{"s":"sbr-16-watch-1","c":"sbr-16-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"Investors usually see a move to IFRS negatively.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, implications of adopting new standards. It's generally welcomed: comparability improves.","i":"41240e3c5a"}],"i":"6ab34fbfc5"}],"i":"2fecbc734c"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"16.A Scenarios","s":"sbr-16-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-16-apply-1","c":"sbr-16-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"Nash's first IFRS statements are for the year to 31 December 20X5, with one comparative. What's the date of transition?","a":"1 January 20X4","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards. Rule: the start of the earliest period with full comparatives. Common mistake: using the start of the first IFRS year.","i":"d206006114"},{"s":"sbr-16-apply-1","c":"sbr-16-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"Nash took $3m of advance orders as revenue before the transition date, though nothing had been sent by then. What's the entry at transition?","a":"Contract liability $3m; retained earnings down $3m","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers. Rule: the opening statement applies IFRS 15, and adjustments go to retained earnings. Applied: control hadn't passed. Common mistake: going through P/L.","i":"32a6a24312"},{"s":"sbr-16-apply-1","c":"sbr-16-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"Nash has a $0.5m restructuring provision that doesn't meet IAS 37. What's the entry at transition?","a":"Remove it; retained earnings up $0.5m","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: don't recognise liabilities IFRS doesn't permit. Common mistake: keeping old GAAP balances.","i":"9cd63c37b9"},{"s":"sbr-16-apply-1","c":"sbr-16-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"Nash's reasonable estimate of accruals later turns out to be too low. How's that treated?","a":"Prospectively, in the comparative year's P/L","d":"Reference: IFRS 1 First-time Adoption of International Financial Reporting Standards; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: estimates are consistent with old GAAP unless shown wrong. Applied: reasonable at the time, so not an error. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: restating with hindsight.","i":"91675047d5"},{"s":"sbr-16-apply-1","c":"sbr-16-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"A new standard will cut profit and raise gearing for a company near its covenant limits. What should be done first?","a":"Assess the effect and talk to lenders","d":"Reference: Kaplan, implications of adopting new standards. Rule: adopting new standards needs planning for covenants, bonuses and EPS. Ethics: a standard can't be avoided because it's unwelcome. Common mistake: dealing with it after adoption.","i":"22b968df2e"}],"i":"4b7b10de43"}],"i":"9b07b56e83"}],"i":"a589ab9cef"},{"x":"chapter","t":"Chapter 23: Changes and developments in financial reporting","ch":23,"d":"Study Text pages 633–642.","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"23.1 Current issues","s":"sbr-23-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#0","x":"card","t":"Cryptocurrency isn't cash because it can't readily be exchanged for goods and services","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Nor a cash equivalent: its value is too volatile.","i":"3b0679b9d4"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#1","x":"card","t":"Cryptocurrency isn't a financial asset because it isn't equity of another entity or a contractual right to cash","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. So IFRS 9 doesn't apply.","i":"3cecf88c6d"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#2","x":"card","t":"Cryptocurrency most likely falls under IAS 38, as an identifiable non-monetary asset without substance","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. But neither IAS 38 model fits well.","i":"363d70f9aa"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#3","x":"card","t":"IAS 38 suits crypto badly because cost isn't relevant and revaluation puts gains in OCI, while traders want them in P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. It's still IAS 38 (or IAS 2 for a trader); the Framework explains why fair value through P/L would be more useful for trading.","i":"1fbf910c10"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#4","x":"card","t":"An ICO raises money by issuing tokens, and the credit entry depends on what the token gives the holder","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Financial liability, equity, revenue (customer contract), provision, or an IAS 8 policy.","i":"7dcdfbc9d2"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#5","x":"card","t":"After a natural disaster, assets need an impairment review, destroyed PPE is derecognised and inventory is written down to NRV if needed","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Lenders also check for higher credit risk (lifetime ECL).","i":"2d5835c210"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#6","x":"card","t":"An insurance recovery is recognised only when virtually certain (disclosed if probable)","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. A high bar under IAS 37.","i":"e31d36f393"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#7","x":"card","t":"A disaster-related restructuring provision needs a detailed plan announced or started, with direct costs only","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Decommissioning and environmental provisions may also need review.","i":"ff2f5d3032"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#8","x":"card","t":"A disaster can stop deferred tax assets being recognised because future taxable profits become less likely","d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. Going concern and loan covenants must be reassessed too.","i":"0241d5b869"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#9","x":"card","t":"A pandemic-hit retailer should test stores and its website for impairment, make no provision for future losses or unplanned closures, and review going concern and loan classification","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Middleshop example.","i":"f0a2b1b2de"},{"s":"sbr-23-1","c":"sbr-23-1#10","x":"card","t":"Cryptocurrency held for sale in the ordinary course of business is inventory under IAS 2 (a broker-trader: at fair value less costs to sell)","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Otherwise it's an intangible asset under IAS 38.","i":"26db3a7622"}],"i":"3a067f023c"},{"x":"group","t":"Steps and workings","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"23.S Working through current issues","s":"sbr-23-structure-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-23-structure-1","c":"sbr-23-structure-1#0","x":"steps","t":"Accounting for a cryptocurrency holding","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Disclose the judgements made; the Framework explains why fair value through P/L would suit trading.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Is it cash? No: not readily exchangeable","i":"73858eb92b"},{"x":"step","t":"Is it a cash equivalent? No: value changes significantly","i":"02a33d6d94"},{"x":"step","t":"Is it a financial asset? No: no contractual right or equity","i":"77615502c8"},{"x":"step","t":"Is it held for sale in the ordinary course of business? Then IAS 2 (a broker-trader: fair value less costs to sell)","i":"55c39a4579"},{"x":"step","t":"Otherwise it's an intangible asset under IAS 38: cost, or revaluation through OCI","i":"5770a6dfed"}],"i":"325f9003ab"},{"s":"sbr-23-structure-1","c":"sbr-23-structure-1#1","x":"steps","t":"Deciding the credit entry for an ICO","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. It depends on what the token gives the holder.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Record the cash or crypto received","i":"5f88f9149e"},{"x":"step","t":"Must the entity deliver cash or a financial asset? Financial liability","i":"856381cd2b"},{"x":"step","t":"Does the holder share in distributable reserves, with no obligation? Equity","i":"e179fcb3dc"},{"x":"step","t":"Is the holder a customer under a contract? Revenue","i":"3c315be28c"},{"x":"step","t":"Is there a legal or constructive obligation? Provision","i":"311922ddb1"},{"x":"step","t":"Otherwise use IAS 8 and the Framework","i":"3e4456e73a"}],"i":"6cf788f856"},{"s":"sbr-23-structure-1","c":"sbr-23-structure-1#2","x":"steps","t":"A natural disaster: the financial reporting checklist","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. No single standard covers it. Not part of it: Provide for next year's expected trading losses; Recognise an insurance claim that's only probable.","k":[{"x":"step","t":"Derecognise destroyed assets and test others for impairment","i":"e3341b846f"},{"x":"step","t":"Reassess credit risk and inventory values","i":"c5af1d7d27"},{"x":"step","t":"Consider insurance: asset only if virtually certain","i":"46949a7ada"},{"x":"step","t":"Provide only for present obligations","i":"52ae079f79"},{"x":"step","t":"Reassess deferred tax assets","i":"71235ef5f7"},{"x":"step","t":"Assess going concern and covenants","i":"1e2a6a2d0a"}],"any":true,"i":"eb1a2f8777"}],"i":"32dfa6c7c4"}],"i":"1ca69ab968"},{"x":"group","t":"Side by side","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"23.C Side by side","s":"sbr-23-compare-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#0","x":"sort","t":"Token requiring the issuer to pay cash to holders","a":"Financial liability","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. A contractual obligation.","i":"2be08094df"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#1","x":"sort","t":"Token giving a share of distributable reserves, with no obligation to pay","a":"Equity","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Like shares.","i":"ba8bdc65e6"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#2","x":"sort","t":"Token giving a customer access to a service under a contract","a":"Revenue","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. IFRS 15.","i":"cda65bcdb9"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#3","x":"sort","t":"Token with a promise that fits no standard","a":"Provision or IAS 8","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. A constructive obligation or the IAS 8 hierarchy.","i":"2c0a41ea0d"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#4","x":"sort","t":"A factory destroyed in an earthquake","a":"Derecognise","d":"Reference: IAS 16 Property, Plant and Equipment; IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. No more benefits are expected.","i":"58761b77e9"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#5","x":"sort","t":"Stores open again, but demand has collapsed","a":"Test for impairment","d":"Reference: IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. An indicator.","i":"1ad33a42f0"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#6","x":"sort","t":"An insurance recovery that's virtually certain","a":"Recognise","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. No longer contingent.","i":"511c780445"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#7","x":"sort","t":"An insurance recovery that's probable","a":"Disclose only","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Contingent asset.","i":"d33a28b9b6"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#8","x":"sort","t":"An insurance recovery that's only possible","a":"Ignore","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Not disclosed.","i":"d9cda02ee9"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#9","x":"sort","t":"Planned closures with no firm plan at the year end","a":"Don't provide","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. No obligation.","i":"a4eb1fbdde"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#10","x":"sort","t":"Next year's expected trading losses","a":"Don't provide","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. No obligation.","i":"9dc6f62f8e"},{"s":"sbr-23-compare-1","c":"sbr-23-compare-1#11","x":"sort","t":"A detailed plan announced before the year end","a":"Provide","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Direct costs only.","i":"0794f070b8"}],"i":"f14c28cfaa"}],"i":"f80e5a3055"},{"x":"group","t":"Traps","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"23.W Traps","s":"sbr-23-watch-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-23-watch-1","c":"sbr-23-watch-1#0","x":"trap","t":"Cryptocurrency meets the definition of cash.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. It can't be readily exchanged for goods and services.","i":"a7369c1b58"},{"s":"sbr-23-watch-1","c":"sbr-23-watch-1#1","x":"trap","t":"Cryptocurrency qualifies as a cash equivalent.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 7 Statement of Cash Flows; IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. It's subject to significant changes in value.","i":"0caa7fdfa7"},{"s":"sbr-23-watch-1","c":"sbr-23-watch-1#2","x":"trap","t":"Under IAS 38's revaluation model, crypto gains would go to OCI.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. One reason it fits badly.","i":"ed78449b54"},{"s":"sbr-23-watch-1","c":"sbr-23-watch-1#3","x":"trap","t":"An ICO's credit entry is always a liability.","v":false,"d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. It depends on the token's terms.","i":"1689429c89"},{"s":"sbr-23-watch-1","c":"sbr-23-watch-1#4","x":"trap","t":"A disaster can make deferred tax assets unrecoverable.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 12 Income Taxes. If future taxable profits fall.","i":"5c967c0583"},{"s":"sbr-23-watch-1","c":"sbr-23-watch-1#5","x":"trap","t":"Disasters can require decommissioning provisions to be reviewed.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Timing or amounts of cash flows may change.","i":"0753c6e810"},{"s":"sbr-23-watch-1","c":"sbr-23-watch-1#6","x":"trap","t":"There's a single IFRS covering climate change.","v":false,"d":"Reference: Kaplan, key financial reporting issues. Existing standards apply where the effect is material.","i":"5a30caed37"},{"s":"sbr-23-watch-1","c":"sbr-23-watch-1#7","x":"trap","t":"A covenant breach after a disaster can raise going concern questions.","v":true,"d":"Reference: IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Material uncertainties must be disclosed.","i":"21f26ae389"}],"i":"31dbad8c1e"}],"i":"332be2dd77"},{"x":"group","t":"Worked examples","k":[{"x":"stage","t":"23.A Scenarios","s":"sbr-23-apply-1","k":[{"s":"sbr-23-apply-1","c":"sbr-23-apply-1#0","x":"example","t":"A broker-trader buys and sells Bitcoin for customers in the ordinary course of its business. How does it measure the Bitcoin it holds?","a":"As inventory at fair value less costs to sell, through P/L","d":"Reference: IAS 38 Intangible Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: crypto isn't cash or a financial asset; it's an intangible asset under IAS 38, unless it's held for sale in the ordinary course of business, when IAS 2 applies. Applied: a commodity broker-trader can measure that inventory at fair value less costs to sell, with changes in P/L. A company holding Bitcoin as an investment would use IAS 38 instead: cost, or revaluation through OCI if there's an active market. Common mistake: calling it cash or a financial asset because it's traded.","i":"504d67039a"},{"s":"sbr-23-apply-1","c":"sbr-23-apply-1#1","x":"example","t":"An entity sells tokens giving holders a right to future cash from a project. What's the credit?","a":"Financial liability","d":"Reference: IAS 32 Financial Instruments: Presentation; IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers; IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 8 Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements. Rule: a contractual obligation to deliver cash is a financial liability. Users: gearing rises. Common mistake: taking it to revenue because the entity is a start-up.","i":"aef91c1094"},{"s":"sbr-23-apply-1","c":"sbr-23-apply-1#2","x":"example","t":"A retailer has store closures mandated by a pandemic, and weak online sales. The directors want to provide for redundancies and next year's losses, with no firm plan. What's right?","a":"Impairment review and going concern, but no provisions","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets; IAS 36 Impairment of Assets. Rule: no obligation without a detailed announced plan, and future losses are never provided for. Applied: stores and website are tested for impairment; loans and going concern are reviewed. Common mistake: taking a 'big bath' in the bad year.","i":"5ee413ebb0"},{"s":"sbr-23-apply-1","c":"sbr-23-apply-1#3","x":"example","t":"A flood damages a warehouse. The insurer has accepted the claim and agreed $2m, to be paid next month. What's the treatment?","a":"Recognise a $2m asset","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: recognise an insurance asset when receipt is virtually certain. Applied: an accepted, agreed claim meets this. P/L category: Operating. Common mistake: waiting for the cash.","i":"037750d3f3"},{"s":"sbr-23-apply-1","c":"sbr-23-apply-1#4","x":"example","t":"Another claim is being disputed, though lawyers think success is probable. What's the treatment?","a":"Disclose a contingent asset","d":"Reference: IAS 37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets. Rule: probable inflows are disclosed, not recognised. Common mistake: recognising because the loss has been recognised.","i":"f13b8dee37"},{"s":"sbr-23-apply-1","c":"sbr-23-apply-1#5","x":"example","t":"After a drought, a lender's farm loans look much riskier. What should it do?","a":"Reassess credit risk; lifetime ECL if significantly higher","d":"Reference: IFRS 9 Financial Instruments. Rule: under IFRS 9, forward-looking information is used. Applied: a significant increase moves the allowance to lifetime ECL. Common mistake: waiting for a default.","i":"ec29c3ed88"}],"i":"4bc7788416"}],"i":"c34bed1ed5"}],"i":"efaf919694"}],"i":"662284cd2a"},{"x":"area","a":"G","t":"G · Employability and technology skills","k":[{"x":"group","t":"Use the exam software well: the word processor, the spreadsheet and the scratch pad","i":"c00bd9576d"},{"x":"group","t":"Practise on ACCA's Practice Platform, above all Question 1's spreadsheet","i":"f5ff627f62"}],"i":"75ac310023"}],"i":"e1aac6a0fa"};
